window.SHOTLIST={"lessons":[{"w":2,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 2.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 7 & 8)","file":"lesson-2-1-frisby-ch7-8.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-2-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 2.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 7 & 8). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track conflict and rising action. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 7 & 8, watch the conflict get sharper. Mrs. Frisby climbs the owl’s tree in the dark, and the owl is ready to send her away — until he hears her husband’s name. What does she want, and what is standing in her way? What new thing does she have to do because of the owl’s advice?","Mrs. Frisby climbs the owl's tree in the dark, terrified. The owl is ready to dismiss her — until she says her husband's name. What changes in the owl's attitude when he hears \"Jonathan Frisby,\" and what does he tell her to do?","Mrs. Frisby follows the owl's advice and heads toward the rosebush near the Fitzgibbon farm. Why is she puzzled by the owl's reaction to Jonathan's name, and what questions is she carrying as she approaches the rats?"],"chapterQs":["Jeremy flies Mrs. Frisby to the owl's tree at dusk. Use details from Chapter 7 to describe two things that make the visit frightening for Mrs. Frisby — one about the journey and one about the owl himself.","The owl's attitude toward Mrs. Frisby changes completely when she mentions her husband's name. How does he act before and after hearing \"Jonathan Frisby\"? Use at least one detail from the text for each.","At the end of Chapter 8, Mrs. Frisby heads toward the rosebush to find the rats. What questions is she wondering about, and why does the owl's advice feel strange to her?"],"focus":"Today: PLAN your story. Even a true story needs a SHAPE — a beginning, a middle with rising action, and an ending that shows what you learned.","nowTry":"Write below. Brainstorm one story idea of your own in the form: \"This story is about a character who has a problem .\" Write one sentence."},"words":[{"w":"enormous","d":"Extremely large in size; huge.","e":"🦉"},{"w":"intimidated","d":"Feeling frightened or nervous because someone or something seems powerful or threatening.","e":"😨"},{"w":"ancient","d":"Very, very old — having existed for a long time.","e":"🌳"}]},{"w":2,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 2.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 9)","file":"lesson-2-2-frisby-ch9.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-2-2","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 2.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 9). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track conflict and rising action. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 9, keep a list of complications . Mrs. Frisby goes into the rosebush and finds a lit hallway, smooth floors, and rats who are nothing like the rats she knows. Justin leads her in. Does finding these rats solve her problem or hand her a bigger one? What does she still not understand about them?","What feeling does the poet have for the mouse? Find one line that shows it.","Mrs. Frisby enters the rosebush and discovers an underground world with electricity and smooth floors. What details tell you this is not an ordinary rat colony? Why is Mrs. Frisby so amazed?"],"chapterQs":["Name two specific details Mrs. Frisby notices when she enters the rats' underground home that show her this is not an ordinary rat colony. Why do these details surprise her?","Justin says, “We’ve had electricity for years now.” What does this single sentence tell you about the rats?","Why do you think O'Brien shows us the rosebush entrance and underground tunnels through Mrs. Frisby's eyes instead of a rat's? How does that choice help the reader?"],"focus":"Today: PLAN your story. Even a true story needs a SHAPE — a beginning, a middle with rising action, and an ending that shows what you learned.","nowTry":"Write below. Describe the SETTING of your own story in one or two sentences. Use one specific detail (a smell, a sound, an object) the way O'Brien used the electric lights and smooth tunnel floors.","rwm":"“Ahead of her stretched a long, well-lit hallway.”"},"words":[{"w":"enormous","d":"Extremely large in size; huge.","e":"🦉"},{"w":"intimidated","d":"Feeling frightened or nervous because someone or something seems powerful or threatening.","e":"😨"},{"w":"ancient","d":"Very, very old — having existed for a long time.","e":"🌳"}]},{"w":2,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 2.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 10 & 11)","file":"lesson-2-3-frisby-ch10-11.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-2-3","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 2.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 10 & 11). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 10 & 11, watch the stakes rise . A huge rat named Brutus blocks her way, and saying Jonathan’s name is the only thing that gets her past him. What could she lose in that moment? Then Justin leads her deeper, all the way to Nicodemus. Is she safer now than she was outside the rosebush, or is she in further than she meant to go?","Mrs. Frisby meets a huge rat named Brutus who blocks her way. What happens when she says she is Mrs. Jonathan Frisby? Why do you think Jonathan’s name has such power?","Justin leads Mrs. Frisby through the rats’ colony to meet Nicodemus. What surprises Mrs. Frisby most about the colony, and what does Nicodemus promise her?"],"focus":"Today: PLAN your story. Even a true story needs a SHAPE — a beginning, a middle with rising action, and an ending that shows what you learned.","nowTry":"Write below. Plan three events for your own story in order: first, then, finally . Write one sentence for each.","rwm":"“She stepped into the entrance, and then stopped in her tracks.”"},"words":[{"w":"enormous","d":"Extremely large in size; huge.","e":"🦉"},{"w":"intimidated","d":"Feeling frightened or nervous because someone or something seems powerful or threatening.","e":"😨"},{"w":"ancient","d":"Very, very old — having existed for a long time.","e":"🌳"}]},{"w":2,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 2.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 12)","file":"lesson-2-4-frisby-ch12.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-2-4","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 2.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 12). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Lear's animals talk and act silly. Isabella the rat is chatty and friendly, almost like a human neighbor. How does each writer make animal characters feel real in different ways?","As you read Ch. 12, ask where the tension is heading . Isabella chatters away and shows Mrs. Frisby around the colony, and for a few pages things feel calm. But Timothy is still sick and moving day is still coming. What has Mrs. Frisby gained this week, and what problem is still waiting for her?","Isabella is young, chatty, and full of energy. What does Mrs. Frisby learn about the rats' daily life from Isabella, and how does it change the way she sees the colony?"],"chapterQs":["In Ch. 12, Mrs. Frisby meets Isabella, a young rat who shows her around the colony. What kind of personality does Isabella have, and how can you tell? Use evidence from the text.","As Isabella shows Mrs. Frisby around, the colony starts to look less like a group of ordinary rats. What details about the rats' daily life surprise Mrs. Frisby, and why?","By the end of Ch. 12, Mrs. Frisby begins to see the rats as a real community. How has her view of the rats changed from when she first arrived, and what caused that change?"],"focus":"Today: PLAN your story. Even a true story needs a SHAPE — a beginning, a middle with rising action, and an ending that shows what you learned.","nowTry":"Write below. Looking back at this week, what is one thing you understand about Mrs. Frisby's life now that you didn't at the start? Write one sentence."},"words":[{"w":"enormous","d":"Extremely large in size; huge.","e":"🦉"},{"w":"intimidated","d":"Feeling frightened or nervous because someone or something seems powerful or threatening.","e":"😨"},{"w":"ancient","d":"Very, very old — having existed for a long time.","e":"🌳"}]},{"w":2,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 2 Fluency: Mrs. Frisby","file":"lesson-2-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-2-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"enormous","d":"Extremely large in size; huge.","e":"🦉"},{"w":"intimidated","d":"Feeling frightened or nervous because someone or something seems powerful or threatening.","e":"😨"},{"w":"ancient","d":"Very, very old — having existed for a long time.","e":"🌳"}]},{"w":3,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 3.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 13 & 14)","file":"lesson-3-1-frisby-ch13-14.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-3-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 3.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 13 & 14). In your warm-up, you'll focus on Story Stones — Plan Your Draft — Lay a path of stones for your story. Each stone = one sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 13 & 14, keep a wonder list . The rats have a plan to put sleeping powder in Dragon’s bowl, and Mrs. Frisby is the one who volunteers. Then Nicodemus starts telling how the rats were caught at a marketplace. What do you want to ask about the plan? What do you want to ask about the men in the white coats? Which of your questions did the chapter answer?","The rats have a plan to drug Dragon with sleeping powder. Why does Mrs. Frisby volunteer to put the powder in Dragon's bowl? What makes her the right one for the job?","Nicodemus describes how he and the other rats were captured at a marketplace. What were the rats doing at the market, and how did the men in white coats catch them?"],"chapterQs":["In Ch. 13, the rats plan to drug Dragon with sleeping powder. Why does Mrs. Frisby volunteer for the dangerous part of the plan, and what makes her the best choice?","In Ch. 14, Nicodemus begins telling how the rats were captured. What were the rats doing at the marketplace, and how did the men in white coats catch them?","Looking at Ch. 13 and Ch. 14 together: How does the story shift between the present-day plan to help Mrs. Frisby and the backstory of the rats' capture? Why does Nicodemus tell this history now?"],"focus":"Today: DRAFT YOUR BEGINNING. Your first sentence has one job — HOOK the reader. Try one of the six Beginning Leads. Don’t start with the weather.","nowTry":"Write below. Write the OPENING of your story (2–3 sentences). Try to raise a question in the reader's mind, the way O'Brien builds suspense around the plan for Dragon in Ch. 13."},"words":[{"w":"experimenting","d":"Trying out new ideas or methods to learn something.","e":"🔬"},{"w":"recognition","d":"Knowing or being able to identify something you have seen before.","e":"👁️"},{"w":"futile","d":"Useless — not able to produce a result.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":3,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 3.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 15)","file":"lesson-3-2-frisby-ch15.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-3-2","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 3.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 15). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Ask questions and revise predictions. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 15, make a prediction and then be ready to revise it. Nicodemus describes the wire cages and the injections, and the rats begin noticing changes in themselves. Predict what those changes will let them do. Then read on and check: what did the chapter confirm, and what did it change your mind about?","What feeling does the poet have for the mouse? Find one line that shows it.","The rats are locked in small wire cages and given injections. What changes do the rats begin to notice in themselves, and how does that make them feel?"],"chapterQs":["In Ch. 15, the rats are kept in cages at NIMH and given injections. What begins to change about the rats, and how do they know something is different?","What is life like for the rats inside the cages at NIMH? Use details from the chapter to describe what they experience day to day.","The rats' intelligence is growing, but the experience is also frightening and confusing. Why might growing smarter actually make being in a cage feel worse?"],"focus":"Open your Story Stones plan from Day 1. Today you’ll DRAFT YOUR BEGINNING — turning Stone 1 into a real opening paragraph. Your first sentence has one job: HOOK the reader. Try one of the Beginning Leads below.","nowTry":"Write below. Write 2–3 sentences introducing the MAIN CHARACTER of your story. Include their name, one thing they want, and one thing they fear.","rwm":"“Double it! Even now, years later, years after the injections were stopped, we seem scarcely any older than we were then.”"},"words":[{"w":"experimenting","d":"Trying out new ideas or methods to learn something.","e":"🔬"},{"w":"recognition","d":"Knowing or being able to identify something you have seen before.","e":"👁️"},{"w":"futile","d":"Useless — not able to produce a result.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":3,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 3.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 16 & 17)","file":"lesson-3-3-frisby-ch16-17.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-3-3","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 3.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 16 & 17). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Chapters 16 & 17, what new questions do you have? How have your predictions changed now that you know more about the rats' abilities?","The rats get better and better at the mazes until they can remember complex paths after a single try. What does this tell you about how the injections are changing them?","Nicodemus figures out how to read the letters on signs and labels. Why is learning to read the biggest change of all? How does it change what the rats can do?"],"chapterQs":["The scientists put the rats through mazes to test their intelligence. How do the rats' results change over time, and what does this prove about the injections?","Nicodemus describes how he first made sense of the letters on signs and labels. Why is learning to read more important than solving mazes? Use evidence from Ch. 17.","By the end of Ch. 17, the rats can read the scientists' notes. How does this knowledge change what the rats want? What do they begin to do?"],"focus":"Keep your Story Stones plan open. Today, continue DRAFTING YOUR BEGINNING — build on what you started yesterday. Use your stones to keep the story moving forward.","nowTry":"Write below. Write 2–3 sentences of RISING ACTION for your story. Show your character facing the problem you set up. Use at least one strong action verb.","rwm":"“By teaching us to read, they had taught us how to get away.”"},"words":[{"w":"experimenting","d":"Trying out new ideas or methods to learn something.","e":"🔬"},{"w":"recognition","d":"Knowing or being able to identify something you have seen before.","e":"👁️"},{"w":"futile","d":"Useless — not able to produce a result.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":3,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 3.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 18)","file":"lesson-3-4-frisby-ch18.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-3-4","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 3.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 18). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Dickinson watches a bird closely without it knowing. In the story, the rats of NIMH are watched and studied too. How is being observed without knowing it different from choosing to be seen?","Think back across Chapters 13–18: How have your predictions about the rats changed? What questions are you still wondering about?","The rats are navigating the air ducts of the NIMH laboratory, trying to reach the outside. What made their escape possible? Why did they also free the mice?"],"chapterQs":["The rats used their ability to read to plan their escape through the air ducts. Explain TWO ways that reading helped them escape, using evidence from the chapter.","The escape through the air ducts was tense and dangerous. What made it so risky, and how did the rats handle the danger?","Looking back across the whole week (Ch. 13–18): How is Mrs. Frisby's view of the rats different now than it was when she first met Brutus at the rosebush?"],"focus":"Look back at your Story Stones and what you’ve drafted so far. Today, finish DRAFTING YOUR BEGINNING — make sure your opening hooks the reader and sets up the first few events from your plan.","nowTry":"Write below. Read your DRAFT out loud one more time. Type ONE sentence describing the most important moment in your story so far — the moment that surprises the reader the most."},"words":[{"w":"experimenting","d":"Trying out new ideas or methods to learn something.","e":"🔬"},{"w":"recognition","d":"Knowing or being able to identify something you have seen before.","e":"👁️"},{"w":"futile","d":"Useless — not able to produce a result.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":3,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 3 Fluency: Mrs. Frisby","file":"lesson-3-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-3-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"experimenting","d":"Trying out new ideas or methods to learn something.","e":"🔬"},{"w":"recognition","d":"Knowing or being able to identify something you have seen before.","e":"👁️"},{"w":"futile","d":"Useless — not able to produce a result.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":4,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 4.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 19 & 20)","file":"lesson-4-1-frisby-ch19-20.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-4-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 4.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 19 & 20). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character motivation. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 19 & 20, explain the rats’ motivation . They find the abandoned Boniface Estate with its library and its tools, and they dig a colony with a main hall, storerooms, and electricity taken from the farm. Why do they build it this way instead of living like ordinary rats? What do they seem to want most, and which detail shows it?","The rats find the abandoned Boniface Estate with its library and tools. Why is this place so important for the rats? How does living there change them?","The rats build their underground colony with a main hall, storerooms, and stolen electricity. What does the way they build their colony tell you about how much they've changed since NIMH?"],"chapterQs":["In Ch. 19, the rats discover the abandoned Boniface Estate. What do they find there, and why is it such an important place for them?","While living at the Boniface Estate, the rats read books and learn new skills. How does this time change them as a community? Give at least one example from the text.","In Ch. 20, Nicodemus describes building the underground colony beneath the rosebush. What does the colony include, and how do the rats get electricity?"],"focus":"Today: ADD CRAFT. Your draft has the events. Now make it VIVID. Add specific words, sensory details, and dialogue. Show feelings instead of naming them.","nowTry":"Write below. Add ONE line of dialogue to your story draft. Use quotation marks correctly. Pick a character and write something they would actually say."},"words":[{"w":"permanently","d":"In a way that lasts a long time or forever.","e":"📍"},{"w":"electrocuted","d":"Killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"assorted","d":"Of many different kinds mixed together.","e":"🧰"}]},{"w":4,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 4.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 21)","file":"lesson-4-2-frisby-ch21.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-4-2","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 4.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 21). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character motivation. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 21, work out a motivation the book never states outright. The rats watch an old man make small mechanical toys, and they study the tools he carries in his truck. Nobody makes them do it. Why spend their time this way instead of simply taking what they need? Which detail in the chapter gives you the best evidence?","What feeling does the poet have for the mouse? Find one line that shows it.","The Toy Tinker is an old man who makes small mechanical toys near the Boniface Estate. What do the rats learn from watching him work, and why is this knowledge important?"],"chapterQs":["Who is the Toy Tinker, and what does Nicodemus tell us about his life near the Boniface Estate? Use at least two details from the chapter.","What skills or craftsmanship did the rats learn from watching the Toy Tinker? Why was learning by watching so important for them?","The chapter is called \"The Toy Tinker.\" How does this chapter connect to the theme of passing down knowledge and learning from others?"],"focus":"Today: ADD CRAFT. Your draft has the events. Now make it VIVID. Add specific words, sensory details, and dialogue. Show feelings instead of naming them.","nowTry":"Write below. Add a sentence of DESCRIPTION to your draft. Pick ONE object in your story and describe it with three specific details, the way O'Brien describes the Toy Tinker's tools.","rwm":"“He had, in his truck, besides the toys, a collection of tools such as we had never seen.”"},"words":[{"w":"permanently","d":"In a way that lasts a long time or forever.","e":"📍"},{"w":"electrocuted","d":"Killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"assorted","d":"Of many different kinds mixed together.","e":"🧰"}]},{"w":4,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 4.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 22 & 23)","file":"lesson-4-3-frisby-ch22-23.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-4-3","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 4.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 22 & 23). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Chapters 22 & 23, ask: Why do the rats risk everything for their plan to move to Thorn Valley? What motivates Mrs. Frisby to keep helping even when it becomes dangerous?","Nicodemus describes the rats' plan for Thorn Valley. Why do the rats want to stop stealing from humans — and what kind of life are they hoping to build?","Mrs. Frisby is caught and locked in a birdcage. What does she overhear from the cage — and why does it fill her with dread?"],"chapterQs":["In Ch. 22, Nicodemus describes the Thorn Valley plan. Why do the rats believe they must stop stealing from humans? What kind of life do they want to build instead?","In Ch. 23, Mrs. Frisby volunteers to put sleeping powder in Dragon's food. Why does she volunteer for such a dangerous task? What does this show about her character?","Mrs. Frisby is caught by Billy and locked in a birdcage. From the cage, she overhears a phone conversation. What does she hear — and why does it fill her with dread?"],"focus":"Today: ADD CRAFT. Your draft has the events. Now make it VIVID. Add specific words, sensory details, and dialogue. Show feelings instead of naming them.","nowTry":"Write below. Add a TRANSITION sentence to your draft. Use a time-or-sequence phrase: At ___, By that time, A few minutes later, Finally .","rwm":"“She had not the heart to eat the food that lay on the cage floor.”"},"words":[{"w":"permanently","d":"In a way that lasts a long time or forever.","e":"📍"},{"w":"electrocuted","d":"Killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"assorted","d":"Of many different kinds mixed together.","e":"🧰"}]},{"w":4,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 4.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 24)","file":"lesson-4-4-frisby-ch24.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-4-4","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 4.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 24). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Dickinson says hope sings loudest in a storm. Mrs. Frisby just heard terrifying news—NIMH is coming. Where does her hope come from when everything feels dangerous?","Think back across Chapters 19–24: What motivated the rats' plan for Thorn Valley? What motivated Mrs. Frisby to keep helping? How are their motivations similar or different?","Mrs. Frisby overhears Mr. Fitzgibbon's phone call about seven dead rats and NIMH scientists coming. Why is this news so urgent, and what must Mrs. Frisby do?"],"chapterQs":["Mrs. Frisby overhears Mr. Fitzgibbon talking on the phone about seven dead rats found at a hardware store. What does she learn is going to happen next, and why is it so dangerous for the rats?","Mrs. Frisby is trapped in a cage, yet she knows she must warn the rats. What does this tell you about her character and how she has changed since the beginning of the book?","Looking back across the whole week (Ch. 19–24): How is Mrs. Frisby different now than she was when she first went to find Mr Ages in Ch. 2?"],"focus":"Today: ADD CRAFT. Your draft has the events. Now make it VIVID. Add specific words, sensory details, and dialogue. Show feelings instead of naming them.","nowTry":"Write below. Read your draft out loud. Add ONE FINAL CRAFT MOVE: a strong verb, vivid description, sharp transition, OR a real-sounding line of dialogue. Type what you added."},"words":[{"w":"permanently","d":"In a way that lasts a long time or forever.","e":"📍"},{"w":"electrocuted","d":"Killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"assorted","d":"Of many different kinds mixed together.","e":"🧰"}]},{"w":4,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 4 Fluency: Mrs. Frisby","file":"lesson-4-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-4-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"permanently","d":"In a way that lasts a long time or forever.","e":"📍"},{"w":"electrocuted","d":"Killed or badly hurt by an electric shock.","e":"⚡"},{"w":"assorted","d":"Of many different kinds mixed together.","e":"🧰"}]},{"w":5,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 5.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 25)","file":"lesson-5-1-frisby-ch25.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-5-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 5.1: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 25). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain theme using details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 25, watch what Mrs. Frisby chooses to do the second she is free. She could run straight home to her children, and instead she runs toward the danger to tell the rats the truth. What big idea about honesty and courage do her choices add up to? Which detail from the escape would you use to prove it?","Mrs. Frisby escapes from the birdcage with Justin's help and races to warn the rats. What does her decision to run straight into danger tell you about how she has changed?","The rats flee through their escape tunnels just in time. What makes the timing so tense, and how does the author build that urgency?"],"chapterQs":["Mrs. Frisby escapes from the birdcage with help from Justin. How does she get free, and what does she do immediately after?","The rats must evacuate through their escape tunnels before the exterminators from NIMH arrive. What is their emergency plan, and why is the timing so dangerous?","The chapter is called \"Escape.\" What makes this escape feel urgent, and what does it show about how the rats have planned ahead?"],"focus":"Today: REVISE & EDIT. Two different jobs. REVISE = big changes (use ARMS). EDIT = small fixes (use CUPS). Do both before publishing.","nowTry":"Write below. Read ONE sentence from your story draft out loud. Look at it like a reader: Is it clear? Could a word be stronger? Revise that one sentence and type the new version."},"words":[{"w":"vantage","d":"A place from which you can see well — a good view.","e":"👀"},{"w":"thoughtfully","d":"In a careful, considerate way — thinking about others.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"patchwork","d":"Something made of many small pieces put together.","e":"🧩"}]},{"w":5,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 5.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 26)","file":"lesson-5-2-frisby-ch26.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-5-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 5.2: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Ch. 26). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain theme using details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 26, watch what the truth costs. Nicodemus could have kept Jonathan’s story to himself forever, and he tells Mrs. Frisby anyway. The rats could stay where it is safe, and they decide to leave. Which two details from this meeting would you use to prove one big idea about telling hard truths? Say that idea in a single sentence.","What feeling does the poet have for the mouse? Find one line that shows it.","The rats announce they must leave for Thorn Valley before the NIMH scientists arrive. Why is this meeting so urgent? What does Mrs. Frisby learn about her husband's connection to the rats?"],"chapterQs":["At the meeting, the rats confirm they have successfully moved the Frisbys' cinderblock house. Why was moving the house so important, and what does it mean for Mrs. Frisby's family now?","The rats must leave for Thorn Valley before the NIMH scientists come. Why can't they stay? What does their decision to leave tell you about what kind of community they are?","Mrs. Frisby reflects on everything she has learned about her husband's connection to the rats. How has knowing Jonathan's story changed the way she sees herself and her family?"],"focus":"Today: REVISE & EDIT. Two different jobs. REVISE = big changes (use ARMS). EDIT = small fixes (use CUPS). Do both before publishing.","nowTry":"Write below. REVISE the opening of your draft. Read it like a stranger. Is it interesting in the first sentence? Type a new version of your opening sentence.","rwm":"“Nicodemus stood before them all, his voice quiet but steady, and told them the time had come to leave.”"},"words":[{"w":"vantage","d":"A place from which you can see well — a good view.","e":"👀"},{"w":"thoughtfully","d":"In a careful, considerate way — thinking about others.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"patchwork","d":"Something made of many small pieces put together.","e":"🧩"}]},{"w":5,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 5.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Theme Discussion)","file":"lesson-5-3-theme.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-5-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 5.3: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Theme Discussion). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["You have two possible themes and one novel to test them against. Which of your two ideas has more moments behind it? Which single moment is your strongest piece of evidence, and what exactly does it prove?","Pick ONE moment that has stayed in your mind. Why that moment? What did it show you about a character or about life?","Try this: name your theme in one sentence. Then find two moments from the book that prove it."],"chapterQs":["Pick ONE theme of Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH from this list, or write your own. Then find TWO details from the book that support it: courage, family, friendship, freedom, the cost of being smart.","How does Mrs. Frisby's love for Timothy drive almost every choice she makes? Find at least TWO moments where she acts because of him.","Nicodemus says of the rats' plan: \"It would be a long book, full of trouble and danger, too much to tell now.\" Why do you think Nicodemus wants the rats to live differently from regular rats? What is the BIG IDEA he believes in?"],"focus":"Today: REVISE & EDIT. Two different jobs. REVISE = big changes (use ARMS). EDIT = small fixes (use CUPS). Do both before publishing.","nowTry":"Write below. REVISE your character. Does your reader know what your character WANTS and what they FEAR? Add one sentence that shows either their want or their fear more clearly."},"words":[{"w":"vantage","d":"A place from which you can see well — a good view.","e":"👀"},{"w":"thoughtfully","d":"In a careful, considerate way — thinking about others.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"patchwork","d":"Something made of many small pieces put together.","e":"🧩"}]},{"w":5,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 5.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Assessment)","file":"lesson-5-4-assessment.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-5-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 5.4: Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH (Assessment). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The star is beautiful but disappears. Mrs. Frisby's time with the rats is the same—brief but unforgettable. What moment in the story felt “too lovely to be bought or sold”?","Before you write, read your theme sentence back to yourself. Does it say something about people, or does it only name a topic? Then look at your two moments and ask: would a reader who has never opened this book understand why each one proves my idea?","Think for a minute. What ONE theme of this book do you want to write about — and what evidence will you use?"],"chapterQs":["Write your LITERARY RESPONSE. In 3–5 sentences, name one theme of Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH and explain it using TWO specific events from the book.","Pick ONE character who changed across the book (Mrs. Frisby, Timothy, or someone else). Describe how they changed and what caused the change.","Which moment in the book was MOST important to you, and why? Defend your choice with at least one specific detail from the book."],"focus":"Today: REVISE & EDIT. Two different jobs. REVISE = big changes (use ARMS). EDIT = small fixes (use CUPS). Do both before publishing.","nowTry":"Write below. EDIT your draft. Fix any sentences with wrong capital letters, missing commas, or unclear words. Type the one sentence you fixed and why."},"words":[{"w":"vantage","d":"A place from which you can see well — a good view.","e":"👀"},{"w":"thoughtfully","d":"In a careful, considerate way — thinking about others.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"patchwork","d":"Something made of many small pieces put together.","e":"🧩"}]},{"w":5,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 5 Fluency: Mrs. Frisby","file":"lesson-5-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-5-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"vantage","d":"A place from which you can see well — a good view.","e":"👀"},{"w":"thoughtfully","d":"In a careful, considerate way — thinking about others.","e":"🧠"},{"w":"patchwork","d":"Something made of many small pieces put together.","e":"🧩"}]},{"w":6,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 6.1: Final Revision","file":"lesson-6-1-revise.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-6-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 6.1: Final Revision. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Reread important passages to deepen understanding. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you reread today, watch how the writing makes you feel something without announcing it. Which words or details do that work? Then open your Week 5 draft: where does your reader have to be told what to feel instead of being shown it?"],"chapterQs":["What was the WEAKEST sentence in your draft? Type the original. Then type your FARM-revised version below it.","What was the FLATTEST moment in your draft (one with no specific details)? Type the original. Then type a version where you ADDED a sight, sound, or feeling.","Reorder check: Read your draft from start to finish. Is there any moment where a reader might get confused? Type which sentence you would MOVE, and where."],"focus":"Today is the FIRST publish day: REVISE with ARMS. Add, Remove, Move, Substitute. Big changes only — not typos yet.","nowTry":"Write below. Type your strongest virtue moment — the sentence where your reader sees the virtue come alive."},"words":[{"w":"courage","d":"Choosing to act even when you are afraid.","e":"🦁"},{"w":"perseverance","d":"Not giving up — even when something is hard or takes a long time.","e":"⛰️"},{"w":"courtesy","d":"Treating others with kindness and respect.","e":"🎩"}]},{"w":6,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 6.2: Final Edit","file":"lesson-6-2-edit.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-6-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 6.2: Final Edit. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Reread important passages to deepen understanding. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you reread today, look closely at how the author punctuates a character speaking. Where does the comma go? Does the period land inside the quotation mark or outside it? Then open your own narrative and check every line of dialogue against what you just saw. Read from your last sentence back to your first so you see each one on its own.","Dickinson says a word comes alive when you speak it. When you revise your writing today, which words will you bring to life?"],"chapterQs":["MICE — Capital letters check. Type ONE sentence from your draft where you fixed a missing capital. What did you fix?","MICE — Quotation mark check. Find ONE line of dialogue in your draft. Type it. Did the comma or period end up INSIDE the quotation marks?","MICE — End mark check. Read three sentences in a row. Do they all end with periods, or do you have variety? Type two sentences that end DIFFERENTLY."],"focus":"Today is publish Day 2: EDIT with CUPS. Capitals, Usage, Punctuation, Spelling. Read your draft line by line.","nowTry":"Write below. Type ONE sentence you completely fixed today — show the before and after."},"words":[{"w":"courage","d":"Choosing to act even when you are afraid.","e":"🦁"},{"w":"perseverance","d":"Not giving up — even when something is hard or takes a long time.","e":"⛰️"},{"w":"courtesy","d":"Treating others with kindness and respect.","e":"🎩"},{"w":"honesty","d":"Telling the truth, even when it is hard to say.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":6,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 6.3: Type Final","file":"lesson-6-3-type.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-6-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 6.3: Type Final. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"chapterQs":["Type your TITLE here. Make it short, intriguing, and a hint at the virtue your character lives.","Type your FINAL DRAFT here. This is the clean version that goes to your reader.","Type your ABOUT THE AUTHOR line — one sentence about yourself as a writer."],"focus":"Today is publish Day 3: TYPE your final draft. Add the parts that make a story look like a real book — title page, author line, dedication.","nowTry":"Write below. What is ONE thing you are most proud of in your final story?"},"words":[{"w":"courage","d":"Choosing to act even when you are afraid.","e":"🦁"},{"w":"perseverance","d":"Not giving up — even when something is hard or takes a long time.","e":"⛰️"},{"w":"honesty","d":"Telling the truth, even when it is hard to say.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"courtesy","d":"Treating others with kindness and respect.","e":"🎩"}]},{"w":6,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 6.4: Share","file":"lesson-6-4-share.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-6-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 6.4: Share. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 🪶 Poetry Corner — Read, then notice something the poet did.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Whitman says every person has their own song. Today you share your writing—your own voice. What makes your story yours and no one else’s?"],"chapterQs":["Type your TITLE here. (Same as Lesson 6.3 — paste it in again so it lives with your recording and reflection.)","VIRTUE REFLECTION. Which of the seven virtues did your character live out? Explain in 2–3 sentences HOW your story shows that virtue.","What did YOU learn as a writer over these six weeks? Write 2 sentences about your growth."],"focus":"Final publish day: SHARE your story. Read it aloud. Score yourself against the rubric. Reflect on what you learned this quarter.","nowTry":"Write below. RECORD YOUR READ-ALOUD. Use the Canvas recording tool. Read your title, then \"by [your name],\" then your story — with feeling. Then paste a short note about how it went."},"words":[{"w":"courage","d":"Choosing to act even when you are afraid.","e":"🦁"},{"w":"perseverance","d":"Not giving up — even when something is hard or takes a long time.","e":"⛰️"},{"w":"honesty","d":"Telling the truth, even when it is hard to say.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"courtesy","d":"Treating others with kindness and respect.","e":"🎩"}]},{"w":6,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 6 Fluency: Your Own Story","file":"lesson-6-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-6-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"courage","d":"Choosing to act even when you are afraid.","e":"🦁"},{"w":"perseverance","d":"Not giving up — even when something is hard or takes a long time.","e":"⛰️"},{"w":"courtesy","d":"Treating others with kindness and respect.","e":"🎩"},{"w":"honesty","d":"Telling the truth, even when it is hard to say.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":7,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 7.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 1)","file":"lesson-7-1-anne-ch1.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-7-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 7.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 1). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Distinguish narrator point of view from character perspective. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Mrs. Lynde’s welcome","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Mrs. Rachel Lynde, and today I’ll be guiding you through your lesson. I live at the bottom of the hollow, and nothing in Avonlea gets past my window. Today’s question is how a writer makes a place feel real. By the end, you’ll be able to tell how the narrator describes Avonlea from how I see it. We’ll sort out some stubborn plurals, too."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Now, you’ll hear two voices telling you about me today. One is the narrator, who stands outside the story and sees everything. The other is me, and I’m generally right, of course. Keep the two of us apart as you read. When you meet a strong opinion, ask whose it is. You may find the narrator notices a thing or two about me that I’d never say myself."},{"id":"dol","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-dol","done":false,"script":"Mind your capitals, that’s what I always say. A lady’s title, her name, and the name of her village all deserve a capital letter. The sentence you’re fixing has forgotten every one of them, and it’s forgotten how to stop, besides. Study the correct one first. Ordinary words stay small. Proper names stand up straight. Everybody in Avonlea knows that."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before the word work","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-guide1","done":true,"script":"Words are a good deal like the families of Avonlea. Once you know who belongs with whom, everything makes sense. Today’s word parts both mean with, or together, and I know a thing or two about together. I run the Sewing Circle, I help with the Sunday-school, and I keep an eye on everybody else besides. Work through these carefully. I’ll be watching."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about the word parts","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-morphology","done":false,"script":"Look at the start of a word before you look at the end of it. Every word today begins with a little part that means together. Conversation is my specialty, as anyone in Avonlea will tell you. It means turning words over together with a neighbor, and I can’t think of a better use of a spare hour. Spot that first part, and the long words stop looking so grand."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s words","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Four new words, and you’ll want them before the week is out. Placid means calm and still, like the brook by the time it reaches my house. It minds its manners there, you know. As for imagine, well, I’ve no great use for it myself. Plain sense serves me better. But try the three steps on every word, and see how close you get."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"Most words make more than one the sensible way. You add a letter and have done with it. But some words refuse to behave. One mouse, two mice. One goose, a whole yard of geese. You can’t reason those out. You simply have to know them, the way I know every family on this road. Sort them into two groups, and give each group a name of your own."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-spelling","done":false,"script":"Here’s something I’ve noticed about plurals, and I notice a great deal. Most words simply add their letter and carry on. But a word that ends in a hissing sound needs an extra bit, or you’d never get your tongue round it. And a y at the end, with a consonant in front of it, has to change its ways. Sort them, then work out what makes the difference."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you open the book","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"Open the book, and you’ll find me at my window. That’s where I do my best thinking. Now, I’m not one for imagination. I deal in plain facts. But when I saw Matthew Cuthbert drive off in his best suit, my mind went off in a dozen directions at once. Watch how much a person can imagine about a thing, just from looking out of a window."},{"id":"read","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you read","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-read","done":true,"script":"Take your time with this chapter. It starts slowly, the way Avonlea does, with the road and the brook and the orchards. Don’t hurry past them. They’re how you come to know the place. There are two spots to stop and think along the way. By the end, you’ll know exactly where Matthew was going. I found out, eventually. It took me a walk to Green Gables."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you write","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well. A boy from an orphan asylum, if you please. I told Marilla exactly what I thought of it, and I don’t take back a word. Now it’s your turn to say what you noticed. Don’t just tell me what happened. Point to the words in the book that show it. And mind you, the narrator may have noticed a thing or two about me as well."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about the questions","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-chapter","done":false,"script":"These questions are mostly about me, which is only right. But here’s the question underneath them. Can you trust what I think? I was very sure about Matthew, and very sure about that orphan. When you answer, sort out what I believed from what the narrator actually showed you. They aren’t always the same thing, though I’d never admit that to Marilla."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Mrs. Lynde · how it connects","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your word parts answer is saved already, so here’s how it joins up. All week, you’re working with words that mean together. And that’s the whole of today’s chapter, when you think about it. A brother and sister who’ve lived alone at Green Gables for years, deciding to bring somebody new into the house. Company, you might call it. Whether it turns out well is another matter."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s writing","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-workshop","done":false,"script":"You’re starting a story of your own, and every good story needs a question at the start of it. Mine was, where is Matthew Cuthbert going? I couldn’t rest until I knew. Give your character something they can’t leave alone. A worry, a wish, a mystery down the road. Then write your one sentence about who they are and what they’ll learn."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-rwm","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"copia","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about Say It Three Ways","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-7-1-copia","done":false,"script":""}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Keep the narrator and the characters apart. The narrator tells the story; each character sees it their own way, and can be wrong.","wordParts":"con- and com- mean “with” or “together”: connect, combine, company, conversation.","grammar":"Most nouns add -s to make more than one. Irregular plurals don’t follow that rule, so you have to know them: child → children, woman → women, foot → feet.","spelling":"Most words just add -s. Words ending in s, x, ch, sh or z add -es (box → boxes). A consonant + y changes y to i and adds -es (story → stories), but a vowel + y keeps the y (day → days).","dol":[{"raw":"mrs rachel lynde walked through avonlea every morning","fixed":"<strong>Mrs. Rachel Lynde</strong> walked through <strong>Avonlea</strong> every <strong>morning.</strong>","why":"Capital letters on the title and names (Mrs. Rachel Lynde), a capital on the place name (Avonlea), a period at the end. Common nouns like \"morning\" stay lowercase."},{"raw":"the brook ran past mrs rachels door in lyndes hollow","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> brook ran past <strong>Mrs. Rachel’s</strong> door in <strong>Lynde’s Hollow.</strong>","why":"Capital at the start of the sentence, capitals on the title and names, apostrophes to show the door belongs to Mrs. Rachel and the hollow to the Lyndes, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 1, keep an eye on Mrs. Rachel Lynde. She is curious about Matthew and sure she knows what he must be up to. Which parts of the chapter are her opinions, and which parts does the narrator tell you straight? What does the narrator let you notice about her that she would never say about herself?","Mrs. Rachel Lynde is curious — and a bit nosy — about Matthew. Why do you think she gets so involved in other people's business?","By the end of the chapter, Mrs. Rachel has heard the Cuthberts’ news, and she does not think much of it. What does her reaction tell you about how Avonlea feels about anything new?"],"chapterQs":["Mrs. Rachel Lynde notices something unusual at the start of Ch. 1. What does she see, and why does it bother her so much?","How does Montgomery help you picture Avonlea in Ch. 1? Pick TWO details about the setting and describe them.","At the end of Ch. 1, what does Mrs. Rachel decide to do with the news she has learned? What does this show about her character?"],"focus":"This is the start of an eight-week project. You are going to write one realistic-fiction story, about one page long, one piece at a time. Realistic fiction means a kid who could be real, in a place that could be real, facing something kids really face. Today you pick the kid and the thing.","nowTry":"Pick a growing-up thing your kid will face (imagination, friendship, a mistake, wanting something, being new, being scared). Then write the one sentence that starts everything: “My story is about a [kid] who [realistic situation] and learns about [that thing].”","plate":"plates/anne-ch1.png"},"words":[{"w":"placid","d":"Calm and peaceful, without strong feeling or movement.","e":"😌"},{"w":"imagine","d":"To make a picture in your mind of something not in front of you.","e":"💭"},{"w":"delight","d":"A feeling of great pleasure or joy.","e":"✨"}]},{"w":7,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 7.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 2)","file":"lesson-7-2-anne-ch2.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-7-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 7.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 2). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Distinguish narrator point of view from character perspective. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Matthew’s welcome","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Matthew Cuthbert. Today I’ll be guiding you through your lesson. I’m not much of a talker, so I’ll keep it short. Today’s question is about voice, what makes a person sound like themselves and nobody else. I met someone once who sounded like nobody I’d ever heard. By the end of this lesson, you’ll be able to say what made her voice her own."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Matthew · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Now, here’s a thing I’ve noticed. When a story gets told about me, the teller knows things I never said out loud. That’s the narrator. Then there’s what I’m thinking, sitting on that buggy seat. That’s me. Keep the two apart today. When a plain old road starts sounding beautiful, ask yourself who’s doing the seeing. The narrator, or somebody else?"},{"id":"dol","label":"Matthew · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-dol","done":false,"script":"Marilla’s the one for rules, but I’ll do my best. The sentence you’re fixing is somebody talking, and excited about it. There’s a little word at the front that wants a pause after it. There’s a word squeezed short that’s lost its mark. And the talking itself wants something around it, both ends. Look at the one that’s done right first. It shows you every piece."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Matthew · before the word work","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"Well now, words. I’m a farmer, not a scholar, but I know a good tool when I see one, and these word parts are tools. Two of them mean with, or together. I’ve been thinking about that word, together, more than usual lately. You’ll see why when you read. For now, work through these the way you’d work a field. One row at a time."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Matthew · about the word parts","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"Here’s something I didn’t know till lately. That word, companion. It comes from an old word for bread. A companion was somebody you shared your bread with. I like that. Sitting beside someone on a long drive is a bit like sharing bread, I suppose. Match up the parts, and notice how each one carries its own little piece of the meaning."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Matthew · about today’s words","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Four words today, and you met every one of them yesterday. Placid. That was me, mostly, before this week. Imagine. That was never me, not much. Companion, and delight. Well, I’ll let you work out who those belong to. Match each one to its meaning. If one gives you trouble, don’t fret. Say it out loud, slowly, and it often comes back."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Matthew · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"Some words don’t do what you’d expect. You’d think one child, two childs. But no. They change on the inside instead. We had a bit of that at Green Gables this week, if I’m honest. Somebody arrived who wasn’t what we’d planned for. Look through the sentence and find every word that won’t follow the ordinary rule. There’s more than one."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Matthew · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Marilla’s a great one for doing things properly, and she’d want these spelled right. Here’s how I’d go about it. Say the word as more than one, and listen. If your mouth needs an extra bit at the end, or a y at the end has to change its ways, that’s one to tap. The plain ones just add their letter and carry on."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Matthew · before you open the book","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"I’ll not tell you what happened at the station. That’s yours to read. But I’ll say this much. I drove eight miles there expecting one thing, and eight miles home with something quite different beside me. Listen to what she does with every plain thing we passed, the road, the pond, the trees. I’ve never looked at that road the same way since."},{"id":"read","label":"Matthew · before you read","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-read","done":true,"script":"Take your time with this chapter. It’s a long drive, and most of it is talk. As you go, keep a count of how many things get given a brand new name. I lost count myself, somewhere past the pond. And when you reach the end, sit still a moment before you go on. Ask yourself what somebody who names everything might be hoping for."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Matthew · before you write","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well, now you’ve read it. I went for a boy and came home with Anne, and I’d not change it. I didn’t say much on that drive. But I think you know me a little now anyway, just from what I did. That’s worth thinking on as you answer. You can know a person by what they do, not only by what they say."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Matthew · about the questions","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"These questions look simple enough. But here’s the one underneath them. Was Anne as happy as she sounded? She talked and talked, and named everything she saw. But think back to what she told me first, at the station, about that cherry tree. When you describe her, don’t stop at her hair and her hat. Think on what she was afraid of."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Matthew · how it connects","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your grammar answer’s saved already, so here’s how I’d join it up. All week you’ve been looking at words that don’t follow the ordinary rule. Well, the ordinary plan at Green Gables was a boy. A boy to help me with the farm. And what was waiting for me at the station was Anne. Some things are worth the rule being broken."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Matthew · about today’s writing","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"Well now. A name, a want, and a fear. I never got the name out of that child, she had too much else to say. The want I had before we passed the pond: Green Gables. The fear she didn’t say. I reckon it was being sent back. I didn’t say mine either, and mine was the same one from the other side. Give your kid all three, and make the fear a real one."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Matthew · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-7-2-rwm","done":true,"script":""}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Follow one character and notice what they can’t see. A character can be completely sure of something the narrator has quietly shown you isn’t so.","wordParts":"con- and com- mean “with” or “together”: connect, combine, company, conversation.","grammar":"Most nouns add -s to make more than one. Irregular plurals don’t follow that rule, so you have to know them: child → children, woman → women, foot → feet.","spelling":"Most words just add -s. Words ending in s, x, ch, sh or z add -es (box → boxes). A consonant + y changes y to i and adds -es (story → stories), but a vowel + y keeps the y (day → days).","dol":[{"raw":"oh im so happy to be here she cried","fixed":"<strong>“Oh,</strong> <strong>I’m</strong> so happy to be <strong>here!”</strong> she <strong>cried.</strong>","why":"Opening quotation mark, a capital on Oh with a comma after it, a capital I and an apostrophe in I’m, the exclamation point inside the closing quotation mark, and a period after the dialogue tag."},{"raw":"oh im going to love it here anne whispered","fixed":"<strong>“Oh,</strong> <strong>I’m</strong> going to love it <strong>here!”</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> <strong>whispered.</strong>","why":"Same pattern as the model: quotation marks around her words, Oh followed by a comma, a capital I and an apostrophe in I’m, the exclamation point inside the quotation marks. One thing more: Anne is a name, so it takes a capital even in the middle of the sentence."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 2, ride along with Matthew. He went to the station expecting a boy. Which parts of the chapter are Matthew thinking and worrying, and which parts is the narrator telling you straight? When a plain road and a small pond get new names, is the narrator saying those places are beautiful, or is that Anne’s way of seeing them?","We can’t see the wind, but we know it’s there. Anne is like that—you can’t always see what she feels, but her words show it. Find one thing Anne says that shows a feeling she never names directly.","Anne talks the whole drive to Green Gables. Why might a girl who has lived in many places talk so much?"],"chapterQs":["Matthew goes to the train station expecting one thing and finds another. What does he expect, and what does he find?","Describe Anne in your own words. Use at least three specific details from Ch. 2 — what she looks like, how she talks, or what she does.","On the drive to Green Gables, Anne renames a road. What does she name it — and what does this show about her imagination?"],"focus":"Your kid needs three things before they can talk: a name, one thing they want, and one thing they’re afraid of. Make all three specific. “Wants to be happy” is not specific. “Wants to make the team” is.","nowTry":"Build your kid. In 2–3 sentences: their name, ONE specific thing they want, ONE specific thing they’re afraid of. Specific means a real detail, not a feeling word.","rwm":"“They should call it—let me see—the White Way of Delight.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch2.png"},"words":[{"w":"placid","d":"Calm and peaceful, without strong feeling or movement.","e":"😌"},{"w":"imagine","d":"To make a picture in your mind of something not in front of you.","e":"💭"},{"w":"delight","d":"A feeling of great pleasure or joy.","e":"✨"}]},{"w":7,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 7.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 3 & 4)","file":"lesson-7-3-anne-ch3-4.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-7-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 7.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 3 & 4). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-welcome","done":true,"script":"Marilla Cuthbert here, and I’ll be guiding your lesson today. Matthew has driven over to Bright River to fetch the boy we sent for, and I’ve everything ready for him. Today’s question is how a writer shows you what somebody feels without saying it outright. By the end, you’ll be able to point to the very words that prove it. We’ll put some awkward plurals in order, too."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-wgrd","done":false,"script":"A word can carry an opinion the way a basket carries eggs. When somebody calls a thing plain, or useless, that’s a judgment, not a fact. You’ll meet two of us today, Matthew and me, and we don’t always choose the same words about things. As you read, catch one word that sounds like me and one that sounds like Matthew. Then ask who’s doing the judging."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-dol","done":false,"script":"Somebody is talking in this sentence, and saying two things at once, which is one more than most people need. Each part wants its proper ending. Two words have been squeezed short and need their marks. One little word is standing small when it ought to stand tall. Study the model before you start, and then do it neatly. I’ve no patience for slapdash work."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-guide1","done":true,"script":"Now then, word study. I’ll tell you what I tell Matthew: a job done properly the first time saves doing it twice. Today’s word parts both mean with, or together. That’s how Matthew and I have run Green Gables all these years, the two of us together. Soon there’ll be a boy to lend a hand as well. Work steadily, and check as you go."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Every word you build today starts with a part that means together. Here’s a sensible way to choose its spelling. Say the word aloud and mind your lips. If they press shut for the next sound, that part ends in an m. If they don’t, it ends in an n. There’s no guessing needed. Your own mouth will tell you, if you’ll only pay attention to it."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"A word is only worth knowing if you can put it to use. Read each sentence right through to the end before you choose. The words around the gap will tell you what belongs there. Placid is a word I approve of. It means calm and steady, which is how a sensible person ought to be. Imagine, I’ve less use for. But you’ll need it this week, so learn it all the same."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"No word bank today. Every plural has to come out of your own head. These are the words that won’t follow the ordinary rule, and there’s no reasoning with them. One mouse, two mice. One tooth, a whole row of teeth. You simply have to know them, the way I know where every dish in my pantry goes. Say each one aloud before you type it. If the ordinary ending sounds silly, it’s wrong."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"Look at the last letter of a word before you do anything to it. That’s plain good sense. If the word ends in a hissing sound, it needs a little extra to make it more than one, or nobody could say it. If it ends in a y with a consonant in front, the y has to change. Most words just take their letter and behave themselves. I wish more people did."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"Matthew ought to be back from the station by now. I’ve been listening for the buggy. It’ll be a relief to have a sturdy boy about the place, with Matthew getting on in years. You have two chapters today. As you read, don’t just take anybody’s word for how they feel. Watch what they do, and what they say, and what they leave unsaid."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-read","done":false,"script":"There are two places to stop and think, one after each chapter. Stop both times, even if you’d rather keep going. I’ve never held with rushing a job. The first stop asks about Matthew and me, so mind you’re fair to both of us. The second takes you to a window at Green Gables. Keep your book open beside you. You’ll want it for the questions."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well. A girl. I sent word for a boy, plainly and properly, and Mrs. Spencer brought a girl. What use is a girl on a farm? That’s what I said to Matthew, and he said perhaps we could be some use to her. I don’t know what’s come over him. Now it’s your turn. Say what you noticed, and point to the words in the book that show it."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-chapter","done":false,"script":"These questions are about how that child took the news. Now, she never once said she was sad. She didn’t need to. When the second question asks for one sentence from her, choose one where she doesn’t name the feeling, but you know it anyway. That’s your proof. And be fair to me in the first question. I had my reasons for wanting a boy."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · how it connects","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your spelling answer is saved, so here’s how it fits. All week, the last letter of a word has decided what happens to it. And one small fact has decided a great deal at Green Gables. She’s a girl, not a boy. One detail at the end of a word, one detail about a person, and everything after it has to change. Whether that’s for good or ill, I can’t say yet."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-workshop","done":false,"script":"Cordelia, if you please. And then Anne, but only with an e, as if one letter made a particle of difference. I never heard such talk in my life. But I’ll say this for the child: you’d not mistake her for anybody else on the Island. That’s what you’re after. Not nice. Not bad. Not went. The word your kid would pick, and nobody else. If it sounds like everybody, it’s wrong."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Marilla · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-7-3-rwm","done":true,"script":""}],"cards":{"wgrd":"When a word carries an opinion (ugly, eager, useless), ask whose word it really is: the narrator’s, or a character’s.","wordParts":"con- and com- mean “with” or “together”: connect, combine, company, conversation.","grammar":"Most nouns add -s to make more than one. Irregular plurals don’t follow that rule, so you have to know them: child → children, woman → women, foot → feet.","spelling":"Most words just add -s. Words ending in s, x, ch, sh or z add -es (box → boxes). A consonant + y changes y to i and adds -es (story → stories), but a vowel + y keeps the y (day → days).","dol":[{"raw":"you cant send me back i need a home she wept","fixed":"<strong>“You</strong> <strong>can’t</strong> send me <strong>back!</strong> <strong>I</strong> need a <strong>home,”</strong> she <strong>wept.</strong>","why":"Opening quotation mark and a capital to start the speech, an apostrophe in can’t, a capital on I, an exclamation point for the strong feeling, a comma inside the closing quotation mark, and a period after wept."},{"raw":"please dont send me away im trying to be good anne sobbed","fixed":"<strong>“Please</strong> <strong>don’t</strong> send me <strong>away!</strong> <strong>I’m</strong> trying to be <strong>good,”</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> <strong>sobbed.</strong>","why":"The same two-part pattern: quotation marks around everything she says, apostrophes in don’t and I’m, a capital I, an exclamation point after the first part, a comma inside the closing quotation mark, and a capital on Anne because it is a name."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 3 and 4, Marilla and Matthew look at the same girl and do not see the same thing. Watch the words each of them uses about Anne. Which words belong to Marilla, which belong to Matthew, and which are the narrator’s own? Then notice how Anne describes her room and the view from her window — whose words are those?","Marilla wants to send Anne back. Why does Matthew want to keep her? What does he see that Marilla doesn't?","Anne wakes up and looks out the window. How does her view of Green Gables make YOU see the place differently?"],"chapterQs":["Marilla is surprised when Anne arrives at Green Gables. Why? What did she expect — and what does she decide must happen?","Anne reacts to the news with strong feelings. Describe her reaction — and pick ONE sentence from Anne that shows how she feels.","The next morning at Green Gables, Anne looks out the window. What does she SEE that other people might miss? What does this tell you about her?"],"focus":"VOICE is the way your kid sounds — and it comes from the words you pick. Not “nice,” not “bad,” not “went.” Specific verbs and specific nouns make a kid sound like a real person instead of a list of facts.","nowTry":"Write 2–3 sentences in YOUR kid’s voice. Don’t describe them — let them say or do something. Pick the words they would really use.","rwm":"“You don't want me because I'm not a boy!”","plate":"plates/anne-ch3-4.png"},"words":[{"w":"placid","d":"Calm and peaceful, without strong feeling or movement.","e":"😌"},{"w":"imagine","d":"To make a picture in your mind of something not in front of you.","e":"💭"},{"w":"delight","d":"A feeling of great pleasure or joy.","e":"✨"}]},{"w":7,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 7.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 5)","file":"lesson-7-4-anne-ch5.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-7-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 7.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 5). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Anne’s welcome","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello! I’m Anne Shirley, Anne spelled with an e, and I’m guiding your lesson today, which is the most thrilling thing that’s happened to me in ages. Today’s question is how a person’s past shapes who they are. By the end, you’ll be able to explain how mine shaped me. We’ll finish off our plurals, too, and I promise to talk a little less than usual."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Anne · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Here’s a test I think is perfectly delicious. Choose a moment you know well from these chapters, and imagine someone else telling it. Marilla would tell my story very plainly, I expect, with hardly an adjective in it. Today you’ll hear me tell it myself, so you can decide which parts are what happened and which parts are how I chose to say it."},{"id":"dol","label":"Anne · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-dol","done":false,"script":"The model sentence is mine, and I’m rather proud of it. Notice how my words are split in two, with my name tucked in the middle. The sentence you fix is simpler, but it uses the same pieces. A little word that should always stand tall appears twice. And the pause belongs inside the quotation marks, where it can keep the speaker company."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Anne · before the word work","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-guide1","done":true,"script":"Word study! I do love words, especially long, grand ones. It’s the last day of the week, so you’ve met all of these words before. It’s rather like meeting old friends again, which I haven’t had much chance to do. Go quickly, but carefully. Marilla says carefully matters more, and I suppose she’s right, though I’d never tell her so."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Anne · about the word parts","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"One more look at the word parts that mean with, or together. You’ve matched them and built with them all week, so this should go quickly. I’ve always liked words about being together. Notice how much faster you are now than at the start of the week. That’s the whole point. Knowing a word part by heart saves you time on every long word you ever meet."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Anne · about today’s words","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"You get to choose how to show what you know, which is exactly the sort of thing I like. If a word still feels slippery, try explaining it to someone younger. That’s the quickest way to find out whether you really own it. Placid is the hardest one for me. I don’t believe I’ve been placid for a single minute of my life."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Anne · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"You’re the editor today, and nobody will point to the mistakes for you. Some plurals simply refuse to follow the ordinary rule. One man, two men. One tooth, a whole mouthful of teeth. Read each sentence slowly and listen for the ones that sound almost right. Almost right is the most dangerous kind of wrong. I found that out about spelling ages ago."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Anne · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"A proofreader trusts nothing, and today that’s you. Some of these words are spelled perfectly, and some only pretend to be. For each one, say the word it came from, look closely at how it ends, and decide what the plural ought to be. If it’s already right, leave it alone. Tapping a word that was fine is its own sort of mistake, and a very humbling one."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Anne · before you open the book","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"Marilla is driving me to White Sands to see Mrs. Spencer, to sort out the mistake about the boy. I’m trying very hard not to think about what happens after that. So instead, listen to how I tell things in this chapter. Notice when I stop saying what happened and start saying what I imagined. I do that rather a lot, I’m afraid."},{"id":"read","label":"Anne · before you read","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-read","done":true,"script":"This chapter is mostly me talking, which I know won’t surprise you in the least. As you read, keep a list in your head of every home I mention, and who I lived with there. There are more than you’d think. When you reach the stopping point, you’ll be asked what kept me hopeful. Your list will help you answer truthfully. Don’t rush. It’s a long drive."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Anne · before you write","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well, now you know all about me. Bolingbroke, and Mrs. Thomas, and Mrs. Hammond with her three pairs of twins, and the asylum. It isn’t a very romantic history, is it? But I’d rather you didn’t feel too sorry for me. Now it’s your turn to write. Point to my words in the book when you answer. I said a great many of them, so you have plenty to choose from."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Anne · about the questions","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"The last question reaches across all five chapters, so think about the whole of me, not just this drive. Before you write, remember the moment that surprised you most. Then ask yourself whether I was ever really talking about a road, or a tree, or a pond. Perhaps I was talking about what I needed. Choose one moment, and let my exact words show it."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Anne · how it connects","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your vocabulary answer is saved, so here’s how the words connect. Placid, imagine, companion, delight. When I look at them together, they’re almost a map of me. I imagine all the time, I find delight nearly everywhere, and I’ve wanted a real companion for as long as I can remember. Placid is the one word that isn’t me at all."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Anne · about today’s writing","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-7-4-workshop","done":true,"script":"Disappointment! I’m quite an expert. Marilla was driving me away from the one place I ever wanted, and did I say I was sad? I did not. I said I’d made up my mind to enjoy the drive. I talked about the shore, which is what a person does when she can’t bear the other thing. What I said, what I did. Give your kid those, and what their body does too."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Retell a scene from another character’s point of view. Same events, a different teller, and different things start to matter.","wordParts":"con- and com- mean “with” or “together”: connect, combine, company, conversation.","grammar":"Most nouns add -s to make more than one. Irregular plurals don’t follow that rule, so you have to know them: child → children, woman → women, foot → feet.","spelling":"Most words just add -s. Words ending in s, x, ch, sh or z add -es (box → boxes). A consonant + y changes y to i and adds -es (story → stories), but a vowel + y keeps the y (day → days).","dol":[{"raw":"i never feel lonely when i imagine said anne","fixed":"<strong>“I</strong> never feel lonely when <strong>I</strong> <strong>imagine,”</strong> said <strong>Anne.</strong>","why":"Opening quotation mark, a capital I every time the pronoun appears, a comma inside the closing quotation mark, a capital on the name Anne, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"i think i will imagine something nicer said anne","fixed":"<strong>“I</strong> think <strong>I</strong> will imagine something <strong>nicer,”</strong> said <strong>Anne.</strong>","why":"The same pattern as the model: quotation marks around her words, a capital I both times, the comma tucked inside the closing quotation mark, a capital on Anne, and a period to finish."}],"pauses":["Wordsworth sees ordinary daffodils and turns them into something magical. Anne sees a plain road and calls it “The White Way of Delight.” How does imagination change what we see?","In Ch. 5, Anne tells Marilla about her life before Green Gables, so you are hearing it in Anne’s words rather than the narrator’s. Which parts sound like Anne deciding how to say it? Then think back over Ch. 1 to 5: if Marilla had been telling this whole story, what would you have been told about Anne, and what would you never have found out?","Anne has lived a hard life. What has kept her hopeful? Find one specific thing she relies on."],"chapterQs":["Anne tells Marilla about the families she has lived with before. Name at least TWO places or families from Anne's history.","Anne says she lived a hard life — but she rarely sounds bitter. What kept her going? Find one thing Anne says in Ch. 5 that shows what she relied on.","Looking across Ch. 1–5: What kind of person is Anne? Find ONE specific moment that shows her gift for making the world bigger with her words."],"focus":"You find out who a kid really is when something goes wrong. Today your kid gets bad news. Show how they sound and what they do — three ways: what they say, what they do, what their body does. Don’t write “she was sad.”","nowTry":"Show your kid REACTING to disappointment. Use the ladder: what they say, what they do, what their body does. No feeling words.","plate":"plates/anne-ch5.png"},"words":[{"w":"placid","d":"Calm and peaceful, without strong feeling or movement.","e":"😌"},{"w":"imagine","d":"To make a picture in your mind of something not in front of you.","e":"💭"},{"w":"delight","d":"A feeling of great pleasure or joy.","e":"✨"}]},{"w":7,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 7 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-7-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-7-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"placid","d":"Calm and peaceful, without strong feeling or movement.","e":"😌"},{"w":"imagine","d":"To make a picture in your mind of something not in front of you.","e":"💭"},{"w":"delight","d":"A feeling of great pleasure or joy.","e":"✨"}]},{"w":8,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 8.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 6)","file":"lesson-8-1-anne-ch6.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-8-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 8.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 6). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track how a character begins to change. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-welcome","done":false,"script":"Marilla Cuthbert again. I’ve the buggy out and that child beside me, and we’re bound for White Sands to have Mrs. Spencer’s mistake put right. Today’s question, I’m told, is what I might see in her that would change my mind. Well, my mind is made up, and I don’t change it lightly. Read, and judge for yourself. We’ll also sort out the little mark that shows a thing belongs to somebody."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-wgrd","done":true,"script":"People don’t change all at once. I certainly don’t. If a person changes at all, it starts small: a pause, a second thought, a thing done that doesn’t match what was said. Today you’re to watch me, of all people. Write down what I mean to do at the start. Then mark the very moment I stop and think, and ask yourself what I’d just seen."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-dol","done":false,"script":"Something in this sentence belongs to somebody, and it wants its proper mark to say so. There’s a place named in it, too, and a place deserves its capitals the same as a person does. Look at the model first and see how it’s done. Then set the second sentence to rights. Neatly, mind. A sentence is like a kitchen. Everything belongs in its place."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-guide1","done":false,"script":"Now then, word study. Two word parts this week, and both of them tell you where a thing stands. One means under. The other means between. I find myself between two things just now, a mistake I didn’t make and a child I didn’t send for. So I’ll be glad of some plain, orderly work. Take the words one at a time, and check as you go."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-morphology","done":true,"script":"Every word here begins with one of the two parts, the one for under or the one for between. Cover the front of the word with your finger and look at what’s left. Quite often it’s a word you know already. Then put the front part back and ask what it adds. Does the meaning sit under something, or between things? Decide that first, and the spelling will follow."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Each of these words comes with its own sentence from the book. Read that sentence right through before you so much as touch the card. Then look inside the word for any part you know. Only then turn it over and check. Guess first and check after, and a word stays with you. Flip the cards as fast as you can, and it’s gone again like a hen out of the yard."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"You’re to sort these without being told what the groups are. I like that. It makes a person think. In every one, something belongs to somebody. The question is how many somebodies. Look hard at where the little mark sits. Say them aloud, and you’ll find they sound exactly alike. So your eyes will have to do the work your ears can’t."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-spelling","done":true,"script":"More than one, or belonging to? That’s today’s question, and it matters more than people think. I’ve seen grown people get it wrong on a shop sign. Before you drop a word into its column, ask whether anything in it belongs to anybody. Then decide. Don’t go sprinkling those little marks about like salt, just to be safe. A mark in the wrong place is worse than none."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"White Sands at last. Mrs. Spencer will sort this out, and the child will go where she was meant to go. This week’s word is belonging, and I’ll say this much: a person belongs where there’s a proper use for them. That’s plain sense. As you read, watch the child’s face whenever somebody talks about where she’s to go. Faces tell more than people mean them to."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-read","done":true,"script":"There are two places to stop. The first comes partway through, just as something is about to be settled. The second comes at the very end. Stop at both, even if you’d rather hurry on. And at the second, don’t expect a speech from me. I’ve never been one for speeches. Ask yourself why a person might keep their feelings close, and whether that means they haven’t got any."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well. I went to White Sands to send her back, and I came home with her still beside me. Mrs. Blewett would have taken her, and I’d heard what she’s like to work for. Then I saw the child’s face. I told Matthew she may stay. It seems a sort of duty, and I’ll leave it at that. Now it’s your turn. Say what you saw, and prove it from the book."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-chapter","done":true,"script":"Three questions, and the last is about me, so be fair. For the first, I had my reasons for hesitating. One of them I’d heard, and one I saw with my own eyes. For the second, you’ll need the author’s own words about that face, two of them, copied exactly. As for the third, I said it was duty. Whether that’s the whole of it, I leave to you."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · how it connects","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your word-part answer is saved, so here’s how it fits. All week you’ve had one part for under and one for between. Well, I found myself standing between that child and Mrs. Blewett, and I didn’t care for it. And I couldn’t picture her under that woman’s roof. A word part tells you where a thing stands. What you do tells people where you stand."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-workshop","done":true,"script":"Scared. Nervous. Those words tell me nothing, and I’d not have them in a story of mine. I’ll tell you what did tell me something. That child sat on Mrs. Spencer’s ottoman and never moved. Her face went white. And she didn’t say one word, which from her is as good as a shout. Nobody said frightened. I knew. Yours may talk. Take your feeling word out, and put in what a person would see."},{"id":"copia","label":"Marilla · about Say It Three Ways","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-1-copia","done":true,"script":"Here’s a plain sentence about where Mrs. Spencer lives, and you’re to say it three ways. Put a when or a where at the front of it. Swap the doing word for one that fits better. Then tell us more about the where or the how. Change one thing at a time, and keep it true. I’ll be at that yellow house soon enough myself, so mind you describe it properly."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Catch the first small crack of change: a pause, a second thought, a choice that doesn’t match how the character usually acts.","wordParts":"sub- means “under” or “below”; inter- means “between” or “among”: submarine, submerge, interrupt, international.","grammar":"A possessive noun shows who owns something. One owner: add ’s (Anne’s trunk). More than one owner, when the word already ends in s: add just ’ (the girls’ room).","spelling":"Plurals and possessives can sound the same. A plural just means more than one and has no apostrophe (the girls laughed); a possessive shows belonging and needs one (the girl’s trunk).","dol":[{"raw":"marillas mind was made up before they reached green gables","fixed":"<strong>Marilla’s</strong> mind was made up before they reached <strong>Green Gables.</strong>","why":"A capital to start, an apostrophe and s to show the mind belongs to Marilla, capitals on the place name Green Gables, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"marillas hands held the reins all the way to white sands","fixed":"<strong>Marilla’s</strong> hands held the reins all the way to <strong>White Sands.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: apostrophe and s because the hands belong to Marilla, capitals on the place name White Sands, and a period at the end. Reins is only more than one, so it needs no apostrophe."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 6, watch Marilla. She has made up her mind to send Anne back, and when Mrs. Spencer offers her to Mrs. Blewett instead, something stops her. What does she notice about Anne that changes her mind? Is this Marilla becoming a different person, or Marilla finally admitting something she already felt?","Marilla is about to give Anne up to Mrs. Blewett. What stops her? What does she see in Anne’s face that changes her mind?","Marilla makes up her mind to keep Anne, but she does not make a warm speech about it. Why do you think Marilla keeps her feelings so quiet?"],"chapterQs":["When Mrs. Spencer suggests Mrs. Peter Blewett could take Anne, Marilla pauses. Why does Marilla hesitate to hand Anne over to Mrs. Blewett?","Find the moment when Anne realizes she might get to stay at Green Gables. How does Montgomery describe Anne's face changing? Quote at least two specific details.","Marilla tells Matthew, “She may stay.” What reason does Marilla give for keeping Anne? Does that match what she's actually feeling?"],"focus":"Real characters have specific feelings, and the writer never names them. Instead you use three kinds of detail: something the body does, something the senses catch, and something the kid says. This is the most useful trick in the whole project.","nowTry":"Write ONE sentence about your kid that uses a feeling word (scared, happy, angry, embarrassed). Then rewrite it with no feeling word: a body detail, a sensory detail, and one line of dialogue.","plate":"plates/anne-ch6.png"},"words":[{"w":"benevolent","d":"Kindly; meaning well; gentle in spirit.","e":"🌷"},{"w":"irresistible","d":"Too tempting or strong to resist.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"penitent","d":"Feeling truly sorry for doing something wrong.","e":"🙏"}]},{"w":8,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 8.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 7 & 8)","file":"lesson-8-2-anne-ch7-8.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-8-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 8.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 7 & 8). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track how a character begins to change. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"Marilla Cuthbert again, and I’ll be at your elbow all lesson. Today’s question is what you do when everybody around you knows something you were never taught. That’s a hard spot to be in, so don’t rush it. While you read, keep an eye out for an old habit starting to bend. And in word study, you’ll be hunting for the words whose apostrophe shows they own something."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Here’s how I’d go about it. Before you start the chapter, settle in your head how that child has always done things, in other people’s houses. One plain habit will do. Then, as you read, watch for somebody asking her to do it differently. Does she go along with it? Keep hold of the one thing she won’t give up. You needn’t write it. Just carry it with you."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-dol","done":false,"script":"Tap the colored words in the model first, and read what each one tells you. Then copy the second sentence onto paper just as it is, mistakes and all. Fix it one thing at a time. Start with the very first word. Then look for anybody who owns something, because that needs an apostrophe. Then the names. Check it once, at the end, and not before."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"All four of today’s jobs are tapping jobs, matching or picking out words. If a match won’t go, don’t keep jabbing at it. Stop, read both sides again, and think. That child loses her way the minute her mind wanders, and so will you if you hurry. Go steadily. If one beats you, leave it and come back. The others will make it easier."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"Start with the two you’re surest of and match those first. Once they’re done, the other two have fewer places to go, and the answer often comes on its own. If you’re stuck, think of a long word you know that has the piece in it. Say that word out loud and ask what the piece is adding. Your own words will help you here."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Try this before you tap anything. Cover the meanings with your hand and say what you think each word means. Then uncover them and match. If you get one wrong, a line will come up telling you why. Read it properly before you try again. That’s the bit that teaches you. Guessing until it turns green teaches you nothing at all."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"Go through the sentence one word at a time, with your finger if it helps. Every word ending in s, ask whether something belongs to it. Here’s a test I trust. See if you can turn it round with the word of. The buggy of Matthew. If that works, you’ve found an owner. If it only means more than one, leave it alone."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Tap the ones you’re sure of first, then go back for the ones you’re not. When you think you’re finished, go over what you’ve tapped. If one of them only means more than one of something, untap it. Then the question underneath wants one you tapped and one you left, so choose a pair you could explain to me without stumbling."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"I’ve never brought up a child before, so I suppose the two of us are beginners. This week’s watch is about learning what nobody taught you. If you come to a place where she’s plainly out of her depth, stop there a moment. Ask yourself what you’d do in her shoes. Would you own up, or bluff it? Then read on and see which she chooses."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-read","done":true,"script":"Read the stopping question now, before you start, so it’s already in your head. It comes at the end of the first chapter. Put a finger there, or a scrap of paper, so you don’t sail right past it. The second chapter is longer. If your mind wanders the way hers does, go back a paragraph. Nobody will think the worse of you."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well. I told the child to say her prayers, and she told me she never said any. Nobody had taught her. So she’s learning the Lord’s Prayer, and she asked if she might call me Aunt Marilla. I said no. Now, before you type a word, go back and find the places in the book you’ll need. Keep a finger on each one while you write."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"For the first question, find where she tells me why she never prays. There are two reasons, not one, so don’t stop at the first. For the second, think about what a real friend does for you, and what a make-believe one can’t. For the third, look hard at the word she wanted to call me. Start each answer with because, and it’ll keep you honest."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · how it connects","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your grammar answer is saved, and it’ll show up with the rest of your work. Read it over once before you hand it in. Did you explain why, and not just which? Here’s how it joins up. All week an apostrophe has been showing that something belongs to somebody. And that child asked to call me Aunt, a word for somebody you belong to."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"Here’s how I’d start. Put your character alone somewhere real, a bedroom or the top of the stairs. Then ask what they’d wish for, if nobody could hear. That child made friends out of a reflection and an echo when she was lonely. Your character might do something quite different. Borrow one of the sentence starters if you’re stuck. Write it in their words, not yours."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Marilla · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-8-2-rwm","done":true,"script":"Three boxes, and each one wants something different. In the first, just say plainly what’s happening. Don’t dress it up. For the second, try the sentence without its last word, then with it. Say what the word changes. For the third, use the pattern under the box and put somebody you know into it. Somebody real is easier to write than somebody invented."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Change often shows first when an old habit bends. Watch for the moment a character does something a little differently.","wordParts":"sub- means “under” or “below”; inter- means “between” or “among”: submarine, submerge, interrupt, international.","grammar":"A possessive noun shows who owns something. One owner: add ’s (Anne’s trunk). More than one owner, when the word already ends in s: add just ’ (the girls’ room).","spelling":"Plurals and possessives can sound the same. A plural just means more than one and has no apostrophe (the girls laughed); a possessive shows belonging and needs one (the girl’s trunk).","dol":[{"raw":"annes prayer was unlike any marilla had ever heard","fixed":"<strong>Anne’s</strong> prayer was unlike any <strong>Marilla</strong> had ever heard<strong>.</strong>","why":"A capital A and an apostrophe and s, because the prayer belongs to Anne, a capital on the name Marilla, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"matthews buggy stood in the yard at green gables","fixed":"<strong>Matthew’s</strong> buggy stood in the yard at <strong>Green Gables.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: apostrophe and s because the buggy belongs to Matthew, capitals on the place name Green Gables, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 7 and 8, watch Anne being taught. Marilla begins her bringing-up, and Anne has never lived by anyone’s rules before. Which of Anne’s old habits bends to fit Green Gables, and which one does she hold on to? What does the part she keeps tell you about her?","The child in this poem notices small, everyday things—candlelight, birds, footsteps. What small, everyday detail does Anne notice that most people would walk right past?","Anne’s very first prayer ends with “Yours respectfully, Anne Shirley.” What does this odd prayer reveal about Anne’s ideas of God and grown-ups?"],"chapterQs":["Anne tells Marilla she's never said a prayer in her life. What reason does Anne give for never praying? What does her reason reveal about her life so far?","Anne tells Marilla about Katie Maurice (the bookcase girl) and Violetta (the echo). Why does Anne invent imaginary friends? What is she missing?","In Ch. 8, Anne tells Marilla, “I’d love to call you Aunt Marilla.” Why does this matter so much to Anne?"],"focus":"What does your kid think when no one is there? Readers feel close to a character when they get to hear the private thoughts — the wishes, the worries, the things they’d never say out loud.","nowTry":"Write 2–3 sentences of your kid alone, thinking. What do they wish for? What do they worry about? Use their own words — the ones they’d use in their head.","rwm":"“Anne knelt at Marilla’s knee and looked up gravely.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch7-8.png"},"words":[{"w":"benevolent","d":"Kindly; meaning well; gentle in spirit.","e":"🌷"},{"w":"irresistible","d":"Too tempting or strong to resist.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"penitent","d":"Feeling truly sorry for doing something wrong.","e":"🙏"}]},{"w":8,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 8.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 9)","file":"lesson-8-3-anne-ch9.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-8-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 8.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 9). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Mrs. Lynde’s welcome","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-welcome","done":true,"script":"Mrs. Rachel Lynde here, and I’ll keep you on track today, never fear. Today’s question is what makes a person’s faults easy to forgive, and what makes them hard to like. I see you’ll be tracking somebody’s temper. I can’t think whose. You’ll be typing possessive words yourself, too, apostrophes and all. Mind you read every sentence right to the end before you answer."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Here’s a trick I’ve learned from years of watching my neighbors. When somebody flares up, don’t just look at the shouting. Look at the line just before it. Whatever was said right there is nearly always the cause. So when you reach a flare-up, stop, go back a few lines, and find what set it off. Then ask what they were protecting."},{"id":"dol","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-dol","done":false,"script":"Copy the sentence you’re fixing onto paper first, just as it’s printed. Then go hunting for apostrophes, one at a time. Each time you add one, ask yourself a question. Has a letter been left out here, or does somebody own something? Both need one, but for different reasons. Look at how the model writes Mrs., too. And capitals on names, mind. Mine especially."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before the word work","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-guide1","done":true,"script":"You type every answer yourself today, so here’s my best advice. After each one, read the whole sentence back to yourself before you move on. You’ll catch half your own mistakes that way, I promise you. I always read a letter over twice before I send it. It’s saved me more embarrassment than I can tell you."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about the word parts","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Read the clue after each word first. It tells you what the whole word means, and that’s your biggest help. Then ask yourself, is this about something under, or something between? Try the word out loud both ways if you’re not sure. One of them will sound like a word you’ve heard a hundred times. The other won’t sound like anything at all."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s words","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Do the gap you’re surest of first, and cross that word off in your head. Now you’ve only three to choose from for the next one, and two for the last. That spare word is there to catch you out, so don’t let it. If two words both seem to fit, say the sentence out loud with each. Your ear usually knows."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"Before you type, count the owners. Is there one, or more than one? Hold up a finger for each if it helps. Once you know how many, you know where the apostrophe goes. If you’ve forgotten how that works, go back to what the lesson taught you on the first day. There’s no shame in checking. I check everything, twice."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"This one’s a real puzzle, and I do like a puzzle. Some gaps want more than one of something and some want an owner. Here’s how to tell. Read past the gap to the next word. Could that next thing belong to it? Then you want an owner. If not, you just want more than one. People scatter apostrophes about like pepper, and I call it a disgrace."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you open the book","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"Here I am at Green Gables at last. Marilla says the child is out in the orchard. This week’s watch is about having a temper and having a reason. Here’s how to keep track of it. Whenever somebody gets upset, ask two things. What set them off? And were they right to be upset? You can say yes to that even if they behaved badly."},{"id":"read","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you read","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-read","done":true,"script":"Read both stopping questions now, before you begin. Then you’ll know what to look for. They both come near the end of the chapter, so you can read the first part straight through. When you reach the first stop, don’t just think a quick answer. Think of a reason, too. Keep your book open when you’re done. You’ll want it for the questions."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Mrs. Lynde · before you write","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well! I’ve never been so insulted in all my born days. I only said what anybody could see, that she’s skinny and homely with hair as red as carrots, and she flew at me, stamping and shouting. Marilla took her part, if you please. Now, answer these questions, and quote the book. I want proof, written down, of who was in the wrong."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about the questions","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-chapter","done":true,"script":"For the first question, go back and find the shouting. Copy two things exactly as they’re printed, one she did and one she said. For the second, find what Marilla said to me after the child went upstairs. Read it twice. It surprised me. For the third, you’ll need what the child told Marilla, because she certainly told me nothing. Look for her reasons."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Mrs. Lynde · how it connects","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your spelling answer is saved, and it’ll show up with your other work. Read it over once before you hand it in, and check every apostrophe has a reason to be there. Here’s how it fits. All week you’ve been deciding what one apostrophe means. Well, one remark of mine turned a whole visit upside down. Small things matter. You judge whether mine did."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about today’s writing","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-workshop","done":true,"script":"Two things. First, where your kid comes from. I called that child’s hair carrots and got a tempest for it, and Marilla said after that nobody had ever taught her better. Well, knowing where she came from, I believed it, that’s what. Two sentences of before, for your kid. Second, the other person. Every story needs one. Pick yours and say what they are to your kid. This chapter, it’s me."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Mrs. Lynde · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/rachel/g4ela-8-3-rwm","done":true,"script":"I heard those words with my own ears, so I know how they sounded. In the first box, just tell me plainly what’s happening. In the second, look at everything after the words she shouts. What is her body doing, and why put it in? For the third, think of somebody you know with a temper, and write one line in their voice using the pattern."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Change isn’t a straight line. When a character slips backwards, ask what they’re trying to protect.","wordParts":"sub- means “under” or “below”; inter- means “between” or “among”: submarine, submerge, interrupt, international.","grammar":"A possessive noun shows who owns something. One owner: add ’s (Anne’s trunk). More than one owner, when the word already ends in s: add just ’ (the girls’ room).","spelling":"Plurals and possessives can sound the same. A plural just means more than one and has no apostrophe (the girls laughed); a possessive shows belonging and needs one (the girl’s trunk).","dol":[{"raw":"mrs lyndes words about annes looks werent kind","fixed":"<strong>Mrs. Lynde’s</strong> words about <strong>Anne’s</strong> looks <strong>weren’t</strong> kind<strong>.</strong>","why":"A capital M and a period after Mrs., an apostrophe and s on Lynde’s and Anne’s to show belonging, an apostrophe in the contraction weren’t, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"marillas voice wasnt as sharp as mrs lyndes tongue","fixed":"<strong>Marilla’s</strong> voice <strong>wasn’t</strong> as sharp as <strong>Mrs. Lynde’s</strong> tongue<strong>.</strong>","why":"The same two jobs for the apostrophe: belonging in Marilla’s and Mrs. Lynde’s, and a squeezed-together word in wasn’t. Capitals on the names, a period after Mrs., and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 9, watch Anne lose her temper and then refuse to say sorry. Marilla has been settling her into Green Gables, and now Anne digs in harder than ever. What is Anne protecting when she refuses? Is this the old Anne coming back, or a new Anne who finally has something worth defending?","Marilla shuts Anne up alone in her room and goes back down to her work. Who has the harder wait — Anne upstairs, or Marilla downstairs?","Anne refuses to apologize. Why is the apology so hard for her, even harder than being shut up alone in her room?"],"chapterQs":["Mrs. Rachel Lynde calls Anne “skinny and homely” and “red as carrots.” How does Anne react — what specifically does she do and say? Use at least two details.","Marilla is angry at Anne for losing her temper, but she also tells Mrs. Rachel, “You were too hard on her.” Why does Marilla say BOTH things? What is she starting to feel?","Anne refuses to apologize. What reason does she give? What does this show about how Anne feels her wounds — the ones about her looks?"],"focus":"Two things today. First, a little history: where your kid is from and what they’ve already been through. Second, the other person in your story — a friend, a grown-up, or a rival. A story needs at least two people.","nowTry":"Write 2–3 sentences of your kid’s history (where they’re from, what they’ve been through). Then one sentence naming the other person in your story and what they are to your kid.","rwm":"“‘I hate you,’ she cried in a choked voice, stamping her foot on the floor.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch9.png"},"words":[{"w":"benevolent","d":"Kindly; meaning well; gentle in spirit.","e":"🌷"},{"w":"irresistible","d":"Too tempting or strong to resist.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"penitent","d":"Feeling truly sorry for doing something wrong.","e":"🙏"}]},{"w":8,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 8.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 10)","file":"lesson-8-4-anne-ch10.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-8-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 8.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 10). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Matthew’s welcome","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Matthew Cuthbert again. I’ll be with you today. Here’s the question. By the end of this chapter somebody calls Green Gables home, and you’re to work out what had to happen first. It’s the last day of the week, so you’ll look back at how far somebody’s come. And you’ll play editor, finding apostrophes that went astray. Take it slow. That’s how I do things."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Matthew · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Before you read, take a moment and picture Anne at White Sands, sitting quiet while grown-ups decided where she’d go. Hold that picture. Then as you read today, watch for one small thing she does that the girl at White Sands couldn’t have done. When you find it, you’ve measured how far she’s come. And watch whoever’s standing next to her. They may have moved too."},{"id":"dol","label":"Matthew · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-dol","done":false,"script":"Here’s how I’d go at it. Copy the sentence out first, just as it is. Then read the model aloud, slowly, and listen for where your voice pauses and where it stops. Now look at the model again and see what the writer put in those spots. Do the same in yours. Last of all, look at the names. The model will show you what they need."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Matthew · before the word work","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four jobs today, and one of them you get to choose. Take them one box at a time. If one goes wrong, it’s no great trouble. You can always try again. I never got anything right the first time myself, not even driving the mare. After the words there are two drafts to fix, like a careful editor. Read those slowly, out loud if you can."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Matthew · about the word parts","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"You’ve matched these before, so this is just a check. If you get one wrong, it’s no trouble. Leave it, do the others, and come back when there are only two left. You’ll have a fair chance then. If you’re properly stuck, think of a long word you know that uses the piece, and say that word out loud. The meaning’s usually sitting right in it."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Matthew · about today’s words","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"If you can’t decide which to pick, I’d try explaining a word to somebody younger. Picture a little one sitting beside you. Say it out loud first, in plain words, before you write anything. If you can make a little one understand it, you understand it yourself. Whatever you choose, use the word in a sentence about a real feeling."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Matthew · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"Read each sentence out loud, slowly. When you come to a word with an apostrophe, stop and ask what it’s doing there. Does somebody own something? And when a word owns something, check that it has its apostrophe. Don’t tap every sentence with an apostrophe in it. Some of them are right just as they are."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Matthew · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Same sort of job, different draft. This time, some sentences have an apostrophe on a word that only means more than one. Those need tapping too. For each sentence, ask yourself one thing. Is this word just more than one of something, or does it own something? That question settles most of them. Take your time over it. There’s no prize for finishing first."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Matthew · before you open the book","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"Anne hasn’t come down, and Marilla won’t say why. I’ve been hanging about the kitchen like a lost calf. This week’s watch is belonging. If you want a way to keep track of it, notice every time somebody does a small kind thing without being asked. I’d keep a count. Belonging doesn’t come with a speech. It comes in little things like that."},{"id":"read","label":"Matthew · before you read","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-read","done":false,"script":"There’s one stop, at the very end, so you can read straight through. Some of this chapter is funny, and you’re allowed to laugh. When you reach the end, though, stop before you turn to the questions. Read the stopping question and sit with it for a bit. Think about what one small thing might mean to somebody who’s never had it."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Matthew · before you write","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well, now you know. I went upstairs. I hadn’t been up there in four years, not since I helped Marilla paper the spare bedroom. I asked Anne if she’d just smooth it over, and she said she’d do anything for me. Don’t tell Marilla I had a hand in it. Now, go and find your answers in the book. Take your time. I’ll wait."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Matthew · about the questions","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-chapter","done":true,"script":"For the first question, think about what I’m like with people, and what it cost me to climb those stairs. That’s your clue. For the second, find the part where Anne says sorry and read it again. Notice how she says it, not just what. For the last one, look back over what you wrote earlier this week. You may already have the moment written down."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Matthew · how it connects","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your vocabulary answer is saved, and it’ll show up with your work. If you’ve got a moment, read it over once. Here’s something to think on. One of this week’s words was penitent, somebody who’s truly sorry. Anne said sorry to Rachel, and Rachel forgave her. Whether Anne was penitent, or just enjoying herself a good deal, I’ll leave to you."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Matthew · about today’s writing","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-8-4-workshop","done":true,"script":"Here’s how I’d start, if it were me. Write one line about how your character feels at the very beginning of your story. Then write one line about how they feel at the very end. Now you know the shape. The story is just how they get from one line to the other. Anne came a long way this week. Your character needn’t go so far."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Measure how far a character has come. The proof is usually a small action, not a big speech.","wordParts":"sub- means “under” or “below”; inter- means “between” or “among”: submarine, submerge, interrupt, international.","grammar":"A possessive noun shows who owns something. One owner: add ’s (Anne’s trunk). More than one owner, when the word already ends in s: add just ’ (the girls’ room).","spelling":"Plurals and possessives can sound the same. A plural just means more than one and has no apostrophe (the girls laughed); a possessive shows belonging and needs one (the girl’s trunk).","dol":[{"raw":"its lovely to be home anne whispered to marilla","fixed":"<strong>“It’s</strong> lovely to be <strong>home,”</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> whispered to <strong>Marilla.</strong>","why":"An opening quotation mark and a capital, an apostrophe in the contraction It’s, a comma inside the closing quotation mark, capitals on the names Anne and Marilla, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"its time to go home marilla said to anne","fixed":"<strong>“It’s</strong> time to go <strong>home,”</strong> <strong>Marilla</strong> said to <strong>Anne.</strong>","why":"The same pieces: quotation marks around what is said, an apostrophe in It’s because it means it is, a comma inside the closing quotation mark, capitals on both names, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Frost’s speaker invites someone along—“You come too.” By the end of Ch. 10, Anne finally has a place that invites her to stay. What moment tells you Green Gables has become her home?","As you read Ch. 10, watch Anne apologize. Think back to Ch. 6, when Marilla nearly handed her to Mrs. Blewett, and to Ch. 9, when Anne would not say sorry to anyone. What has changed in Anne so that she can do this now? And walking home afterward, what has changed in Marilla?","Walking home, Anne slips her hand into Marilla’s. What does this small action mean for a girl who has never had a home? What does it mean for Marilla?"],"chapterQs":["Matthew sneaks upstairs to talk to Anne — even though he hates going upstairs. What does this small action show about how Matthew has come to feel about Anne?","When Anne apologizes to Mrs. Rachel, she goes “rapt and radiant” — she's almost enjoying it. Why is Marilla dismayed by this? What does it tell us about Anne's imagination?","Looking across Ch. 6-10, name one specific moment when Anne begins to belong at Green Gables. Then explain how this moment is different from how she felt at the start of the week."],"focus":"A story is a kid who is different at the end than at the start. Today you decide what that change is. You don’t have to know how it happens yet. You just need the two ends: how they think at the start, how they think at the end.","nowTry":"Write 2–3 sentences: how your kid thinks or feels at the START of your story, and how they think or feel at the END. That change is your story’s shape.","plate":"plates/anne-ch10.png"},"words":[{"w":"benevolent","d":"Kindly; meaning well; gentle in spirit.","e":"🌷"},{"w":"irresistible","d":"Too tempting or strong to resist.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"penitent","d":"Feeling truly sorry for doing something wrong.","e":"🙏"}]},{"w":8,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 8 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-8-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-8-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"benevolent","d":"Kindly; meaning well; gentle in spirit.","e":"🌷"},{"w":"irresistible","d":"Too tempting or strong to resist.","e":"🍎"},{"w":"penitent","d":"Feeling truly sorry for doing something wrong.","e":"🙏"}]},{"w":9,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 9.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 11)","file":"lesson-9-1-anne-ch11.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-9-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 9.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 11). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice cause and effect in character choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-welcome","done":true,"script":"Marilla Cuthbert here, guiding you again. Today’s question is about wanting something and being told no. You’re to watch how the author shows the wanting and the disappointment in the very same breath. Keep hold of one thing you want yourself that somebody has called foolish. It’ll help you understand her. In word study there’s a new word part, the one that means above, or too much."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Here’s how to go about it. Before you open the book, settle what you’re looking for: one thing the child decides to do, even a small one. When you find it, don’t move on. Back up a line or two and find what happened just before. That’s your cause. Then read a little past it and see who has to deal with what she did. Hold all three in your head together."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-dol","done":false,"script":"Tap the colored parts of the model before you touch the second sentence, and read what each one tells you. The one you’re fixing starts with a person, and a person’s name starts with a capital letter. Then it tells you where she is, and after that what she was doing. Look at what the model puts between those two parts. Write yours in your notebook, check it against the model, then tap the button."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-guide1","done":true,"script":"Word study, and there’s a new word part this week. It means above, or across, or too much, and I dare say you’ll meet it more often than you’d like. Work through the four boxes in order. In each one, read the whole thing before you start tapping and typing. Most mistakes I’ve seen come from starting before you’ve looked. Check as you go, not at the end."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-morphology","done":true,"script":"Read the four words at the top and press the button to see them cracked open. Then, in the game, cover the front of each word with your finger and read what’s left. Read the little description after it, too. That description is your clue: does it point above something, or beneath it? Type the part that fits. If you miss one, read the line that comes up before you try again."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Do the example at the top first, all three steps, even though the answer’s printed. That’s practice. Then for each of the three cards, read the book’s sentence, guess out loud, and only then tap. In the match at the bottom, do the words you’re sure of first and leave the hardest for last, when there’s only one meaning left. If you match wrong, a line comes up. Read it."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"Three columns and nobody’s told you what they are. Good. Move the two words you’re surest about first, one to each side, and ask yourself what makes them different. Then find each one’s partner. The pair left over belong together for a reason of their own, so say what that reason is before you drop them. Then name all three groups in the box, and say how you told them apart."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-spelling","done":true,"script":"Say each word out loud before you drag it, and say it the lazy way you would in a hurry. One syllable goes mushy. Find that syllable, then look at which vowel letter is sitting in it. That letter tells you the column, not the sound, because the sound is the same in all six. If you get one wrong, a line will tell you why. Read it before you try again."},{"id":"copia","label":"Marilla · about Say It Three Ways","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-copia","done":true,"script":"Here’s a plain sentence from before she’d said a word about the dresses. Say it three ways. First, put something at the front, a where or a when. Second, swap the marked verb for one that shows how she looked, since a person can look at a thing a dozen different ways. Third, tell us more about where or how. Keep it true to the picture. Change one thing at a time."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"The child starts Sunday school this week, and she’ll meet other children there, which she’s had little enough of. This week’s watch is friendship. I’ve never had a great deal of use for it myself, but I’ll say this: a friend is somebody you choose, and who chooses you back. As you read, notice whether she has anybody like that yet, and what it costs her that she hasn’t."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-read","done":true,"script":"Two stops, and the first comes before she’s even left the house, so look for it early. It asks why a child would care so much about a thing I’ve called ridiculous. Don’t answer for me. Answer for her. The second stop is at the very end, and it’s about honesty. Ask yourself whether saying the true thing is brave or only careless, and have a reason ready either way."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Well. She went to Sunday school alone, since I had a headache coming on, and came home and told me it was horrid. Mr. Bell’s prayer was too long, Miss Rogerson asked a great many questions, and every other girl in the class had puffed sleeves. I ought to have scolded her. The trouble is, some of what she said I’ve thought myself for years. Your turn. Say what you noticed, and show me where."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is about the dresses I made. Look at what she said when she first saw them, and at what she didn’t say. For the second, something happened on the road to church that nobody has told me about yet, so you’ll have to find it yourself. It’s small. Read slowly. The third quotes her exactly. Before you answer, count how many girls in that class had what she didn’t."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · how it connects","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your morphology answer is saved, so let’s tie it up. This week’s new word part means above, or too much. Well, that child does everything too much, if you ask me. Too much imagination, too much feeling, too much talk. And yet I keep listening. Look back at the sentence you wrote. Does your word say something true about her world? If it doesn’t quite, you’ll know what to reach for next time."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-9-1-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you name a want and a wall. Make them small and real. That child wanted puffed sleeves, and I was the wall, and I had my reasons. Notice it wasn’t a grand want. It was sleeves. Then look at what the want tells you about the person: she wanted to look like the other girls. Pick your character’s want the same way. Something you could point at, that shows who they are underneath."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"When a character surprises you, back up and find what pushed them to act that way.","wordParts":"over- means “above,” “across,” or “too much”: overlook, overcoat. Review: sub- and under- mean “below”: submarine, underground.","grammar":"Concrete nouns name things you can see, touch, or hear (hat). Abstract nouns name feelings or ideas (imagination, despair). Collective nouns name a group acting as one (class, congregation).","spelling":"In an unstressed syllable the vowel often blurs into a lazy “uh” sound called schwa. It can be spelled a, e, or o (about, garden, lesson), so you have to know which letter the word uses.","dol":[{"raw":"anne sat on the bed looking sadly at the plain sleeves","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> sat on the <strong>bed,</strong> looking sadly at the plain <strong>sleeves.</strong>","why":"A capital letter on the name Anne, a comma before the describing phrase that tells what she was doing, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"marilla stood in the doorway waiting for a word of thanks","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> stood in the <strong>doorway,</strong> waiting for a word of <strong>thanks.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on the name Marilla, a comma before the describing phrase that tells what she was doing, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 11, follow the dress with no puffed sleeves. Marilla calls the sleeves ridiculous and says no, and Anne cares anyway. What in Anne’s life makes her want them so badly? How might that disappointment shape the way she sees her first Sunday school?","Anne wants puffed sleeves. Marilla says no. Why does Anne care so much about something Marilla calls ridiculous?","Anne comes home and tells Marilla exactly what she thought of Sunday school, including the parts she did not like. Why does Anne say the honest thing when the polite thing would be easier?"],"chapterQs":["Marilla makes Anne three new dresses but Anne is disappointed. Why is Anne disappointed, and what does this tell you about what she wants most right now?","On the way to Sunday school, Anne adds flowers to her plain hat. What does this small choice show about Anne’s character?","Anne tells Marilla, “life was really not worth living without puffed sleeves.” This is a small thing. Why does it feel so huge to Anne? What does she really want?"],"focus":"Conflict is simple: your kid wants something, and something is in the way. Today you name both, as specifically as you can. The want is from your character card. The wall is new.","nowTry":"Conflict starts with a want. Write 2–3 sentences: name ONE specific thing your kid wants. Then name ONE thing or person that makes it hard to get.","plate":"plates/anne-ch11.png"},"words":[{"w":"solemn","d":"Serious; deeply earnest; not joking.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"resolute","d":"Firm; determined; not easily changed.","e":"💪"},{"w":"vindictive","d":"Wanting to get back at someone; full of revenge.","e":"⚡"}]},{"w":9,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 9.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 12 & 13)","file":"lesson-9-2-anne-ch12-13.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-9-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 9.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 12 & 13). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice cause and effect in character choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Diana’s welcome","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Diana Barry, and I’m your guide today, for the first time. Your question is about how a friendship starts, and you won’t find it in speeches. You’ll find it in small things people do, mostly with their hands. So watch hands. In word study you’ll be finding the words for things you can’t pick up. And there’s a poem to learn by heart. Say it out loud, not in your head."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Diana · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-wgrd","done":true,"script":"This one’s like a game, and I love games. Pick something small that happens, the smaller the better. Then keep asking, and then what? Follow it in your head. When the chain seems to run out, push once more, because the last link is usually somebody who wasn’t even there when it started. If you can follow it all the way to what somebody is hoping for by the end, you’ve done it."},{"id":"dol","label":"Diana · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-dol","done":false,"script":"The model is somebody talking, so look at the marks that hold the talking in, and at what sits inside the last one. The sentence you’re fixing isn’t talking at all. It’s two whole ideas squashed together with nothing between them. Read it aloud and listen for where your voice drops. That’s where the first idea needs a period and the next one needs a capital letter. And two names want capitals too."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Diana · before the word work","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"Word study! First there are word parts to match, and one of the four isn’t a front part at all, so read the meanings carefully. Then the same four words as yesterday, then a sentence to tap, then a row of words to say out loud. Saying words out loud helps more than you’d think. Do the four boxes in order, and don’t rush the last one because it looks easy."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Diana · about the word parts","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"Tap a word part, then its meaning. Before you tap, look at the three example words in brackets after each meaning and say them. If the part you’re holding fits at the front of all three, that’s your match. One of the four isn’t a front part at all. It goes at the end of a word, and its meaning is a thing, not a direction. Match that one last and it’ll nearly match itself."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Diana · about today’s words","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"You met these four yesterday. Before you tap anything, say each word and try to picture the sentence it came from. Do the two you remember best first. Then there are only two words and two meanings left, and you can test each pair by saying them together and listening. If you tap a wrong one, a little line comes up to explain. Read it properly. It’s there to help, not to scold."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Diana · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"Tap every one, and there’s more than one. Here’s my test. For each naming word, ask: could I put it in a box and carry it over to Green Gables? If I could, leave it alone. If I couldn’t, because it’s a feeling or an idea, tap it. Be careful. Things you could carry sit right next to the ones you can’t. Read to the very last word."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Diana · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"You’re after the odd ones out, so don’t tap the words that do the usual thing. Say each word slowly, then quickly. In most of them one syllable goes soft and muddy. Those are the ordinary ones. Leave them be. The ones you want stay clear at both ends however fast you say them. Say each one twice before you decide. Then pick one you tapped and explain it in the box."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Diana · before you open the book","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"I’m at home with a book, which is where I usually am. Mother thinks I read too much, and Father keeps giving me more. This week’s watch is about letting yourself look forward to things. I’m good at that. I’m not so good at the waiting. As you read, notice what somebody lets herself hope for, and how she behaves while she waits. Waiting badly and waiting well look very different."},{"id":"read","label":"Diana · before you read","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-read","done":true,"script":"There’s one stop, after the first chapter, before you go on to the second. It asks what a promise means to somebody who has never had a family to promise her anything. When you get there, think about it from the other side, too. What would a girl who does have a family get out of a promise like that? Then read the second chapter straight through and count the things she looks forward to."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Diana · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-rwm","done":true,"script":"Well. That’s me in there, and it’s true, I do. I never noticed till I read it. Try this: read the two sentences, then cover the second one with your finger. With just the first, I laughed once. Put the second back, and I’m a person who laughs. When you write yours, make the habit match who your character is, not just any old habit. Mine tells you I’m not much of a worrier."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Diana · before you write","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-guide3","done":false,"script":"So now you know. She asked me straight out if I could like her enough to be her bosom friend, and I said I guessed so. Then we joined hands over the garden path, pretending it was a stream, and swore. I got the giggles, but I meant it. And I’ve been explaining ice cream to her, which is harder than it sounds. Go on. Say what you noticed, and show it from the book."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Diana · about the questions","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is about something she said to Mother when we met, and I was sitting right there. Why would a person use such big words at a moment like that? For the second, look at the ceremony, not just the promise. Who set it up, and why so grand? The third is about something Marilla said to her at home. I wasn’t there. Read it carefully and decide whether Marilla is right."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Diana · how it connects","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your grammar answer is saved. You tapped the words for things you can’t put in a box. Well, I’ve just got one of those. Friendship. You can’t hold it, but you can watch it: two hands joined over a path, a promise said twice, a book lent. Look back at the word you wrote about and ask what somebody would have to do to show it. That’s how a writer makes an invisible thing visible."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Diana · about today’s writing","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-9-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you sort walls: a person, a rule, or a fear. Here’s something I only found out afterwards. Anne was frightened of meeting me. She thought I mightn’t like her. That was her wall, and it was inside her the whole time. So when you pick your kid’s main wall, check whether it’s really a person, or a fear wearing a person’s face. Then add a smaller wall of a different kind."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Follow one small cause forward and see how far its effects reach.","wordParts":"over- means “above,” “across,” or “too much”: overlook, overcoat. Review: sub- and under- mean “below”: submarine, underground.","grammar":"Concrete nouns name things you can see, touch, or hear (hat). Abstract nouns name feelings or ideas (imagination, despair). Collective nouns name a group acting as one (class, congregation).","spelling":"In an unstressed syllable the vowel often blurs into a lazy “uh” sound called schwa. It can be spelled a, e, or o (about, garden, lesson), so you have to know which letter the word uses.","dol":[{"raw":"diana and anne promised to be friends forever they joined hands","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> and <strong>Anne</strong> promised to be friends forever<strong>.</strong> <strong>They</strong> joined hands<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capital letters on the names Diana and Anne, a period to end the first thought, a capital T to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"diana dropped her book the callers had come in","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> dropped her book<strong>.</strong> <strong>The</strong> callers had come in<strong>.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on the name Diana, a period where the first thought ends, a capital T to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 12 and 13, follow the promise Anne and Diana make to each other. Start there and trace forward: what does having a bosom friend let Anne do or feel that she could not before? By the end of Ch. 13, how does that friendship change what Anne is looking forward to, and what she would hate to lose?","Dickinson’s two “nobodies” find each other and share a secret. Anne and Diana clasp hands over a stream and vow to be bosom friends. What makes finding one person who understands you feel so important?","Anne and Diana hold hands and swear to be friends. For a child who has had no family, what does it mean to be promised forever by another person?"],"chapterQs":["Anne tells Mrs. Barry “I am well in body although considerably rumpled up in spirit.” What does Anne mean? Why does she talk this way?","Anne and Diana make a “solemn vow” in the Barry garden. What promise do they make, and why is it important to Anne in particular?","Marilla tells Anne, “You set your heart too much on things, Anne.” Pick one example from these chapters (the picnic, the solemn vow, Diana’s friendship). Why does setting her heart on things create a problem?"],"focus":"Walls come in three kinds: a person (someone who blocks them), a rule (something they aren’t allowed to do), or a fear (something inside them). Most good stories have one main wall and a second, smaller one.","nowTry":"Name the kind of wall your kid faces — a person, a rule, or a fear — and say exactly what it is. Then add one smaller wall of a different kind. 2–3 sentences.","rwm":"“Diana laughed. Diana always laughed before she spoke.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch12-13.png"},"words":[{"w":"solemn","d":"Serious; deeply earnest; not joking.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"resolute","d":"Firm; determined; not easily changed.","e":"💪"},{"w":"vindictive","d":"Wanting to get back at someone; full of revenge.","e":"⚡"}]},{"w":9,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 9.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 14)","file":"lesson-9-3-anne-ch14.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-9-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 9.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 14). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Anne’s welcome","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Anne Shirley, and I’m guiding you again, which is a great honor. Today’s question is how one bad choice grows into a bigger trouble, and you’re to follow the chain link by link. I know something about that, though I’d rather not say what yet. In word study you build the answers yourself instead of sorting them. And the reading move isn’t in the warm-up today. It’s waiting on the Reading tab."},{"id":"dol","label":"Anne · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-dol","done":false,"script":"The model has a list in it, and I adore lists. Look at what separates the things in the list, and at the little word before the last one. The sentence you’re fixing has three places and one person’s name. Write it out first, then put in what’s missing, one thing at a time. Read it aloud. If you run out of breath in the wrong spot, something is still missing."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Anne · before the word work","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-guide1","done":true,"script":"Word study! Today you produce the answers yourself, so there’s nowhere to hide, which I find rather thrilling and slightly alarming. Four word parts to type, three of this week’s words to fit into sentences, four feeling words, and four single letters. In every game, read the whole sentence before you type into its gap. A gap on its own will fool you every time. Check each box before you move on."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Anne · about the word parts","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Read the little description after each word before anything else. It tells you exactly where the part points: above, or too much, or under, or below. Then choose. If two parts seem to fit, say the whole word aloud both ways and trust your ear, because you’ve heard these words before, even if you didn’t know it. If you miss one, a line will tell you why. Read it, then try again."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Anne · about today’s words","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Four words in the bank and only three gaps, so one gets left out, poor thing. Read each sentence to the end. Then ask what job the gap is doing. Is it describing a mouth, a feeling, or a temper? That narrows it wonderfully. Try your word in the sentence out loud. If it sounds foolish, it is. Leave the hardest gap for last and the bank will have done half your work."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Anne · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-grammar","done":true,"script":"Every answer is in the bank, and every one names a feeling or an idea, not a thing you could hold. I have a great many. Read the whole sentence and ask what the person is feeling, or what two people have between them. Then find the bank word for it. When you’ve finished, combine the two short sentences in the box. Put the feeling in as a noun. Don’t just glue them with and."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Anne · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-spelling","done":true,"script":"Two decisions each time. First, say the word and find the syllable that gets mumbled. The description tells you which one. Second, decide which letter is doing the mumbling. Your ear can’t tell you that. The sound is the same for all three letters, which I think is very unfair. So picture the word the way you’ve seen it in a book. If you’ve never seen it, guess, and read the line that comes back."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Anne · before you open the book","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-guide2","done":true,"script":"Two more days till the picnic, and I have thought of very little else, which Marilla says is setting my heart on things. This week’s watch is about finding out what you’ll trade to get what you want. I want that picnic more than anything. As you read, keep two lists in your head: what somebody wants, and what they give up for it. Then decide whether the trade was worth it."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Anne · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Careful, now. Next is not the same as because. I get those mixed up when I’m upset. Before you start, get ready to list the order of things as they happen: first, second, third, and nothing else. Then take each pair and say them with because in the middle. If it sounds wrong, it was only next. When you find the real cause, it will be a feeling, not an event. It nearly always is."},{"id":"read","label":"Anne · before you read","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-read","done":true,"script":"Two stops. The first comes partway through, right after somebody makes a confession, and it asks what that tells you about her and the truth. Don’t decide too quickly. The second is for when you’ve finished, and it asks whether getting what you want by telling somebody what they already believe really counts. Keep your book open beside you. The questions want the chain of events in order."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Anne · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-rwm","done":true,"script":"That sentence is about Marilla, and I only saw the outside of it, which was quite bad enough. The author lets you see the inside. Before you write, read it once and put your hand where that feeling would go in your own body. When you write yours, give the feeling a verb that moves, and send it somewhere: a throat, a stomach, a pair of hands. Don’t name the feeling and stop."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Anne · before you write","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well, now you know the worst of me. Marilla thought I’d lost her brooch, and I hadn’t, and she shut me in my room until I confessed. So I confessed to something I never did, with a bridge in it. Then she found the brooch on her shawl, and I went to the picnic after all, and the ice cream was sublime. Now say what you noticed, and show it from the book."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Anne · about the questions","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question asks two things, what I said I did and what Marilla decided I’d done. Keep them apart, because I did keep telling her the truth. The second is about my reasoning, and it made perfect sense at the time. The third wants the chain right to the end, and the last link isn’t the brooch turning up. It’s what Marilla did after. Find it before you answer."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Anne · how it connects","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your spelling answer is saved, so here’s the connection. All week you’ve been dealing with the vowel you can’t hear, the lazy one that sounds the same however it’s spelled. Your ear can’t tell you which letter it is. You have to look. Well, my confession sounded exactly like the truth. Marilla couldn’t hear the difference either. She had to go and find the brooch to know. When you can’t hear it, look."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Anne · about today’s writing","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-9-3-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you map your story: a beginning, three things that go wrong in the middle, and an end. Take my week. I tried on a brooch, and then three things made it worse, each one worse than the last, and it ended with Marilla saying she was wrong and me eating ice cream. Count your middle. One thing is a moment, not a story. Make the third the worst, and make the end change somebody."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"“Next” isn’t the same as “because.” Test it before you say one thing caused another.","wordParts":"over- means “above,” “across,” or “too much”: overlook, overcoat. Review: sub- and under- mean “below”: submarine, underground.","grammar":"Concrete nouns name things you can see, touch, or hear (hat). Abstract nouns name feelings or ideas (imagination, despair). Collective nouns name a group acting as one (class, congregation).","spelling":"In an unstressed syllable the vowel often blurs into a lazy “uh” sound called schwa. It can be spelled a, e, or o (about, garden, lesson), so you have to know which letter the word uses.","dol":[{"raw":"marilla looked in the drawer the closet and the shelf for the brooch","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> looked in the drawer<strong>,</strong> the closet<strong>,</strong> and the shelf for the brooch<strong>.</strong>","why":"A capital M on the name Marilla, commas between the items in the list, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"marilla packed bread cookies and a pie into the basket","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> packed bread<strong>,</strong> cookies<strong>,</strong> and a pie into the basket<strong>.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on the name Marilla, commas between the three items in the list, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 14, watch Anne make a confession she knows is not true. Being shut in her room came first, but was that really the cause? Test it with the word because . What does Anne want so badly that she will lie to get it — and is her wanting it the real cause of what she does?","Anne invents a false confession to escape her room. What does this tell you about Anne’s relationship to truth, and to what she wants?","Anne tells Marilla the story Marilla already believes, hoping it will open the door. Would getting what you want that way really count as getting it?"],"chapterQs":["Anne goes into Marilla’s room and tries on the amethyst brooch. What does Anne say she did with the brooch? What does Marilla think happened?","Anne invents a long, dramatic false confession just to get to the picnic. Why does this seem reasonable to Anne in the moment? What does this show about how she thinks?","Marilla doesn’t let Anne go to the picnic even after the false confession. What causes the resolution at the end of the chapter? Track the chain: brooch lost → false confession → picnic refused → ___?"],"focus":"Today you map the whole story: a beginning, a middle where three things make the problem worse, and an end. You aren’t writing scenes yet. You’re drawing the shape so that when you write, you know where you’re going.","nowTry":"Fill in YOUR map. Beginning (kid + setting + want). Middle (THREE things that get in the way, worst last). End (how it resolves, what changes).","rwm":"“Marilla felt hot anger surge up into her heart again.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch14.png"},"words":[{"w":"solemn","d":"Serious; deeply earnest; not joking.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"resolute","d":"Firm; determined; not easily changed.","e":"💪"},{"w":"vindictive","d":"Wanting to get back at someone; full of revenge.","e":"⚡"}]},{"w":9,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 9.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 15)","file":"lesson-9-4-anne-ch15.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-9-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 9.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 15). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Gilbert’s welcome","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello. Gilbert Blythe here, and it’s my first go at guiding, so go easy on me. Your question is about a moment in the schoolroom where somebody does something sudden, and what was inside her that made her do it. I’ll be honest, I don’t know what’s coming any better than you do. In word study you’re checking a draft somebody else wrote, so you get to be the teacher for once. Enjoy that."},{"id":"dol","label":"Gilbert · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-dol","done":false,"script":"Somebody’s shouting in the model, and the marks show it. Look at what sits inside the closing quotation mark and what kind of stop it is. The sentence you’re fixing has two people doing two different things, so it wants to be two sentences. Say it aloud with some feeling and you’ll hear where the shout ends and the telling begins. Names get capital letters. Write it out first, then check."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Gilbert · before the word work","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-guide1","done":true,"script":"Word study, last day of the week. You match the word parts one more time, pick one thing to do with the words, and then mark two drafts the way a teacher would. Here’s the thing about marking. Tapping a good one costs you, so you can’t just tap everything and hope. Read each one twice and decide. I’ve been on the wrong end of a teacher’s marking. It’s more fun on this side."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Gilbert · about the word parts","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"You’ve matched these before, so make it harder on yourself. Cover the meanings with your hand and say what each part means before you look. Then match. The three real front parts all point somewhere: above, under, below. The fourth one isn’t a front part at all. If you’re stuck between two, say the example words in brackets out loud and listen for where the part sits."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Gilbert · about today’s words","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Five choices and you only do one, so don’t pick the easy one. Pick the one you’d actually enjoy. If you like arguing, teach a word to a younger kid; you’ll find out fast whether you really know it. If you like the book, hunt the chapter for a word close to one of ours. Whatever you choose, use the word the way the book does. Read your answer back once before you move on."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Gilbert · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-grammar","done":true,"script":"Somebody wrote a draft about the chapter and labeled the naming words. Some labels are wrong. Don’t trust them just because they sound sure. For each one, ask: could I drop this on my foot? If yes, it’s a thing, and check the label says so. If it’s a feeling, an idea, or a word for a bunch of people, it’s something else. Tap only the wrong ones. A right one tapped costs you."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Gilbert · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six words, some spelled wrong, and your ear won’t catch a single one. Say them and they all sound fine. That’s the trick of the lazy vowel. So don’t listen. Look. Picture each word the way you’ve seen it printed, and compare it letter by letter with what’s on the screen. Tap only the ones that don’t match your picture. Then fix one in the box and say why your ear failed."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Gilbert · before you open the book","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-guide2","done":true,"script":"First day back, and I mean to make an impression. This week’s watch is friendship, and I’ll tell you what I know about it: you find out who your friends are after you’ve made a fool of yourself. As you read, watch who stands by whom when things go wrong, and what it costs them. And watch me. I’m not saying I’ll behave. I’m saying watch."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Gilbert · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Today you’re weighing causes, not just finding one. Here’s how. Every time something pushes her toward what she does, make a mark in your head. Big pushes and small ones. When you get to the sudden moment, stop and line them up. Then take away the biggest and ask whether she’d still have done it. If yes, it wasn’t the biggest. Then zoom out over the whole week, the way the box asks."},{"id":"read","label":"Gilbert · before you read","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-read","done":false,"script":"One stop, at the end. Read straight through, and pay attention to the schoolroom: who sits where, who’s watching whom. It matters. When you reach the stop, don’t rush it. It asks what somebody did wrong and what it hit. Answer the second part carefully. What a thing hits depends on the person, not the thing. And keep your book handy. The questions will send you back into it."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Gilbert · before you write","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-guide3","done":true,"script":"Right. So I called her Carrots, holding up her braid, and she cracked her slate over my head. I told Mr. Phillips it was my fault, which it was. I said sorry after, honest, and she walked past like I wasn’t there. Then I gave her a candy heart and she ground it underfoot. I don’t know why it landed so hard. You’ve read more of her than I have. Tell me."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Gilbert · about the questions","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is why it hit her so hard, and I’m the wrong person to ask. Look back at what somebody else called her earlier in the book, and what she did then. The second is about a punishment. Think what sitting next to me meant after what I’d done. Not to me. To her. The third goes back over the whole week. Pick one chain, and make each link touch the next."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Gilbert · how it connects","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your vocabulary answer is saved. One of this week’s words was vindictive, and Mr. Phillips used it on her in front of the whole school. Well, she wouldn’t take my apology, and she ground my candy heart to powder, so maybe he had a point. Or maybe there’s a difference between wanting revenge and refusing to be laughed at. Look at your answer and decide which word fits her better. Say why."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Gilbert · about today’s writing","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-9-4-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you write five scenes, one line each. Look at what the author did with me. The whole slate business is one scene, one room, told all the way through, and it’s the only thing anybody will remember about this chapter. So when you write your five lines, don’t cram. Each scene gets one place and one thing that happens. And make the worst moment a choice somebody makes, not just bad luck."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Big choices have more than one cause. List them, then decide which mattered most.","wordParts":"over- means “above,” “across,” or “too much”: overlook, overcoat. Review: sub- and under- mean “below”: submarine, underground.","grammar":"Concrete nouns name things you can see, touch, or hear (hat). Abstract nouns name feelings or ideas (imagination, despair). Collective nouns name a group acting as one (class, congregation).","spelling":"In an unstressed syllable the vowel often blurs into a lazy “uh” sound called schwa. It can be spelled a, e, or o (about, garden, lesson), so you have to know which letter the word uses.","dol":[{"raw":"carrots gilbert called out anne grabbed her slate","fixed":"<strong>“Carrots!”</strong> <strong>Gilbert</strong> called out<strong>.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> grabbed her slate<strong>.</strong>","why":"Quotation marks around the shouted word, a capital to start it, an exclamation point inside the closing quotation mark, a period to end the first sentence, a capital on the name Anne, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"ouch gilbert yelled the slate lay in two pieces","fixed":"<strong>“Ouch!”</strong> <strong>Gilbert</strong> yelled<strong>.</strong> <strong>The</strong> slate lay in two pieces<strong>.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: quotation marks around the shout with an exclamation point inside them, a period to end the first sentence, a capital T to start the next, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Wilcox says people share your joy but leave you alone in sadness. After the slate incident, Anne refuses to go back to school—and the hardest part is losing Diana’s company every day. Is Wilcox right? Does Anne’s experience prove or disprove the poem?","As you read Ch. 15, watch what Anne does when Gilbert teases her, and then watch her refuse to go back to school at all. List everything pushing her: her temper, her pride, what she has been called before, what she cannot stand to hear. Which one of those mattered most? Take it away in your head — would she still break the slate?","Anne breaks her slate on Gilbert’s head and refuses to ever go back to school. What did Gilbert do wrong, and what wound of Anne’s did he hit?"],"chapterQs":["Gilbert pulls Anne’s hair and calls her “Carrots.” Anne hits him with her slate. What about Anne’s past makes her react SO strongly to being called carrots?","Mr. Phillips punishes Anne by making her sit with Gilbert as a punishment. Why is this punishment so much worse to Anne than standing on the platform was?","Looking back across Ch. 11-15: name ONE specific cause-and-effect chain you noticed. Pick a moment where one of Anne’s choices led directly to a bigger problem or a bigger good thing."],"focus":"A story is written in scenes. Your story will have five: an opening, the first wall, the worst moment, what your kid does about it, and an ending. Today you name all five in one line each. Next week you start writing them.","nowTry":"Turn your map from yesterday into five scenes, one line each, in the box below (Beginning → Scene 1, the three middle things → Scenes 2, 3, 4, End → Scene 5). Then copy the list into My Story above so it’s there when you start drafting next week.","plate":"plates/anne-ch15.png"},"words":[{"w":"solemn","d":"Serious; deeply earnest; not joking.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"resolute","d":"Firm; determined; not easily changed.","e":"💪"},{"w":"vindictive","d":"Wanting to get back at someone; full of revenge.","e":"⚡"}]},{"w":9,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 9 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-9-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-9-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"solemn","d":"Serious; deeply earnest; not joking.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"resolute","d":"Firm; determined; not easily changed.","e":"💪"},{"w":"vindictive","d":"Wanting to get back at someone; full of revenge.","e":"⚡"}]},{"w":10,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 10.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 16)","file":"lesson-10-1-anne-ch16.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-10-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 10.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 16). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare character perspectives. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Diana’s welcome","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-welcome","done":true,"script":"It’s Diana again. The question at the top is about an honest mistake turning into a real problem. I don’t know anything about that. All I know is I’m going to tea, and I’m awfully excited. So as you go, keep two lists in your head: what somebody meant to do, and what actually happened. Then there are doing words to sort, and a c-and-g rule that isn’t as simple as it looks."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Diana · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Mother and I can watch the same thing and come away with two different stories. It used to make me cross. Now I think it’s just how people are. So when the box says pick one event, pick a small one, and put each person beside it. Then find the one thing somebody didn’t know. That’s usually the whole gap, right there. It was for Mother and me, anyway."},{"id":"dol","label":"Diana · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-dol","done":false,"script":"That’s my name in the bottom sentence without its capital letter, and Anne’s too. I mind that. The top one shows you how a sentence ends and starts again. The bottom one has two whole thoughts squashed together with nothing between them. Read it out loud and listen for the place your voice drops. That’s where the first thought wants a period and the next wants a capital letter."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Diana · before the word work","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four boxes. Word parts to type, then words to guess and match, then two sorts. I like sorts, because you can tell when you’re finished. Do them in order, and read every bit of a box before you tap anything, because two of them have a third column, and the third column is the tricky one. Anne would say the tricky one is the interesting one. Anne isn’t doing your sorting."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Diana · about the word parts","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-morphology","done":true,"script":"Four little chunks for one job. I’d have said that was silly, four chunks that all mean not. But look at the letter right after each blank. That letter picks the chunk. Cover the blank, say what’s left, and try the chunk that sounds like it belongs on the front of it. The wrong one sounds wrong out loud. Anne says everything sounds better out loud, and for once she’s right."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Diana · about today’s words","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Three steps, then the flip cards, then a match. Here’s the thing about the flip cards. Guess first, out loud, before you tap. If you tap first there’s nothing to guess, and then the match is just remembering. In the match, do the two you’re surest of first. Then there are only two left and you can try them both ways and listen for which pair sounds right."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Diana · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"Sorting doing words. Gertie Pye squeaks her pencil through every grammar lesson and it makes my blood run cold, so I’d rather do it here. Say each chip with its little word in front, the she or the they. Listen to the end of the doing word. Some change, some don’t, and some have a helper word stuck on the front. That’s your three groups. Name them in your own words."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Diana · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-spelling","done":true,"script":"Two letters, and each one has two sounds. Say every word first, before you look at the columns, and listen for a hard sound or a soft one. Then look at the letter right after the c or the g. That letter decides it. Two of the words have both letters in them, one hard and one soft, so check those twice. Ruby Gillis would just guess. Don’t be Ruby."},{"id":"copia","label":"Diana · about saying it three ways","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-copia","done":true,"script":"One sentence, said three ways. Anne can say a thing six ways before she gets to the end of the lane, and I used to think that was showing off. It isn’t. It’s trying them on. Read the sentence, find the doing word, and change just that first. Then start it somewhere else. Then add where or how. Keep the one that sounds most like you."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Diana · before you open the book","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-guide2","done":true,"script":"I’m dressed and I’m nervous, which is a silly thing to be about tea. The box on courage says it’s doing the hard thing while you’re still frightened. I’d have said courage was not being frightened. So while you read, watch for somebody who’s scared and goes anyway. And watch what fear looks like from the outside. Mostly it looks like standing very still."},{"id":"read","label":"Diana · before you read","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-read","done":true,"script":"There are two stops, and I’ve read them, and I don’t like the sound of either one. One of them has my tea in it. The first stop asks whether somebody did anything wrong. Decide that before you read one more word, and then see if the rest of the chapter changes your mind. The second one asks the difference between a mistake and a wrong thing. Take your time on it."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Diana · before you write","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well. Now you know. I was awfully sick, I had a headache all the next day, and Mother smelled my breath and that was that. She says Anne did it on purpose. I don’t believe it for one minute. Anne didn’t have any herself, she said she didn’t want any after the apples. But nobody’s asking me. They’re asking you, so show it from the book, not from how you feel."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Diana · about the questions","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is about what I drank, and why Anne didn’t know. Look at what Anne said when she set it down in front of me. The second is about Mother, and she’s my mother, so I’m not the one to ask. Put what she believes happened next to what happened. The third is about Marilla, and I wasn’t there. The hint gives you two things she did. Use both."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Diana · about the word connection","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your word-parts answer is saved. You made a word that means not something. Well, the whole afternoon was a not. Anne did not mean it. Mother does not believe her. I wasn’t even home to say goodbye. Look back at the word you chose and ask which person in the chapter it fits best. If it fits somebody, put them in your sentence. If it doesn’t, pick a different word."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Diana · about today’s writing","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-10-1-workshop","done":true,"script":"Sam wrote about the weather first, and then crossed it out. Anne’s chapter starts with October, a whole page of it, and I love October, but the story starts when Marilla says I can come to tea. So write your two first sentences, two different kinds, and read each one out loud. The one that makes you want to know what happens next is the one you star."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Two people at the same event can see it differently. It depends on what each one was paying attention to.","wordParts":"il-, ir-, im-, and in- all mean “not”: il- before l (illegal), ir- before r (irregular), im- before b, m, or p (impossible), in- before other letters (incorrect).","grammar":"Present-tense verbs must agree with their subject: I, you, we, they walk; he, she, it walks. Use is or are + -ing for something happening right now (she is walking).","spelling":"c and g are usually soft (/s/ and /j/) before e, i, or y (cellar, gentle, energy) and hard before a, o, or u (cordial, golden). A few words break the rule (get, girl).","dol":[{"raw":"anne carries the bottle to the sitting room she pours diana a drink","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> carries the bottle to the sitting <strong>room.</strong> <strong>She</strong> pours <strong>Diana</strong> a <strong>drink.</strong>","why":"Capital letters on the names Anne and Diana, a period to close the first thought, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"diana knocked at the front door anne opened it in her second best","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> knocked at the front <strong>door.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> opened it in her second <strong>best.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: capitals on the names Diana and Anne, a period where the first thought ends, a capital A to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 16, compare how Anne sees the tea and how the grown-ups see it. Anne serves currant wine believing it is raspberry cordial. What does that mistake look like from Anne’s side, and what does it look like to someone who was not in the kitchen with her? Whose view does the story want you to sit with?","Anne accidentally serves currant wine instead of raspberry cordial. Did Anne do anything wrong? If yes, what? If no, why is she being punished?","The tea party ends in trouble, and one grown-up decides Anne is to blame without believing her side. What is the difference between making a mistake and doing something wrong?"],"chapterQs":["Anne thinks she is giving Diana raspberry cordial. What does she actually give her, and why didn’t Anne notice the difference?","Mrs. Barry decides Anne is a “bad, wicked little girl” and bans Diana from playing with her. Is Mrs. Barry being fair? Use details from the chapter to explain.","Marilla doesn’t let Anne off the hook completely, but she also doesn’t blame her for the mistake. How does Marilla react to the situation? What does this show about how she has come to see Anne?"],"focus":"This week you start writing the story for real. Today is only the first sentence. Its one job is to make a reader want the second sentence. There are six kinds of first sentence that do that. You will try two and pick one.","nowTry":"Write TWO different first sentences for your story, using two different leads from the list. Label each with its lead type. Put a star by the one you’ll use tomorrow.","plate":"plates/anne-ch16.png"},"words":[{"w":"cordial","d":"Warmly friendly. (Also: a sweet drink offered to friends.)","e":"🍷"},{"w":"relent","d":"To soften; to become less harsh; to change one’s mind.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"inexorable","d":"Refusing to give in or change; unable to be moved by pleading.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":10,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 10.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 17 & 18)","file":"lesson-10-2-anne-ch17-18.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-10-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 10.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 17 & 18). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare character perspectives. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Matthew’s welcome","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"Well now, it’s Matthew, and I’m your guide for these two chapters. The question up there says the hard part of Anne’s past turns into a gift. I dunno how yet. I can believe it, though. Anne never had much, and she made the most of every bit. So as you go, keep an eye on who freezes and who moves. Then there’s doing words to find, and some c’s and g’s that don’t behave."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Matthew · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-wgrd","done":true,"script":"Marilla and I look at Anne and see two different girls. Marilla sees the mistakes. I see, well, I dunno what I see. The girl. And it’s not that one of us is wrong. It’s what each of us has lived through. So do what the box says. One sentence for each person, then ask what they’ve been through that makes them see it that way. That’s the whole gap, most times."},{"id":"dol","label":"Matthew · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-dol","done":false,"script":"Marilla does the grammar in this house. But I can hear a sentence that runs on too long, the way I can hear a wagon that needs grease. The bottom one is two thoughts with no stop between them. Read it out loud and stop where you’d take a breath. That’s your period. Then the next word stands up straight with a capital letter. Anne’s name wants one too."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Matthew · before the word work","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"Well now. Four boxes, and I’m no scholar, so I’ll say what I’d do. Two are matching, tap one thing, then tap its partner. Two are tapping words in a row, and in those you’re hunting, so don’t tap the first thing that looks likely. Read the whole row first. The spelling one is looking for the words that break the rule, not the ones that keep it. Easy to get that backwards."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Matthew · about the word parts","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-morphology","done":true,"script":"Four ways to say not. I dunno why a word needs four, one would do me. But there’s a rule about which one goes where, and the rule is in the little words in brackets. Say those out loud, and look at the first letter of each one after the chunk comes off. That letter is what you’re matching, not the meaning. The meanings are all the same. That’s the trick of it."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Matthew · about today’s words","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"You met these four yesterday. Well now, I forget words the day after I learn them, so here’s what I do. Say the word, then say the meaning, and listen to whether they sit together. If it sounds like two strangers, it probably is. Do the pair you’re surest of first, and then the next, and the last two nearly match themselves. And if you get one wrong, read the little line that comes up."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Matthew · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"There’s more than one doing word in that sentence, and I’d have read it and just nodded. So go slow. Ask of each word, is somebody doing this right now? A name isn’t. A little word like and isn’t. Then check the end of each one you tapped against who’s doing it, one person or more than one. I dunno the rule for that. Anne does. You will, by the end of the week."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Matthew · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Most of those words do what the rule says. One or two don’t, and those are the ones you tap. So say each word, listen for the hard sound or the soft one, then look at the letter after the c or the g and ask if the rule would have guessed right. When it wouldn’t, that’s your word. Leave the good ones be. Tapping a good one costs you."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Matthew · before you open the book","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-guide2","done":true,"script":"The box says courage is doing the hard thing while you’re still afraid. Well now, I’m not a brave man. Going up those stairs to talk Anne into apologizing, when Marilla had told me not to interfere, that was about my limit. But I went. So watch for that. Not somebody who isn’t scared. Somebody who is, and does the thing anyway. And watch what the other people do while she’s doing it."},{"id":"read","label":"Matthew · before you read","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-read","done":true,"script":"One stop, at the end of the first chapter, and it asks whether a person can enjoy being sad. Well now, Anne can enjoy pretty near anything. When you get there, ask yourself if she’s only sad, or something else along with it. Then read the second chapter straight through, no stopping, and keep count of who moves and who stands still. That’s what the reading move is about."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Matthew · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-rwm","done":true,"script":"Well now, that’s Anne all over. Sorry and thrilled in the one breath. The sentence works because the although part holds the feeling you’d expect, and the main part holds the one you wouldn’t. Cover the first half with your finger and it’s just a girl enjoying herself. Put it back and she’s a person. When you write yours, put the ordinary feeling first, and save the surprising one for the end."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Matthew · before you write","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-guide3","done":true,"script":"I went to Carmody for the doctor and there wasn’t one, so I went clear to Spencervale, and it was the small hours when we got back. And it was over. Anne had done it. I walked her home and she talked the whole way, and I let her. Now it’s your turn to say what you noticed, and I dunno that you need to say much. Just say it with the book open."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Matthew · about the questions","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is about that farewell, and I wasn’t at the spring, but I heard about it at supper. Look at the words Anne chose and ask why a person picks those. The second is where her skill came from. She tells Diana, not the doctor. Find that. The third is about Mrs. Barry. I was doing chores when she came. Marilla heard it. Quote the moment, not the feeling."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Matthew · about the word connection","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your grammar answer is saved. When I tell about that night it all comes out in the past, went and drove. The page wanted you to find the doing words that happen now, and I dunno why, but it makes a story feel closer. Take the verb you tapped, ask who’s doing it, and ask whether the ending fits that person. Then look at your own writing and try one sentence in the now."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Matthew · about today’s writing","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-10-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"Sam’s boy wants to make the team, and part of him wants to skip it. Well now, I wanted to keep Anne, and couldn’t say so out loud. That’s a want and a wall in the one person. Write your first scene so we know where your kid is and what they want. Then one line that hints at the thing in the way. One line. Don’t explain it. Let it show."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"When views split, ask why. What a person has lived through shapes what they see.","wordParts":"il-, ir-, im-, and in- all mean “not”: il- before l (illegal), ir- before r (irregular), im- before b, m, or p (impossible), in- before other letters (incorrect).","grammar":"Present-tense verbs must agree with their subject: I, you, we, they walk; he, she, it walks. Use is or are + -ing for something happening right now (she is walking).","spelling":"c and g are usually soft (/s/ and /j/) before e, i, or y (cellar, gentle, energy) and hard before a, o, or u (cordial, golden). A few words break the rule (get, girl).","dol":[{"raw":"anne knows what to do for croup she races through the snow","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> knows what to do for <strong>croup.</strong> <strong>She</strong> races through the <strong>snow.</strong>","why":"A capital letter on the name Anne, a period to close the first thought, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"matthew harnesses the sorrel mare he drives off for the doctor","fixed":"<strong>Matthew</strong> harnesses the sorrel <strong>mare.</strong> <strong>He</strong> drives off for the <strong>doctor.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on the name Matthew, a period where the first thought ends, a capital H to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 17 and 18, compare how Anne sees the night at the Barry house with how Diana and Young Mary Joe see it. All three are looking at the same sick child. Why can Anne see a job to do while the others see only fear? What in Anne’s past put her on a different side of that gap?","Kipling describes keeping calm when everyone else panics. Anne does exactly this when Diana’s baby sister is sick. Find a moment in the chapter where Anne “keeps her head” while others lose theirs.","Anne and Diana have a tearful “eternal farewell.” Is it possible to enjoy sadness? What does this scene tell you about Anne’s feelings?"],"chapterQs":["Anne and Diana have an “eternal farewell” in Ch. 17. Why does Anne enjoy this farewell almost as much as she suffers from it? What does this show about her?","Young Mary Joe is “helpless and bewildered” while Anne “went to work with skill and promptness.” What gives Anne the skill she needs in this moment? Use a detail from the chapter.","Mrs. Barry comes to apologize to Anne. What changes in Mrs. Barry, and what changes in Anne’s relationship with Diana? Quote one specific moment from the chapter."],"focus":"Today Scene 1 goes into My Story. Four to six sentences: your lead from yesterday, then where your kid is, then what they want. Don’t explain the problem yet. Just make us want the same thing your kid wants.","nowTry":"After Scene 1 is in My Story, write here: which lead did you use, and what does your kid want? One or two sentences.","rwm":"“Anne, although sincerely sorry for Minnie May, was far from being insensible to the romance of the situation and to the sweetness of once more sharing that romance with a kindred spirit.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch17-18.png"},"words":[{"w":"cordial","d":"Warmly friendly. (Also: a sweet drink offered to friends.)","e":"🍷"},{"w":"relent","d":"To soften; to become less harsh; to change one’s mind.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"inexorable","d":"Refusing to give in or change; unable to be moved by pleading.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":10,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 10.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 19)","file":"lesson-10-3-anne-ch19.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-10-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 10.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 19). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Miss Barry’s welcome","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-welcome","done":true,"script":"Josephine Barry. I am told I am to be your guide, and I have never guided anything but a knitting needle. I have read the question. Two girls, it says, jump into a bed with an old woman asleep in it. I am the old woman. I have not read what comes next and am not looking forward to it. You are to watch what the girl does afterward. So am I."},{"id":"dol","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-dol","done":true,"script":"The sentence you are to mend has me in it, spelled without a single capital letter. I never. The top sentence shows you how it ought to go: a name with its capital, a full stop at the end. The bottom one is two thoughts run together. Find where the first one ends and put a period there. Then the capitals. Every name gets one, and mine has two parts, so it gets two."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Miss Barry · before the word work","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four boxes of blanks. When I was young we called this a lesson and got on with it. The first wants a word part typed in, the second a whole word from a list, the third the right shape of a verb, the fourth a single letter. Read the whole line before you type anything. People type the first thing they think of and then argue with the box. The box is not listening."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Miss Barry · about the word parts","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-morphology","done":true,"script":"Four little chunks that all mean not, and only one will fit each word. I find that fussy. But there is a rule, and the rule is the letter that starts what’s left. Look at that letter first. Then say the whole word with your chunk on the front, out loud, and if it sounds like a mouthful you have the wrong one. Improper sounds right. Inproper does not. That is the whole method."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s words","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Three sentences, four words in the list, one word left over. One of the sentences is about me, and I shall not tell you which word it wants. I dare say I earned it. Read the whole sentence first, then try each word from the list in the blank, out loud. Three will sound foolish and one will sound like what the sentence meant all along. The spare word is a spare."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-grammar","done":true,"script":"Subjects and verbs must agree. In my experience nothing agrees with anything unless it is made to. So make it. Look at who is doing the thing, one person or more than one, and let that decide the ending. Then say the whole sentence out loud. A wrong ending grates on anyone who has listened to English for more than a year, and you have. Let it grate."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-spelling","done":true,"script":"One letter each, from a list of three. This is nonsense, and I intend to do it perfectly. Say the word first, so you hear the soft sound. Then try each of the three letters after the c or the g. Two will give you the wrong sound, or no word at all. One gives you the word you just said. Take that one. Then the next word, from the beginning."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Miss Barry · before you open the book","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-guide2","done":true,"script":"The box says courage is doing the hard thing while you are still afraid. At my time of life you learn that most people do the easy thing and call it sense. So watch for the other kind. Not somebody who is not frightened. Somebody who is, and walks up to the thing anyway. And watch what fright does to a person’s voice. It makes it very small. You will see what I mean."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The page appears to think something is going to happen to me. I know only that I have traveled all day and gone to bed. When you reach the part where two people fall out, do what the box says. Write down what each one actually had in front of them, then find the piece one of them could not know. Judge nobody until you have done that. I did, and I was wrong."},{"id":"read","label":"Miss Barry · before you read","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops, and both are about somebody walking into my room. Nobody walks into my room. At the first stop, decide what walking in alone shows, before you read what I say to her. At the second, the question gives you two choices, what she told me or the way she told it. Pick one and be ready to defend it. I have my own opinion. I am not going to give it to you."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Miss Barry · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-rwm","done":true,"script":"I am the lion, it seems. I was knitting. The sentence has two halves and a hinge, and the halves are two people. The grand half is how it felt to that child, marching up to a door. The plain half is what I heard from my chair: a very faint knock. When you write yours, say the enormous thing first, then hinge it, then say what a person in the hall would have seen."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Miss Barry · before you write","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-guide3","done":true,"script":"She stood in my doorway, white as paper, and said she had come to confess. I meant to be cross for a month. I lasted about four minutes. I have unpacked my valise and told them I am staying, simply to get better acquainted with that Anne-girl. Now you are to say what you noticed. Say what you saw her do, not what you felt. Evidence, hey? I would ask it of a witness."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Miss Barry · about the questions","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question mentions a brooch. I know nothing of any brooch. Look back to the chapter where it happened and put that girl beside the one at my door. The second is about what she asked me to do, and I was there. Look at what I said just after she asked it. The third is about Diana. Diana stayed in the kitchen. I heard her through the door, once. Ask why she stayed."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Miss Barry · about the word connection","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your spelling answer is saved. You are to find a word in the chapter where a c or a g goes soft, and I shall not find it for you. But your ear is faster than your eye. Read a line of the chapter aloud, any line, and listen for the soft sound. When you hear it, look at the letter after it. If it is one of your three, you have your word."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s writing","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-10-3-workshop","done":true,"script":"What that child thought as she stood at my door I can only guess. What she said, I heard. Your second scene wants the thing nobody hears, one line of thinking inside your kid’s head. Sam’s boy thinks three words, three times over. When I was young we thought in longer sentences. Yours needn’t be long. It need only be true. Then the wall, whatever it is, comes into view."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"People judge with what they know. Look for the piece of the story someone is missing.","wordParts":"il-, ir-, im-, and in- all mean “not”: il- before l (illegal), ir- before r (irregular), im- before b, m, or p (impossible), in- before other letters (incorrect).","grammar":"Present-tense verbs must agree with their subject: I, you, we, they walk; he, she, it walks. Use is or are + -ing for something happening right now (she is walking).","spelling":"c and g are usually soft (/s/ and /j/) before e, i, or y (cellar, gentle, energy) and hard before a, o, or u (cordial, golden). A few words break the rule (get, girl).","dol":[{"raw":"anne enters the sitting room aunt josephine watches her closely","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> enters the sitting <strong>room.</strong> <strong>Aunt</strong> <strong>Josephine</strong> watches her <strong>closely.</strong>","why":"A capital letter on the name Anne, a period to close the first thought, capital letters on both parts of Aunt Josephine, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"diana waits in the kitchen miss barry laughs out loud","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> waits in the <strong>kitchen.</strong> <strong>Miss</strong> <strong>Barry</strong> laughs out <strong>loud.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on the name Diana, a period where the first thought ends, capital letters on both parts of Miss Barry, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 19, put Anne’s side of the catastrophe next to Aunt Josephine’s side. Anne knows what she meant; Aunt Josephine only knows what happened to her. What can Aunt Josephine not possibly know until Anne walks in and tells her? What changes once she does?","Anne walks into Aunt Josephine’s room to confess. What does Anne’s decision to walk in alone show about how she has changed since she came to Green Gables?","Aunt Josephine hears Anne out before she decides anything. What do you think changed her mind — what Anne told her, or the way Anne told it?"],"chapterQs":["Both Anne and Diana jumped into the bed. Why does Anne decide to confess that it was HER idea? What does this show about how she has changed since the brooch confession in Ch. 14?","Anne tells Miss Barry, “Have you any imagination, Miss Barry? If you have, just put yourself in our place.” How does Anne’s imagination, which usually causes problems, actually save the day this time?","Compare Diana’s perspective on the situation with Anne’s. Both girls jumped, both got in trouble. But how do they respond differently? What does this contrast reveal?"],"focus":"Scene 2 is the first thing that gets in the way. Your kid tries, or almost tries, and something makes it harder. Put one line of thinking inside their head — the words they’d never say out loud.","nowTry":"Copy the one line of inner thought you gave your kid in Scene 2. Then one sentence: what does that line tell a reader that “he was nervous” wouldn’t?","rwm":"“Anne bearded the lion in its den — that is to say, walked resolutely up to the sitting-room door and knocked faintly.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch19.png"},"words":[{"w":"cordial","d":"Warmly friendly. (Also: a sweet drink offered to friends.)","e":"🍷"},{"w":"relent","d":"To soften; to become less harsh; to change one’s mind.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"inexorable","d":"Refusing to give in or change; unable to be moved by pleading.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":10,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 10.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 20)","file":"lesson-10-4-anne-ch20.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-10-4","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 10.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 20). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-welcome","done":false,"script":"It’s Marilla. Imagination as a trap, the question says. Well. I could have told you that a year ago, and I believe I did. So watch for the moment a made-up thing stops being fun. Then there’s the last of the week’s word work, and it’s the checking kind, where some of what you see is already right. Don’t go fixing what isn’t broken. I’ve had enough of that from Anne."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-dol","done":false,"script":"That’s me in the top sentence, and unfeelingly is the author’s word, not mine. I was feeling plenty. Look at how the talking is held in: the marks around it, the comma tucked inside before the said. The bottom one is me too, and it’s lost all of that. Put the marks where my words start and stop, the comma inside the last one, and a capital letter on my name. Then a period."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four boxes and then you’re done with words for the week. I never had much use for busywork, so I’ll tell you how to be quick about it. Two are matching, tap a thing, tap its partner. One is a choice: pick one, do it, don’t dither. Two are checking, and in those some of what you see is already right. Tapping a right one costs you. Read every word before you touch it."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Four chunks that all mean not, and each one is particular about where it will go. Like Rachel Lynde. Each has its own place, and the place is decided by the letter that comes right after it. So read the little words in the brackets, out loud, and look at what letter starts each once the chunk is off. Match by that letter. The meaning is no use to you here."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Five choices and you do one. Pick it before you’ve read them a third time. Choose the one you can finish with the book open beside you, and finish it. Half of two is nothing. If you take the one about your own story character, mind the words fit the person, not just the sentence. If you take the teaching one, teach it plain. A child won’t sit still for a lecture. Neither would I."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"One of those sentences has me watching from a window, and I’ll not say whether I do. That isn’t what you’re checking. Some sentences are right and some aren’t. Tap only the wrong ones. Find the person doing the thing first. One, or more than one? Then look at the end of the doing word and see if it agrees. Say it aloud if you’re not sure. Wrong ones jar. Leave the right ones be."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Some of those words are spelled right and some aren’t, and a wrong one can sound perfectly right, which is what makes it sneaky. Say each word. Then look at the letter making the sound, and the letter after it, and ask whether that’s how the rule spells it. Sound right, letter wrong: tap it. Right both ways: leave it alone. I’d like to catch you tapping a good one."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-guide2","done":true,"script":"The courage box is about the night Anne saved the Barry baby. Matthew told me. I never saw a case of croup in my life, and I’ll say plainly she knew what to do and I wouldn’t have. So that’s one kind of courage, being afraid of a real thing and going anyway. There’s another kind this week. Being afraid of a thing you made up yourself. Watch for which one this chapter is."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The story keeps you inside Anne’s head. It never once puts you in mine. I only get the outside, the dishes and the patchwork. So do what the box says and tell it from my side for once. Then look across the whole week, the tea, the baby, the bed, the wood, and ask whose side you were kept on every single time. That tells you what kind of book this is."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-read","done":true,"script":"One stop, at the end, and it asks how an imagination can be a gift and a problem at once. Well, I could answer that, but I’m not the one reading. Read the chapter through, and when you reach the part after dark, slow down. Ask what she’s afraid of, and who put it there. Then decide what she’s learning. Mind, learning and being told are two different things."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-guide3","done":true,"script":"I sent her through that spruce grove for a lesson and a warning, and I said I’d risk it, and I did. She came back over the bridge with her teeth chattering. Whether I was right you can decide. I’ve decided already. I usually have. Now say what you noticed, and show it from the chapter. Not from what you’d have done. Nobody asked what you’d have done. They asked what happened."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-chapter","done":true,"script":"The first question is about what turns a game frightening. The hint tells you when they made it up; look at when they stopped enjoying it. The second is about me. I’m the one on trial, so argue it with the book, not with my feelings. Use what I said and did. The third is the whole week. Look back to the tea, forward to the bridge, and put those two Annes side by side."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · about the word connection","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your vocabulary answer is saved. I’ll tell you one thing about this week’s words. The author uses one of them for me, on the bridge, and it isn’t flattering. Well. It isn’t wrong, either. Look at your answer and ask whether the word you chose fits the person you put it on the way that one fits me. A word that fits is a word you’ve learned. A word that nearly fits is a guess."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-10-4-workshop","done":true,"script":"Her gift is her imagination and her problem is her imagination, and I’ve been saying so since she came. Sam’s boy is the same: caring too much is what makes him want to quit. So name your kid’s gift as a thing people like about them. Then find the one moment it makes the wall taller. Read your two scenes out loud first, and count your words. Fix anything you stumbled on. Then write."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Notice whose side the story keeps you on, then step out and look from somewhere else.","wordParts":"il-, ir-, im-, and in- all mean “not”: il- before l (illegal), ir- before r (irregular), im- before b, m, or p (impossible), in- before other letters (incorrect).","grammar":"Present-tense verbs must agree with their subject: I, you, we, they walk; he, she, it walks. Use is or are + -ing for something happening right now (she is walking).","spelling":"c and g are usually soft (/s/ and /j/) before e, i, or y (cellar, gentle, energy) and hard before a, o, or u (cordial, golden). A few words break the rule (get, girl).","dol":[{"raw":"you know i always mean what i say said marilla","fixed":"<strong>&ldquo;You</strong> know <strong>I</strong> always mean what <strong>I</strong> <strong>say,&rdquo;</strong> said <strong>Marilla.</strong>","why":"An open quote and a capital letter to start what Marilla says, a capital I every time, a comma inside the closing quote before <em>said</em>, a capital on the name, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"ill cure you of imagining ghosts into places said marilla","fixed":"<strong>&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll</strong> cure you of imagining ghosts into <strong>places,&rdquo;</strong> said <strong>Marilla.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: an open quote and a capital I (with its apostrophe), a comma inside the closing quote before <em>said</em>, a capital on the name Marilla, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Henley wrote this poem from a hospital bed after losing his leg. Anne’s hard past—orphanages, unwanted homes—gave her the skills that saved a baby’s life. How can difficult experiences make a person stronger instead of smaller?","As you read Ch. 20, notice how close the story stays to Anne while she is frightened of the wood she made up herself. You hear her fear from the inside. Now tell it from Marilla’s side, standing outside that fear. Which version is fairer to Anne, and why do you think the story keeps you with her instead?","Anne is terrified by the Haunted Wood she invented. How can an imagination be both a gift and a problem? What is Anne learning?"],"chapterQs":["Anne and Diana invented the Haunted Wood for fun. What turns this game from fun into something scary for Anne?","Marilla makes Anne walk through the Haunted Wood as “a lesson and a warning.” Is this fair, or is it cruel? Defend your answer with details.","Looking back across Ch. 16-20: compare Anne’s perspective at the start of the week (after the wine mistake) with her perspective at the end (after the Haunted Wood). How has she changed?"],"focus":"Two scenes are on the page. Read them from the top, out loud if you can. Check the word count under My Story — most stories are around 80–120 words right now. Then think about the thing that makes your kid special, because next week it’s going to cause trouble.","nowTry":"What is your kid’s greatest gift, and how could that same gift become a problem? Two or three sentences. Then write your word count.","plate":"plates/anne-ch20.png"},"words":[{"w":"cordial","d":"Warmly friendly. (Also: a sweet drink offered to friends.)","e":"🍷"},{"w":"relent","d":"To soften; to become less harsh; to change one’s mind.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"inexorable","d":"Refusing to give in or change; unable to be moved by pleading.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":10,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 10 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-10-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-10-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"cordial","d":"Warmly friendly. (Also: a sweet drink offered to friends.)","e":"🍷"},{"w":"relent","d":"To soften; to become less harsh; to change one’s mind.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"inexorable","d":"Refusing to give in or change; unable to be moved by pleading.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":11,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 11.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 21)","file":"lesson-11-1-anne-ch21.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-11-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 11.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 21). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how setting shapes events. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-welcome","done":true,"script":"It’s Marilla. The question says watch how one small mistake turns into a big embarrassment. Well, I’ve watched a good many of Anne’s. This one happens in my own kitchen, with the minister at my table, so you can imagine I have opinions. First there are two word parts that point a direction, and some doing words that tell you when. Then the chapter. Mind the bottles."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says a place decides how big a mistake feels. I’ll tell you why that’s so in Avonlea. Everybody knows everybody, and nothing stays in a kitchen. Rachel Lynde will have heard before the dishes are dry. So as you read, ask who’s close enough to see, and who’ll hear about it after. Then ask whether the same mistake would matter in a town where nobody knew your name."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-dol","done":true,"script":"The top sentence is a plain one, and it’s correct. Look at what makes it so. The names start with capitals, the doing word tells you it’s already done, and it stops where it should. The bottom one is about me and my vanilla, and it runs two thoughts together without a breath. Find where one stops and the next starts. Then give my name its capital. I’d thank you to."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four boxes of word work, and I’ll not pretend they’re exciting. But a girl who can’t tell what’s done from what’s coming is a girl who mixes up her bottles. Two of these are sorting. Put each card where it belongs, then name the groups yourself. Nobody’s going to name them for you. One is typing in a missing piece. Look before you type. I never saw the good of guessing."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-morphology","done":true,"script":"Two word parts this week, and each one points a direction. One means against, like a thing that pushes back. The other means away, or down, like leaving the house, or going down the cellar stairs. Read the four words in the box, cover the front, and see what’s left. Then ask which way the front is pushing. Against, or away and down. That’s all there is to it."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Four words, and one of them I’d never say myself. Vivacious. Anne would, and often. Read the sentence above each card before you flip it, the way the box shows you with the first. The words around it will tell you more than you think. Two of those sentences are about Mrs. Allan, and you’ll meet her today. I think well of her. Guess first, then flip. Not the other way round."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"Anne baked a cake. Anne bakes a cake. Anne will bake a cake. Same girl, same cake, and I’d wager she does all three before the week is out. What changes is when. Sort the cards by when, then give each group a name of your own. Don’t wait for mine, I haven’t given you any. And say them out loud. Your ear knows more grammar than you think."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-spelling","done":true,"script":"Six words, and every one ended in a y before something got added. Sometimes the y stayed put and sometimes it turned into an i. That isn’t whim. Look at the letter sitting just in front of the y, and then look at what got added after it. Sort them by what happened, then name your three groups. A rule you work out yourself sticks better than one I hand you."},{"id":"copia","label":"Marilla · about saying it three ways","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-copia","done":true,"script":"One sentence from the start of this chapter, the day the schoolmaster left. I never thought him worth two handkerchiefs, but there it is. Now say it three ways. Change the doing word first. Then start the sentence somewhere else, with a when or a where. Then tell how. Anne could do this in her sleep, and frequently does. You’ll do it awake."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-guide2","done":true,"script":"There’s a box here about being seen. I’ll be honest, I didn’t hold with that kind of talk once. A child was fed and clothed and that was that. But there’s a new minister’s wife in Avonlea, and Anne has fallen for her entirely. Watch how Mrs. Allan looks at Anne. Not at what Anne does. At Anne. Then watch whether I do the same. I’m not saying I will."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-read","done":true,"script":"Two stops, and the first one comes when the cake goes wrong. Read slowly up to the tea. Count who’s at the table, and who’d tell whom. When you reach the stop, think about what Mrs. Allan does, not only what she says. Then read on to the end and listen to what Anne says to me after. I had an answer for her. You can decide whether it was a fair one."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well. You’ve read it now, so I needn’t tell you what was in that bottle, or whose fault it was. I said partly mine, and I stand by partly. Now you write. Put things in the order they happened. Use the book, not your memory of it. And when you get to Mrs. Allan, use her own words. They’re better than mine, and I’m not too proud to say so."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-chapter","done":true,"script":"Three questions. The first is about the bottle and how it got where it was. The hint points at me, and I won’t argue. The second is about why Anne couldn’t smell it. She told me, through a good deal of crying. Find what she said. The third is about Mrs. Allan coming up the stairs. Look for the small thing she asks to see. It tells you more than a speech would."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Marilla · about the word connection","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your word-parts answer is saved from before. Look at it again now that you’ve read the chapter. Anne went up those stairs as fast as anyone ever has, and Mrs. Allan came up them after her. Ask whether your sentence points a direction, the way those stairs do. Against, or away, or down. If it could belong in this chapter, good. If it couldn’t, think how it might."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-11-1-workshop","done":true,"script":"Anne’s cake is a story you can only tell in order. The bottle first, then the guests, then the first bite. Tell it backwards and nobody laughs. Today you’re giving the middle of your own story its order. Three scenes, and each one starts with a word that says when. First, then, after that. Pick the ones that fit, not the fanciest. I’d take plain and right over fancy and wrong any day."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Setting is more than scenery. Where a story happens decides who sees a mistake and how big it feels.","wordParts":"anti- means “against” or “opposite to” (antifreeze, antibody, antiseptic). de- means “away from” or “down” (depart, descend, decrease).","grammar":"Past tense tells what already happened: baked, arrived. Many past verbs end in -ed, but some don’t. Future tense tells what hasn’t happened yet: will + the plain verb, as in will bake.","spelling":"Change y to i before a suffix when a consonant comes before the y (happy → happier, story → stories). Keep the y when a vowel comes before it (play → played) and always before -ing (carry → carrying).","dol":[{"raw":"marilla flavored the cake with vanilla anne added the liniment by mistake","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> flavored the cake with <strong>vanilla.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> added the liniment by <strong>mistake.</strong>","why":"Capital letters on the names Marilla and Anne, a period to close the first sentence, a capital A to start the next one, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"the minister came to tea marilla set out her yellow plum preserves","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> minister came to <strong>tea.</strong> <strong>Marilla</strong> set out her yellow plum <strong>preserves.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital to start, a period where the first thought ends, a capital M on Marilla to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 21, pay attention to where this all happens. Anne is baking in the Green Gables kitchen for a guest who matters, in a village where news travels fast. How does that place make one ruined cake feel so huge? Would the same mistake matter this much anywhere else?","Anne’s cake is ruined because she couldn’t smell the difference. What does Mrs. Allan’s reaction tell us about the kind of person she is?","By the end of the chapter, Anne is sure she will never make this same mistake again — she will only find new ones. Is that a comfort or a worry? Why?"],"chapterQs":["Anne accidentally flavors the cake with the wrong bottle. What was in the bottle, and how did it get there?","Anne didn’t notice the mistake even though the bottle smelled wrong. Why couldn’t she smell it?","Mrs. Allan comes upstairs to comfort Anne. What does Mrs. Allan say and do that shows she is kind, and how does Anne respond?"],"focus":"The middle of a story is where pressure builds, and it only works if the reader can follow the order. Today you take the three middle scenes from your list — Scene 2 (written), Scene 3 and Scene 4 (this week) — and give each one a transition word to start on, so nobody gets lost.","nowTry":"Fill in your own stoplight: for Scenes 2, 3 and 4, write the transition word the scene starts with, then what happens in it. One line each. Scene 2 is already in My Story — check it has one. Scenes 3 and 4 you write this week.","plate":"plates/anne-ch21.png"},"words":[{"w":"kindred","d":"Alike in spirit; sharing the same feelings, ideas, or imagination.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"precarious","d":"Unsafe; dangerously unsteady; not securely held in place.","e":"🪜"},{"w":"exquisite","d":"Beautiful in a delicate, refined way; very fine.","e":"🌹"}]},{"w":11,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 11.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 22 & 23)","file":"lesson-11-2-anne-ch22-23.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-11-2","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 11.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 22 & 23). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how setting shapes events. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Diana’s welcome","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Diana. The question’s about pride, and how it makes people do dangerous things. I’ve known Anne a whole year now, and I’ll tell you, she has an awful lot of it. Today there’s a tea, and then there’s a party, and the party is at my house. Before all that there’s word work, and some sentences with more than one doing word in them. Find every one."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Diana · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says every place lets you do some things and stops you doing others. That’s ever so true. At the manse Anne has to sit up straight and mind her manners. In our garden nobody’s minding anything. So as you read, keep asking what each place lets Anne do, and what it won’t. I know which place I’d rather be. I’m not sure Anne does."},{"id":"dol","label":"Diana · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-dol","done":true,"script":"The top sentence is Anne’s, word for word, and it’s awfully grand. Look at everything it does. The marks around her words, my name tucked in between commas, the period inside at the very end. The bottom one is two thoughts squashed together, one already done and one not yet. Give them each a sentence of their own. A stop, then a capital. And Anne’s name wants its capital too."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Diana · before the word work","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four bits of word work. Two you’ve seen before, so you’ll be quick. One asks you to tap every doing word that isn’t happening right now, and there’s more than one, so don’t stop at the first. The other asks for words that kept their y when you’d think it would change. Those are the sneaky ones. Ruby Gillis would tap everything in sight. Don’t be Ruby."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Diana · about the word parts","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-morphology","done":true,"script":"Matching today. Two little word parts, and two word roots that go with them. One part means against. One means away, or down. The roots are the rest of words you already know. Say the whole word out loud, then take it apart. If you get stuck, think of coming down the stairs, or going away from home. Those two will tell you a great deal."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Diana · about today’s words","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"These are the same four words as yesterday. Now you match them without the cards, and that’s the real test. Don’t just pick what sounds nice. Say each word in a sentence about someone you know. If it fits the person, you’ve likely got it. I said kindred about Anne and it fitted perfectly. Then I said it about Josie Pye. It did not."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Diana · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"One long sentence, and lots of doing words in it. Some are already done and some haven’t happened yet. Tap every one that isn’t happening right now. There’s more than one, so keep going after the first. Read it slowly, a word at a time, and ask of each one, is this now? If it isn’t, tap it. Anne reads too fast. I read slowly, and I get things right."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Diana · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"These words all ended in y once. Most changed it to an i when something got added. You don’t want those. You want the ones that kept their y when you might have expected it to change. So for each word, look at the letter in front of where the y was, and look at what got added. The y had a reason to stay. Find the ones with a reason."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Diana · before you open the book","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about being brave and being dared. I’ll tell you about daring, because everybody in Avonlea is doing it this summer. Somebody says you can’t, and then you simply have to. The boys started it, and now it’s us. It’s mostly silly things, climbing trees and hopping round gardens. Watch whether Anne picks the dare, or the dare picks Anne. I’m only saying watch."},{"id":"read","label":"Diana · before you read","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-read","done":true,"script":"One stop, after the tea. Anne gets a letter with Miss on the front, and she can’t stop talking about it. Think about why one little word could matter so much to her. Then read on into my party. Watch the garden, and watch who dares whom, and in what order. The order matters more than you’d think. That’s all I’m going to say. Truly."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Diana · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-rwm","done":true,"script":"That sentence is Anne coming up the lane with her letter, and it’s exactly how she moves when she’s happy. Like a sprite the wind is blowing about. The book doesn’t say she went fast. It says dancing, and then it gives you the sunshine and the shadows. When you write yours, choose the moving word first. Then the comparison. Then two things the place looks like."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Diana · before you write","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-guide3","done":true,"script":"Oh, Anne. I begged her not to. I told her it wasn’t fair to dare anybody to do anything so dangerous. She had an answer ready, of course. She always does. When she fell I truly thought she was killed. She wasn’t. Now you write about it. Use what people said, the actual words, not what you think they meant. And don’t forget the tea. That was the nice part."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Diana · about the questions","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about the tea, and it wants two details, not one. Look at what Mrs. Allan invites Anne to do. The second is about the roof. Look at who dared whom first, and then at what Anne says up there. The third is about Marilla. I didn’t see her face. I was looking at Anne. Find the line that tells you what hit Marilla. It’s a strong one."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Diana · about the word connection","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your grammar answer is saved. Read it again now. Anne said she would walk that ridgepole, and she did, a few steps anyway. Think about the word you picked. Was it something that already happened, or something somebody said would happen? Anne lives in both at once, mostly in the would. That’s why you have to keep an eye on her. I always do."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Diana · about today’s writing","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-11-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"This part is like the roof. Your kid wants something and is afraid of something, both at once, and everybody’s watching. Write it so the reader feels both. Then stop, right before your kid decides. Don’t tell what happens next. I couldn’t breathe, standing in our garden, not knowing. That’s what keeps a reader reading. Leave them standing in the garden too."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Every place opens some doors and closes others. Ask what a place lets a character do, and what it stops them from doing.","wordParts":"anti- means “against” or “opposite to” (antifreeze, antibody, antiseptic). de- means “away from” or “down” (depart, descend, decrease).","grammar":"Past tense tells what already happened: baked, arrived. Many past verbs end in -ed, but some don’t. Future tense tells what hasn’t happened yet: will + the plain verb, as in will bake.","spelling":"Change y to i before a suffix when a consonant comes before the y (happy → happier, story → stories). Keep the y when a vowel comes before it (play → played) and always before -ing (carry → carrying).","dol":[{"raw":"anne climbed the ladder she will walk the ridgepole no matter what","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> climbed the <strong>ladder.</strong> <strong>She</strong> will walk the ridgepole no matter <strong>what.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period after the first thought (climbed is past), a capital S to start the next sentence (will walk is future), and a period at the end."},{"raw":"anne opened the letter she will keep it forever","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> opened the <strong>letter.</strong> <strong>She</strong> will keep it <strong>forever.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on Anne, a period after the past-tense thought (opened), a capital S to start the future-tense one (will keep), and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["As you read Ch. 22 and 23, keep asking what each place allows. At the manse tea, Anne is a guest in somebody else’s house, and being called Miss Anne Shirley there means something. Later she is somewhere very different. What could Anne do in one of these places that she could never do in the other?","Millay’s speaker finds joy just by watching—not taking, not changing anything. When does Anne find happiness in simply noticing the world around her?","Anne is invited to tea at the manse and is called “Miss Anne Shirley” for the first time. Why does being called “Miss” mean so much to Anne?"],"chapterQs":["Anne goes to tea at the manse. Find TWO specific details from the visit that show Mrs. Allan and Anne becoming closer.","Josie Pye dares Anne to walk the ridgepole of Mr. Barry’s kitchen roof. Why does Anne agree to do something so dangerous?","Anne falls and breaks her ankle. Marilla’s reaction is described as a “revelation.” What does Marilla realize in this moment?"],"focus":"Scene 3 is the worst moment. Your kid faces a choice under pressure: what they want against what they fear. This is where last week’s gift turns into the problem. Write it so the reader can feel both. Stop the scene right before your kid decides.","nowTry":"Write Scene 3 in My Story (3–5 sentences): a choice under pressure. Then, here, one sentence: what does your kid want in that moment, and what do they fear?","rwm":"“She had come dancing up the lane, like a wind-blown sprite, through the mellow sunshine and lazy shadows of the August evening.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch22-23.png"},"words":[{"w":"kindred","d":"Alike in spirit; sharing the same feelings, ideas, or imagination.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"precarious","d":"Unsafe; dangerously unsteady; not securely held in place.","e":"🪜"},{"w":"exquisite","d":"Beautiful in a delicate, refined way; very fine.","e":"🌹"}]},{"w":11,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 11.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 24)","file":"lesson-11-3-anne-ch24.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-11-3","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 11.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 24). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Miss Stacy’s welcome","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-welcome","done":true,"script":"I’m Miss Stacy. Your question asks about the difference between a grown-up who notices you and one who doesn’t. I’m a teacher, so I had better have an answer, hadn’t I? Today the word work is mostly building, where you put the pieces together yourself. Then there’s a chapter about my schoolroom, and a concert. Keep an eye on who’s in the room."},{"id":"dol","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is a plain one, and every capital in it is earning its place. A title, a name, a day of the week. The bottom one is about my Friday afternoons, I think. It holds two thoughts, one that hasn’t happened yet and one that already has, and they’ve been run together. Find where the first one ends. Then look for every word that is somebody’s name, or a day’s."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Miss Stacy · before the word work","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-guide1","done":true,"script":"Today you build. You’ll type in a missing word part, fill in sentences, put a doing word into the time the bracket asks for, and add endings to words that end in y. Building is harder than spotting. When you spot, the answer is in front of you. When you build, it’s in you. Say each one aloud before you type. Your ear is a fine teacher. Better than me, some days."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Miss Stacy · about the word parts","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Four words, and each is missing its front. You know the two word parts by now. One means against, the other means away or down. Read the meaning after each blank first. Then ask which direction the meaning is pushing, against something, or away and down. Type the part that fits. On our field afternoons we look at a fern before we name it. Same here."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s words","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Three sentences, three blanks, and four words in the bank. So one word is left over, and that’s on purpose. Read every sentence through before you choose. Then say it aloud with your word in the blank. If it sounds like something a real person would say about a real person, you’ve likely got it. One of these sentences comes from today’s chapter. Listen for it later."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"Each sentence names a time in brackets, past or future, and your job is to make the doing word fit it. Some of these are about things Anne and Diana have already done, and you’ll recognize them. Be careful with the past ones. Not every doing word just adds an ending. A few change on the inside. Put yesterday in front and listen. Then type."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"Two rules at once now. Look at the letter before the y. Then look at what’s being added. Both matter, and sometimes they pull different ways. I’d write each word on scrap paper first, the way you’d sketch a leaf before you draw it properly. If it looks strange, it probably is. Then type it. A good speller is mostly a careful looker."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Miss Stacy · before you open the book","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about being called by your right name. I take some trouble over names. A child who hears her name said carelessly learns she doesn’t matter much, and that’s a hard lesson to unlearn. So I listen to how each pupil says her own, and I say it back the same way. It costs nothing at all. As you read, watch for a name that matters to somebody."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says part of a setting is who’s there. A schoolroom full of children is a different place from the same room empty. Add a teacher who listens, and it changes again. Add an audience and a stage, and it changes again. So as you read, count who is in the room for each thing that happens. Then ask whether it would go the same way with different people there."},{"id":"read","label":"Miss Stacy · before you read","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes early, when Anne talks about her new teacher. That’s me, so I’ll try not to listen. Think about why something so small could feel so large to her. The second comes once the concert is underway. Ask yourself what Anne wants most out of it. The acting, or being seen? You’ll have to decide. I’m not sure Anne knows herself."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Miss Stacy · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-rwm","done":true,"script":"That sentence is about me, which makes it rather awkward to read aloud. But look at how it’s put together. Two describing words up front, then with the happy gift of, and then what the person can do. It tells you what I’m like by what I do, not how I look. When you write yours, try the same. Pick someone you know, and name the thing they’re good at."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Miss Stacy · before you write","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-guide3","done":true,"script":"Now it’s your turn to write, and I’ll tell you what I tell my class. Don’t write what you think I want. Write what you noticed, and then show me where in the book you noticed it. A good answer points. If you can put your finger on the line, you’ve got it. If you can’t, go back and look. Some of the best compositions I get come from children who looked twice."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Miss Stacy · about the questions","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question asks for two things I do that Anne loves. I’d rather not guess at that myself, so look at what she tells Marilla. The second asks what the concert is for, and what parts Anne has. She has several, so count them all. The third asks why some grown-ups frown at my ways. They’re not wrong to be careful. Show both sides, in their own words."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Miss Stacy · about the word connection","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your spelling answer is saved. Read it again now that you know this chapter. Did you take a y word from it, the way the box asked? Then check your two endings the way you’d check a sum, one letter at a time. Did each one follow the rule you said it did? Nobody in my class has ever been marked down for checking twice. Quite the opposite."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s writing","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-11-3-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today your kid has to do something. Not be rescued, not get lucky. Do. Anne in this chapter stops waiting and starts doing. She practices, she rehearses, she argues her case with Marilla. It doesn’t have to be grand. Getting up off the ground counts. Write the things your kid does, put a when word in front, and let somebody say one line. Then read it aloud."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Part of a setting is who is there. Change the people nearby and you change what happens.","wordParts":"anti- means “against” or “opposite to” (antifreeze, antibody, antiseptic). de- means “away from” or “down” (depart, descend, decrease).","grammar":"Past tense tells what already happened: baked, arrived. Many past verbs end in -ed, but some don’t. Future tense tells what hasn’t happened yet: will + the plain verb, as in will bake.","spelling":"Change y to i before a suffix when a consonant comes before the y (happy → happier, story → stories). Keep the y when a vowel comes before it (play → played) and always before -ing (carry → carrying).","dol":[{"raw":"miss stacy will take us to the woods on friday she taught the lesson well","fixed":"<strong>Miss Stacy</strong> will take us to the woods on <strong>Friday.</strong> <strong>She</strong> taught the lesson <strong>well.</strong>","why":"Capitals on the title and name (Miss Stacy), a capital F on the day Friday, a period after the future-tense thought (will take), a capital S to start the past-tense one (taught), and a period at the end."},{"raw":"the concert will raise money for a flag anne practiced her recitation","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> concert will raise money for a <strong>flag.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> practiced her <strong>recitation.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital to start, a period after the future-tense thought (will raise), a capital A on Anne to start the past-tense one (practiced), and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Ch. 24 is called Miss Stacy and Her Pupils Get Up a Concert . Watch who is nearby in this chapter. A new teacher is in the schoolroom, and a concert means an audience. When Anne feels Miss Stacy saying her name with an e , part of why it lands so hard is who is saying it. How do the people around Anne change what these events mean to her?","Anne says that when Miss Stacy says her name, she feels Miss Stacy is spelling it with an e . Why is this small detail so important to Anne after years of being misnamed?","Miss Stacy and her pupils begin getting up a concert, and Anne wants a part in it badly. Is it the acting Anne wants, or the being watched?"],"chapterQs":["Anne loves Miss Stacy from the moment she meets her. Name TWO specific things Miss Stacy does that Anne loves.","Miss Stacy plans a Christmas concert. What is the concert for? What part will Anne play in it?","Back in Ch. 22, Mrs. Lynde said a lady teacher was “a dangerous innovation.” In Ch. 24, Marilla grumbles about field afternoons and the concert. Why do the grown-ups disapprove, but why are Anne and the other students excited about Miss Stacy?"],"focus":"Scene 4 is your kid acting. Not being rescued, not getting lucky — doing something. It can be small. Getting up counts. Include one transition word and one line of dialogue.","nowTry":"Write Scene 4 in My Story (3–5 sentences). Then, here: list the three things your kid DOES in that scene, in order.","rwm":"“Miss Stacy was a bright, sympathetic young woman with the happy gift of winning and holding the affections of her pupils and bringing out the best that was in them mentally and morally.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch24.png"},"words":[{"w":"kindred","d":"Alike in spirit; sharing the same feelings, ideas, or imagination.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"precarious","d":"Unsafe; dangerously unsteady; not securely held in place.","e":"🪜"},{"w":"exquisite","d":"Beautiful in a delicate, refined way; very fine.","e":"🌹"}]},{"w":11,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 11.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 25)","file":"lesson-11-4-anne-ch25.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-11-4","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 11.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 25). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Matthew’s welcome","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-welcome","done":true,"script":"Well now, it’s Matthew. The question says I do the bravest thing. I dunno about bravest. It was the hardest, I’ll say that. Today there’s a sentence to fix, and word work where some of it is already right, so you leave that part alone. Then the chapter. You’ll see a dress in it. I’ll not say any more, or I’ll get flustered."},{"id":"dol","label":"Matthew · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-dol","done":false,"script":"Well now, the top sentence is me, and it’s put together proper. A capital on my name, a capital on Anne’s, a capital on Christmas. The bottom one runs two things together, the unwrapping and the watching. One thing finishes and then the next one starts. Put a stop where the first is done. Then capitals on both names. I never was much for writing, but I can see where a thing ends."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Matthew · before the word work","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four things to do, and in two of them some of it’s already right. I’d go careful there. When I went to the store, well, never mind about the store. I got flustered and said yes to things I didn’t want. Don’t you do that. Read each one through. If it’s right, leave it be. Only tap what’s wrong. The other two are matching and choosing. You’ll manage."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Matthew · about the word parts","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Well now, this is the matching again. Two word parts and two roots, and you’ve seen them all week. Tap one, then tap its partner. If you’re not sure, say the whole word out loud, and think about going down the cellar stairs, or going away down the lane. Then ask which way the word points. I’m no scholar. But I know down from away, and so do you."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Matthew · about today’s words","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Five ways to show you know your words, and you only pick one. Well now, I’d pick the one about teaching it to somebody younger. I learned more explaining things to Anne than I ever did at school, and I didn’t go long. Pick whichever one you can finish. Then do it all the way through. A thing half done is a thing you’ll be thinking about for days."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Matthew · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"These are sentences about Anne’s fall off the Barry roof, and after. Some tell the when right, and some don’t. Look for the word that says when, like yesterday or tomorrow. Then see if the doing word agrees with it. If the two of them argue, tap it. If they get along, leave them be. Marilla and I don’t always agree either, but we generally know when we don’t."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Matthew · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six words, and some of them are spelled wrong. Well now, a wrong word looks wrong if you look at it long enough. Say each one slowly and picture where the y went, or where it should have gone. If you’d be embarrassed to see it painted on a store sign, tap it. I’ve seen a few store signs in my time. Leave the good ones alone."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Matthew · before you open the book","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about being seen. Well now, I dunno much about that. I’m not one people look at, and I like it that way. But I look. I’ve been looking at Anne for most of a year, and lately something has been bothering me about her that I can’t put my finger on. As you read, look at what I’m looking at. Then you’ll know what I know."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Matthew · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says try moving the scene somewhere else. Well now, I’ll help you. Put me in a store where a man’s behind the counter, and I do all right. Put me in a store I don’t usually go to, with somebody I don’t expect behind that counter, and, well. You’ll see. As you read, ask which parts could happen anywhere at all, and which parts only happen here."},{"id":"read","label":"Matthew · before you read","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-read","done":false,"script":"One stop, at the end. It asks why it was me that noticed. Well now, I’ve wondered that myself. Read the start slow, where I’m sitting in the corner. Then read the store part as fast as you like. It goes about as well as you’d expect. When you get to Christmas, slow down again. That’s the part I’d want you to see."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Matthew · before you write","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-guide3","done":true,"script":"Well now. A rake. And twenty pounds of brown sugar. Marilla’s never going to let me forget the sugar. Now you write about it. I guess you could make me sound foolish, and you’d not be wrong. But look at why I went at all. That’s the part worth writing about. Use the book. It’s got it about right, though I’d have left out the sugar."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Matthew · about the questions","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"Three questions. The first is about what I noticed about Anne’s dresses. Look at the other girls, then look at Anne. The second is about the store, and why I got so mixed up. Look at who was behind the counter. The third is about the whole week, and who really saw Anne. You get to pick. I’d not pick me, if I were you. But you pick."},{"id":"wordconn","label":"Matthew · about the word connection","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-wordconn","done":false,"script":"Your vocabulary answer is saved. Read it over now. If you picked a word for your own story character, well now, ask yourself if it fits them the way kindred fits Anne and Diana. If it nearly fits, that’s a guess. If it fits like a good boot, you’ve learned it. I’ve got one pair of boots that fits like that. You’ll know the feeling when you get there."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Matthew · about today’s writing","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-11-4-workshop","done":true,"script":"Well now, today you just finish it. That’s all. Get to the end. It doesn’t need to be good yet. Anne wanted puffed sleeves for a long while, and it took me till Christmas. A rough ending is better than none. Write two or three sentences, what happened and how your kid feels. Then read the whole thing top to bottom. You might be surprised."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"To test a setting, move the scene somewhere else. If it wouldn’t happen the same way there, the place is doing real work.","wordParts":"anti- means “against” or “opposite to” (antifreeze, antibody, antiseptic). de- means “away from” or “down” (depart, descend, decrease).","grammar":"Past tense tells what already happened: baked, arrived. Many past verbs end in -ed, but some don’t. Future tense tells what hasn’t happened yet: will + the plain verb, as in will bake.","spelling":"Change y to i before a suffix when a consonant comes before the y (happy → happier, story → stories). Keep the y when a vowel comes before it (play → played) and always before -ing (carry → carrying).","dol":[{"raw":"anne unwrapped the dress matthew watched her happily from the kitchen corner","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> unwrapped the <strong>dress.</strong> <strong>Matthew</strong> watched her happily from the kitchen <strong>corner.</strong>","why":"Capital letters on the names Anne and Matthew, a period to close the first sentence, a capital M to start the next one, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"matthew drove to the store he bought a garden rake by mistake","fixed":"<strong>Matthew</strong> drove to the <strong>store.</strong> <strong>He</strong> bought a garden rake by <strong>mistake.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on Matthew, a period where the first thought ends, a capital H to start the next sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Rossetti asks what a poor person can give—and answers: their heart. Matthew can barely speak to a stranger, but he braves a store, buys a rake by accident, and asks for help—all so Anne can have the dress she dreams of. Why is Matthew’s gift so powerful even though he never says much?","Ch. 25 is called Matthew Insists on Puffed Sleeves . Try moving it. Matthew has to go into a store and talk to a clerk, and he is the shyest man in Avonlea. Would this be the same brave act in a place where nobody knew him? Where does the small village make his errand harder — and where does it help him?","Matthew picks the dress with puffed sleeves. Why is it MATTHEW — the shyest, quietest person in Anne’s life — who notices what she wants most?"],"chapterQs":["Matthew notices that Anne is dressed differently from the other girls. What is different about Anne’s dresses, and what does Matthew decide to do about it?","Matthew goes to Lawson’s store to buy the dress and accidentally buys a garden rake and 20 pounds of brown sugar. Why does he get so confused? What does this show about Matthew?","Looking across Ch. 21-25: Name one adult who has truly SEEN Anne this week. What did they see, and how did Anne change because of it?"],"focus":"Today you finish the first draft. Scene 5 is the ending, and today it only has to exist. A rough ending is better than no ending — you’ll make it strong in Week 14. Two or three sentences. Just land it.","nowTry":"After your rough ending is in My Story, look back at your whole draft. Has the conflict GROWN since Scene 1? Write 2–3 sentences: (1) what your kid wanted at the start, (2) what they feared most in Scene 3, (3) one thing that made it worse.","plate":"plates/anne-ch25.png"},"words":[{"w":"kindred","d":"Alike in spirit; sharing the same feelings, ideas, or imagination.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"precarious","d":"Unsafe; dangerously unsteady; not securely held in place.","e":"🪜"},{"w":"exquisite","d":"Beautiful in a delicate, refined way; very fine.","e":"🌹"}]},{"w":11,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 11 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-11-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-11-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"kindred","d":"Alike in spirit; sharing the same feelings, ideas, or imagination.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"precarious","d":"Unsafe; dangerously unsteady; not securely held in place.","e":"🪜"},{"w":"exquisite","d":"Beautiful in a delicate, refined way; very fine.","e":"🌹"}]},{"w":12,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 12.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 26)","file":"lesson-12-1-anne-ch26.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-12-1","done":true,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 12.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 26). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Summarize important events without retelling everything. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Diana’s welcome","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-welcome","done":true,"script":"Hello, it’s Diana. Today is about our story club, and how Anne works out what she thinks a good story is. She has a great many ideas about it. Before the reading there’s word work, doing words that don’t follow the usual rule for the past, and a spelling rule about a quiet letter e. Then you’ll tell the chapter in a few sentences. Just a few. Anne would need fifty."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Diana · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says to tell a chapter in three sentences, not thirty. I’m rather good at that, because I never know what to do with my people, so I simply stop. Anne is the opposite. She keeps every lovely word and every sigh. So as you read, stop now and then and ask what has changed. If nothing has, that part can go."},{"id":"dol","label":"Diana · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is Anne’s, and it’s about Matthew, who likes every story she writes. Look at the two doing words that tell what already happened. Neither one takes the usual ending. The bottom sentence is about me, I’m afraid, and somebody forgot my capital. There’s also a story that belongs to someone, and a sentence that never stops. Mend all three."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Diana · before the word work","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four bits of word work, and two of them sort into three columns, not two. That’s harder than it sounds. Ruby would put everything in the middle and hope. Don’t do that. Say each word out loud before you move it, because your ear knows more than you think. And the fill-ins want the back half of each word, not the front. Read the meaning first."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Diana · about the word parts","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-morphology","done":true,"script":"Two old roots today, living inside words you already use. One means to carry. The other means to pull, or to draw. The front of each word is already there, and you type the rest. Read the meaning after the blank first. Then ask, is something being carried here, or pulled? Think of a wagon, then a magnet. Anne would think of a chariot and a lodestone. A wagon does just as well."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Diana · about today’s words","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-vocabulary","done":true,"script":"Four new words, and each card has a real sentence from the book above it. Some of them will sound strange to you. They sounded strange to me too, the first time. Use the sentence anyway, then look for word parts, then guess before you flip. Anne flips first and thinks afterward. I’d think first. Then tap."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Diana · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-grammar","done":true,"script":"All of these doing words are already in the past, and not one of them takes the usual ending. But they break the rule in three different ways. Say each pair out loud, the now word and then the past one. Listen for how much changes. A little, a lot, or nothing at all. Anne says breaking a rule beautifully is half the fun. Here it’s the whole lesson."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Diana · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-spelling","done":true,"script":"These words all end in a quiet e, and then something gets added. Sometimes the e stays and sometimes it goes. Look at the first letter of what’s been added, and look at what comes just before the e. Those two together decide it. One column is for words that never had the tricky sound at all. You’ll know them when you say them."},{"id":"copia","label":"Diana · about saying it three ways","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-copia","done":true,"script":"That sentence is about me, and it’s quite true. I did write to Aunt Josephine about our club. Now say it three ways. Change the doing word first, but keep it about a letter. Then start the sentence somewhere else, with a when or a where. Then tell how I wrote it. Anne says one sentence said three ways is three times as good. I only half believe her."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Diana · before you open the book","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-guide2","done":true,"script":"There’s a box here about learning from your mistakes. I know a little about that. Anne and I made up the Haunted Wood together, and we frightened ourselves so badly that neither of us would go through it after dark. Anne said she’d be contented with commonplace places after that. As you read, watch whether she is. I’m not sure she is."},{"id":"read","label":"Diana · before you read","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes when Anne tells how each girl in the club writes. One of them is me, and I’ll say no more. Think about why four friends would make up such different stories. The second comes at the very end. Ask yourself why none of us ever writes about Avonlea. It’s right outside the window, and nobody thinks of it at all."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Diana · before you write","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-guide3","done":true,"script":"Now you write, and it’s your answers, not your stories. Anne writes three pages when one line would do. Don’t. When a question asks what happened, say it plainly, then show me where in the book. When it asks why, give a reason and a line to back it up. And keep your summary short. Short is hard. I know, because Anne never manages it."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Diana · about the questions","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question asks what the club is called and what each of us has to do. Anne made the rules, so look at what she tells Marilla. The second wants Anne’s story in two sentences. Two! I heard every word of it, and I couldn’t. Keep the people and the ending. The third asks why a moral matters to her. Look at who she says agrees."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Diana · about today’s writing","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-12-1-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you add. Find one place in your story where things happen too fast, where somebody falls or wins or cries and we never feel it. Then slow down and put in what we’d see or hear or feel. Anne’s stories are full of things happening, horses running away and people drowning. The part I love best is always the little bit in between. Put a little bit in."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Summarizing means telling what happened without retelling everything. Keep the events that changed something, and leave out the small details.","wordParts":"port means “to carry” (transport, portable, export). tract means “to pull” or “to draw” (attract, distract, extract).","grammar":"Irregular past-tense verbs don’t just add -ed. Some change a vowel (sing → sang), some turn into a new word (go → went), and some don’t change at all (put → put).","spelling":"After ce or ge, keep the silent e when the suffix starts with a or o, so the c or g stays soft (courageous, manageable). Drop it when the suffix starts with e or i (noticing, changing).","dol":[{"raw":"diana read annes story and cried during the saddest part","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> read <strong>Anne’s</strong> story and cried during the saddest <strong>part.</strong>","why":"A capital on the name Diana, an apostrophe-s on Anne’s because the story belongs to Anne, and a period at the end. Read is already in the past: same spelling, said like red."},{"raw":"jane read rubys story and wept at the saddest part","fixed":"<strong>Jane</strong> read <strong>Ruby’s</strong> story and wept at the saddest <strong>part.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on Jane, an apostrophe-s on Ruby’s because the story is hers, and a period at the end. Wept is the past of weep, not weeped."}],"pauses":["Ch. 26 is called The Story Club Is Formed . When you finish, try telling the chapter in three sentences. Which events would you keep? Which details are fun but not needed to explain what happened?","Anne’s story-club friends each have a different style — Ruby loves romance, Jane is sensible, Diana kills off characters. What does this say about how different people think when they make up stories?","The Story Club writes tales that are nothing like ordinary life in Avonlea. Why might it be easier to write about far-away, dramatic things than about home?"],"chapterQs":["Anne starts a club with Diana that grows to include Jane and Ruby. What is the club called, and what does each member have to do?","Anne’s first story is called “The Jealous Rival.” Without retelling every detail, give a 2-sentence summary of what happens in Anne’s story.","Anne tells Marilla that the moral of every story matters. Why does Anne think a moral is important? What does this tell you about Anne as a young writer?"],"focus":"Revising is big changes, not fixing typos. This week you use ARMS: Add, Remove, Move, Substitute. Today is A. Find one place where your story moves too fast — where you skipped something the reader needed — and add it.","nowTry":"Pacing pass. Find ONE place that moves too fast and write the 1–3 sentences you added there. Then find ONE place that drags, and say what you’d cut (you’ll cut it tomorrow).","plate":"plates/anne-ch26.png"},"words":[{"w":"vanity","d":"Too much pride in how one looks; caring too much about appearance.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"implicitly","d":"Without questioning; trusting completely.","e":"👂"},{"w":"epoch","d":"A memorable period of time; a turning point.","e":"🌟"}]},{"w":12,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 12.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 27 & 28)","file":"lesson-12-2-anne-ch27-28.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-12-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 12.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 27 & 28). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Summarize important events without retelling everything. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-welcome","done":true,"script":"It’s Marilla. Two chapters, and two scrapes, and I was home for only one of them. The question is what Anne learns from her mistakes, and she will tell you herself, at some length. First there’s word work. Find every doing word in the past that breaks the usual rule, and every word that lost its quiet e. Then read. Then tell each chapter short. Shorter than she would."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-wgrd","done":true,"script":"The box says the hard part is leaving out the good bits. That child could make a funeral out of a broken teacup, so I know exactly what it means. These two chapters are full of things you’ll want to tell. Most of them you won’t need. Keep why something happened. Let what it looked like go. And if a detail only makes you laugh, it goes first."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is from the book, I’m sorry to say, and every word of it is true. Look at the two doing words that changed inside themselves, and the one that only took the usual ending. The bottom sentence is two thoughts jammed together with no stop between them. Give each its own. And the girl in it wants her capital, whatever she’s done."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-guide1","done":true,"script":"Four pieces of word work. One is a matching game, and one asks you to remember words without any help. Then two where you tap. Tap only what the directions ask for, not everything that looks interesting. Anne does whatever catches her eye, and I’ve seen where that leads. Read the directions twice. Then do exactly that, and nothing more."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"Matching today. Two roots and two little front parts, and what each one means. You met the roots before. The front parts are new company. For each one, think of a whole word you already use that has it inside, and say it out loud. Then take that word apart. What’s left over usually tells you what the part means."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"The same four words as yesterday, and no cards this time. Match each one to its meaning. If you’re not sure, put the word in a sentence about somebody at home. I could think of one for every word on that list, and I’d rather not say who. Don’t guess. Guessing is how a girl ends up believing a stranger at the back door."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"One long sentence with more than one past doing word in it. Tap only the ones that don’t take the usual ending. There’s more than one, so don’t stop at the first. Read it slowly, a word at a time, and ask of each one, did this already happen, and how did it change to show it? I’ve no patience for halfway work. Find all of them."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six words, and each one once ended in a quiet e. In half of them the e stayed, and in half it was dropped. Tap only the ones where it was dropped. Look at the first letter of what was added. That letter decides whether the e is still needed. It’s a plain rule. I like plain rules. They don’t argue back."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about learning from mistakes. I’ve had a front seat for most of hers. The brooch, the Haunted Wood, the cake with the liniment in it. Every one of them happened on my watch. What I want to know is whether she ever learns anything from them, or only finds a new way to be wrong. Read, and judge for yourself."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-read","done":true,"script":"One stop, at the end of the first chapter. Think about what that scrape teaches her, about strangers, and about wanting to look different. Then read on into the second. I was away that afternoon, over at Carmody, so I only learned of it afterward. Watch who comes along, and watch what she says to him. I have my own opinion. Form yours."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Marilla · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-rwm","done":true,"script":"I wasn’t there to see this, but I can tell you what happened from the sentence alone. A boy jumps into a boat, and his cheeks are red. Three things, and you know he’s angry and he’s leaving. She doesn’t tell you what he thought. She doesn’t need to. When you write yours, pick three things somebody could see, and trust your reader with the rest."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-guide3","done":true,"script":"Now the writing. I’ll say what I’d say to Anne. Answer the question that’s asked, not the one you wish had been asked. If it wants what happened, say what happened. If it wants a reason, give one, and show where in the book you found it. Plain answers. You can be as romantic as you like in your own story. Not here."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question asks what color her hair really turned and what had to be done about it. I was the one who had to do it, and I’ll say no more. The second asks how she saved herself, and who came along after. The third gives you her own list and asks you to choose one. Use her words. She chose them carefully enough."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-12-2-workshop","done":true,"script":"Today you cut something. One sentence that wanders off from the scene, even if you’re fond of it. Then move one, to where it does the most good. The last sentence of a scene is the strongest spot. I’ve never seen the use of saying a thing twice, so this suits me. A good story, like a good kitchen, has nothing lying about that doesn’t belong."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"The hard part of summarizing is leaving out the fun details. Keep why something happened, and let go of what it looked like.","wordParts":"port means “to carry” (transport, portable, export). tract means “to pull” or “to draw” (attract, distract, extract).","grammar":"Irregular past-tense verbs don’t just add -ed. Some change a vowel (sing → sang), some turn into a new word (go → went), and some don’t change at all (put → put).","spelling":"After ce or ge, keep the silent e when the suffix starts with a or o, so the c or g stays soft (courageous, manageable). Drop it when the suffix starts with e or i (noticing, changing).","dol":[{"raw":"anne sank to the floor and wept she had ruined her hair","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> sank to the floor and <strong>wept.</strong> <strong>She</strong> had ruined her <strong>hair.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period where the first thought ends, a capital S to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Sank and wept are both irregular past-tense verbs."},{"raw":"marilla cut the green hair off it was the only cure","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> cut the green hair <strong>off.</strong> <strong>It</strong> was the only <strong>cure.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: a capital on Marilla, a period after off, a capital I to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Cut is irregular too: its past looks just like the present."}],"pauses":["Ch. 27 and 28 are full of details you will want to repeat. Anne dyes her hair green after a peddler promises raven black, and Ch. 28 is called An Unfortunate Lily Maid . Now be strict with yourself. Which details does a listener actually need to understand what happened — and which are only fun to tell?","Longfellow’s arrow and song both come back—one stuck in a tree, one living in a friend’s heart. Think of something Anne said or did early in the story that comes back to matter later.","Anne dyes her hair green because a peddler promised it would turn raven black. What does this scrape teach Anne about trusting strangers and about vanity?"],"chapterQs":["Anne dyes her hair to make it raven black. What does her hair actually turn, and what has to happen to fix it?","Anne and the girls dramatize Elaine on the pond. The flat begins to leak. How does Anne save herself, and who unexpectedly rescues her?","After the Lily Maid disaster, Anne tells Marilla each mistake has “cured” her of something. Pick ONE of the mistakes Anne lists and name what it cured her of."],"focus":"Two moves today. REMOVE: cut a sentence that doesn’t belong in its scene, even if you like it. MOVE: find a sentence that’s in the wrong place and put it where it hits hardest.","nowTry":"Write the one sentence you cut and why. Then write the sentence you moved, and where it lives now.","rwm":"“Gilbert sprang into his skiff with an angry color in his cheeks.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch27-28.png"},"words":[{"w":"vanity","d":"Too much pride in how one looks; caring too much about appearance.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"implicitly","d":"Without questioning; trusting completely.","e":"👂"},{"w":"epoch","d":"A memorable period of time; a turning point.","e":"🌟"}]},{"w":12,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 12.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 29)","file":"lesson-12-3-anne-ch29.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-12-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 12.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 29). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Miss Barry’s welcome","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-welcome","done":false,"script":"Josephine Barry. I have had two girls from Avonlea in my house this week, which is two more than usual. Your question asks what Anne discovers about herself in town. I asked her very nearly the same thing myself, and she made me wait for the answer. First, some word work, where you build the words yourself. Then the chapter. Then three sentences. Not four."},{"id":"dol","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is about me, and quite right too. I did keep them there all day. Count the capitals in it. A day of the week, a title, a name, and a show grand enough to deserve one. The bottom sentence is about the concert, and it has run two thoughts together, as young people do. Put a stop between them. And give the girl her capital."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Miss Barry · before the word work","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-guide1","done":false,"script":"Building, today. You type every answer yourself, and there is nothing to choose from. A missing root, a missing word, a doing word put into the past, and a piece added onto the end. When I was young we did this sort of thing on a slate with no help whatever. You have a box that checks your work. Say each one aloud first. Then type it."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Miss Barry · about the word parts","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Four words, and each has lost its back half. The front is there, and the meaning is there, so you are not entirely abandoned. Read each meaning slowly. Then ask yourself one question. Is something being carried here, or is something being pulled? There are only two roots to choose from. At my time of life, I find most questions come down to two choices."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s words","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Three sentences, three blanks, and four words to choose from. One word is left over, which is perfectly in order. Read each sentence to the end before you choose. Then say it aloud with your word in the blank. If it sounds like something a sensible person would say, keep it. If it sounds like nonsense, I dare say it is."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"Four sentences, told as though they were happening now. Put each doing word into the past, where it belongs. None of them takes the usual ending, so do not try. Say yesterday in front of it, and listen. Your ear will tell you, if you let it. Then the box at the end asks for two sentences of your own about this chapter. Make them good ones."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"Four words, and a piece to add to each. Two rules at once. First, does the word end with a soft sound before its quiet e? Then, what letter does the new piece begin with? Both answers decide whether the e stays. Write each one out in full before you check. Carelessness in spelling is like carelessness in anything. It shows."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Miss Barry · before you open the book","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"There is a box here about learning from one’s mistakes. I have made a few myself, though I seldom admit it. Once I was very angry with two little girls for jumping on my bed. I meant to go home the very next day. Then one of them came to confess, and I unpacked. Watch for the moment Anne is glad about something she would once have sulked over. I was there, and I noticed."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box says most chapters lean on one moment. Find it, and the rest falls into place. Anne will tell you a great deal about horses, and balloons, and ice cream at eleven o’clock at night. Not all of it matters equally. As you read, ask which moment the chapter would collapse without. I know which one I would choose. Whether she would choose the same, I cannot say."},{"id":"read","label":"Miss Barry · before you read","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes when Anne gives her verdict on town life. I laughed, I am afraid. Think about what her answer tells you about her. The second comes at the very end, after she has left my house. I was not there for it, so you must tell me. Which part do you think she will remember longest?"},{"id":"rwm","label":"Miss Barry · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-rwm","done":false,"script":"I was not in that lane, of course. I do not keep cows. But that sentence tells me more about the Anne-girl than an hour of her conversation. The cows plod along. She dreams. And her head is full of a battle. Three things, and not one word about the weather. When you write yours, choose your three as carefully, and leave the sunset out."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Miss Barry · before you write","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now you write, and I shall be particular. Say what happened plainly. If you are asked why, give a reason, and point to the line in the book that proves it. Do not decorate. I have read a great many stories by Avonlea girls, and the decoration was never the best part. The best part was always the one true thing in the middle."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Miss Barry · about the questions","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question asks for two things the girls did in town. I took them to most of them, so choose well. The second asks why the concert moved Anne so. She told me she did not see how she could ever go back to common life. I suggested ice cream. The third asks what she decided about town and country. Use her words, not mine."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Miss Barry · about today’s writing","path":"guide/josephine/g4ela-12-3-workshop","done":false,"script":"Today you swap. A dull doing word for a lively one. A line anybody might say for one only that person would say. And one small action that belongs to your character and nobody else. The Anne-girl kissed my wrinkled cheek on the veranda. Nobody else would have dared. Diana certainly did not. That is the sort of small thing that tells you exactly who someone is."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Find the turning point, the one moment the chapter leans on. Then say what led up to it and what it changed.","wordParts":"port means “to carry” (transport, portable, export). tract means “to pull” or “to draw” (attract, distract, extract).","grammar":"Irregular past-tense verbs don’t just add -ed. Some change a vowel (sing → sang), some turn into a new word (go → went), and some don’t change at all (put → put).","spelling":"After ce or ge, keep the silent e when the suffix starts with a or o, so the c or g stays soft (courageous, manageable). Drop it when the suffix starts with e or i (noticing, changing).","dol":[{"raw":"anne saw the prima donna sing she felt enraptured by the music","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> saw the prima donna <strong>sing.</strong> <strong>She</strong> felt enraptured by the <strong>music.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period after sing to close the first thought, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Saw is the past of see; felt is the past of feel."},{"raw":"miss barry took the girls to a restaurant they ate ice cream at eleven o’clock","fixed":"<strong>Miss Barry</strong> took the girls to a <strong>restaurant.</strong> <strong>They</strong> ate ice cream at eleven <strong>o’clock.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: capitals on Miss and Barry, a period after restaurant, a capital T to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Took and ate are both irregular past-tense verbs."}],"pauses":["Ch. 29 is called An Epoch in Anne’s Life — an epoch is a moment things turn on. Find that moment. Anne goes to the city and finds she likes ice cream at eleven o’clock at night, and also that she would rather sleep in the east gable at home. Which of those is the turning point, and which one just leads up to it?","Anne discovers she likes ice cream at eleven o’clock at night in the city — but she’d rather sleep in the east gable at home. What does her conclusion show about how she has grown?","The visit is over and Anne is back in the east gable with all of it behind her. Which part do you think she will remember longest — the Exhibition, or the coming home?"],"chapterQs":["Aunt Josephine invites Anne and Diana to Charlottetown for the Exhibition. Name TWO specific things Anne and Diana do during the trip.","At the prima donna concert, Anne is “in enraptured silence.” Why does this concert affect Anne so much? How does it change how she thinks about being good?","At the end of the trip, Anne realizes something about herself. What does she decide about city life vs. life at Green Gables?"],"focus":"SUBSTITUTE means swap something weak for something true. Three swaps today: one boring verb for a strong one, one line of dialogue for what that person would really say, and one small action that only your kid would do.","nowTry":"Write your three swaps: the verb (before → after), the line of dialogue (before → after), and the small action you added that only your kid would do.","rwm":"“The cows swung placidly down the lane, and Anne followed them dreamily, repeating aloud the battle canto from Marmion.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch29.png"},"words":[{"w":"vanity","d":"Too much pride in how one looks; caring too much about appearance.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"implicitly","d":"Without questioning; trusting completely.","e":"👂"},{"w":"epoch","d":"A memorable period of time; a turning point.","e":"🌟"}]},{"w":12,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 12.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 30)","file":"lesson-12-4-anne-ch30.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-12-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 12.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 30). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Miss Stacy’s welcome","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-welcome","done":false,"script":"I’m Miss Stacy. This chapter begins with a visit I paid to Green Gables, and it ends on the last day of a school year. Your question is about something Anne loses, and I’ll be honest, I can’t tell you what it is. A teacher sees a great deal, but not everything. First there’s word work, and some of it is already correct. Then read, and watch closely."},{"id":"dol","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is about me, and I remember that day well. Look at what it does right. I count four things, and one of them is a comma doing a quiet job. Find all four before you tap. The bottom sentence runs two thoughts together. Find where the first one ends. Then look for a word that names a language. It wants a capital, and so does the girl."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Miss Stacy · before the word work","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-guide1","done":false,"script":"Review day, and two of these are traps. In the grammar and the spelling, some words are right and some are wrong, and tapping a right one costs you. So slow down. Read each word as if you were marking a composition. Then the vocabulary gives you a choice of what to write. Choose the one that will make you think hardest. That’s usually the one worth doing."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Miss Stacy · about the word parts","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Matching once more. Two roots, two front parts, and four meanings to pair them with. You’ve had a week with these, so trust yourself. If one won’t come, think of a whole word with that part inside and say it aloud. On our field afternoons I never let anyone name a fern until they had looked at it properly. Look first, then tap."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s words","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"The last day for this week’s four words, and you choose how to show you know them. You can write sentences, explain the parts, hunt for a word in today’s chapter that comes close, or teach one to someone younger. I like the teaching one best. You never really know a word until you’ve had to explain it to a small person with a great many questions."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"A short draft, and some of the doing words are wrong. Not all of them. Tap only the ones a careful writer would fix, and leave the good ones alone. Say each sentence aloud and listen for the word that makes you wince. Then, in the box, tell me what the writer was reaching for. Mistakes usually make sense from the inside. That’s what makes them worth studying."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six words, and some are spelled wrongly. Tap only those. For each one, ask whether the quiet e is doing its job. Is it there when the sound needs it? Is it missing when the next letter can do the work instead? Then, in the box, mend one and tell me what would happen to the sound without that e. Say it both ways. You’ll hear it."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Miss Stacy · before you open the book","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about learning from mistakes. I see a good many in a schoolroom, and I’ve come to think the mistake matters less than what happens after. When a pupil tells me the truth about one, I forgive it, and we don’t speak of it again. As you read, watch what Anne carries with her this year, and what she finds she has let go."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box asks you to zoom out. Not one chapter, but the whole week, in two sentences. That means most of what happened has to go. I ask my older pupils to do the same with a year of history, and it hurts at first. Keep only what still matters at the end. If a moment changed nothing later, let it go, however much you enjoyed it."},{"id":"read","label":"Miss Stacy · before you read","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-read","done":false,"script":"One stop, at the very end. By then the year is over, and something has changed in Anne that I didn’t see. Nobody did, I think. Think about what it costs her, and why she keeps it to herself. Then think about what it tells you about how she’s growing up. You’ll know more about it than I do. That’s the privilege of a reader."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Miss Stacy · before you write","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now it’s your turn, and I’ll tell you what I tell my Queen’s class: don’t tell me, show me. For the first two questions, stay inside this chapter and put your finger on the line. The last one looks back across the whole week, and that takes a steadier hand. Anne would pick five mistakes and follow all of them. Pick one, and follow it to what it taught."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Miss Stacy · about the questions","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question asks what my new class is for, and who joined it. Count them all, and notice who isn’t there. The second asks about Anne and Gilbert. I can tell you they work very hard to beat each other. The rest the book knows, and I don’t. The third asks you to look back over the whole week and choose one mistake."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Miss Stacy · about today’s writing","path":"guide/stacy/g4ela-12-4-workshop","done":false,"script":"Today, don’t fix anything. Read your whole story from the first line to the last, the way a stranger would. Then answer two questions. Which part is strongest, and which part still needs work? Name the scene. Name the sentence. Anne takes stock in this chapter of where she is and where she’s going. At the end of a year, so should a writer."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"To sum up several chapters, zoom out. Keep only what still matters by the end, and say the whole stretch in two sentences.","wordParts":"port means “to carry” (transport, portable, export). tract means “to pull” or “to draw” (attract, distract, extract).","grammar":"Irregular past-tense verbs don’t just add -ed. Some change a vowel (sing → sang), some turn into a new word (go → went), and some don’t change at all (put → put).","spelling":"After ce or ge, keep the silent e when the suffix starts with a or o, so the c or g stays soft (courageous, manageable). Drop it when the suffix starts with e or i (noticing, changing).","dol":[{"raw":"anne began to study latin she taught herself extra words at night","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> began to study <strong>Latin.</strong> <strong>She</strong> taught herself extra words at <strong>night.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a capital L on Latin because it names a language, a period to close the first thought, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Began and taught are irregular past-tense verbs."},{"raw":"diana went home alone anne hid her tears behind her latin grammar","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> went home <strong>alone.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> hid her tears behind her <strong>Latin</strong> <strong>grammar.</strong>","why":"The same pattern: capitals on Diana and Anne, a capital L on Latin, a period after alone, and a period at the end. Went and hid are irregular past-tense verbs."}],"pauses":["A tiny accident—snow from a crow—changes the speaker’s whole day. Anne says each of her mistakes cured her of something: the brooch, the hair dye, the liniment cake. Can a mistake be a gift? Pick one of Anne’s and explain what it “cured.”","Ch. 30 is called The Queen’s Class Is Organized , and in it Anne notices her old anger at Gilbert is gone. Now zoom out. Think back over this whole week of reading and ask which few things still matter now. Can you sum up the week in two sentences without listing every scrape along the way?","Anne realizes her old anger at Gilbert has “gone just when she most needed its sustaining power.” What does this realization cost Anne — and what does it show about how she is growing up?"],"chapterQs":["Miss Stacy organizes a special class. What is the class for, and which students join it?","Anne realizes something about her old anger at Gilbert. What has happened to it, and why does she keep this realization a secret?","Looking back across Ch. 26-30: name ONE mistake Anne has made this week, and what she has learned from it."],"focus":"Your draft has been through Add, Remove, Move and Substitute. Today, read it start to finish without stopping to fix anything. Then answer two questions: what’s the strongest part, and what still needs work.","nowTry":"Read your whole draft. Write 2–3 sentences: what is the strongest part (name the scene and a sentence), and what still needs work (name the scene and why)?","plate":"plates/anne-ch30.png"},"words":[{"w":"vanity","d":"Too much pride in how one looks; caring too much about appearance.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"implicitly","d":"Without questioning; trusting completely.","e":"👂"},{"w":"epoch","d":"A memorable period of time; a turning point.","e":"🌟"}]},{"w":12,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 12 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-12-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-12-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"vanity","d":"Too much pride in how one looks; caring too much about appearance.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"implicitly","d":"Without questioning; trusting completely.","e":"👂"},{"w":"epoch","d":"A memorable period of time; a turning point.","e":"🌟"}]},{"w":13,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 13.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 31)","file":"lesson-13-1-anne-ch31.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-13-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 13.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 31). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify stated and implied theme. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-welcome","done":false,"script":"It’s Marilla. That child grew two inches in one summer, and somewhere along the way she stopped chattering. I can’t say I expected to miss it. The question is how a girl can change and stay the same at once. First there are verbs that do nothing at all but join, and words that compare. Then the chapter. Watch what Anne says out loud, and what she only shows."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Marilla · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box talks about the big idea of a story. Anne says hers right out loud in this chapter, and she says it to me. That’s the easy one. The harder one nobody says. Watch what she chooses, and how it turns out, and what the title is a picture of. A brook doesn’t ask permission to become a river. It simply does."},{"id":"dol","label":"Marilla · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is Miss Stacy’s opinion, by way of Anne, and I happen to agree with it. Short words are stronger. Find the joining word, and the two that compare. The bottom one is two sentences run together, and somebody’s name has lost its capital. I made those dresses myself, so you’ll forgive me for wanting it written properly."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four pieces of word work, and two of them ask you to sort into three piles, not two. That third pile is where the thinking is. Don’t push a word into a pile because it looks like its neighbor. Say it in a sentence first. I sort the mending the same way: what’s finished, what isn’t, and what I’m not sure of yet."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Marilla · about the word parts","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-morphology","done":false,"script":"Two old roots today, one that means to look and one that means to build. They don’t stand on their own. They sit inside other words the way the yeast sits inside the bread. You never see it, and you’d notice fast if it weren’t there. When a blank stumps you, ask whether the word is about seeing or about making. That will tell you which root belongs there."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Marilla · about today’s words","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Four words, and two of them describe Miss Stacy. Mrs. Lynde has her doubts about Miss Stacy, which is reason enough to learn them. Read each sentence before you turn the card. Then, when you match, try each word against a person you know. If it fits nobody at all, you’ve likely got the wrong meaning."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Marilla · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-grammar","done":false,"script":"Some verbs do something. Some only join a person to a description and do no work at all, rather like certain neighbors I could name. A few go either way, depending on the sentence. For those, put each one in two sentences of your own and see whether it’s doing a job or just standing there. The box at the bottom has a test. Use it."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Marilla · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-spelling","done":false,"script":"Comparing words. Some take a short ending, some need more or most in front, and some have a y that must change first. Count the beats in the plain word before you decide. Clap them if you must; I won’t tell anyone. Anne used to pile on the longest words she could find. These days she counts more carefully, and so should you."},{"id":"copia","label":"Marilla · about saying it three ways","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-copia","done":false,"script":"Here’s one plain sentence about a girl and some books. You’re to say it three ways: add a time, change the doing word, then stretch it out. Anne used to stretch every sentence until it snapped. Miss Stacy taught her better. Try all three, then ask which says the most with the least. That’s the one a sensible person would keep."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about noticing your own change. Anne noticed hers, and said so. I noticed it too, standing beside her one day and finding I had to look up. I’ll not say what I did about it that night. Matthew caught me at it, and that’s more than enough witnesses. Read, and watch who sees the change, and who only feels it."},{"id":"read","label":"Marilla · before you read","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes when she tells me what she’s been worrying over, on Sunday afternoons of all times. Listen for why growing up feels serious to her, not just that it does. The second comes near the end, and it’s about me. I’d rather you didn’t dwell on it. You will anyway. Keep an eye on how much she talks. Then on how much she doesn’t."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Here’s where you write it down. I’ve no patience for answers that wander. Read the question, decide what it wants, and give it that, with something from the book to show you didn’t make it up. Anne is learning to be her own sharpest critic. Read over what you write once before you call it done. You’ll find something. Everybody does."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Marilla · about the questions","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about the doctor’s message. I took it seriously, and I’d do it again. The second wants two changes in her, one you could measure with a tape and one you’d only hear. The third is about me, and that night. Be fair to me. I’ll own I had a cry, and Matthew caught me at it. The book doesn’t say I was wrong."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Marilla · about today’s writing","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-13-1-workshop","done":false,"script":"Editing. This is the part of writing I understand best. It’s like going over a floor with a lamp after you’ve swept it: you find what you missed. Today it’s a capital at the start and a stop at the end of every sentence. Read one line at a time, slowly, with your finger under it. Slow is how you catch things. Fast is how you miss them."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A theme is the big idea a story teaches about life. Sometimes a character says it out loud; sometimes you work it out from what happens.","wordParts":"spect means “to look” (inspect, respect). struct means “to build” (construct, instruct).","grammar":"A linking verb joins the subject to a word that describes or renames it: Anne was happy. Some verbs, like felt and looked, can do either job. The test: can you swap in was?","spelling":"One syllable: add -er or -est (small, smallest). Three or more syllables: use more or most (most difficult). Two syllables ending in y: change the y to i, then add the ending (funny, funnier).","dol":[{"raw":"anne grew two inches her dresses seemed too short by autumn","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> grew two <strong>inches.</strong> <strong>Her</strong> dresses seemed too short by <strong>autumn.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period after inches, a capital H to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Seemed is a linking verb."},{"raw":"marilla looked up at anne the girl was taller than she was","fixed":"<strong>Marilla</strong> looked up at <strong>Anne.</strong> <strong>The</strong> girl was taller than she <strong>was.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Marilla and Anne, a period after Anne, a capital T to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Was links the girl to taller."}],"pauses":["Ch. 31 is called Where the Brook and River Meet , and Anne says it is a serious thing to grow up. That is a theme said out loud. What else in this chapter shows the same idea without saying it? What does the chapter title hint at?","Anne grows two inches and stops chattering as much. She also says, “It’s a serious thing to grow up.” What part of growing up feels serious to her? What is she having to think about that she didn’t before?","Marilla notices the change in Anne too, and one night it makes her cry. Why would a change that is good still make someone sad?"],"chapterQs":["The Spencervale doctor sends Marilla a message about Anne after seeing her one afternoon. What does the doctor say, and what does Marilla do because of it?","By the end of Ch. 31, Anne has changed in two ways — one outside, one inside. Name TWO specific changes in Anne, one physical and one in how she talks or thinks.","Marilla cries one night thinking about Anne. Why is Marilla crying? What does she realize?"],"focus":"Editing is the small stuff, and it’s slow work: you read line by line. This week you use CUPS — Capitals, Usage, Punctuation, Spelling. Today is C and the end of P: every sentence starts with a capital and ends with a mark.","nowTry":"EDIT pass. Read your whole draft slowly, line by line. Fix every missing capital and every missing end mark in My Story. Then list three fixes you made here.","plate":"plates/anne-ch31.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"broadminded","d":"Open to new ideas; willing to consider different points of view.","e":"🌈"},{"w":"tactful","d":"Careful not to hurt other people’s feelings; thoughtful in dealing with people.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":13,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 13.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 32)","file":"lesson-13-2-anne-ch32.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-13-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 13.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 32). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify stated and implied theme. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Diana’s welcome","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-welcome","done":false,"script":"Hello, it’s Diana! This is the chapter where the pass list comes out, and I simply can’t keep still. The question is whose pride matters most to Anne when she sees her name. First there’s word work: find the verbs that only join, and the comparing words that went wrong. Then read it for yourself. I promised I wouldn’t spoil it, and I won’t. Nearly."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Diana · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box says one word isn’t enough, and I think that’s perfectly fair. If somebody asked me what this chapter is about, I’d say success and be done. But that’s only the topic. What does the chapter say success is for? Watch who Anne thinks of first. I know who I’d run to. I ran to Anne, didn’t I?"},{"id":"dol","label":"Diana · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-dol","done":false,"script":"Anne said the top sentence the minute she got home from town, and I agree with every word, except perhaps for Orchard Slope. Find the joining word, and the two that mean the very most. The bottom sentence has two names wanting capitals, and two thoughts squeezed together with nothing between them. Give them each a little room."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Diana · before the word work","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four bits of word work, and two of them are tapping games with more than one right answer hiding. Don’t stop at the first one you find. That’s what I always do, and then I miss the rest and have to start over. Take each sentence one word at a time. Miss Stacy says slow and careful wins, and she’s generally right."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Diana · about the word parts","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"A matching game with yesterday’s two roots and two new little front parts. I always mix up the one that means in with the one that means down or away. So I say a real word out loud first, one I know, and listen to what it means. Try it. It’s ever so much easier than staring."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Diana · about today’s words","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"The same four words as yesterday, but no cards this time, so it’s all up to you. If you get stuck, picture somebody you know who fits the meaning. Jane Andrews fits one of them perfectly. Nothing rattles her. If nobody you know fits, try another meaning. It’s like choosing a dress, really."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Diana · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"Two sentences, and the joining verbs are hiding in with the doing ones, the way I hide behind Anne when Josie Pye comes along. There’s more than one. I always find the first and go skipping off, and then I have to come back. Ask of each verb, could I watch somebody do it? If not, try putting was in its place and see if the sentence still holds."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Diana · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"This one’s a bit of a trick. Most of the comparing words are right, and you’re only to tap the ones that are made wrong. Say each one out loud. If it sounds like something a very little child would say, it probably is wrong. Count the beats in the plain word, too. That always tells."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Diana · before you open the book","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about who you want to tell first when something good happens. I know who Anne told, because I was there, running behind her across the yard. I never once thought she’d do anything else. I’d have done the very same thing, only with more shrieking. Read, and watch where her feet take her."},{"id":"read","label":"Diana · before you read","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-read","done":false,"script":"Most of this chapter is waiting, and Anne’s letter to me from town, which I’ve read at least eleven times. Notice how frightened she is, and how she still manages to cheer up Moody Spurgeon. Then the pass list. There’s one stop, at the end. Before you get there, keep asking why she wanted it so badly. It wasn’t only Gilbert."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Diana · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-rwm","done":false,"script":"That sentence is my favorite in the whole chapter. Look how it names two people and then goes back for one of them, as if it couldn’t help it. That little dash is doing such a lot. When you write yours, name two people, then pick one out on purpose. Your reader will know at once who matters most."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Diana · before you write","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now the writing part. I always want to put everything in, every lovely little thing, and Miss Stacy always crosses most of it out. Answer just what the question asks, and show where in the chapter you found it. If you use somebody’s exact words, put them in quotation marks. Then read it over once before you say it’s finished."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Diana · about the questions","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about the very words I said when I burst in, so do look at them closely. I was rather out of breath. The second asks about Anne’s other reason for working so hard, the nobler one. The third is about Matthew. He hardly ever says anything, so when he does, it’s worth listening to."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Diana · about today’s writing","path":"guide/diana/g4ela-13-2-workshop","done":false,"script":"Dialogue today, which is my favorite part of anybody’s story. Read every line out loud, the way I read Anne’s letter. Quotation marks around the words people say, a comma before the part that tells who said it, and a capital to start. If a line sounds like nobody would ever say it, change the words too, not just the marks."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A topic is one word; a theme is a whole sentence. Success is a topic. What the book says about success is the theme.","wordParts":"spect means “to look” (inspect, respect). struct means “to build” (construct, instruct).","grammar":"A linking verb joins the subject to a word that describes or renames it: Anne was happy. Some verbs, like felt and looked, can do either job. The test: can you swap in was?","spelling":"One syllable: add -er or -est (small, smallest). Three or more syllables: use more or most (most difficult). Two syllables ending in y: change the y to i, then add the ending (funny, funnier).","dol":[{"raw":"matthew was proud of anne his eyes were filled with joy","fixed":"<strong>Matthew</strong> was proud of <strong>Anne.</strong> <strong>His</strong> eyes were filled with <strong>joy.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Matthew and Anne, a period after Anne, a capital H to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Was and were are linking verbs."},{"raw":"diana was breathless she had run all the way","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> was <strong>breathless.</strong> <strong>She</strong> had run all the <strong>way.</strong>","why":"A capital on Diana, a period after breathless, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Was links Diana to breathless."}],"pauses":["Ch. 32 is called The Pass List Is Out , and Anne ties with Gilbert for first place. The topic is easy — success. The theme is harder. Anne’s very first thought is to tell Matthew. What is this chapter saying about what success is actually for ? Say it in a full sentence.","The shadow grows and shrinks in surprising ways—not like “proper children.” Anne’s growing up isn’t smooth either. How has Anne changed in ways that surprised you?","Anne ties with Gilbert for FIRST place on the pass list. Her first thought is to tell Matthew. Why is his pride more important to her than the medal itself?"],"chapterQs":["The pass list is published in the Charlottetown newspaper. Where does Anne’s name appear, and what does this mean? Use a detail from the chapter.","Anne studied hard not just to beat Gilbert. What is her “other and nobler motive” for wanting to do well? Quote a specific line if you can.","When Anne tells Matthew the news, he says, “I knew you could beat them all easy.” What does this tell you about how Matthew sees Anne — and why is his pride so important to her?"],"focus":"Dialogue has its own rules, and readers notice when they’re broken. Quotation marks around the words. A comma before the tag. A capital letter starting the speech. Read every line of dialogue out loud today.","nowTry":"Read every line of dialogue in your draft out loud. Find ONE line that doesn’t sound right or isn’t punctuated right. Write it before and after. Then check: quotation marks, comma before the tag, capital on the speech.","rwm":"“She wanted to ‘pass high’ for the sake of Matthew and Marilla — especially Matthew.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch32.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"broadminded","d":"Open to new ideas; willing to consider different points of view.","e":"🌈"},{"w":"tactful","d":"Careful not to hurt other people’s feelings; thoughtful in dealing with people.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":13,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 13.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 33)","file":"lesson-13-3-anne-ch33.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-13-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 13.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 33). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Gilbert’s welcome","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-welcome","done":false,"script":"Hi, it’s Gilbert. Today’s question is about one small moment at a concert, and what it shows about how Anne has grown. I was in the room for that moment, as it happens. First, some word work: building comparing words and filling in joining verbs. Then read the chapter, and decide for yourself what happened on that stage. I’ve got my own idea."},{"id":"dol","label":"Gilbert · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is from the book, and from where I sat, it was awful true. Find the joining verb, and what it joins Anne to. The bottom one is two sentences that ran into each other. Split them, and give everybody their capitals, Anne included. She’d want hers. She’s particular about that sort of thing, and about how her name is spelled."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Gilbert · before the word work","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four pieces of word work, and most of them ask you to build something instead of just picking. That’s harder, but it’s the kind of hard that sticks. If a blank stumps you, say the whole sentence out loud. Your ear knows more than you think. Mine has gotten me out of a few tight spots at the blackboard, anyhow."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Gilbert · about the word parts","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"These blanks are all over the place: in the middle of a word, at the end, even at the very front. So read the whole line before you type. Most of them ask whether the word is about looking or about building. A crowd at a ball game is doing one of those. A barn raising is doing the other. The odd one out wants the ending for a person who does it."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Gilbert · about today’s words","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Three sentences, three blanks, and four words in the bank, so one gets left over. Read each sentence all the way through before you choose; the end of a sentence usually tells you more than the start. I’ll own up that one of these words fits me pretty well. I won’t say which. Anne would say it was the wrong one."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Gilbert · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"Four blanks and four joining verbs, and each one gets used once. They’re close cousins, so try every choice in every blank and listen hard. Read the whole sentence, not just the words around the gap. Sometimes the end of it settles the matter, like a but halfway through that changes everything."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Gilbert · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"Four comparing words to build, and two decisions every time: how many beats the plain word has, and whether there’s a y at the end that needs changing first. Do them in that order. Anne and I have spelled each other down more times than I can count. Whoever checks the second thing usually wins."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Gilbert · before you open the book","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about deciding what counts as rich. I wasn’t in the buggy going home, so I can’t tell you what was said there. But I saw that hotel, all lit up and dripping with diamonds, and I saw Anne in a plain white dress. Read, and watch what she decides is worth counting. I’d bet it isn’t the diamonds."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Gilbert · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box says some big ideas are never said out loud. That’s true at concerts, too. Nobody stands up and tells you what a moment meant; you have to work it out. So watch what Anne chooses, and what happens right after. Then write the sentence nobody in the chapter says. Don’t look to me for it. I was only smiling."},{"id":"read","label":"Gilbert · before you read","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes when Anne is up on that platform and can’t get a word out. Think about what changes for her, and what gets her started. The second stop comes on the drive home, after the concert. Listen to what she says she’s rich in, and ask whether she means it. I think she does. Honest."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Gilbert · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-rwm","done":false,"script":"I like this sentence because Anne doesn’t argue. She just says it, flat out, and then backs it up with three things nobody can take away from her. Look at the one word that tells you how she said it. Take that away and she sounds like she’s guessing. When you write yours, choose your how-word carefully. Then give three reasons. Three’s a strong number."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Gilbert · before you write","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now you write. Here’s how I’d go about it. Read the question twice, then find the spot in the chapter where the answer lives before you write a word. When a question asks for an exact moment, quote it. If you’re guessing why someone did something, say what in the book made you guess. Anne calls that being dull. I call it being right."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Gilbert · about the questions","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about Anne’s dress and her beads, next to all those city jewels. The second asks what she sees from the stage, and wants you to quote it. I’ll admit I’m curious what you’ll say about that one. The third is about the drive home, where Jane wants diamonds and Anne doesn’t."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Gilbert · about today’s writing","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-13-3-workshop","done":false,"script":"Read slowly today, slower than feels right. You’re hunting three things: the wrong word that sounds like the right one, a spelling you wouldn’t bet a nickel on, and a sentence that ought to be two. When I’m not sure of a word, I look it up. There’s no shame in that. The shame is leaving it wrong when you could have checked."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Some big ideas are never said out loud. Look at what a character chooses and what the story rewards, then write the sentence the book never wrote.","wordParts":"spect means “to look” (inspect, respect). struct means “to build” (construct, instruct).","grammar":"A linking verb joins the subject to a word that describes or renames it: Anne was happy. Some verbs, like felt and looked, can do either job. The test: can you swap in was?","spelling":"One syllable: add -er or -est (small, smallest). Three or more syllables: use more or most (most difficult). Two syllables ending in y: change the y to i, then add the ending (funny, funnier).","dol":[{"raw":"the audience seemed unfriendly anne was afraid for a moment","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> audience seemed <strong>unfriendly.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> was afraid for a <strong>moment.</strong>","why":"A capital T to start, a period after unfriendly, a capital on Anne to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Seemed and was are both linking verbs."},{"raw":"gilbert was at the back of the room he looked happy","fixed":"<strong>Gilbert</strong> was at the back of the <strong>room.</strong> <strong>He</strong> looked <strong>happy.</strong>","why":"A capital on Gilbert, a period after room, a capital H to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Looked links he to happy."}],"pauses":["In Ch. 33, The Hotel Concert , Anne is frozen with stage fright until she sees Gilbert smiling at the back of the room. Nobody in the chapter stops to tell you what that means. So say it yourself: what big idea is this chapter teaching without ever announcing it? What in the chapter made you think so?","Anne is frozen with stage fright. Then she sees Gilbert smiling at the back of the room. How does this small moment change Anne’s state? What does her reaction reveal about how she has grown?","After the concert, Anne tells her friends they are rich already, counting years and happiness and imagination instead of money. Do you think Anne believes that, or is she talking herself into it?"],"chapterQs":["Anne is invited to recite at the White Sands Hotel concert. What does Anne think about her dress and her pearl beads compared to the other women’s clothes and jewels?","Anne suffers a terrible attack of stage fright. What does she see that gives her the courage to start? Quote the specific moment from the chapter.","On the way home, Anne tells Jane and Diana that they are “rich” even without diamonds. What does Anne mean? How does her view differ from Jane’s?"],"focus":"Usage means the right word: their / there / they’re, two / too / to, your / you’re. Spelling means every word looked up if you’re not sure. And run-ons: a sentence that should be two, split it.","nowTry":"Grammar and spelling pass. Find ONE run-on in your draft and split it. Find ONE usage or spelling fix. Write the corrected versions here.","rwm":"“‘We are rich,’ said Anne staunchly. ‘Why, we have sixteen years to our credit, and we’re happy as queens, and we’ve all got imaginations, more or less.’”","plate":"plates/anne-ch33.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"broadminded","d":"Open to new ideas; willing to consider different points of view.","e":"🌈"},{"w":"tactful","d":"Careful not to hurt other people’s feelings; thoughtful in dealing with people.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":13,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 13.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 34)","file":"lesson-13-4-anne-ch34.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-13-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 13.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 34). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Anne’s welcome","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-welcome","done":false,"script":"Hello, it’s Anne, and today’s question is about me, which feels very strange to say out loud. Who am I really, when everything is changing? I’ll try not to make a speech of it. First there’s word work, and you’ll be hunting for mistakes. Then you read about the day I leave Green Gables. Then you decide what stays the same. I’m still deciding myself."},{"id":"dol","label":"Anne · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is about me, and I wish it weren’t quite so true. Lonesomer. Isn’t that a lovely, sad word? Find the joining verb, and the word that compares. The bottom sentence needs a stop in the middle and two capitals, one of them mine. I’d be grateful if you gave it back. A girl’s name ought to stand up straight, even when she’s homesick."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Anne · before the word work","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-guide1","done":false,"script":"This is the last day of word work this week, and two of the four ask you to catch a mistake. Here’s a secret I learned at the Entrance: some of the sentences are perfectly right, and tapping those costs you. So don’t tap because you’re nervous. Tap because you’re sure. I wish someone had told me that about geometry."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Anne · about the word parts","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Matching again: the two roots and the two little front parts. I adore roots. A word that means to look can grow into something grand to stare at, and a word that means to build can end up meaning to tear down, depending on what you put in front. Say a word you know aloud before you match. Then ask which part is doing the work."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Anne · about today’s words","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"There’s a little menu of ways to show what you know, and you only pick one. I would want to do all five, but I’m learning that choosing is a skill too. Pick the one you could do best, not the one that sounds grandest. I’ve learned that the hard way. Then use the words as if they were your own. They are, now."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Anne · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"Six sentences, each telling you what kind of verb something is, and some of them are fibbing, which I used to do about geometry. Only tap the fibs. The test is simple: put was where the verb is and see whether the sentence still makes sense. I used to think every verb was full of action. Most of mine were, I suppose."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Anne · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six comparing words, and some are made all wrong, with too many endings piled on, or more stuck in front of a word that already has an ending. I used to pile on extra words exactly like that, to sound grand. Now I try to say a thing once, properly. Tap only the ones that say it twice, or say it wrong."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Anne · before you open the book","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about knowing who you are. It sounds like something you’d find out all at once, like a fairy in a dell. It isn’t, I’ve discovered. It comes in little bits, and one of them came on Marilla’s lap, in the kitchen, in my new green dress. Read, and watch what I tell her. Then decide whether I was right."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Anne · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box says a big idea has to hold up twice. I like that. It’s like trying on a dress in front of two different mirrors. So when you think you’ve found what this week is really about, take it back to another chapter and see if it still fits. If it doesn’t, it was only one page’s idea. Keep going until it fits both."},{"id":"read","label":"Anne · before you read","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-read","done":false,"script":"This chapter has a leaving in it, and a lonely little room, and Josie Pye, which is a very odd mixture. There’s one stop, at the end. Before you get there, watch what I hold onto and what I let go. And watch the last page, because something happens there that makes everything feel enormous again. I won’t say what. I’m trying to be less dramatic."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Anne · before you write","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now the writing. I used to answer every question with three paragraphs and a poem. Miss Stacy taught me that the short way is stronger. So answer what’s asked, in plain words, and prove it with something from the book. If you quote me, quote me exactly. I chose those words very carefully, even the little ones."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Anne · about the questions","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question wants what I told Marilla, with the picture of the tree in it. The second is about the scholarship, and why I went to bed with my cheeks tingling. The third asks you to look back over the whole week. That one’s the hardest, and the best. Look for what I say out loud, and what you can only see."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Anne · about today’s writing","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-13-4-workshop","done":false,"script":"The last edit is your ears. Read your whole story out loud, slowly, the way you’d recite at a concert, and wherever you stumble, something is wrong. Fix it. Then say what you’re proud of. That part felt like boasting to me at first. It isn’t. Knowing what you did well is how you do it again."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A theme has to hold up more than once. Test your big idea on a second scene; if it doesn’t fit, change it until it fits both.","wordParts":"spect means “to look” (inspect, respect). struct means “to build” (construct, instruct).","grammar":"A linking verb joins the subject to a word that describes or renames it: Anne was happy. Some verbs, like felt and looked, can do either job. The test: can you swap in was?","spelling":"One syllable: add -er or -est (small, smallest). Three or more syllables: use more or most (most difficult). Two syllables ending in y: change the y to i, then add the ending (funny, funnier).","dol":[{"raw":"anne was homesick on her first night her room felt strange and small","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> was homesick on her first <strong>night.</strong> <strong>Her</strong> room felt strange and <strong>small.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period after night, a capital H to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Was and felt are linking verbs."},{"raw":"josie pye was no comfort she talked about herself","fixed":"<strong>Josie Pye</strong> was no <strong>comfort.</strong> <strong>She</strong> talked about <strong>herself.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Josie Pye, a period after comfort, a capital S to start the next sentence, and a period at the end. Was is a linking verb; talked is an action verb."}],"pauses":["Dickinson says we don’t know how tall we can stand until we have to. Anne tells Marilla she is “pruned down and branched out”—the same girl, just taller. What “called Anne to rise,” and how did she grow to meet it?","Ch. 34 is called A Queen’s Girl . The night before she leaves, Anne tells Marilla that the real her has not changed. That is a theme said out loud. Now test it. Look back at another moment from this week and ask whether the same idea holds there too. Does your sentence survive the second scene, or does it need fixing?","On the night before Anne leaves, she sits on Marilla’s lap and says, “The real me — back here — is just the same.” What is she trying to assure Marilla — and herself — of?"],"chapterQs":["Marilla cries when Anne models the new green dress. What does Anne tell Marilla about who she is ‘at heart’? Quote the key line.","The Avery scholarship is announced. What is it, and why does it matter so much to Anne? Use a detail from the chapter.","Looking across Ch. 31-34: identify ONE stated theme and ONE implied theme of this week’s reading. A stated theme is named directly. An implied theme is shown through the events."],"focus":"The last edit is your ears. Read your whole story out loud, slowly, to yourself. Anywhere you stumble, something is wrong with the sentence. Fix it. Then say what you’re proud of and what you’d change with more time.","nowTry":"FINAL POLISH. Read your story aloud one more time and fix what you hear. Then write 2–3 sentences: what you are most proud of in this story, and what you would change if you had more time.","plate":"plates/anne-ch34.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"broadminded","d":"Open to new ideas; willing to consider different points of view.","e":"🌈"},{"w":"tactful","d":"Careful not to hurt other people’s feelings; thoughtful in dealing with people.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":13,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 13 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-13-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-13-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"broadminded","d":"Open to new ideas; willing to consider different points of view.","e":"🌈"},{"w":"tactful","d":"Careful not to hurt other people’s feelings; thoughtful in dealing with people.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":14,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 14.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 35)","file":"lesson-14-1-anne-ch35.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-14-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 14.1: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 35). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how a character changes across a whole novel. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Gilbert’s welcome","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-welcome","done":false,"script":"Hi, it’s Gilbert. Today’s question is about winning, and how wanting to win can change. I know a bit about that. I’ve been racing Anne Shirley since our first term in Avonlea school. First, some word work: two old roots, and verbs that come in pieces. Then the chapter, our winter at Queen’s. Watch what Anne wants now. It isn’t what it used to be."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Gilbert · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box asks you to hold two Annes side by side: the girl who came to Avonlea and the young lady at Queen’s. I can help with the first one. She once broke a slate over my head. This winter she still won’t say a word to me, and I don’t say one to her. That isn’t the whole change, and it isn’t mine to explain. Find it in what she says and does."},{"id":"dol","label":"Gilbert · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is three words long, and it’s Jane Andrews talking about exams. I’ll admit I felt the same way. Find the little helper word, and the word it’s helping. The bottom one is two sentences crammed together, the way people talk when they’re nervous. Pull them apart and give them their capitals. Getting the small things right is half of any exam."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Gilbert · before the word work","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four pieces of word work, and two of them ask you to sort into three groups instead of two. Don’t rush the third group. That’s where the thinking is. At Queen’s, the professors don’t much care whether you guessed right. They want to know how you knew. So for every one you place, be ready to say why. It’s a good habit, and a hard one."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Gilbert · about the word parts","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-morphology","done":false,"script":"Two old roots today. One means to write, and one means to throw. They don’t stand on their own; they hide inside longer words, and you have to dig them out. When a blank stumps you, ask what the word is doing. Is somebody putting words on paper, or sending something flying? That tells you which root belongs. It works on Latin, too, I’m finding."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Gilbert · about today’s words","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Four new words, and each one has its sentence from the book sitting right on top. Read that sentence first, then take the word apart, then check. One of these words fits a girl I know rather too well. I won’t say which, or she’d prove it on the spot. The matching game at the end is quick. Don’t let quick turn into careless."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Gilbert · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-grammar","done":false,"script":"Some verbs come in pieces: a helper out front, and the word that carries the meaning at the end. Sometimes the helpers stack up two deep. Sometimes there’s no helper at all. For each one, ask which word is doing the real work and which ones are only standing beside it. Then count the ones standing beside it. That count is your group."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Gilbert · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-spelling","done":false,"script":"These are mistakes from real drafts, and three different rules got broken. Before you sort, say each word with its ending, then picture how it should look on the page. All three rules wake up at the same moment: when an ending meets a base word. I lost a spelling match at Avonlea school on exactly that moment once. Anne didn’t let me forget it."},{"id":"copia","label":"Gilbert · about saying it three ways","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-copia","done":false,"script":"Here’s a plain sentence about where Anne spent her spare hours this winter. Say it three ways: give it a new opener, swap the doing word, then add a where or a how. Anne used to crowd every fine big word she knew into a composition. Miss Stacy taught us both to say a thing plainly first. Try all three, then keep the one that tells the most. It usually has the best verb."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Gilbert · before you open the book","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about a rivalry without bitterness. I can only tell you my half. For years Anne wouldn’t look at me, and this winter she still doesn’t. We haven’t said a word since the pond. But racing her feels different now, at least on my side. It feels like racing someone, not fighting them. Read, and see what she wants out of it now. I’d like to know myself."},{"id":"read","label":"Gilbert · before you read","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-read","done":false,"script":"Two stops. The first comes when the girls are fretting about exams and Anne says something about trying and failing. Ask yourself whether she means it. The second stop comes at the very end, when she’s looking out a window. Watch what she’s dreaming about. I wasn’t there for either of those, so you’ll know more than I do. That doesn’t happen to me often."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Gilbert · before you write","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now you write. Here’s a trick from exam week. Read the question twice, and the second time look for the trap. Then find the line in the chapter before you write a word. If a question asks for a quote, copy the words exactly, commas and all. The professors notice, and so will your teacher. Anne thinks checking is cheating. It isn’t. It’s checking."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Gilbert · about the questions","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about Ruby and me, and what Anne wishes she had instead. Don’t ask me; I didn’t know. The second wants you to set the slate day beside this winter, and to quote the book. The third is about her dreaming at the window. Use what the book says she sees, not what you guess she sees."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Gilbert · about today’s writing","path":"guide/gilbert/g4ela-14-1-workshop","done":false,"script":"Publishing starts with the ending. I like that. An ending is where a reader decides what the whole story meant. Your rough last scene has been waiting a long time. Today you replace it, using one move. Pick the move that fits your character, not the one that sounds fanciest. Then read your first line and your last line together. They should sound like they know each other."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A character changes a little at a time, so compare far apart: the start of the book and now. Say it like this: she used to ___, and now she ___.","wordParts":"script and scribe mean “to write” (describe, manuscript). ject means “to throw” (reject, project).","grammar":"A helping verb comes before the main verb and sets the time: had won, will go. The main verb carries the meaning. Helpers can stack: had been waiting. Some verbs need no helper: went.","spelling":"Three suffix rules. Doubling: one syllable, one vowel, one consonant, so double it (stop, stopped). Drop the e when the suffix starts with a vowel (write, writing). Change y to i after a consonant, unless the suffix is -ing.","dol":[{"raw":"anne and gilbert have studied together for months they will graduate in june","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> and <strong>Gilbert</strong> have studied together for <strong>months.</strong> <strong>They</strong> will graduate in <strong>June.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Anne, Gilbert and June, a period after months, a capital T to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Have studied and will graduate are helping verb + main verb."},{"raw":"the exams will start next week jane has lost seven pounds","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> exams will start next <strong>week.</strong> <strong>Jane</strong> has lost seven <strong>pounds.</strong>","why":"A capital T to start, a period after week, a capital on Jane to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Will start and has lost are helping verb + main verb."}],"pauses":["In Ch. 35 Anne is spending the winter at Queen’s, away from Green Gables. Compare her here with the Anne who first came to Avonlea. What can she do now that she could not do then? What about her has stayed exactly the same?","Anne tells her friends, “Next to trying and winning, the best thing is trying and failing.” What does this tell you about how Anne is growing up?","Anne no longer wants to beat Gilbert. She wants someone to think alongside. What has to change inside a person before a rival starts to look like a friend?"],"chapterQs":["When Anne hears that Gilbert walks home with Ruby Gillis, she has a thought. What does Anne wish she could share with Gilbert? What does this tell you about how she has grown?","Anne’s rivalry with Gilbert is described as different now. How is it different from the slate-breaking day? Quote a specific line.","Anne ends the chapter dreaming about her future. What does she imagine? What does her dreaming tell you about who Anne is now?"],"focus":"Publishing starts with the ending, because your rough Scene 5 from Week 11 is still sitting there. A strong ending does one of four things: circles back to your opening, tells the feeling, names the lesson, or shares a hope for the future. Pick one and rewrite Scene 5.","nowTry":"Pick ONE strong-ending move. Rewrite Scene 5 in My Story. Then, here: which move did you pick, and copy your new last sentence.","plate":"plates/anne-ch35.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"sanctifying","d":"Making something feel holy or set apart; deeply changing.","e":"✨"},{"w":"obstinate","d":"Stubborn; refusing to give in.","e":"💪"}]},{"w":14,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 14.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 36)","file":"lesson-14-2-anne-ch36.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-14-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 14.2: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 36). In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how a character changes across a whole novel. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Matthew’s welcome","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-welcome","done":false,"script":"Well now, it’s Matthew. Today’s question is about two feelings sitting side by side, glad and worried, in the very same day. I dunno much about books, but I know that feeling. First there’s word work, roots and helping words. Then you read about the day the results came out, and Anne coming home. Watch for the happy parts. And notice where folks are fretting, too."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Matthew · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box says you can tell how a person’s grown by what they wish for. When Anne first came, she wished she could stay. That was about all she wanted. I remember it pretty well, being the one driving the buggy. Today, listen for what she wishes for next. Then set the two wishes side by side. That’s quite a road to come down, I reckon."},{"id":"dol","label":"Matthew · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is Anne’s, in all the fuss. She wanted to write home right away, and I’m mighty glad she did. Find the little helper word and the word it helps. The bottom one runs two sentences together, the way folks talk when they’re in a hurry. Split it, and give the names their capitals. Anne always did care about a name being done right."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Matthew · before the word work","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four bits of word work. One is matching, one is remembering, and two ask you to tap. Tapping is easy to do too fast. I’ve found most things go better slow. Plowing, for one. Read every word before you tap it, and ask yourself if you’re sure. If you’re not, leave it be a minute and come back. No harm in that."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Matthew · about the word parts","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"Two old roots again, the writing one and the throwing one, and two little pieces that go on the front. Match each piece to what it means. If you get stuck, think of a word you know and say it slow. I’m no scholar. But Anne says the old roots are like seeds. You plant one, and a whole row of words comes up."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Matthew · about today’s words","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Same four words as yesterday, and now you match them up without the sentences to lean on. That’s how you find out what stuck. If one won’t come, try to remember the sentence it lived in. Anne says words have a home sentence. I wouldn’t know about that. I just know one of these words sounds an awful lot like her, once she’s set her mind."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Matthew · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"One long sentence, and you’re to tap every helper in it. There’s more than one, and some stand right next to each other. Don’t tap the words doing the real work, just the ones helping. It’s a bit like a barn raising. Lots of folks hold the beam, but only one swings the hammer. You want the folks holding the beam."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Matthew · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Here’s a little paragraph somebody wrote too fast, and three words in it got their endings put on wrong. Tap only those. The rest are fine, and tapping a fine one won’t help you any. Say each word slow in your head, and look at the spot where the ending joins on. That’s where the trouble usually hides. Same as with fences."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Matthew · before you open the book","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about knowing a good evening while you’re still in it. Anne’s better at that than I am. She notices the sky and the pointed firs and the old Snow Queen, and she says so out loud. I just walk along beside her. Read slow today, the way she would. Take your time over the happy parts. They’re worth it."},{"id":"read","label":"Matthew · before you read","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-read","done":false,"script":"There’s one stop, near the end, when Anne and I are bringing the cows in. She says something about the boy we sent for, and I say something back. You’ll get to it. Before then, there’s the morning the news comes out, and the commencement. I was there for that. I didn’t say a word till she’d finished reading her essay."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Matthew · before you write","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now the writing part. I’m not much of a talker, so here’s the short of it. Read each question till you know what it wants. Answer it plain, and show where in the book you found it. You don’t need fancy words. Anne’s got enough of those for the both of us. Just true ones."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Matthew · about the questions","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about how Anne heard her news. The second is about something I said to her, coming back from the pasture. I meant every word of it. The third question is about a line the writer put in, not anybody in the story. I’ll leave that one to you and the book. Read it slow."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Matthew · about today’s writing","path":"guide/matthew/g4ela-14-2-workshop","done":false,"script":"Today you score your own story, row by row, then fix just one thing. Just one. I like that. When a fence needs mending, you don’t tear the whole fence down. You find the one rail that’s loose, and you put it right. Be honest with your scores. Nobody’s going to think less of you for a two."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Watch what a character wants. Set her old wish beside her new one; the difference between them shows how she has grown.","wordParts":"script and scribe mean “to write” (describe, manuscript). ject means “to throw” (reject, project).","grammar":"A helping verb comes before the main verb and sets the time: had won, will go. The main verb carries the meaning. Helpers can stack: had been waiting. Some verbs need no helper: went.","spelling":"Three suffix rules. Doubling: one syllable, one vowel, one consonant, so double it (stop, stopped). Drop the e when the suffix starts with a vowel (write, writing). Change y to i after a consonant, unless the suffix is -ing.","dol":[{"raw":"matthew has worked his whole life he must rest now","fixed":"<strong>Matthew</strong> has worked his whole <strong>life.</strong> <strong>He</strong> must rest <strong>now.</strong>","why":"A capital on Matthew, a period after life, a capital H to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Has worked and must rest are helping verb + main verb."},{"raw":"anne has won the avery she will go to redmond in september","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> has won the <strong>Avery.</strong> <strong>She</strong> will go to <strong>Redmond</strong> in <strong>September.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Anne, Avery, Redmond and September, a period after Avery, a capital S to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Has won and will go are helping verb + main verb."}],"pauses":["In Ch. 36 Anne finishes at Queen’s and looks ahead to what comes next. Name what Anne wants now. Then think back to the Anne who first arrived at Green Gables and name what she wanted then. What happened in between to change the wish?","Frost’s speaker pauses in a beautiful, quiet place—but has promises to keep and keeps moving. Where is Anne pausing in these chapters, and what promises pull her forward?","Matthew says to Anne: “My girl — my girl that I’m proud of.” Why is this line so important? Why does the author end the chapter just after it?"],"chapterQs":["When the results are posted, Gilbert has won the medal. What does Anne win? How does she find out?","Matthew tells Anne, “I’d rather have you than a dozen boys.” What does this small line mean in light of the very first day Matthew met her at the train station?","The chapter ends with the line: “It was the last night before sorrow touched her life.” What does this line warn the reader is coming? Why does the author tell us this before it happens?"],"focus":"Today you score your own story against a rubric — the same kind a teacher uses — so you know what’s strong and what still needs work. Then you pick ONE row to make better before you publish. Not everything. One.","nowTry":"Score your story 1–3 in all five rows. Then pick ONE row to improve and name TWO specific things you changed in your draft to do it.","rwm":"“It was the last night before sorrow touched her life; and no life is ever quite the same again when once that cold, sanctifying touch has been laid upon it.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch36.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"sanctifying","d":"Making something feel holy or set apart; deeply changing.","e":"✨"},{"w":"obstinate","d":"Stubborn; refusing to give in.","e":"💪"}]},{"w":14,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 14.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 37)","file":"lesson-14-3-anne-ch37.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-14-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 14.3: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 37). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Marilla’s welcome","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-14-3-welcome","done":false,"script":"It’s Marilla. Today’s question is about grief, and how it moves. I’ll be plain, the way I always am. Matthew died, and this chapter is about the days after. Anne couldn’t cry at first. Then she could. There’s word work to do first, and that’s no bad thing. It steadies the hands. Then the chapter. Go gently with it."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Marilla · before the word work","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-14-3-guide1","done":false,"script":"Word work first. It’s ordinary work, and I’ll tell you something about ordinary work. After Matthew, the cows still needed milking and the bread still needed baking, and I found the doing of it held me up. So take this the same way. One blank at a time, one word at a time. Do each one properly, and the next one will come."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Marilla · before you open the book","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-14-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about being the one who carries it. Anne carried a great deal those first days. There were neighbors in and out, and there was me, and she kept herself steady for all of us. I didn’t see what it cost her until later. Read, and watch what she does in front of people, and what she saves for when she’s alone."},{"id":"rwm","label":"Marilla · about Read like a Writer","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-14-3-rwm","done":false,"script":"This sentence is about me, and I’ll own it. I heard her, and I crept in. Notice the writer didn’t say I walked, or marched, the way I generally do. Crept is a careful word. It tells you somebody was trying very hard not to make things worse. When you write yours, choose that verb slowly. One right word can say a great deal."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Marilla · before you write","path":"guide/marilla/g4ela-14-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now you write. Take your time with these. One asks what happened that morning, one asks why Anne wanted to be alone, and one asks about laughing again. That last one surprised her. She told Mrs. Allan, not me, and I don’t blame her. Answer each one plain and true, with something from the book to show it. Then rest a minute before the workshop. I’d recommend it."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A hard moment shows who a character has become. Watch what she does, then ask what her younger self would have done.","wordParts":"script and scribe mean “to write” (describe, manuscript). ject means “to throw” (reject, project).","grammar":"A helping verb comes before the main verb and sets the time: had won, will go. The main verb carries the meaning. Helpers can stack: had been waiting. Some verbs need no helper: went.","spelling":"Three suffix rules. Doubling: one syllable, one vowel, one consonant, so double it (stop, stopped). Drop the e when the suffix starts with a vowel (write, writing). Change y to i after a consonant, unless the suffix is -ing.","dol":[{"raw":"anne could not cry at first her grief was too deep for tears","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> could not cry at <strong>first.</strong> <strong>Her</strong> grief was too deep for <strong>tears.</strong>","why":"A capital on Anne, a period after first, a capital H to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Could cry is helping verb + main verb."},{"raw":"diana had offered to stay anne wanted to be alone","fixed":"<strong>Diana</strong> had offered to <strong>stay.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> wanted to be <strong>alone.</strong>","why":"A capital on Diana, a period after stay, a capital on Anne to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Had offered is helping verb + main verb; wanted needs no helper."}],"pauses":["In Ch. 37 Anne faces the hardest thing that has happened to her since she came to Green Gables. Watch how she carries it — what she does for Marilla, and what she says out loud. Then ask yourself: what would the Anne of the first chapters have done in this same moment?","Anne can’t cry at first. Then in the night, she remembers Matthew’s voice. Why do the tears come then and not before?","Marilla hears Anne crying in the night and creeps in to comfort her. Why can Marilla do that in the dark when she could never do it in the daytime?"],"chapterQs":["Matthew dies suddenly in Ch. 37. What caused the shock that brought it on, and how do Anne and Marilla find him?","When Diana offers to sleep with Anne, Anne says no. Why does Anne want to be alone? What is she trying to do?","A few days after Matthew’s death, Anne finds herself laughing at something Diana said. Why does this make Anne feel guilty? What does Mrs. Allan tell her about it?"],"focus":"Today your story gets the parts that make it feel like a real book: a title, an author line, and a dedication. The title should hint at the conflict, not just name the topic.","nowTry":"Design your title page. Write a TITLE that hints at the conflict. Add your name as the author. Write a one-sentence DEDICATION to someone who matters to you. Put all three at the top of My Story too.","rwm":"“Anne wept her heart out. Marilla heard her and crept in to comfort her.”","plate":"plates/anne-ch37.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"sanctifying","d":"Making something feel holy or set apart; deeply changing.","e":"✨"},{"w":"obstinate","d":"Stubborn; refusing to give in.","e":"💪"}]},{"w":14,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 14.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 38)","file":"lesson-14-4-anne-ch38.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-14-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 14.4: Anne of Green Gables (Ch. 38). In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Anne’s welcome","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-welcome","done":false,"script":"Hello, it’s Anne. Today’s question is about two people choosing love over ambition, and what that teaches. One of those people is me, so I may not be perfectly fair. First there’s word work, and some of it is catching mistakes. Then you read the last chapter. Then you look back over the whole road, all the way from the train station to the gate."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"Anne · about today’s reading move","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-wgrd","done":false,"script":"The box asks what changed in me, and what stayed exactly the same. I’ve thought about it a great deal, and I’m not the right person to judge. Nobody sees herself straight, not even in the best looking glass. So find one early moment and one late one, and set them side by side. Then decide for yourself. I’d honestly rather you did."},{"id":"dol","label":"Anne · about today’s sentence","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is Mrs. Lynde’s, and when she said it, I sprang straight to my feet. Find the helper and the word it helps. The bottom sentence is two sentences that ran together, which is exactly how I talked when I was eleven. Split it, and give everyone their capitals. Gilbert included. Especially Gilbert, just now."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Anne · before the word work","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-guide1","done":false,"script":"The last word work of the book, and most of it asks you to catch mistakes. Some sentences are perfectly right, and tapping those costs you. So tap only when you’re sure. I spent years being sure about things I was wrong about. Gilbert, for one. So I’ve learned to look twice before I decide. You do the same."},{"id":"morphology","label":"Anne · about the word parts","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Matching again: the writing root, the throwing root, and two little pieces that go in front. I adore them, because they’re hiding in so many of the words I use. Before you match, say a word you know with that root in it. Then ask which piece is doing which job. It’s rather like working out who in a story is really doing the work."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"Anne · about today’s words","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"This one is a menu, and you only choose one thing from it. I would want to do all five, of course. But I’m about to give up something big, and I’ve found that choosing one thing and doing it well feels better than I expected. Pick the one you could do best. Then use the words as if they’d always been yours."},{"id":"grammar","label":"Anne · about today’s grammar","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"A little paragraph about this chapter, and some of the sentences are missing a helper. Tap only those. Read each sentence out loud in your head. Your ear will catch a missing word before your eye does. Mine always has, even when my eyes were busy imagining something else entirely. Leave the good sentences alone."},{"id":"spelling","label":"Anne · about today’s spelling","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"Six words, and some have their endings put on wrong. Tap only those. Look closely at the spot where the ending meets the word. That’s where every mistake in this list lives. I’m going to be a teacher now, you know, so I’ll be hunting for exactly this on a whole room of slates. Practice on these for me."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Anne · before you open the book","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"There’s a box here about the bend in the road. When I left Queen’s, my future looked like a straight road, and I thought I could see a long way down it. Then it turned. I didn’t choose the turn. I only chose how to walk around it. Read, and watch two people give something up. Then decide whether either of us lost anything."},{"id":"read","label":"Anne · before you read","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-read","done":false,"script":"One stop, at the very end, with what I said to Marilla about the bend. Before you get there, watch the kitchen when Marilla comes home from town. Watch who gives up what, and why. And watch the hill, near the end. Something happens there that I’d been waiting five years for, though I didn’t know I was waiting."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Anne · before you write","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-guide3","done":false,"script":"The writing now. The last question asks you to look back across the whole book, and that’s a great deal to hold at once. Don’t try to hold all of it. Pick one early moment and one late one, and set them side by side. Two good details are better than ten hurried ones. Miss Stacy taught me that, and she was right, as usual."},{"id":"chapter","label":"Anne · about the questions","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"The first question is about what I gave up and what I planned instead. The second is about Gilbert and the school, and the hill. The third is the whole book at once. For that one, you might begin at the station, where Matthew found me sitting on a pile of shingles, and end at the gate. Everything in between is yours to choose from."},{"id":"workshop","label":"Anne · about today’s writing","path":"guide/anne/g4ela-14-4-workshop","done":false,"script":"Your story is finished. Today you read it out loud to someone. Go slowly, and pause where it matters. I used to recite so fast that nobody could follow me. Then write a little about how your writing has grown, and how I’ve grown, too. Be specific. Specific is always better than splendid, even though splendid is such a lovely word."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"At the end of a novel, name what changed in a character and what stayed the same, and prove it with one early scene and one late scene.","wordParts":"script and scribe mean “to write” (describe, manuscript). ject means “to throw” (reject, project).","grammar":"A helping verb comes before the main verb and sets the time: had won, will go. The main verb carries the meaning. Helpers can stack: had been waiting. Some verbs need no helper: went.","spelling":"Three suffix rules. Doubling: one syllable, one vowel, one consonant, so double it (stop, stopped). Drop the e when the suffix starts with a vowel (write, writing). Change y to i after a consonant, unless the suffix is -ing.","dol":[{"raw":"anne and gilbert have decided to be friends they will work hard together","fixed":"<strong>Anne</strong> and <strong>Gilbert</strong> have decided to be <strong>friends.</strong> <strong>They</strong> will work hard <strong>together.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Anne and Gilbert, a period after friends, a capital T to start the second sentence, and a period at the end. Have decided and will work are helping verb + main verb."},{"raw":"gilbert has given up the school anne will teach at avonlea","fixed":"<strong>Gilbert</strong> has given up the <strong>school.</strong> <strong>Anne</strong> will teach at <strong>Avonlea.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Gilbert, Anne and Avonlea, a period after school, and a period at the end. Has given and will teach are helping verb + main verb."}],"pauses":["Dickinson’s house of “Possibility” has more windows and doors than any real house. Anne ends the novel looking down an unknown road and believing “the best” lies ahead. How is Anne’s whole story a house of possibility?","Ch. 38 is the last chapter, so today you can see the whole road at once. How has Anne changed between the train station in Ch. 2 and the gate in Ch. 38? Name one thing about her that is different now, and one thing that never changed at all.","Anne says: “I don’t know what lies around the bend, but I’m going to believe that the best does.” What is the bend in YOUR road? What helps you believe the best is coming?"],"chapterQs":["Anne tells Marilla she is NOT going to Redmond. What does she give up, and what does she plan instead?","Gilbert gives up the Avonlea school for Anne. How does this small kindness lead to Anne and Gilbert finally becoming friends?","Looking across the WHOLE novel: name TWO ways Anne has changed from the orphan at the train station in Ch. 2 to the young teacher at the end. Use specific details."],"focus":"Your story is finished. Today you read it aloud to someone — slowly, with feeling, pausing where it matters. Then you write a short reflection on two things that grew over these eight weeks: Anne, and your writing.","nowTry":"Final reflection. Write 3–4 sentences: (1) name ONE way Anne has changed across the novel, with a specific detail, and (2) name ONE thing about your own writing that got stronger while you wrote this story, with a specific example from it.","plate":"plates/anne-ch38.png"},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"sanctifying","d":"Making something feel holy or set apart; deeply changing.","e":"✨"},{"w":"obstinate","d":"Stubborn; refusing to give in.","e":"💪"}]},{"w":14,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 14 Fluency: Anne of Green Gables","file":"lesson-14-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-14-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"ambition","d":"A strong desire to achieve something important.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"sanctifying","d":"Making something feel holy or set apart; deeply changing.","e":"✨"},{"w":"obstinate","d":"Stubborn; refusing to give in.","e":"💪"}]},{"w":15,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 15.1: Number the Stars Bridge (WWII & Nazi Germany)","file":"lesson-15-1-bridge-wwii.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-15-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to the bridge week. Before we open Number the Stars, we spend four days on the real history behind it. Today’s reading tells how World War II began in 1939: Germany, Adolf Hitler and the Nazis, and the Allies who fought back. As you read, collect the names of countries and leaders so they already feel familiar when the novel starts. This is hard history. It’s okay if it feels uncomfortable, and it’s worth staying with.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Welcome","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-welcome","done":false,"script":"Welcome. You’ve just said goodbye to Anne and Green Gables, and a new book is close. Before we open it, we spend this week on the true history behind it. Today’s question is how World War Two began, and who started it. First you’ll warm up and do some word work. Then a short reading, and a few questions to answer in your own words."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"About today’s reading move","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Today’s reading move is a bit like packing for a trip. A novel set in real history expects you to bring some facts along. As you read, collect the names of countries, leaders, and dates, like things going into a suitcase. When the story starts next week, you’ll be glad they’re already packed."},{"id":"dol","label":"About today’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-dol","done":false,"script":"The top sentence is a model, and every capital in it is there for a reason. Look at the names of places, months, and people. The bottom one has lost all of its capitals, and it’s really two sentences squeezed into one. Find where one thought ends and the next one begins."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Before the word work","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-guide1","done":false,"script":"Four kinds of word work today. Some of it asks you to notice before anyone tells you the rule, and that’s on purpose. A rule you find yourself stays with you longer than one you’re handed. So go slowly at the start, look hard, and make your best guess before you check."},{"id":"morphology","label":"About the bridge","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-morphology","done":false,"script":"This week we’re building a bridge, one word at a time. Two small word parts at the front of words will do the building. One means across, and one means forward. Read each meaning first, then choose the part that fits. Every right choice lays a plank. By the end of the week, the bridge will reach the other shore."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"About today’s words","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"These four words will follow you all through the new book. Each one is about what can happen to a country, and to the people in it, during a war. Read the sentence, take the word apart, then make your guess before you flip the card. Guessing first is how a word starts to belong to you."},{"id":"grammar","label":"About today’s grammar","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-grammar","done":false,"script":"Some words stand in for people: she, they, him, them. They don’t all do the same job. Some do the action, some receive it, and a couple can do both. Sort them by the job they’re doing, then name the groups yourself. Names you give stick better than names you’re given."},{"id":"spelling","label":"About today’s spelling","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-spelling","done":false,"script":"Long words from history often hide two vowels working together to make one sound. The trick is that the same pair doesn’t always sound the same. Say each word out loud, slowly, and listen for what the pair is really saying before you sort it. Your ear knows more than you think."},{"id":"copia","label":"About saying it three ways","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-copia","done":false,"script":"Here is one plain sentence about the end of the war. Your job is to say it three different ways: a new beginning, a stronger verb, and a detail about where or how. Writers have practiced this way for hundreds of years. Each new way shows you something the first sentence left out."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Courage Watch","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-guide2","done":false,"script":"This quarter we watch for courage, and here is the first thing to know about it. Courage isn’t the same as not being scared. It’s being scared and doing the right thing anyway. Even reading about a hard time in history takes a little of that. If today’s facts feel heavy, that’s all right. Stay with them. That’s the first small brave thing."},{"id":"read","label":"Before you read","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-read","done":false,"script":"The passage is short, and it covers six long years. Watch for two things: the countries that were taken over, one after another, and the countries that joined together to stop it. When you reach the pause questions, stop and think about a child your age living in one of those places."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Before you write","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-guide3","done":false,"script":"Now it’s your turn to write. The questions ask you to go back to the passage, so keep it open beside you. Use the passage’s own facts, the dates and the names, to show how you know. Then you’ll finish with a short piece of your own writing."},{"id":"chapter","label":"About the questions","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-chapter","done":false,"script":"These three questions build on each other. The first asks when and who. The second asks what was happening. The third asks who stood against it. Put them together, and you have the whole shape of the war in a few sentences. That shape is what you’ll carry into the novel."},{"id":"workshop","label":"About today’s writing","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-1-workshop","done":false,"script":"Your writing project this quarter is an opinion essay, and it starts with one simple skill. A fact can be checked. An opinion is what someone thinks, and someone else could disagree. Today you’ll practice telling them apart, using things from your own life. Soon you’ll use the same skill on the new book."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Before reading historical fiction, build background knowledge: collect the names, places and dates the story will expect you to know, and ask what each one meant for a child living then.","wordParts":"trans- means “across” or “through” (transport, translate). pro- means “forward” (progress, protect).","grammar":"Subject pronouns do the action: I, he, she, we, they. Object pronouns receive it: me, him, her, us, them. You and it can do both. Test: swap in he or him.","spelling":"A vowel team is two letters making one sound: ie and ea can say a long E (achieve, retreat); ai and ay can say a long A (campaign, betrayal). In a quick, unstressed part of a word a team can go soft, like the ai in mountain.","dol":[{"raw":"the war began in europe in 1939 hitler led the german army","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> war began in <strong>Europe</strong> in <strong>1939.</strong> <strong>Hitler</strong> led the <strong>German</strong> army<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capital T to start, a capital on Europe, a period after the year, a capital H on Hitler, a capital G on German, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"the allies fought against germany britain and france were two of them","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>Allies</strong> fought against <strong>Germany.</strong> <strong>Britain</strong> and <strong>France</strong> were two of them<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The, Allies, Germany, Britain and France, a period after Germany, and a period at the end. Allies takes a capital here because it names one group."}],"pauses":["This passage covers 1939 to 1945 and names the countries Germany invaded. Which of these facts do you think you will need once the novel begins? What would daily life have been like for a child in one of those countries?","By 1941, Germany had conquered most of Europe. What does that tell you about how powerful Germany was at that point?","The passage says six years of fighting changed the world. What is one way a war that long could change ordinary people’s lives?"],"chapterQs":["When did World War II begin, and who started it? Use details from the passage.","What was Hitler trying to do across Europe? List at least TWO countries he invaded.","Who were the Allies? What was their role in the war?"],"focus":"This quarter you’ll write an opinion essay about Number the Stars . It starts with one skill: telling a FACT from an OPINION. A fact can be checked. An opinion is what someone thinks, and other people could disagree.","nowTry":"Write below. Write ONE fact and ONE opinion about something from your own life (a food, a game, a place, a pet). Label each one.","plate":"plates/bridge-15-1.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":15,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 15.2: Number the Stars Bridge (Denmark in 1940)","file":"lesson-15-2-bridge-denmark.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-15-2","done":false,"script":"Today we zoom in on one country: Denmark. Germany took it over in a single day in April 1940, then tried to keep the Danes calm. Watch the dates and numbers in the passage and ask what each one meant for ordinary families. Then meet King Christian X in the Courage Watch, a king who showed courage without fighting.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Welcome","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-welcome","done":false,"script":"Welcome back. Today we zoom in on one country, the one our new book is set in: Denmark. Germany took it over in a single day. So what was everyday life like after that? You’ll warm up, work with some words, then read about Denmark under the Germans, and study one sentence the way a writer would."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"About today’s reading move","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Today’s reading move is about numbers. A date or a time in a history passage can say more than it seems to. When you see a number, stop and ask what it meant for the people living through it. A few hours. One morning in April. Turn each number into a picture of real people."},{"id":"dol","label":"About today’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-dol","done":false,"script":"Today you get to choose which sentence to fix. Both have the same kind of trouble: missing capitals, and two thoughts run together. Pick one, then look back at the model at the top. Notice which words there are names. Names of people, countries, and groups always start tall."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Before the word work","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-guide1","done":false,"script":"Today’s word work asks you to find things, not just sort them. In grammar and spelling, you’ll hunt through a passage and tap only what fits. That’s harder than it sounds, because the right answers are hiding among ones that look almost right. Slow down, and check each one before you tap."},{"id":"morphology","label":"About the bridge","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-morphology","done":false,"script":"The bridge’s first span is already built. Today you lay the next four planks. The same two word parts are at work: one means across, and one means forward. Some of these words may be new to you, so let the meaning lead you, not the sound of the word."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"About today’s words","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Today’s words come straight from the reading about Denmark, so you’ll meet them again in just a few minutes. Read the sentence above each one, and look for clues around it. One of these words is about giving up. Another is about the people who run a country. See if you can tell which, before you flip."},{"id":"grammar","label":"About today’s grammar","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-grammar","done":false,"script":"In each sentence, more than one of these stand-in words is hiding. Your job is to find only the ones receiving the action, the ones the action happens to. A quick test helps: try swapping in he, or him. If him sounds right, you’ve found one."},{"id":"spelling","label":"About today’s spelling","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-spelling","done":false,"script":"Most vowel pairs say what you expect. Today you’re hunting for the ones that don’t. Say each word slowly, and listen. When a pair sits in a part of the word you say quickly, it often goes soft and mumbly. Those are the ones that trip up even grown-up spellers."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Courage Watch","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-guide2","done":false,"script":"Today’s Courage Watch is about Denmark’s king, Christian the Tenth. He didn’t fight with weapons. Instead, during the occupation, he kept riding his horse through the streets of Copenhagen, often with no guards around him. People saw him, and knew their king hadn’t given up. Sometimes courage is simply showing up, again and again, where everyone can see you."},{"id":"read","label":"Before you read","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-read","done":false,"script":"This passage is about how things looked on the surface in Denmark, and what was really going on underneath. Watch for the reason the German leaders had for keeping things calm. Then watch for the people who quietly pushed back. Stop at each pause question before you move on."},{"id":"rwm","label":"About the writer’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-rwm","done":false,"script":"Today’s sentence is built in two halves. The first half tells you what someone wanted. Then two small words, so that, turn the corner and give you the reason. Writers use this shape when the why matters as much as the what. When you write your own, make the second half do real work."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Before you write","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-guide3","done":false,"script":"Time to write about what you read. Keep the passage close, because each question points back to a part of it. You’ll also study one sentence closely and then try its shape yourself. Then you’ll finish with today’s step in your own writing project."},{"id":"chapter","label":"About the questions","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-chapter","done":false,"script":"Two of today’s questions are about differences: how Denmark was treated compared to other countries. A difference needs two sides, so name both. The last question asks about the Resistance. Think about why someone would choose to join, knowing it could be dangerous."},{"id":"workshop","label":"About today’s writing","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-2-workshop","done":false,"script":"Last time, you learned that an opinion is something people could disagree with. Today that opinion becomes a claim: one clear sentence that takes a side. No maybe, no kind of. Practice on something you care about, like the best pet or the best season. A clear claim makes everything after it easier."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"A date or a number in a history passage can say more than it seems. Stop at each one and turn it into a picture of real people: what did “within a few hours” mean for a family in Denmark?","wordParts":"trans- means “across” or “through” (transmit, transport). pro- means “forward” (proclaim, progress).","grammar":"Object pronouns receive the action: me, him, her, us, them. To find one, ask who the action happens to. Test: if him sounds right, it’s the object form.","spelling":"Most vowel teams say what you expect, but some don’t: eu in neutral says /oo/, and the ai in mountain and certain goes soft because that part of the word is unstressed.","dol":[{"raw":"the danes started a resistance they hid jewish people from the nazis","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>Danes</strong> started a <strong>Resistance.</strong> <strong>They</strong> hid <strong>Jewish</strong> people from the <strong>Nazis.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The, Danes, Resistance, Jewish and Nazis, a period after Resistance, a capital T to start the second sentence, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"the german army reached copenhagen in april most danes stayed calm","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>German</strong> army reached <strong>Copenhagen</strong> in <strong>April.</strong> <strong>Most</strong> <strong>Danes</strong> stayed calm<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The, German, Copenhagen, April, Most and Danes, a period after April to end the first sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Denmark was invaded on April 9, 1940, and surrendered within a few hours. The passage also says schools, police, and hospitals kept running. Put those two facts together — what was life in Denmark like? Why might a story set there feel different from one set in a country that fought on?","Read the two couplets with a full pause at the blank line, then read them straight through. What does the pause add?","Denmark’s king stayed on his throne even after Germany took over. Why would the Germans LET him stay? What did they want?","By 1943, the Danish Resistance had grown, and it worked in quiet, careful ways. Why would they push back quietly instead of out in the open?"],"chapterQs":["What happened in Denmark on April 9, 1940? How did the Danish king respond?","Name TWO ways that Germany treated Denmark differently than other countries at first.","What was the Danish Resistance? Why did people join it?"],"focus":"An opinion becomes a CLAIM when it takes a clear side in one sentence. A claim tells your reader exactly what you think, with no “maybe” about it.","nowTry":"Write below. Pick a kid topic (best season, best pet, best lunch, best game). Write ONE claim that takes a clear side.","rwm":"“The German leaders wanted to keep the Danes calm so that food and supplies would keep flowing to Germany.” — from today’s article, Denmark Under German Occupation","plate":"plates/bridge-15-2.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"invade","d":"To enter a country with an army in order to take it over.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"government","d":"The group of people who run a country and make its rules.","e":"🏛️"},{"w":"arrest","d":"To take someone away by police or soldiers, as a punishment.","e":"🚓"}]},{"w":15,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 15.3: Number the Stars Bridge (The Star of David)","file":"lesson-15-3-bridge-star.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-15-3","done":false,"script":"Today’s reading is about the yellow star the Nazis forced Jewish people to wear in many countries, and why Denmark was different. The novel we are about to read is called Number the Stars. Hold on to that title while you read, and make a guess about why the author might have chosen it.","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Welcome","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-welcome","done":false,"script":"Welcome back. Today’s reading is about a law the Nazis made in many countries, forcing Jewish people to wear a yellow star. It’s a hard subject, and an important one. You’ll also learn how Denmark’s answer was different. Then you’ll study one sentence that says a hard truth very carefully, and try writing one like it."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"About today’s reading move","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Today’s reading move is holding on to a puzzle. Sometimes a fact doesn’t make sense yet, and that’s fine. Mark it, make a guess, and keep reading. The new book is called Number the Stars. Keep that title in your mind today, and see whether anything you read helps it make sense."},{"id":"dol","label":"About today’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-dol","done":false,"script":"Pick the sentence you’d like to fix. Both have the same troubles: lost capitals, and two sentences pressed together. Look closely at the model on top. Some words there are names of peoples and groups, and those always take a capital, wherever they fall in the sentence."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Before the word work","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-guide1","done":false,"script":"Today the word work asks you to build, not just find. You’ll choose the right word and write it in yourself. When you’re unsure, say the sentence out loud with each choice. Your ear often catches what your eye misses. Then check, and fix anything that didn’t sound right."},{"id":"morphology","label":"About the bridge","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-morphology","done":false,"script":"Two spans of the bridge are finished, and the lamps at their ends are lit. Today you build the third. The same two word parts are doing the work, one meaning across and one meaning forward. Read every meaning all the way through before you choose. The meaning is your map."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"About today’s words","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Today’s words come from the reading about the yellow star. Some of them are hard words for a hard subject, and they matter. Read the sentence above each one carefully. Think about what the word is doing in that sentence before you guess. Then flip the card, and see how close you came."},{"id":"grammar","label":"About today’s grammar","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-grammar","done":false,"script":"This time you supply the word yourself. Read each sentence and ask one question: is the missing person doing the action, or is the action happening to them? That decides which form you need. Use the word bank, and say each finished sentence out loud to check it."},{"id":"spelling","label":"About today’s spelling","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-spelling","done":false,"script":"Today you fill in the missing vowel pair yourself. There are two decisions for each word: which pair belongs, and how it sounds in that spot. If you’re stuck, say the whole word slowly and stretch out the middle. The sound usually points to the pair."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Courage Watch","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-guide2","done":false,"script":"Today’s Courage Watch is about ordinary people: teachers, fishermen, doctors, neighbors. Most of their courage was small and quiet. A doctor who wrote a different name on a hospital record. A neighbor who kept a child safe for a week. One small act doesn’t look like much. Thousands of them, together, saved thousands of lives."},{"id":"read","label":"Before you read","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-read","done":false,"script":"This passage begins with what happened in many countries, and then it turns. Watch for the word different. That’s where Denmark’s part begins. There’s also a famous story in here that isn’t quite true. As you read, think about why people might keep telling it anyway."},{"id":"rwm","label":"About the writer’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-rwm","done":false,"script":"Look at where today’s sentence pauses. The writer could have stopped early. Instead, a dash holds you still for a moment, and then two more words tell the harder truth. A pause like that makes the end land with weight. Writers save it for the words that matter most."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Before you write","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-guide3","done":false,"script":"Time to write. Today’s questions ask you to explain a law, compare Denmark to other countries, and think about a story that isn’t exactly true. Take your time with that last one. You’ll also try the writer’s sentence shape from the reading, and take today’s step in your own writing."},{"id":"chapter","label":"About the questions","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-chapter","done":false,"script":"The last question is the most interesting one today. A story can be untrue and still tell you something true about the people who told it. Ask yourself what the Danes wanted to believe about their king, and about themselves. Then use the passage to show how you know."},{"id":"workshop","label":"About today’s writing","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-3-workshop","done":false,"script":"A claim has to be something a person could argue with. If no one could ever disagree, it’s just a fact. Today you’ll test your claims. Could a smart, fair person say no? If not, change it until they could. That’s when a claim is ready for reasons."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Some facts don’t make sense yet. Mark the puzzling fact, guess why the author of the novel wants you to know it, and check your guess when the story begins.","wordParts":"trans- means “across” (transatlantic, transport). pro- means “forward” (propose, progress).","grammar":"Use the subject form when the person does the action (They waited) and the object form when the action happens to them (The guard stopped them).","spelling":"Two decisions for each vowel team: which pair spells the sound you hear, and whether that part of the word is stressed. A long E can be spelled ie or ea; a long A can be spelled ai or ay.","dol":[{"raw":"the danish king stood with his jewish people he refused the nazi rule","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>Danish</strong> king stood with his <strong>Jewish</strong> <strong>people.</strong> <strong>He</strong> refused the <strong>Nazi</strong> rule<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The, Danish, Jewish and Nazi, a period after people, a capital H to start the second sentence, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"the danish government refused the star law the nazis did not force it","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>Danish</strong> government refused the star <strong>law.</strong> <strong>The</strong> <strong>Nazis</strong> did not force it<strong>.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The, Danish and Nazis, a period after law to end the first sentence, a capital T to start the second, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["Today you learned that in many countries Jewish people were forced to wear a yellow star, and that Denmark refused to require it. The novel you are about to read is called Number the Stars . What might that title have to do with what you read today? What part are you still unsure about?","Most countries forced Jewish people to wear the yellow star. Denmark refused. What does this choice tell you about Denmark’s leaders?","The story about the king and the yellow star is not exactly true, but Danes told it anyway. Why would people keep telling a story like that?"],"chapterQs":["What was the Star of David law in countries under Nazi rule?","How was Denmark different from other countries on the Star of David law?","The passage says the story about King Christian X wearing a yellow star “isn’t exactly true.” But why does the story still matter? What does it tell us about what Danes believed?"],"focus":"A claim has to be something people could argue about. “Summer has warm weather” is a fact, and no one can argue with it. “Summer is the best season for kids” is a claim, because someone could disagree.","nowTry":"Write below. Write a claim someone could disagree with. Then write the sentence a person who disagrees might say back.","rwm":"“The Nazis used this badge to mark Jewish people as different — and as targets.” — from today’s article, The Star of David Law","plate":"plates/bridge-15-3.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"target","d":"A person or thing that someone is aiming to hurt or attack.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"refuse","d":"To say no; to decide not to do something.","e":"✋"},{"w":"badge","d":"Something worn on clothing to show who a person is or what group they belong to.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":15,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 15.4: Number the Stars Bridge (The October 1943 Rescue)","file":"lesson-15-4-bridge-rescue.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-15-4","done":false,"script":"This is the last history day before the novel. Today’s reading tells about the October 1943 rescue, when ordinary Danes hid Jewish families and carried thousands of them across the sea to Sweden. As you read, put it together with everything from this week and ask: what had to be true about Denmark for this rescue to work?","notes":true},{"id":"welcome","label":"Welcome","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-welcome","done":false,"script":"Welcome back. This is the last history day before we open the novel. Today’s reading tells about October 1943, when the Nazis planned to arrest all the Jewish people in Denmark, and what ordinary Danes did about it. It’s a story about fear, and about courage. When you finish today, you’ll be ready for the book."},{"id":"wgrd","label":"About today’s reading move","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-wgrd","done":false,"script":"Today’s reading move is putting things side by side. Today’s passage is the fourth one this week, and each one explains a little of the next. As you read, look back at the other three. Ask how the occupation, the star law, and the king help explain what happened in October 1943."},{"id":"dol","label":"About today’s sentence","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-dol","done":false,"script":"Choose your sentence. Both are run together, with capitals missing. Today the model is about fishermen and the sea. Look at which words in it are names of peoples and countries. Then make your sentence into two clean sentences, each with a capital at the front and an end mark at the back."},{"id":"guide1","label":"Before the word work","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-guide1","done":false,"script":"Today’s word work is review, with a twist. Some of what you see is already correct, and some isn’t. Your job is to tap only the mistakes. That means reading every item carefully, even the ones that look fine at first glance. Careful readers check before they change."},{"id":"morphology","label":"About the bridge","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-morphology","done":false,"script":"Three spans are finished, and their lamps are lit. Today you lay the last four planks. When the final one is down, the bridge reaches the far shore, the shore of our new book. Read each meaning, choose the part that fits, and watch the bridge reach across."},{"id":"vocabulary","label":"About today’s words","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-vocabulary","done":false,"script":"Today’s words come from the story of the rescue. Each one names a part of what happened: a warning, a plan, a secret journey. Read the sentence above each word, and picture the moment it describes. Then guess, and flip to check. You’ll meet ideas like these again in the novel."},{"id":"grammar","label":"About today’s grammar","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-grammar","done":false,"script":"This is a draft with a few mistakes in it. Some sentences use the wrong form of a stand-in word, and some are already right. Read each one out loud. When something sounds off, try swapping in he, or him, to check. Tap only the sentences that need fixing."},{"id":"spelling","label":"About today’s spelling","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-spelling","done":false,"script":"These words come from a draft, and some are misspelled. The mistakes are mostly in the middle of the word, where the vowels go soft and quiet. Say each word slowly, then picture how it really looks. Tap only the ones that are wrong, and leave the correct ones alone."},{"id":"guide2","label":"Courage Watch","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-guide2","done":false,"script":"Today’s Courage Watch is about remembering. Almost everyone in Denmark’s rescue story is gone now: the fishermen, the families, the king. One kind of courage is choosing not to forget them, and not to forget the people who were hurt. The author of our new book wrote it so that we would remember. Reading it is how you join in."},{"id":"read","label":"Before you read","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-read","done":false,"script":"Today’s passage has a warning, a time of hiding, and a journey across the sea. Watch for all the different kinds of people who helped. Not one hero, but many. When you reach the pause questions, think about what each person risked. And watch for the German man who sent the warning."},{"id":"guide3","label":"Before you write","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-guide3","done":false,"script":"Time for the week’s last writing. The first two questions are about the rescue itself. The third asks you to look back across all four readings, so think about the whole week, not just today. Then take your last step in this week’s writing project."},{"id":"chapter","label":"About the questions","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-chapter","done":false,"script":"The last question asks what made Denmark’s rescue so unusual. To answer it well, you need to compare. Think about what happened to Jewish people in other countries, and then what happened in Denmark. Name a specific fact from each side. A comparison is only as strong as its facts."},{"id":"workshop","label":"About today’s writing","path":"guide/narrator/g4ela-15-4-workshop","done":false,"script":"Next week, you’ll write claims about Number the Stars. Today you practice choosing. Write a claim on two different topics, then ask which one you could give the most reasons for. The best claim isn’t always the one you feel most strongly about. It’s the one you have the most to say about."}],"cards":{"wgrd":"Set several sources side by side. Each one explains part of another, and together they show the whole picture.","wordParts":"trans- means “across” (transport, transmit). pro- means “forward” (protest, progress).","grammar":"Use the subject form to do the action and the object form to receive it. “Her went” should be “She went,” and “between you and I” should be “between you and me.”","spelling":"Watch the soft, unstressed middle of long words: mountain, campaign and certain all keep their ai even though you hardly hear it.","dol":[{"raw":"the danes saved over seven thousand people they sent them to sweden","fixed":"<strong>The</strong> <strong>Danes</strong> saved over seven thousand <strong>people.</strong> <strong>They</strong> sent them to <strong>Sweden.</strong>","why":"Capitals on The and Danes, a period after people, a capital T to start the second sentence, a capital on Sweden, and a period at the end."},{"raw":"georg duckwitz warned the danish leaders word spread through copenhagen","fixed":"<strong>Georg</strong> <strong>Duckwitz</strong> warned the <strong>Danish</strong> <strong>leaders.</strong> <strong>Word</strong> spread through <strong>Copenhagen.</strong>","why":"Capitals on Georg Duckwitz, Danish and Copenhagen, a period after leaders, a capital W to start the second sentence, and a period at the end."}],"pauses":["This passage says more than 7,200 Jewish people reached Sweden in a few weeks, and that only about 120 Danish Jews died in the Holocaust. Set that beside what you read about the occupation and the star law. What had to be true about Denmark for this rescue to work? Which part of it still surprises you?","Why would a poet write about spring rain in the middle of a war? Explain how the gentle sound makes the idea harder, not softer.","More than 7,200 Jewish people were saved in one month. What had to be true about the Danish people for this to happen?","Georg Duckwitz was German, and he warned the Danish leaders anyway. What do you think it took for him to send that warning?"],"chapterQs":["What was the Nazi plan for Denmark’s Jewish people, and when was it set to start?","How did the Danish people hide and save Jewish families? Name THREE specific ways.","Looking across this whole week’s readings: name ONE reason the Danish rescue was so unusual. Use specific facts."],"focus":"Next week you’ll write claims about Number the Stars . Today, practice choosing: write claims on two topics, then pick the one you could defend best.","nowTry":"Write below. Write two claims on two different kid topics. Put a star (*) by the one you could defend best, and say why in one sentence.","plate":"plates/bridge-15-4.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"warn","d":"To tell someone ahead of time that danger is coming.","e":"📢"},{"w":"roundup","d":"When police or soldiers gather many people at once to take them away.","e":"🚔"},{"w":"smuggle","d":"To move people or things secretly so others will not catch them.","e":"⛵"}]},{"w":15,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 15 Fluency: Number the Stars Bridge","file":"lesson-15-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-15-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"invade","d":"To enter a country with an army in order to take it over.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"government","d":"The group of people who run a country and make its rules.","e":"🏛️"},{"w":"arrest","d":"To take someone away by police or soldiers, as a punishment.","e":"🚓"},{"w":"target","d":"A person or thing that someone is aiming to hurt or attack.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"refuse","d":"To say no; to decide not to do something.","e":"✋"},{"w":"badge","d":"Something worn on clothing to show who a person is or what group they belong to.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"warn","d":"To tell someone ahead of time that danger is coming.","e":"📢"},{"w":"roundup","d":"When police or soldiers gather many people at once to take them away.","e":"🚔"},{"w":"smuggle","d":"To move people or things secretly so others will not catch them.","e":"⛵"}]},{"w":16,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 16.1: Number the Stars Ch. 1","file":"lesson-16-1-nts-ch1.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-16-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 16.1: Number the Stars Ch. 1. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Infer danger from small details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Two soldiers stop three girls and ask why they were running. Watch how Annemarie answers — she does not cry and she does not blurt everything out. What does her careful answer tell you about how dangerous this street is? What does it tell you that soldiers with guns stop children at all?","Two soldiers stop the girls on their own street and ask why they were running. What changes about a neighborhood when soldiers can do that?","Annemarie answers the soldier carefully instead of crying or running. What does that tell you about what she has already learned to do?"],"chapterQs":["The German soldier asks the girls why they are running. What small detail at the start of this chapter shows that life in Copenhagen is not normal?","Annemarie is afraid, but she answers the soldier carefully. What does her careful answer show about how she has learned to live with danger?","At the end of Ch. 1, Mrs. Johansen is worried. Why does running on the street worry her so much? Use the chapter to explain."],"focus":"This quarter your opinion writing is novel-linked. Today: find a moment in Ch. 1 where someone shows COURAGE or LOYALTY. You’ll write your big claim about it tomorrow.","plate":"plates/nts-16-1.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":16,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 16.2: Number the Stars Ch. 2","file":"lesson-16-2-nts-ch2.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-16-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 16.2: Number the Stars Ch. 2. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Infer danger from small details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Annemarie remembers King Christian riding alone through Copenhagen with no guards around him. Ask yourself why a king could ride alone then, and whether anyone could do that on these streets now. What does the idea that all of Denmark is his bodyguard tell you about what the Danish people are willing to risk? Why does Annemarie need this memory tonight?","Who is speaking the indented line? How does the way it sits on the page tell you that?","Kirsti falls asleep, so Annemarie tells the king’s story to herself. Why would she keep telling it with no one listening?","The Danish people loved a king who rode through the streets alone every morning. What did that daily ride say to them?"],"chapterQs":["Kirsti wants a fairy tale about kings and queens. What does Annemarie tell her about the real king of Denmark? Use details from the chapter.","A German soldier once asked ‘Who is that man?’ about King Christian. What did the Danish citizen answer? Why is that answer important?","Annemarie thinks about Lise, her older sister who died. What two feelings about Lise live next to each other inside Annemarie? Use a detail from the chapter."],"focus":"Today: turn yesterday’s moment into a CLAIM. A claim is one strong sentence that says what you THINK and takes a side. (You practiced claims with kid topics in Week 15. Now you do it with the novel.)","rwm":"“She shook her head, waiting to hear the answer.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 2","plate":"plates/nts-16-2.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":16,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 16.3: Number the Stars Ch. 3","file":"lesson-16-3-nts-ch3.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-16-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 16.3: Number the Stars Ch. 3. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Infer danger from small details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The button shop is padlocked and a sign in German is posted on the door. Read those two details like a warning and ask who locked it and who it was meant to stop. Papa explains it carefully, and Peter Neilsen comes to the apartment quietly — what do those careful actions add to what you already figured out? What is happening to Mrs. Hirsch and to families like hers?","The button shop is padlocked and a sign in German is on the door. What has changed on Annemarie’s corner, and who changed it?","Annemarie asks Papa about Mrs. Hirsch instead of walking past. Why is asking a hard question its own kind of brave?"],"chapterQs":["When the girls reach Mrs. Hirsch’s button shop, what do they find? Describe the door using details from the chapter.","Papa explains to Annemarie why Mrs. Hirsch’s shop is closed. What did the Germans begin doing to certain Copenhagen shops? Why?","Peter Neilsen comes to the apartment with news. Who is Peter, and why is his visit a small clue that something bigger is happening?"],"focus":"Today: REASONS. Your claim says WHAT. Reasons say WHY. Three good reasons make any claim stronger. Today you take your novel-linked claim and add three reasons that back it up.","rwm":"“There was something frightening about his being here at night.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 3","plate":"plates/nts-16-3.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":16,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 16.4: Number the Stars Ch. 4","file":"lesson-16-4-nts-ch4.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-16-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 16.4: Number the Stars Ch. 4. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Infer danger from small details. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Watch the small signs stack up today: the girls playing with paper dolls, Kirsti furious about her new shoes, and then a room that goes quiet once she is asleep. Add them to what you noticed in the first three chapters and ask what they add up to. Why do Mama and Papa decide that tonight is the night to tell Annemarie the truth? What does it cost her to know?","Sandburg names five battlefields in five lines. Explain what the repetition makes you feel that a single example would not.","The girls play with paper dolls and Kirsti argues about her new shoes. Why would Lowry put such an ordinary evening right here?","Mama and Papa decide Annemarie is old enough to hear the truth. What are they asking of her by telling her?"],"chapterQs":["Mama and Papa decide it is time to tell Annemarie what is really happening. What do they tell her the Nazis are planning to do tonight?","Papa says the word ‘relocate’ carefully. What does Annemarie understand he REALLY means? How can you tell?","By the end of Ch. 4, Annemarie’s life is different. Name ONE thing she now has to do or be that she didn’t before. Use evidence from the chapter."],"focus":"Today: TEXT EVIDENCE. Your reasons tell what you think. Text evidence PROVES it — using the chapter’s words or moments. Plan one piece of text evidence for each reason you wrote yesterday.","plate":"plates/nts-16-4.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":16,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 16 Fluency: Number the Stars","file":"lesson-16-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-16-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"},{"w":"occupation","d":"When one country takes over and controls another.","e":"🪖"},{"w":"deport","d":"To force someone to leave a country, usually against their will.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"rescue","d":"To save someone from danger.","e":"🤝"}]},{"w":17,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 17.1: Number the Stars Ch. 5","file":"lesson-17-1-nts-ch5.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-17-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 17.1: Number the Stars Ch. 5. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character courage through evidence. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Officers pound on the door in the middle of the night, and Annemarie sees Ellen’s Star of David necklace still around her neck. Find the moment she acts and put your finger on it. What exactly does she do, and what could have happened to Ellen if she had done nothing? Which is the better evidence of courage — that Annemarie was scared, or what she did while she was scared?","Soldiers are already in the apartment when Annemarie sees Ellen’s necklace. Why is there no time to stop and make a plan?","Annemarie acted before she had time to think it through. Where do you think that kind of fast courage comes from?"],"chapterQs":["When the Nazis arrive, Annemarie does one important thing in seconds. What does she do? Why does it matter? Use details from the chapter.","The soldier asks why Ellen has dark hair. How do Papa and Mama protect Ellen with their words and with the photo album?","After the soldiers leave, Annemarie looks at her hand. What is imprinted there, and what does that small detail mean?"],"focus":"This week you turn your claim, reasons, and evidence into a real opinion essay. Today: learn to PLUG a short quote into your own sentence, so it sounds smooth, not bumpy.","plate":"plates/nts-17-1.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"}]},{"w":17,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 17.2: Number the Stars Ch. 6","file":"lesson-17-2-nts-ch6.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-17-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 17.2: Number the Stars Ch. 6. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character courage through evidence. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Papa talks on the phone in careful, almost coded words, and Mama agrees to take three girls north by herself. Listen for what is said and what is left out on purpose. Which few words would you copy to prove that a grown-up here is being brave? Whose courage is harder to see — the person speaking in code, or the person who says yes to the dangerous trip?","Annemarie’s courage in this chapter is also small and concrete rather than grand. Find the moment where she does something small that matters enormously. How is that moment like Dickinson’s robin?","Papa talks on the phone as if he were saying something ordinary. Why would he choose his words that way?","Mama agrees to take three girls north by herself and stays calm about it. What is she protecting besides their safety?"],"chapterQs":["After the Nazi visit, Mama and Papa decide on a plan. What is the plan? Use details to explain.","Papa speaks ‘almost as if he were speaking in code’ on the phone. Why? Use evidence from the chapter.","Ellen has to leave her parents behind. What does this tell you about courage — for Ellen and for her family?"],"focus":"Today: write your INTRO paragraph. It does two jobs: hook the reader and state your claim. Keep it short and confident.","rwm":"“She listened while Papa spoke on the telephone to Uncle Henrik, telling him that Mama and the children were coming for a visit.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 6","plate":"plates/nts-17-2.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":17,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 17.3: Number the Stars Ch. 7","file":"lesson-17-3-nts-ch7.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-17-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 17.3: Number the Stars Ch. 7. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character courage through evidence. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The girls reach the house by the sea, and Annemarie takes care of one small thing — Ellen’s necklace. She does not make a speech about it. She just promises, and then she keeps it safe. Is quietly keeping a promise evidence of courage, or does courage have to be loud? What would you point to in this chapter to prove your answer?","The meadow, the sea, and the kitten make Uncle Henrik’s farm feel safe. Has the danger gone away, or has it just moved?","Annemarie promises to keep the necklace safe and says nothing more about it. Why does a quiet promise matter so much here?"],"chapterQs":["When Annemarie sees Ellen’s necklace, what does she do with it? Find a sentence in the chapter that shows her decision.","Ellen has to leave the necklace behind. What does the necklace mean to Ellen? Use evidence from the chapter.","From Uncle Henrik’s house you can see Sweden across the water. Why is Sweden important in this story?"],"focus":"Today: write the FIRST body paragraph. It needs (1) a topic sentence with your first reason, (2) a quote sandwich from the book, (3) a closing sentence that connects back to your claim.","rwm":"“‘I took Ellen down to show her the sea,’ Annemarie explained when they reached the place where Mama waited.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 7","plate":"plates/nts-17-3.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":17,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 17.4: Number the Stars Ch. 8","file":"lesson-17-4-nts-ch8.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-17-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 17.4: Number the Stars Ch. 8. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain character courage through evidence. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Annemarie is told that a Great-Aunt Birte has died, but she has never heard that name before, and Mama says strange things about the casket. Watch the grown-ups say one thing and mean another. Whose courage costs the most today, and which details in the chapter show you that? Is Annemarie brave for going along with a story she does not understand?","Longfellow says one person’s footprints give a stranger courage. Explain how that idea works in this chapter of Number the Stars — whose footprints is Annemarie following?","Annemarie has never heard of a Great-Aunt Birte before today. What does that tell you about what the grown-ups are doing?","All through this chapter the adults say one thing and mean another. What is Annemarie learning to do as she listens?"],"chapterQs":["Uncle Henrik tells Annemarie there has been a death in the family. Why does Annemarie find this strange? Use evidence from the chapter.","Uncle Henrik tells Annemarie: “It is easier to be brave if you do not know everything.” What does that mean? How does it apply to what is happening?","By the end of Ch. 8, Annemarie has figured out something important. What does she begin to suspect about Great-Aunt Birte? Use a detail to support your answer."],"focus":"Today: write the SECOND body paragraph. Same structure (reason + evidence + link), but with a DIFFERENT reason and DIFFERENT quote. Two strong reasons are stronger than one.","plate":"plates/nts-17-4.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":17,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 17 Fluency: Number the Stars","file":"lesson-17-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-17-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"},{"w":"interrupt","d":"To stop something while it is happening.","e":"🛑"},{"w":"conduct","d":"How someone behaves or acts — or, to lead or guide.","e":"🧭"},{"w":"relocate","d":"To move someone or something to a new place — sometimes by force.","e":"📦"},{"w":"suspicion","d":"A feeling that something is wrong, even if you don’t have proof.","e":"👀"}]},{"w":18,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 18.1: Number the Stars Ch. 9","file":"lesson-18-1-nts-ch9.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-18-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 18.1: Number the Stars Ch. 9. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice symbolism and repeated ideas. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Annemarie has put it together — the adults are lying, and there is no Great-Aunt Birte. So she goes outside after supper and asks Uncle Henrik for the truth. Listen for the words and ideas he keeps coming back to while he answers her. Which idea does he say more than once, and why might that idea matter to the whole book?","Annemarie goes outside after supper to ask Uncle Henrik for the truth. Why is asking him harder than staying quiet?","Uncle Henrik tells her it is easier to be brave if you do not know everything. Do you think he is right? Why?"],"chapterQs":["Annemarie tells Henrik he is lying. How can you tell she is sure? Use a detail from the chapter.","Henrik tells Annemarie that it is easier to be brave if you do not know everything. What does that mean? Why might that be true?","Knowing and not-knowing is a repeated idea in this chapter. What does Henrik decide to do — protect Annemarie from the full truth, or tell her? Why?"],"focus":"This week you take last week’s body paragraphs and add WHY. Today: when you give evidence, don’t stop after one line of explanation. Stretch the SO WHAT into 2 or 3 sentences.","plate":"plates/nts-18-1.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":18,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 18.2: Number the Stars Ch. 10","file":"lesson-18-2-nts-ch10.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-18-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 18.2: Number the Stars Ch. 10. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice symbolism and repeated ideas. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The pretend funeral begins, soldiers come to investigate, and they demand that the casket be opened. Watch three things closely: the casket, the candles, and the stars in the psalm Mama reads aloud. The title of this book comes from that psalm. Which of the three keeps coming back, and what might stars stand for in a chapter this dangerous?","What is the lamp? What else does it stand for? Explain how it can be both.","German soldiers walk into the funeral and start asking questions. Why does the plan depend on the grown-ups acting as if nothing is wrong?","Mama reads the psalm aloud slowly, and her voice does not shake. How is a steady voice a kind of courage?"],"chapterQs":["When the soldiers arrive, what does Mama do? Use details to explain.","Mama reads about numbering the stars . What might the stars be a symbol for in this scene?","The casket itself is a symbol in this chapter. What does it really hold, and what does the SYMBOL of a casket let the family hide?"],"focus":"Today: a quote alone never argues for you. YOU have to argue. Today’s skill is explaining WHY a quote proves your reason.","rwm":"“Outside, she knew, the sky was speckled with stars.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 10","plate":"plates/nts-18-2.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"}]},{"w":18,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 18.3: Number the Stars Ch. 11","file":"lesson-18-3-nts-ch11.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-18-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 18.3: Number the Stars Ch. 11. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice symbolism and repeated ideas. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Peter Neilsen gets the Rosens ready to leave for Sweden, and they can bring almost nothing with them. Two objects get real attention here: the candlesticks and the Star of David necklace. Ask which details are only there so you can picture the room, and which ones are carrying an idea. What does leaving the candlesticks behind say about what the Rosens are giving up?","Peter gives Mr. Rosen something to carry to Uncle Henrik without explaining it. Why keep even the Rosens from knowing?","Mrs. Rosen leaves her candlesticks behind and goes anyway. What is she choosing when she leaves them?"],"chapterQs":["Peter brings the Rosens something important to take with them. What is it? Use the chapter to explain why it matters.","Mrs. Rosen has to leave her candlesticks behind. What do the candlesticks SYMBOLIZE for her family? Why are they so hard to leave?","At the end of the chapter, no one says ‘goodbye’ out loud. Why not? What word do they say instead?"],"focus":"Today: each body paragraph should end by connecting back to your CLAIM. Don’t leave the reader hanging — remind them of the big idea.","rwm":"“But their shoulders were as straight as they had been in the past: in the classroom, on the stage, at the Sabbath table.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 11","plate":"plates/nts-18-3.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"}]},{"w":18,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 18.4: Number the Stars Ch. 12","file":"lesson-18-4-nts-ch12.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-18-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 18.4: Number the Stars Ch. 12. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice symbolism and repeated ideas. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["This chapter is short and mostly quiet, but the worry in it is loud. Mama led the Rosens to the boat hours ago and she is not back yet. Watch the first gray light before morning, the window Annemarie keeps looking through, and the path outside. What might that gray light stand for here, and how would you say it using the word maybe ?","Find an object in these chapters that keeps returning. Explain what it is and what it has come to stand for , using two moments from the text.","Mama should have been back hours ago, and Annemarie can only watch the path. Why is waiting so hard when there is nothing you can do?","This chapter is short and mostly quiet, but the worry is loud. How does Lowry make you feel that worry without much happening?"],"chapterQs":["What wakes Annemarie? Use details from the chapter.","Mama has not come back. Why does that worry Annemarie? Use evidence.","Across Ch. 9-12, we’ve seen the idea of KNOWING vs. NOT KNOWING again and again. How does Annemarie’s view of not-knowing change by the end of Ch. 12?"],"focus":"Today: take the moves from this week (stretch, zoom, knot) and revise one of your body paragraphs into a full, elaborated version. This is the version you’ll publish next week.","plate":"plates/nts-18-4.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"}]},{"w":18,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 18 Fluency: Number the Stars","file":"lesson-18-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-18-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"intend","d":"To plan to do something on purpose.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"credible","d":"Believable; easy to trust.","e":"✅"},{"w":"discreet","d":"Careful not to draw attention.","e":"🤫"},{"w":"tense","d":"Tight with worry; nervous.","e":"😬"}]},{"w":19,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 19.1: Number the Stars Ch. 13-14","file":"lesson-19-1-nts-ch13-14.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-19-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 19.1: Number the Stars Ch. 13-14. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare historical fiction with historical information. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Mama crawls home in the dawn light with a broken ankle, and the basket still has to reach Uncle Henrik’s boat before sunrise. Annemarie volunteers and runs the dark path alone. Which parts of this night could really have happened to a girl in Denmark in 1943, and which parts feel shaped to make a good story? What makes you say so?","Mama has a broken ankle, Papa is in Copenhagen, and the basket still has to go. Who is left to carry it?","Annemarie picks up the basket before anyone tells her to. What has she decided about herself in that moment?"],"chapterQs":["Mama crawls home with a broken ankle. What does she ask Annemarie to do, and why? Use details from Ch. 13.","Annemarie runs alone through the woods. Find a small detail in Ch. 14 that shows how scared she is — AND how she keeps going anyway.","How is this scene like the historical Danish rescue you read about in Week 15? Name one similarity AND one difference between Lowry’s story and the real history."],"focus":"This is publish week. Today: draft your CONCLUSION paragraph. A strong conclusion does three things: restates your claim, names the change/growth, and looks forward.","plate":"plates/nts-19-1.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"conform","d":"To match a shape or rule; to fit in with something.","e":"📐"},{"w":"reflect","d":"To think back; or, to bounce light or an image.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"compare","d":"To look at two things and notice what is the same and what is different.","e":"⚖️"},{"w":"conclude","d":"To finish; to come to a final idea.","e":"🏁"}]},{"w":19,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 19.2: Number the Stars Ch. 15","file":"lesson-19-2-nts-ch15.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-19-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 19.2: Number the Stars Ch. 15. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare historical fiction with historical information. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Soldiers and their dogs block Annemarie on the path, and she knows what the basket carries while they do not. Pick one detail from this scene — the dogs, soldiers stopping a child, a basket being searched — and ask whether that is the kind of thing that really happened under this occupation. Then look again at what Annemarie says and how she acts. Which part of this moment is history, and which part is the author building a scene?","What is the ship? What is the fearful trip? How can you tell, when Whitman never says?","Soldiers and their dogs stop Annemarie on the path. What do they see when they look at her?","Annemarie answers like a child who has been interrupted, not like someone in danger. How can pretending be brave?"],"chapterQs":["When the soldier’s dogs sniff the basket, what does Annemarie do? Use details from the chapter.","The basket has a special package wrapped in a handkerchief. What do we (and Annemarie) NOT know about that handkerchief until later? Why does the chapter keep it a mystery?","How is this scene similar to and different from the real history of the Danish rescue? The real rescue used hundreds of small boats and many couriers. The novel uses one girl, one path."],"focus":"Today: REVISION. You read your essay out loud and listen. If a sentence makes you stumble or sounds bumpy, fix it. Revising is for sound, not spelling. (Spelling comes tomorrow.)","rwm":"“In a moment they had disappeared down the path, in the direction from which Annemarie had just come.” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 15","plate":"plates/nts-19-2.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":19,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 19.3: Number the Stars Ch. 16-17","file":"lesson-19-3-nts-ch16-17.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-19-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 19.3: Number the Stars Ch. 16-17. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare historical fiction with historical information. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Uncle Henrik finally explains the handkerchief, and that part is real history — Danish fishermen really did use one. A history book could hand you that fact in a single line. What does this novel give you about the same fact that a history book would leave out?","Uncle Henrik finally explains the handkerchief, and it turns out to be real history. Why did he keep it from Annemarie until now?","The end of the war brings back joy and it also brings a cost. Why does Lowry let you feel both instead of only the happy part?"],"chapterQs":["Uncle Henrik finally tells Annemarie what was in the handkerchief. What was it? What did it do? Use his explanation from Ch. 16.","By the end of Ch. 17, the war is over. What has Annemarie kept safe across the whole novel? What is she ready to give back to Ellen?","What is your strong CONCLUSION about Annemarie’s courage by the end of the novel? Use a moment from these chapters — and connect it to the historical fact that ordinary Danes really did this."],"focus":"Today: EDITING. Now you check for mechanics. Homophones (their/there), contractions (don’t), agreement (she walks vs. they walk), and capital letters.","rwm":"“‘Until then,’ Annemarie told him, ‘I will wear it myself.’” — Lois Lowry, Number the Stars , Ch. 17","plate":"plates/nts-19-3.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":19,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 19.4: Number the Stars Afterword","file":"lesson-19-4-nts-afterword.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-19-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 19.4: Number the Stars Afterword. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare historical fiction with historical information. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Lois Lowry talks straight to you here. She tells you which parts of the book she made up and which were real — the handkerchief, the fishing boats, the story of King Christian X, the courage of a whole country. Read it slowly, with your list from this week in front of you. Which of your guesses turned out to be real history? Which parts did Lowry invent, and why do you think she needed them?","Compare the afterword and this poem as ways of telling history. What can the poem do that the afterword cannot? What can the afterword do that the poem cannot?","Lowry begins naming which parts of the book came from real history. Which part surprised you by being real?","Lowry says the courage belonged to a whole country, not to one girl. How does knowing that change the story you just read?"],"chapterQs":["Lowry tells you that some things in the novel are real. Name ONE thing she says is real and ONE thing she made up. Use her own words.","Lowry writes about an old Danish friend who told her stories about Copenhagen in 1943. How does that real friend connect to Annemarie? What does that tell you about historical fiction?","Now compare your essay’s claim about courage to what Lowry says about the Danish people. Does your claim match? Where does it deepen? Use evidence from both the novel and the Afterword."],"focus":"Today: PUBLISH your final essay AND compare your claim about courage to what Lowry says in the Afterword. The novel meets the history.","plate":"plates/nts-19-4.jpg"},"words":[{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":19,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 19 Fluency: Your Published Opinion Essay","file":"lesson-19-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-19-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"conform","d":"To match a shape or rule; to fit in with something.","e":"📐"},{"w":"reflect","d":"To think back; or, to bounce light or an image.","e":"🪞"},{"w":"compare","d":"To look at two things and notice what is the same and what is different.","e":"⚖️"},{"w":"conclude","d":"To finish; to come to a final idea.","e":"🏁"},{"w":"proceed","d":"To move forward; to continue.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"concede","d":"To give in; to admit defeat or yield.","e":"🤝"},{"w":"access","d":"A way to reach or enter something.","e":"🔑"},{"w":"symbol","d":"An object or image that stands for an idea.","e":"⭐"}]},{"w":20,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 20.1: Freedom Train Ch. 1","file":"lesson-20-1-ft-ch1.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-20-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 20.1: Freedom Train Ch. 1. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify central idea in biography. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 1, watch for the central idea — what this chapter is mostly about. Notice the work Harriet does in the Big House, what she eats, and the window she keeps looking out of. If you had to say in one sentence what Sterling wants you to know about eight-year-old Harriet, what would it be? Which detail would you use to prove it?","Sterling shows us 8-year-old Harriet’s morning. What kind of girl does the chapter want us to see?","Harriet watches birds flying north and whispers her wonder. What is the chapter telling us with this small image?"],"chapterQs":["What is Harriet’s daily life like at age 8? Find TWO facts from the chapter that show how hard her day is.","Harriet watches birds flying NORTH through the window. What do the older slaves say about the North? Why might Harriet remember those stories?","What is the CENTRAL IDEA of Chapter 1? (The big point Sterling wants you to take away.) Use evidence from the chapter."],"focus":"This quarter you shift from opinion writing (\"I think...\") to research writing (\"Sources say...\"). Today: meet research writing. What it is, why it matters, and how it’s different from the opinion essay you just wrote."},"words":[{"w":"plantation","d":"A large farm where one crop is grown (in the South, often using slave labor).","e":"🌾"},{"w":"biography","d":"A true book about someone’s life, written by another person.","e":"📜"},{"w":"extend","d":"To stretch out or reach further.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"postpone","d":"To put off until a later time.","e":"⏳"}]},{"w":20,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 20.2: Freedom Train Ch. 2","file":"lesson-20-2-ft-ch2.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-20-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 20.2: Freedom Train Ch. 2. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify central idea in biography. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 2, turn the chapter’s facts into organized notes — who Harriet works for, what happens to her, and what she decides to do about it. Then read your notes back and look for the one idea they all point to. What is this chapter mostly about? Which of your notes proves it best?","Who is speaking? What does the caged bird stand for?","Miss Susan beats Harriet for things that aren’t her fault. How does Harriet respond? What does that tell us?","Harriet runs away — then comes back. Why? What does each choice tell us about her?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Miss Susan keep beating Harriet? Use specific examples from the chapter.","Harriet runs away for a few days. Where does she go? Why does she come back?","Turn the chapter into 3 FACT NOTES. (Notes look like: ‘Harriet — age, place, problem, action.’) Try to capture the central facts in 3 bullets."],"focus":"Today: BRAINSTORM. A research project starts with curiosity. What do you wonder about Tubman, the Underground Railroad, or the time she lived in? Today you cast a wide net.","rwm":"“She would hide her face against his rough cotton shirt.” — Dorothy Sterling, Freedom Train , Ch. 2"},"words":[{"w":"plantation","d":"A large farm where one crop is grown (in the South, often using slave labor).","e":"🌾"},{"w":"biography","d":"A true book about someone’s life, written by another person.","e":"📜"},{"w":"extend","d":"To stretch out or reach further.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"postpone","d":"To put off until a later time.","e":"⏳"}]},{"w":20,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 20.3: Freedom Train Ch. 3","file":"lesson-20-3-ft-ch3.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-20-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 20.3: Freedom Train Ch. 3. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify central idea in biography. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 3, find the central idea by listening for what is repeated. There is no schoolhouse here, so keep track of who teaches Harriet and what she learns from each of them — Daddy Ben, the songs, the news that passes through. What kind of school is this chapter really describing? What is Sterling telling you about learning?","Daddy Ben teaches Harriet the woods. Old Cudjoe teaches her songs about freedom. What kind of ‘school’ is this?","“We work from can to can’t.” What does this real saying tell us about life on the plantation?"],"chapterQs":["What kinds of work do the children learn to do? Find at least TWO things Harriet learns by working alongside adults.","There is no ‘school’ for Harriet. How do the older slaves teach the children about freedom, even though no one can read or write to them?","What is the CENTRAL IDEA of Chapter 3? Use the repeated idea of ‘learning’ in the chapter to explain."],"focus":"Today: NARROW. From your brainstorm, pick ONE question that’s big enough to research but small enough to answer in a short essay. Goldilocks territory.","rwm":"“We work from can to can’t.” — older women, Freedom Train , Ch. 3"},"words":[{"w":"plantation","d":"A large farm where one crop is grown (in the South, often using slave labor).","e":"🌾"},{"w":"biography","d":"A true book about someone’s life, written by another person.","e":"📜"},{"w":"extend","d":"To stretch out or reach further.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"postpone","d":"To put off until a later time.","e":"⏳"}]},{"w":20,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 20.4: Freedom Train Ch. 4","file":"lesson-20-4-ft-ch4.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-20-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 20.4: Freedom Train Ch. 4. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify central idea in biography. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 4, find the central idea and get ready to support it with evidence. There are two trains in this chapter: the real one whose whistle Harriet hears, and the Underground Railroad she learns about. What does each one mean to her? What one sentence covers both, and which details prove it?","Dunbar spends six lines on freedom and one on the cage. Explain how that imbalance makes the point , and connect it to what you are reading in Freedom Train .","Harriet hears a real train’s whistle in the distance. Why does Sterling make sure we notice it?","Then Harriet learns about the ‘Underground Railroad.’ How is this train different from the first one?"],"chapterQs":["What is the FIRST train Harriet ever hears? Use details from the chapter.","Then Harriet learns about a DIFFERENT kind of train. What is the ‘Underground Railroad’? Use the chapter’s words.","What is the CENTRAL IDEA of Chapter 4? (Think: how does the idea of a ‘train’ change in this chapter?) Use evidence."],"focus":"Today: SOURCES. A research project needs information from books, websites, or people. Today: plan WHERE you’ll go to find answers."},"words":[{"w":"plantation","d":"A large farm where one crop is grown (in the South, often using slave labor).","e":"🌾"},{"w":"biography","d":"A true book about someone’s life, written by another person.","e":"📜"},{"w":"extend","d":"To stretch out or reach further.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"postpone","d":"To put off until a later time.","e":"⏳"}]},{"w":20,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 20 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-20-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-20-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"plantation","d":"A large farm where one crop is grown (in the South, often using slave labor).","e":"🌾"},{"w":"biography","d":"A true book about someone’s life, written by another person.","e":"📜"},{"w":"extend","d":"To stretch out or reach further.","e":"➡️"},{"w":"postpone","d":"To put off until a later time.","e":"⏳"}]},{"w":21,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 21.1: Freedom Train Ch. 5","file":"lesson-21-1-ft-ch5.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-21-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 21.1: Freedom Train Ch. 5. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track chronology in informational text. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 5, track the order of what happens to Harriet. Master calls her ‘not worth a sixpence’ — but what happens to her body and her strength over time in this chapter? Which comes first: his judgment, or her growing tougher?","Master keeps calling Harriet worthless. What is actually happening to her at the same time?","By the end of the chapter, Harriet has grown stronger. What gives her that strength?"],"chapterQs":["Master calls Harriet ‘not worth a sixpence.’ What does he mean? Use details from the chapter.","The chapter shows Harriet getting STRONGER — not weaker. Find at least TWO things that show her growing strength. (Use chronological order: what happens first, then next.)","What is the central idea of Chapter 5? (Hint: the chapter title is ironic.) Use evidence to explain."],"focus":"This week you GATHER NOTES from your three sources. Today: take notes from your primary source, the biography you’re reading."},"words":[{"w":"chronology","d":"The order in which events happened, from first to last.","e":"📅"},{"w":"testimony","d":"A personal account of what someone has seen or done.","e":"🗣️"},{"w":"autograph","d":"A person’s own signature; literally, ‘self-writing.’","e":"✍️"},{"w":"emancipation","d":"The act of being set free.","e":"🕊️"}]},{"w":21,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 21.2: Freedom Train Ch. 6","file":"lesson-21-2-ft-ch6.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-21-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 21.2: Freedom Train Ch. 6. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track chronology in informational text. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 6, build the sequence of the escape . What does Harriet learn first? What does she decide next? What does she carry, and what step comes last before she is off the plantation? Try to list the steps in the order they happen.","Read the first six lines aloud the way you think this mother would say them. Where do you naturally pause?","Harriet decides to leave. What pushes her to decide NOW?","List the steps of her escape in order. What does she do first? Then? Finally?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Harriet decide to leave the plantation NOW? Use details from the chapter.","List the order of events in Harriet’s escape. Use transition words (FIRST, THEN, NEXT, FINALLY).","Why is the chapter called ‘Bound for the Promised Land’? What is the Promised Land for Harriet?"],"focus":"Today: take notes from a different kind of source — a website. (For example, the National Park Service Underground Railroad page.) Same rules: short, bulleted, factual.","rwm":"“I had a right to liberty or death.” — Harriet (quoted), Freedom Train , Ch. 6"},"words":[{"w":"chronology","d":"The order in which events happened, from first to last.","e":"📅"},{"w":"testimony","d":"A personal account of what someone has seen or done.","e":"🗣️"},{"w":"autograph","d":"A person’s own signature; literally, ‘self-writing.’","e":"✍️"},{"w":"emancipation","d":"The act of being set free.","e":"🕊️"}]},{"w":21,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 21.3: Freedom Train Ch. 7","file":"lesson-21-3-ft-ch7.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-21-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 21.3: Freedom Train Ch. 7. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track chronology in informational text. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 7, notice Harriet’s night-and-day pattern : she walks by night and hides by day. How many times does that cycle repeat? What interrupts it? Put the stops along her route in the order she reaches them.","Harriet walks at night, hides by day. Why does she pick that pattern?","Quakers and free black families help her. What risk do they take? Why might they take it?"],"chapterQs":["How does Harriet know which way is NORTH on her journey? Use the chapter to explain.","Who helps Harriet along the way? List at least TWO kinds of people who help, and how each helps.","Track the journey in chronological order. What happens FIRST, then NEXT, then FINALLY in this chapter? (3-4 sentences.)"],"focus":"Today: a third source. Could be another book from the library, a documentary, a museum site, a primary source (like Tubman’s own quoted words). Different sources = a stronger essay.","rwm":"“Follow the drinking gourd.” — spiritual code for the Big Dipper / North Star, Freedom Train , Ch. 7"},"words":[{"w":"chronology","d":"The order in which events happened, from first to last.","e":"📅"},{"w":"testimony","d":"A personal account of what someone has seen or done.","e":"🗣️"},{"w":"autograph","d":"A person’s own signature; literally, ‘self-writing.’","e":"✍️"},{"w":"emancipation","d":"The act of being set free.","e":"🕊️"}]},{"w":21,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 21.4: Freedom Train Ch. 8 & 9","file":"lesson-21-4-ft-ch8.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-21-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 21.4: Freedom Train Ch. 8 & 9. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track chronology in informational text. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 8 and Ch. 9, keep two timelines straight: what happens to Harriet now in Philadelphia, and what she keeps thinking back to in the South. Which events belong to the present, and which are memory?","List the four things wrong with her staircase. Explain what each one stands for in a life.","Chapter 8 follows the next stage of Harriet’s journey north. What has happened so far, and in what order? ⚠️ TO FILL: name one specific event from the first half of Ch. 8 to anchor the order.","Chapter 8 sits between Harriet’s escape in Ch. 7 and her arrival in Philadelphia in Ch. 9. Put this chapter’s events in the order they happen , then say which one had to come first for the rest to be possible. ⚠️ TO FILL: name two specific events from Ch. 8.","Harriet earns her first wages in Philadelphia. What does the money mean to her?","She’s free, but she feels like a stranger. Why? What is missing for her?"],"chapterQs":["Chapter 8 carries Harriet along the next stage of the route north. Retell the chapter’s events in the order they happen , and explain how one event led to the next. ⚠️ TO FILL: name two specific events from Ch. 8 in the prompt.","When Harriet arrives in Philadelphia, what is the FIRST job she finds? Use details from the chapter.","Why does Harriet feel like a STRANGER in Philadelphia, even though she is free? Use evidence.","What is the central idea of Chapter 9? (Hint: the chapter title comes from the Bible.) Use evidence about both the freedom and the loneliness."],"focus":"Today: ORGANIZE. Look at all the notes you’ve gathered. Sort them into 2-3 categories that match your research question. This is the bridge to next week, when you start drafting."},"words":[{"w":"chronology","d":"The order in which events happened, from first to last.","e":"📅"},{"w":"testimony","d":"A personal account of what someone has seen or done.","e":"🗣️"},{"w":"autograph","d":"A person’s own signature; literally, ‘self-writing.’","e":"✍️"},{"w":"emancipation","d":"The act of being set free.","e":"🕊️"}]},{"w":21,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 21 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-21-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-21-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"chronology","d":"The order in which events happened, from first to last.","e":"📅"},{"w":"testimony","d":"A personal account of what someone has seen or done.","e":"🗣️"},{"w":"autograph","d":"A person’s own signature; literally, ‘self-writing.’","e":"✍️"},{"w":"emancipation","d":"The act of being set free.","e":"🕊️"}]},{"w":22,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 22.1: Freedom Train Ch. 10","file":"lesson-22-1-ft-ch9.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-22-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 22.1: Freedom Train Ch. 10. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain cause and effect in history. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 10, watch for cause and effect. Harriet is free and earning wages in Philadelphia, but her family is still on the plantation. What causes her to stop wondering and decide to go back? What is the first effect of that decision?","Harriet has been free for months. What keeps pulling her thoughts back south?","Cause and effect: What CAUSES Harriet’s decision to go back? What is the first EFFECT of that decision?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Harriet decide to go BACK south, when she is finally free? Use details from the chapter to explain.","What is the CAUSE and EFFECT in this chapter? Name one thing that causes Harriet’s decision, and one effect of that decision on her life.","What is the central question of this chapter? What does it tell you about who Harriet is?"],"focus":"This week you start DRAFTING. Today: write a THESIS statement — one sentence that answers your research question."},"words":[{"w":"abolition","d":"The act of ending something, especially slavery.","e":"✊"},{"w":"conductor","d":"A guide on the Underground Railroad; or, the person who runs a train.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"obtain","d":"To get or acquire something.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"express","d":"To make a thought, feeling, or idea known to others.","e":"🗣️"}]},{"w":22,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 22.2: Freedom Train Ch. 11 & 12","file":"lesson-22-2-ft-ch10.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-22-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 22.2: Freedom Train Ch. 11 & 12. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain cause and effect in history. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 11 and Ch. 12, follow the chain of causes. Harriet wants her parents free, but they are old and cannot walk hundreds of miles — so what does she build, and what does building it make possible? Draw the arrows from her wish, to the problem, to her solution. Which link would break the whole chain if you took it out?","Which lines repeat? Draw the shape of this poem in your journal using arrows.","Chapter 11 comes after “Why Not Every Man?” (Ch. 10) and before “The Old Folks” (Ch. 12). What has caused what so far in this chapter? ⚠️ TO FILL: name one specific event from the first half of Ch. 11 to anchor the cause.","Name one cause and one effect from Chapter 11 , then explain why that effect mattered for what Harriet does next. ⚠️ TO FILL: name the specific cause and effect from Ch. 11.","Harriet’s parents can’t walk far. How does she solve that problem?","Why does this rescue matter so much to Harriet? Look at what her parents represent."],"chapterQs":["Explain one cause-and-effect chain from Chapter 11. What happened, what caused it, and what did it make possible? ⚠️ TO FILL: name the specific events from Ch. 11 in the prompt.","How does Harriet bring her old parents north? What does she build or arrange?","Why does this rescue matter so much to Harriet? Use evidence from the chapter to explain.","What does this chapter tell you about how Harriet has CHANGED since the start of the book? (Compare the 8-year-old in Ch. 1 to the woman in Ch. 12.)"],"focus":"Today: write your INTRODUCTION. It does two jobs: HOOK the reader, then state your thesis.","rwm":"“She would not leave them behind.” — narrator (about Harriet’s parents), Freedom Train , Ch. 12"},"words":[{"w":"abolition","d":"The act of ending something, especially slavery.","e":"✊"},{"w":"conductor","d":"A guide on the Underground Railroad; or, the person who runs a train.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"obtain","d":"To get or acquire something.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"express","d":"To make a thought, feeling, or idea known to others.","e":"🗣️"}]},{"w":22,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 22.3: Freedom Train Ch. 13","file":"lesson-22-3-ft-ch11.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-22-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 22.3: Freedom Train Ch. 13. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain cause and effect in history. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 13, sort real causes from things that only happen afterward. The Underground Railroad now reaches all the way to Canada, and the reward for catching Harriet has grown enormous — test whether one of those caused the other. Then look at the danger of the lion’s paw and her choice to keep going back. Does the growing danger cause her to stop, or is something else driving her?","The reward for catching Harriet has grown enormous. What is the ‘Lion’s Paw’? Why is the danger growing?","Most people would stop. Harriet doesn’t. What does that tell us about her?"],"chapterQs":["By Ch. 13, the Underground Railroad stretches farther. Where does it now reach? Why does that matter?","What is the ‘Lion’s Paw’? (Hint: it’s a metaphor for danger.) Explain what it stands for in the chapter.","Cause and effect: The reward for catching Harriet grew bigger. What was the EFFECT on Harriet’s work?"],"focus":"Today: draft your FIRST body paragraph. Take Category 1 from last week’s notes and turn it into a paragraph with topic sentence, facts, and a closing link.","rwm":"“She was the most wanted woman in the country.” — narrator (about the reward), Freedom Train , Ch. 13"},"words":[{"w":"abolition","d":"The act of ending something, especially slavery.","e":"✊"},{"w":"conductor","d":"A guide on the Underground Railroad; or, the person who runs a train.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"obtain","d":"To get or acquire something.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"express","d":"To make a thought, feeling, or idea known to others.","e":"🗣️"}]},{"w":22,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 22.4: Freedom Train Ch. 14","file":"lesson-22-4-ft-ch12.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-22-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 22.4: Freedom Train Ch. 14. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain cause and effect in history. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 14, weigh the causes. The people Harriet rescues and the abolitionists she works with begin calling her Moses. List the reasons that name fits — the many trips into Maryland, the dozens of people she leads out, the fact that she never loses one. Which of those reasons mattered most in earning her that name, and why?","Explain how the shape of this poem says the same thing the words say. Why put the living buffaloes in the middle?","People start calling Harriet Moses . Why that name?","How has Harriet changed across the book? Compare the 8-year-old in Ch. 1 to the woman now called Moses."],"chapterQs":["Why do people start calling Harriet Moses ? Use the chapter to explain.","Across her many trips on the Underground Railroad, Harriet has one rule. What is it? Why does it matter?","What is the central idea of Chapter 14? (Hint: it’s about identity and reputation.) Use evidence."],"focus":"Today: draft your SECOND body paragraph. Take Category 2 from your notes and turn it into a paragraph with the SAME 3 parts (topic, facts, link)."},"words":[{"w":"abolition","d":"The act of ending something, especially slavery.","e":"✊"},{"w":"conductor","d":"A guide on the Underground Railroad; or, the person who runs a train.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"obtain","d":"To get or acquire something.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"express","d":"To make a thought, feeling, or idea known to others.","e":"🗣️"}]},{"w":22,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 22 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-22-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-22-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"abolition","d":"The act of ending something, especially slavery.","e":"✊"},{"w":"conductor","d":"A guide on the Underground Railroad; or, the person who runs a train.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"obtain","d":"To get or acquire something.","e":"🎯"},{"w":"express","d":"To make a thought, feeling, or idea known to others.","e":"🗣️"}]},{"w":23,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 23.1: Freedom Train Ch. 15","file":"lesson-23-1-ft-ch13.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-23-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 23.1: Freedom Train Ch. 15. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain author’s perspective toward a topic. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 15, listen to Sterling’s word choice . Harriet meets Frederick Douglass, John Brown, and other leaders of the movement, and they begin calling her General Tubman. Which words does Sterling pick for Harriet when those famous men are in the room, and which words does she pick for the men? What do those choices tell you about who Sterling admires most?","Harriet meets famous abolitionists. What do they call her? Why?","Wendell Phillips’ praise is the chapter’s title. What does Sterling want us to think of Harriet by the end?"],"chapterQs":["Who are some of the famous abolitionists Harriet meets in this chapter? Name TWO and explain what they think of her.","Wendell Phillips calls Harriet ‘the most of a man.’ What does he mean? Why is it (oddly) one of the highest things he could say in his time?","What is Sterling’s perspective on Harriet? Find evidence of HOW Sterling feels about her (admiring, neutral, critical?)."],"focus":"This week you ELABORATE — you make each body paragraph stronger by adding more specific facts. Today: take each body paragraph from last week and add one more fact."},"words":[{"w":"elevate","d":"To lift up; to raise to a higher level.","e":"⬆️"},{"w":"resist","d":"To stand against; to refuse to give in.","e":"✊"},{"w":"persist","d":"To keep going; to refuse to stop.","e":"🔁"},{"w":"abolitionist","d":"A person who worked to end (abolish) slavery.","e":"📜"}]},{"w":23,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 23.2: Freedom Train Ch. 16","file":"lesson-23-2-ft-ch14.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-23-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 23.2: Freedom Train Ch. 16. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain author’s perspective toward a topic. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 16, watch what Sterling decides to spend her pages on . The Civil War has begun, and Harriet joins the Department of the South as a scout, a spy, and a nurse. Does Sterling give more room to the army and its plans, or to Harriet’s own work? What does that choice tell you about the story she thinks is worth telling?","Find three figures of speech here. What is each one doing to persuade you?","The Union army needs Harriet. What does she agree to do for them?","Why is Harriet specifically valuable as a scout? Use details from the chapter."],"chapterQs":["What jobs does Harriet do for the Union army? List at least TWO and explain what each requires.","Why is Harriet especially valuable as a SCOUT in South Carolina? Use details from the chapter.","Sterling writes about Harriet in uniform with admiration. Find ONE detail or word choice that shows how Sterling FEELS about Harriet’s war work."],"focus":"Today: add SPECIFIC details — names of people, exact dates, specific numbers. These make your research writing real instead of vague.","rwm":"“She served her country at last in uniform.” — narrator, Freedom Train , Ch. 16"},"words":[{"w":"elevate","d":"To lift up; to raise to a higher level.","e":"⬆️"},{"w":"resist","d":"To stand against; to refuse to give in.","e":"✊"},{"w":"persist","d":"To keep going; to refuse to stop.","e":"🔁"},{"w":"abolitionist","d":"A person who worked to end (abolish) slavery.","e":"📜"}]},{"w":23,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 23.3: Freedom Train Ch. 16","file":"lesson-23-3-ft-ch15.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-23-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 23.3: Freedom Train Ch. 16. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain author’s perspective toward a topic. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 16, sort the facts from Sterling’s opinions . The date, the three gunboats, the Combahee River, and the more than 700 people freed by morning can all be checked. What does Sterling add on top of those facts about Harriet and about the officers around her? Which sentences could you look up, and which ones are telling you what Sterling believes?","On June 2, 1863, three gunboats go up the Combahee River. What is Harriet’s role on board?","More than 700 people are freed in one night. What does this tell us about what one person’s knowledge can do?"],"chapterQs":["When and where did the Combahee River Raid happen? Give the date and the location.","How many people did the raid free? What did Harriet do that no other commander could have done?","What does Sterling want you to feel about the Combahee Raid? Find evidence that shows her perspective."],"focus":"Today: add a direct QUOTE. Pick one short, powerful quote from one of your sources. Use it in one of your body paragraphs.","rwm":"“She brought them out by the hundreds.” — narrator (about the Combahee Raid), Freedom Train , Ch. 16"},"words":[{"w":"elevate","d":"To lift up; to raise to a higher level.","e":"⬆️"},{"w":"resist","d":"To stand against; to refuse to give in.","e":"✊"},{"w":"persist","d":"To keep going; to refuse to stop.","e":"🔁"},{"w":"abolitionist","d":"A person who worked to end (abolish) slavery.","e":"📜"}]},{"w":23,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 23.4: Freedom Train Ch. 17","file":"lesson-23-4-ft-ch16.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-23-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 23.4: Freedom Train Ch. 17. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain author’s perspective toward a topic. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["As you read Ch. 17, watch how Sterling handles the long, slow stretch of the war. She keeps her attention on small things — conversations, songs, kindnesses — while Harriet nurses, scouts, and organizes. How would this chapter read if a Union officer told it instead? What would he leave out that Sterling keeps, and what does that difference tell you about her perspective?","Find one place in this week’s reading where the author uses figurative language rather than plain fact. Explain what the figure of speech makes you believe that a plain sentence would not.","The war drags on. Harriet keeps working. What jobs does she do during this stretch?","‘Our time is coming.’ What does that phrase mean to people during the war?"],"chapterQs":["What is Harriet doing during this stretch of the war? Name at least TWO of her jobs.","‘Our time is coming’ — what does this phrase MEAN to enslaved people during the Civil War? Use the chapter to explain.","How does Sterling balance HARD FACTS (dates, places) with FEELINGS (Harriet’s hopes, fears)? Find one example of each."],"focus":"Today: take everything from this week and ELABORATE one full body paragraph. Add facts, specifics, AND a quote. This is the version you’ll polish next week."},"words":[{"w":"elevate","d":"To lift up; to raise to a higher level.","e":"⬆️"},{"w":"resist","d":"To stand against; to refuse to give in.","e":"✊"},{"w":"persist","d":"To keep going; to refuse to stop.","e":"🔁"},{"w":"abolitionist","d":"A person who worked to end (abolish) slavery.","e":"📜"}]},{"w":23,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 23 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-23-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-23-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"elevate","d":"To lift up; to raise to a higher level.","e":"⬆️"},{"w":"resist","d":"To stand against; to refuse to give in.","e":"✊"},{"w":"persist","d":"To keep going; to refuse to stop.","e":"🔁"},{"w":"abolitionist","d":"A person who worked to end (abolish) slavery.","e":"📜"}]},{"w":24,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 24.1: Freedom Train Ch. 18","file":"lesson-24-1-ft-ch17.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-24-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 24.1: Freedom Train Ch. 18. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how details support central idea. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Sterling packs Ch. 18 with specific details — numbers, images, sounds. As you read, ask yourself: Why does Sterling include THIS detail? What does it show about Harriet, the freed people, or freedom itself?","Harriet gives up her trip to Charleston to work in the hospitals instead. Why does she make this choice?","The chapter ends with a conductor forcing Harriet into the baggage car. Why does Sterling end the chapter this way? What is she saying about ‘victory’?"],"chapterQs":["Harriet gives up going to the flag-raising at Fort Sumter to work in Virginia hospitals instead. What does this choice tell you about her character?","Name ONE moment that feels like a real victory and ONE moment that doesn’t feel like victory at all.","Sterling ends the book with a conductor forcing Harriet into the baggage car. What central idea is Sterling trying to leave the reader with?"],"focus":"This week you REVISE and EDIT your research essay. Today: read your essay OUT LOUD."},"words":[{"w":"torpedoes","d":"Underwater explosive devices used to destroy ships.","e":"💣"},{"w":"expedition","d":"An organized journey for a specific military or exploratory purpose.","e":"🗺️"},{"w":"proclamation","d":"An official public announcement, especially by a government leader.","e":"📜"},{"w":"territory","d":"An area of land under the control of a particular group or government.","e":"🌙"}]},{"w":24,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 24.2: Freedom Train Ch. 19 Victory","file":"lesson-24-2-ft-lookback.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-24-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 24.2: Freedom Train Ch. 19 Victory. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how details support central idea. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Sterling’s central idea in Ch. 19 is that the victory is real but not finished. As you read, sort your details into two piles: the ones that show the victory is real , and the ones that show it is not finished . If a detail does neither job, set it aside and be ready to say why. Then look hard at the scene Sterling chose to end on, and decide which pile it belongs in.","Whitman wrote this poem after Lincoln was assassinated — the war was won, but the president was dead. In Chapter 19, Sterling describes a similar bittersweet moment: the war ends, but Harriet is thrown off a train on her way home. What feeling do both the poem and the chapter share? What word would you use to describe it?","Secretary Seward says to Harriet, “This is your victory.” What does he mean? What has Harriet contributed that makes the victory hers?","Harriet reflects: “All those years of fighting, and never a scratch from a Rebel sword or gun. Had to wait till I was coming home to get my first war wound.” What is the “war wound” she is talking about? Why is this moment so painful after everything she has survived?"],"chapterQs":["Secretary Seward says, “This is your victory, Harriet,” but her parents are ailing and reproachful about money, and she herself has been ill. What is the contrast between the public victory and Harriet’s personal situation? Use at least two details from the chapter.","Harriet is appointed Matron of the hospital at Fortress Monroe and organizes the cleanup “like a whirlwind.” What does her work in the hospital reveal about her character — even as the war is ending and others are celebrating?","On her way home, a conductor forces Harriet into the baggage car despite her War Department pass — and it takes three men to drag her down the aisle. She reflects that she fought through the whole war without a wound, “and had to wait till I was coming home to get my first war wound.” What does this incident show about the limits of “victory” for Black Americans in 1865?"],"focus":"Make four passes through your research writing — one check at a time. Don’t try to fix everything at once."},"words":[{"w":"torpedoes","d":"Underwater explosive devices used to destroy ships.","e":"💣"},{"w":"expedition","d":"An organized journey for a specific military or exploratory purpose.","e":"🗺️"},{"w":"proclamation","d":"An official public announcement, especially by a government leader.","e":"📜"},{"w":"territory","d":"An area of land under the control of a particular group or government.","e":"🌙"},{"w":"centenarians","d":"People who are 100 years old or older. Harriet Tubman lived to be about 93.","e":"👴"},{"w":"pension","d":"Regular payments given to a veteran or retired person. Harriet fought for years to receive her Army pension.","e":"💼"},{"w":"mortgage","d":"A loan used to buy property, paid back over time. Harriet bought land in Auburn, NY with a mortgage.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"temperance","d":"The movement to reduce or ban alcohol. Harriet supported temperance as part of her work for social reform.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":24,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 24.3: Freedom Train Ch. 20: The Years of Peace","file":"lesson-24-3-ft-craft.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-24-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 24.3: Freedom Train Ch. 20: The Years of Peace. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✍️ Copywork — Study the sentence, then copy it carefully in cursive.. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Ch. 20 follows Harriet through her long years at home in Auburn. Pick one detail from the chapter and build the bridge: this detail shows ___ about Harriet, because ___. Then do the same work with the last image Sterling leaves you on, and ask what idea she wanted that image to prove.","Harriet has returned home to Auburn after the war. She fixes up the house, takes in blind Alice and Baby Susan, and works beside Ben and Old Rit. What does it tell you about Harriet that she fills her home with people who need help, even though she herself has very little?","Harriet is in a wheelchair in old age. She still tells war stories — and slides out of her chair to demonstrate crawling past sentries. What does this detail show about how she thinks about herself, even at the very end of her life?"],"chapterQs":["How did Harriet continue to serve others after the war ended? Use at least two specific details from Ch. 20.","Why did Harriet struggle financially even though she was famous? What does this reveal about her priorities?","What details from Harriet’s final years show that she never lost her fighting spirit? Choose at least two details and explain what each one shows."],"rwm":"“When they lay her down, the North Star was shining.”"},"words":[{"w":"torpedoes","d":"Underwater explosive devices used to destroy ships.","e":"💣"},{"w":"expedition","d":"An organized journey for a specific military or exploratory purpose.","e":"🗺️"},{"w":"proclamation","d":"An official public announcement, especially by a government leader.","e":"📜"},{"w":"territory","d":"An area of land under the control of a particular group or government.","e":"🌙"},{"w":"centenarians","d":"People who are 100 years old or older. Harriet Tubman lived to be about 93.","e":"👴"},{"w":"pension","d":"Regular payments given to a veteran or retired person. Harriet fought for years to receive her Army pension.","e":"💼"},{"w":"mortgage","d":"A loan used to buy property, paid back over time. Harriet bought land in Auburn, NY with a mortgage.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"temperance","d":"The movement to reduce or ban alcohol. Harriet supported temperance as part of her work for social reform.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":24,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 24.4: Freedom Train — Final Reflection","file":"lesson-24-4-ft-reflection.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-24-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 24.4: Freedom Train — Final Reflection. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The poem says Harriet “left us something harder” than a monument. What does that mean? What did she actually leave behind, and why is it harder to carry than stone?","How does this poem connect to what you read in Freedom Train ? Does Sterling seem to agree with the poet?","You have finished the whole biography. Say in one sentence what it is about . Then find one detail from early in the book, one from the middle, and one from the end that all point at that same sentence. If the ending does not fit your sentence, it may be your sentence that needs changing."],"chapterQs":["How did Harriet change from the little girl rocking the baby in Chapter 1 to the woman who led the Combahee River Raid and organized hospitals? Use details from across the book to explain her growth.","Sterling titled this biography “Freedom Train.” Why is that title a good fit? What different kinds of freedom does Harriet fight for throughout the book?","What moment from the book will you remember most? Describe the moment and explain why it matters — not just in the book, but more broadly."],"focus":"Write the conclusion for your research essay. A strong conclusion does three things: it restates the main idea (in new words), summarizes your strongest evidence, and ends with a lasting thought — why this matters."},"words":[{"w":"centenarians","d":"People who are 100 years old or older. Harriet Tubman lived to be about 93.","e":"👴"},{"w":"pension","d":"Regular payments given to a veteran or retired person. Harriet fought for years to receive her Army pension.","e":"💼"},{"w":"mortgage","d":"A loan used to buy property, paid back over time. Harriet bought land in Auburn, NY with a mortgage.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"temperance","d":"The movement to reduce or ban alcohol. Harriet supported temperance as part of her work for social reform.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":24,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 24 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-24-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-24-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say every word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of the passage?"]},"words":[{"w":"torpedoes","d":"Underwater explosive devices used to destroy ships.","e":"💣"},{"w":"expedition","d":"An organized journey for a specific military or exploratory purpose.","e":"🗺️"},{"w":"proclamation","d":"An official public announcement, especially by a government leader.","e":"📜"},{"w":"territory","d":"An area of land under the control of a particular group or government.","e":"🌙"},{"w":"centenarians","d":"People who are 100 years old or older. Harriet Tubman lived to be about 93.","e":"👴"},{"w":"pension","d":"Regular payments given to a veteran or retired person. Harriet fought for years to receive her Army pension.","e":"💼"},{"w":"mortgage","d":"A loan used to buy property, paid back over time. Harriet bought land in Auburn, NY with a mortgage.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"temperance","d":"The movement to reduce or ban alcohol. Harriet supported temperance as part of her work for social reform.","e":"🚫"}]},{"w":25,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 25.1: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources","file":"lesson-25-1-ft-sources.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-25-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 25.1: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare accounts of the same event. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Both sources describe the same day on the Combahee River. Read them one at a time, then say the event in your own words — the part both writers agree happened. Now look at what only Source A tells you, and what only Source B tells you. Why might one day come out on the page two different ways?","Source A does not name Harriet Tubman. It calls her “a Black woman” and “She-Moses.” Why might the reporter have left out her name?","Source B includes Tubman’s own words. How does hearing from Tubman herself change the way you understand the raid?"],"chapterQs":["What is the Combahee River Raid? Use details from BOTH sources to explain what happened in 2–3 sentences.","Source A calls Tubman “a Black woman” and “She-Moses” but never uses her name. Source B names her directly. Why does it matter whether a source names the person? What difference does it make?","Both sources say the raid was successful. Find ONE detail that Source A includes that Source B does not, OR one detail that Source B includes that Source A does not. Why might one writer have included it and the other left it out?"],"focus":"REVISE your expository essay. Revising means improving your ideas , organization , and word choice — not fixing spelling yet."},"words":[{"w":"gallant","d":"Brave and heroic, especially in a noble cause.","e":"⚔️"},{"w":"commissary","d":"A place where food and supplies are stored, especially for the military.","e":"🏪"},{"w":"rebeldom","d":"The Confederate states; the territory or state of being in rebellion.","e":"🏴"},{"w":"contraband","d":"During the Civil War, enslaved people who escaped to Union lines.","e":"🔗"}]},{"w":25,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 25.2: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources","file":"lesson-25-2-ft-sources.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-25-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 25.2: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare accounts of the same event. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Today you have three accounts of one raid. Pick a fact that more than one source reports — how many people were freed, or what Harriet is called — and set each version down side by side. Where do the sources line up? Where do they clash? Then decide whether that clash is a small gap or one that changes what you think happened.","What feeling or image stands out to you in this poem? The poet uses informal, rhythmic language — almost like a song. Why do you think Greenfield chose to write about Tubman this way instead of in formal English?","Source A and Source B were both written in 1863. Bradford’s biography came 6 years later. How might the time difference affect what each writer included?","Which source feels most personal? Why? Think about whose voice you hear in each account."],"chapterQs":["Create a simple comparison: list ONE way Source A and Source B are SIMILAR and ONE way they are DIFFERENT . Explain why the difference matters.","Source C (Bradford’s biography) was written 6 years after the raid. What is one ADVANTAGE of writing about an event later? What is one DISADVANTAGE ?","If you could only read ONE of the three sources to learn about the Combahee River Raid, which would you choose? Explain your reasoning using at least one specific detail from that source."],"focus":"EDITING. Editing means fixing grammar, spelling, and punctuation — not changing your ideas. Read your essay slowly, one sentence at a time.","rwm":"“Montgomery’s gallant band of 300 soldiers under the guidance of a Black woman, dashed into the enemies’ country.”"},"words":[{"w":"gallant","d":"Brave and heroic, especially in a noble cause.","e":"⚔️"},{"w":"commissary","d":"A place where food and supplies are stored, especially for the military.","e":"🏪"},{"w":"rebeldom","d":"The Confederate states; the territory or state of being in rebellion.","e":"🏴"},{"w":"contraband","d":"During the Civil War, enslaved people who escaped to Union lines.","e":"🔗"}]},{"w":25,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 25.3: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources","file":"lesson-25-3-ft-sources.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-25-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 25.3: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✍️ Copywork — Study the sentence, then copy it carefully in cursive.. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Put your three sources in their places. Who was close to the raid, and who wrote about it years later from other people’s words? Then ask what each kind of writer can give you that the other cannot. Which source would you use to learn what the day looked like , and which would you use to learn what it meant ?","If Source A was the only account that survived, what would we never know about the Combahee River Raid?","How does reading Tubman’s own words (Source B) change the story compared to reading about her (Sources A and C)?"],"chapterQs":["Explain ONE thing you understand about the Combahee River Raid NOW that you would NOT understand if you had only read Source A. Use a specific detail from Source B or Source C.","Source A and Source B are PRIMARY sources — written by people who were alive when the event happened. Source C is a SECONDARY source — written later by someone who researched the event. What is one advantage of each type?","Imagine you are writing a report about the Combahee River Raid. Which source would you quote first, and why? Use a specific detail from that source to support your choice."],"rwm":"“We weakened the rebels somewhat on the Combahee River by taking and bringing away seven hundred and fifty-six head of their most valuable livestock.”"},"words":[{"w":"gallant","d":"Brave and heroic, especially in a noble cause.","e":"⚔️"},{"w":"commissary","d":"A place where food and supplies are stored, especially for the military.","e":"🏪"},{"w":"rebeldom","d":"The Confederate states; the territory or state of being in rebellion.","e":"🏴"},{"w":"contraband","d":"During the Civil War, enslaved people who escaped to Union lines.","e":"🔗"}]},{"w":25,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 25.4: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources","file":"lesson-25-4-ft-sources.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-25-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 25.4: Freedom Train — Comparing Sources. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the facts and dates, and to how one event led to the next. Think about the central idea each section is building. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Hughes wrote this poem about claiming a place at America’s table. How does this poem connect to what you’ve learned about Harriet Tubman this unit?","Harriet fought for a seat at that table — literally and figuratively. What would she say if she read this poem?","You have three accounts of the Combahee River Raid. If you had to tell someone what happened that day, which account would you lean on? Say why — who wrote it, when they wrote it, and how close they were to the raid. Then name one thing about that day that none of the three sources can tell you.","1. You’ve now read a biography of Tubman AND real historical documents. How did the primary sources change or deepen what you understood from the biography?","2. If you could ask Harriet Tubman one question, what would it be? Why?"],"chapterQs":["This week you compared three accounts of the Combahee River Raid. Explain in your own words: WHY is it important to read more than one account of the same event? Use a specific example from this week’s sources.","Think about the Freedom Train biography AND the primary sources you read this week. How did reading Tubman’s actual words (in Source B) compare to reading about her in Dorothy Sterling’s biography? What did each type of writing help you understand?","Harriet Tubman dictated her account of the raid to Franklin Sanborn because she could not read or write. What does this tell you about who gets to tell their own story in history — and why primary sources like hers are so valuable?"]},"words":[{"w":"gallant","d":"Brave and heroic, especially in battle. The newspaper called Montgomery’s soldiers a “gallant band.”","e":"⚔"},{"w":"commissary","d":"A store or supply depot, especially for an army. The raid destroyed “millions of dollars worth of commissary stores.”","e":"🍮"},{"w":"contraband","d":"During the Civil War, enslaved people who escaped to Union lines were called “contrabands.” Tubman used this word with bitter irony — calling people “livestock.”","e":"🔗"},{"w":"rebeldom","d":"The territory or world of the rebels (the Confederacy). The newspaper said the raid struck “terror to the heart of rebeldom.”","e":"💥"}]},{"w":25,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 25 Fluency: Freedom Train","file":"lesson-25-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-25-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[{"w":"gallant","d":"Brave and heroic, especially in a noble cause.","e":"⚔️"},{"w":"commissary","d":"A place where food and supplies are stored, especially for the military.","e":"🏪"},{"w":"rebeldom","d":"The Confederate states; the territory or state of being in rebellion.","e":"🏴"},{"w":"contraband","d":"During the Civil War, enslaved people who escaped to Union lines.","e":"🔗"}]},{"w":26,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 26.1: Prince Caspian Ch. 1","file":"lesson-26-1-pc-ch1.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-26-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 26.1: Prince Caspian Ch. 1. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Orient yourself in fantasy settings. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The children find old stone walls and an orchard that has gone wild. What do those clues tell you about where they are — and how long ago people lived here?","The children feel a strange pulling. Then suddenly they’re somewhere completely different. How would YOU feel if this happened to you?","The children find old stone walls and an orchard that has gone wild. What do you think this place used to be?"],"chapterQs":["Describe the setting of Chapter 1 in your own words. What does the island look like, sound like, and feel like? Use at least two details from the chapter.","The children are pulled from a train station into a completely different world. How does C. S. Lewis make the transition from the real world to the fantasy world feel sudden and surprising? Point to a specific moment in the chapter.","At the end of the chapter, the children find old stone walls and an overgrown orchard. What do these details suggest about the place they’ve arrived? What questions do they raise?"]},"words":[{"w":"thicket","d":"A dense group of bushes or small trees growing closely together.","e":"🌲"},{"w":"brambles","d":"Thorny bushes, especially wild blackberry or raspberry bushes with prickly stems.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"inhabited","d":"Lived in; having people or creatures living there.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"junction","d":"A place where things meet or connect, especially where train lines split.","e":"🚂"}]},{"w":26,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 26.2: Prince Caspian — The Ancient Treasure House","file":"lesson-26-2-pc-ch2.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-26-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 26.2: Prince Caspian — The Ancient Treasure House. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Orient yourself in fantasy settings. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The children dig up gifts they were given long ago. What do those objects tell you about how much time has passed in this world since they were last here?","What feeling or image stands out to you in this poem? The poem describes an ancient place that has fallen to ruin but still holds hints of its past glory. How does that connect to what the children discover in Chapter 2?","Susan finds a golden chess-knight by the well. Why does this small object matter so much to the children? What does it tell them about where they really are?","Peter says “It is my sword Rhindon. With it I killed the Wolf.” How does finding these old gifts change the way Peter thinks about himself?"],"chapterQs":["How do the children figure out that the ruins are Cair Paravel? Trace the clues they find , starting with the chess-knight. Use at least two details .","Each child had a gift from their time as Kings and Queens. What does each gift tell you about that character? Choose TWO characters and explain.","Hundreds of years have passed in Narnia while only one year passed in England. How do the children react to this discovery? What does this tell you about the difference between time in fantasy worlds and time in our world ?"],"rwm":"“It twanged: a chirruping twang that vibrated through the whole room. And that one small noise brought back the old days to the children’s minds more than anything that had happened yet.”"},"words":[{"w":"thicket","d":"A dense group of bushes or small trees growing closely together.","e":"🌲"},{"w":"brambles","d":"Thorny bushes, especially wild blackberry or raspberry bushes with prickly stems.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"inhabited","d":"Lived in; having people or creatures living there.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"junction","d":"A place where things meet or connect, especially where train lines split.","e":"🚂"}]},{"w":26,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 26.3: Prince Caspian Ch. 3","file":"lesson-26-3-pc-ch3.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-26-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 26.3: Prince Caspian Ch. 3. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The Dwarf speaks about a king the children have never heard of. What can you learn about Narnia today just from the things he says about it?","The soldiers were about to drown the Dwarf. What does this tell you about the kind of world Narnia has become?","Trumpkin says he is “a messenger of King Caspian’s.” The children have never heard of Caspian. What questions does this raise?","What does Trumpkin look like? What kind of person does he seem to be?","Lewis packs a LOT of physical detail into two sentences. Notice how he organizes it: height first, then face . What effect does “beak-like nose and twinkling black eyes” create?","Write 2 sentences describing a character. Start with their size or shape, then zoom in on their face."],"chapterQs":["Edmund figures out why the ruins look so ancient even though the children were only gone for one year. Explain his reasoning in your own words.","Susan shoots a soldier to save the Dwarf. This is a serious moment — she could have hurt someone badly. Why does Lewis include this scene? What does it show about Susan and about the kind of world Narnia has become?","At the end of the chapter, Trumpkin introduces himself as a messenger of “King Caspian.” Based on everything you’ve learned so far, what do you think has happened in Narnia since the children left? Make a prediction using at least one detail from the chapter."],"rwm":"“Like most Dwarfs he was very stocky and deep-chested.” — C. S. Lewis, Prince Caspian , Ch. 3"},"words":[{"w":"thicket","d":"A dense group of bushes or small trees growing closely together.","e":"🌲"},{"w":"brambles","d":"Thorny bushes, especially wild blackberry or raspberry bushes with prickly stems.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"inhabited","d":"Lived in; having people or creatures living there.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"junction","d":"A place where things meet or connect, especially where train lines split.","e":"🚂"}]},{"w":26,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 26.4: Prince Caspian Discussion","file":"lesson-26-4-pc-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-26-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 26.4: Prince Caspian Discussion. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["How does this poem connect to what the Pevensie children experience when they return to Narnia? What does the poem suggest the land itself remembers?","Put the three chapters side by side. What kind of world is Narnia now — who has power in it, and what is dangerous there?","Of everything you’ve read so far, what moment surprised you the most? Why?","The children were once Kings and Queens of Narnia. Now they’re back — but everything has changed. How do you think this feels for them?","Trumpkin is about to tell them the story of Prince Caspian. What do you MOST want to know?"],"chapterQs":["Think about the three chapters you read this week. How does the setting of Narnia CHANGE from Chapter 1 to Chapter 3? Describe at least TWO specific ways the children’s understanding of where they are grows or shifts.","Objects play an important role in these chapters — the chess-knight, the sword Rhindon, Susan’s bow, Lucy’s cordial. Choose ONE object and explain what it means to the character who owns it. Why is finding it again important?","Compare the fantasy setting of Narnia with the real-world setting of the train station in Chapter 1. What makes a fantasy setting DIFFERENT from a real-world setting? Use at least one detail from the novel to support your answer."]},"words":[{"w":"junction","d":"A place where things meet or connect, especially where train lines split.","e":"🚂"},{"w":"thicket","d":"A dense group of bushes or small trees growing closely together.","e":"🌲"},{"w":"brambles","d":"Thorny bushes, especially wild blackberry or raspberry bushes with prickly stems.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"inhabited","d":"Lived in; having people or creatures living there.","e":"🏠"}]},{"w":26,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 26 Fluency: Prince Caspian","file":"lesson-26-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-26-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[{"w":"thicket","d":"A dense group of bushes or small trees growing closely together.","e":"🌲"},{"w":"brambles","d":"Thorny bushes, especially wild blackberry or raspberry bushes with prickly stems.","e":"🌿"},{"w":"inhabited","d":"Lived in; having people or creatures living there.","e":"🏠"},{"w":"junction","d":"A place where things meet or connect, especially where train lines split.","e":"🚂"}]},{"w":27,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 27.1: Prince Caspian Ch. 4","file":"lesson-27-1-pc-ch4.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-27-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 27.1: Prince Caspian Ch. 4. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track multiple groups of characters. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Who helps Caspian? Who controls him? Who does he trust?","Miraz forbids Caspian from talking about Old Narnia. Why would a king be afraid of old stories?","Doctor Cornelius teaches Caspian the truth at night, in secret, on top of a tower. Why does Lewis set this scene at night? What does the secrecy tell you?"],"chapterQs":["This chapter introduces a new group of characters — Caspian, Miraz, the Nurse, and Doctor Cornelius. For each character, write ONE word that describes them and explain why you chose it.","Miraz tells Caspian that Old Narnia is “nonsense for babies.” But Doctor Cornelius tells him it’s all true. Why do these two adults give Caspian completely different versions of history? What does each one gain by telling Caspian what they tell him?","Doctor Cornelius reveals that Miraz is a usurper — he killed Caspian’s father and took the throne. How do you think Caspian feels when he hears this? Use a detail from the chapter to support your answer."]},"words":[{"w":"tutor","d":"A private teacher or guide who instructs one student or a small group.","e":"📚"},{"w":"usurper","d":"Someone who takes power illegally, especially by force — like a king who stole the throne.","e":"👑"},{"w":"astronomy","d":"The scientific study of stars, planets, and outer space.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"sternly","d":"In a strict, serious, and firm way — showing disapproval.","e":"😐"}]},{"w":27,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 27.2: Prince Caspian — Caspian's Adventure in the Mountains","file":"lesson-27-2-pc-ch5.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-27-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 27.2: Prince Caspian — Caspian's Adventure in the Mountains. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Track multiple groups of characters. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Caspian meets a band of Old Narnians, and they do not all feel the same way about him. Who welcomes him, who holds back, and what does each one seem to know about him?","What feeling or image stands out to you? The poem describes someone fleeing with almost nothing. How does this connect to what happens to Caspian in Chapter 5?","Doctor Cornelius gives Caspian Queen Susan’s horn and tells him to blow it only at greatest need. Why does Lewis include this detail? What might it mean for later in the story?","Trufflehunter trusts Caspian immediately, but Nikabrik doesn’t. What does each character’s reaction tell you about them?"],"chapterQs":["Why is Caspian’s life suddenly in danger? Explain what changed and how Doctor Cornelius responds.","When Caspian wakes up in the cave, he meets three very different creatures. Compare Trufflehunter and Nikabrik. How does each one feel about Caspian, and why?","Doctor Cornelius is a half-dwarf who has been hiding his identity at Miraz’s court. What risks has he been taking, and why does he take them?"],"rwm":"“Dear Prince, you must leave this castle at once and go to seek your fortune in the wide world. Your life is in danger here.”"},"words":[{"w":"tutor","d":"A private teacher or guide who instructs one student or a small group.","e":"📚"},{"w":"usurper","d":"Someone who takes power illegally, especially by force — like a king who stole the throne.","e":"👑"},{"w":"astronomy","d":"The scientific study of stars, planets, and outer space.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"sternly","d":"In a strict, serious, and firm way — showing disapproval.","e":"😐"},{"w":"subterranean","d":"Underground. The Seven Brothers of Shuddering Wood had a subterranean forge — their workshop was beneath the earth.","e":"⛏️"},{"w":"victuals","d":"Food and provisions. The Old Narnians brought victuals to share at the gathering.","e":"🍖"}]},{"w":27,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 27.3: Prince Caspian Ch. 6","file":"lesson-27-3-pc-ch6.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-27-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 27.3: Prince Caspian Ch. 6. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Who are the warriors? Who are the thinkers? Who are the joyful ones? Tracking by role helps you remember who’s who.","Every Old Narnian creature Caspian meets is different — the Bears are jolly, Pattertwig is excitable, the Centaurs are solemn. What does this variety tell you about the world Lewis is building?","When Caspian watches the Fauns dance in the moonlight, it’s the first time he sees the old stories come alive. How do you think this moment changes him?","What’s happening? Caspian is traveling through the forest and meeting different groups of Old Narnians one after another.","Notice how Lewis transitions between groups — “Then they went on and met…” How does he move from one meeting to the next without it becoming a boring list?","Write 2–3 sentences describing meeting three different people or groups. Use clear transitions so the reader can follow you from one meeting to the next."],"chapterQs":["Caspian meets five different groups of Old Narnians in this chapter. Choose THREE and describe what each group is like and what they contribute to Caspian’s cause.","Glenstorm says, “The time is ripe.” What does he mean? Why do the stars matter to the Centaurs, and what does their support signal to the other creatures?","Not all the Old Narnians agree on everything, but they all accept Caspian as king. Why? What has Caspian done to earn their trust?"],"rwm":"“Their next visit was to the Seven Brothers of Shuddering Wood.” — C. S. Lewis, Prince Caspian , Ch. 6"},"words":[{"w":"tutor","d":"A private teacher. Doctor Cornelius was Caspian’s tutor — he taught him in secret.","e":"📚"},{"w":"usurper","d":"Someone who takes power illegally. Miraz is a usurper — he stole the throne from Caspian’s father.","e":"👑"},{"w":"astronomy","d":"The study of stars and planets. Glenstorm watches the skies — he reads the stars to know when the time is right.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"sternly","d":"In a serious, strict way. Trumpkin spoke sternly — he was firm and no-nonsense.","e":"😐"},{"w":"subterranean","d":"Underground. The Seven Brothers of Shuddering Wood had a subterranean forge — their workshop was beneath the earth.","e":"⛏️"},{"w":"victuals","d":"Food and provisions. The Old Narnians brought victuals to share at the gathering.","e":"🍖"}]},{"w":27,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 27.4: Prince Caspian Discussion","file":"lesson-27-4-pc-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-27-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 27.4: Prince Caspian Discussion. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["What kind of loyalty is the poet describing — earned or given freely? Think about the Old Narnians who chose to follow Caspian. Did he earn their loyalty, or did they give it freely? Is there a difference?","Line up every group you met this week. What does each one want — and which two groups are heading straight for each other?","Think about all the characters you’ve met this week — from Miraz’s court to the Old Narnians. If you had to sort them into two groups, what groups would you choose, and who goes where? Explain your thinking.","Doctor Cornelius, Trufflehunter, and Glenstorm all choose to help Caspian, but for different reasons. What motivates each of them?"],"chapterQs":["This week, you tracked multiple groups of characters. Create a character web: put Caspian in the center, then list the characters/groups around him. For each one, write whether they are an ally , an enemy , or undecided , and give ONE reason.","Compare Doctor Cornelius and Glenstorm. Both are wise and both help Caspian — but they help in different ways. What does each one offer Caspian, and why does he need both of them?","Caspian starts this week as a prince who only knows Old Narnia from stories. By the end of Chapter 6, he’s seen Fauns dance in the moonlight. How has Caspian changed across these three chapters? What has he gained, and what has he lost?"]},"words":[{"w":"tutor","d":"A private teacher who gives individual instruction. Doctor Cornelius was Caspian’s tutor.","e":"📚"},{"w":"usurper","d":"Someone who takes power or a throne without the right to it. Miraz is a usurper.","e":"👑"},{"w":"astronomy","d":"The study of stars, planets, and the sky. Cornelius taught Caspian astronomy on the tower.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"sternly","d":"In a serious, strict, or firm way. Miraz spoke sternly to Caspian about Old Narnia.","e":"😠"}]},{"w":27,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 27 Fluency: Prince Caspian","file":"lesson-27-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-27-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[{"w":"tutor","d":"A private teacher or guide who instructs one student or a small group.","e":"📚"},{"w":"usurper","d":"Someone who takes power illegally, especially by force — like a king who stole the throne.","e":"👑"},{"w":"astronomy","d":"The scientific study of stars, planets, and outer space.","e":"⭐"},{"w":"sternly","d":"In a strict, serious, and firm way — showing disapproval.","e":"😐"},{"w":"subterranean","d":"Underground. The Seven Brothers of Shuddering Wood had a subterranean forge — their workshop was beneath the earth.","e":"⛏️"},{"w":"victuals","d":"Food and provisions. The Old Narnians brought victuals to share at the gathering.","e":"🍖"}]},{"w":28,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 28.1: Prince Caspian — Old Narnia in Danger","file":"lesson-28-1-pc-ch7.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-28-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 28.1: Prince Caspian — Old Narnia in Danger. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify conflict and leadership choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Old Narnia is in danger. Where is the danger coming from — and what does Caspian choose to do about it while everyone around him is still arguing?","The council has Bulgy Bears who want a feast, Reepicheep who wants to storm Miraz now, Moles who want to dig entrenchments, and Fauns who want to dance. Why do you think each group argues for something so different? What does each suggestion reveal about that group?","Trumpkin says: “I know the difference between giving advice and taking orders.” What does he mean? Why is this an important thing for someone to understand?"],"chapterQs":["Choose TWO groups from the council — for example, the Bulgy Bears and Reepicheep, or the Moles and the Fauns. What does each group want, and why do they disagree? What does each group’s argument reveal about what they care about?","Caspian decides to blow Susan’s Horn even though no one knows if it will work. What does this decision tell you about his leadership? Is he being brave, desperate, or wise — or something else?","Trumpkin says “I know the difference between giving advice and taking orders.” Then he volunteers to go to Cair Paravel even though he doesn’t believe the Horn will work. What does this tell you about what loyalty means to Trumpkin?"]},"words":[]},{"w":28,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 28.2: Prince Caspian — How They Left The Island","file":"lesson-28-2-pc-ch8.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-28-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 28.2: Prince Caspian — How They Left The Island. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Identify conflict and leadership choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Notice the decisions the characters make. Who takes charge? Who pushes through? What does “leading” look like when there’s no battle to fight?","What does it feel like to be called to do something you didn’t choose? Think about a time you had to do something difficult that wasn’t your idea. How does this poem connect to what happens to the children in Chapter 8?","The children realize they were “summoned” by the Horn — pulled out of their world without choosing to come. How does each child react to this idea? Who seems most comfortable with it, and why?","Peter takes charge of the rowing even though he’s exhausted. No one makes him — he just does it. Is this a leadership choice? Why or why not?"],"chapterQs":["Lucy says, “We had to come, just like that” — like a Jinn in a story. Edmund jokes about being “whistled for.” How do Lucy and Edmund feel differently about being summoned? What does each reaction tell you about their character?","The children must decide whether to walk through the woods or row by sea. What are the advantages and risks of each choice? Why do they choose to row?","Peter rows until he’s too exhausted to talk, but he keeps going. Is this good leadership, stubbornness, or both? Explain your thinking."],"rwm":"“Do you remember our voyage to Terebinthia… And the silken sails, and the great stern lanterns?… Do you remember when we had the musicians up in the rigging playing flutes so that it sounded like music out of the sky?”"},"words":[]},{"w":28,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 28.3: Prince Caspian — What Lucy Saw","file":"lesson-28-3-pc-ch9.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-28-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 28.3: Prince Caspian — What Lucy Saw. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["The group takes a vote and Lucy loses it. What is each person weighing before they choose — and what does the group risk by going the way it does?","Lucy walks into the moonlit clearing and sees the trees begin to dance. She thinks she sees Aslan. What makes Lucy different from the others? Why can she see things they can’t?","Edmund says: “Wouldn’t it be fair to believe her this time?” Peter still votes to go downstream. Is Peter wrong? Think about this from Peter’s perspective — what is he weighing?"],"chapterQs":["Write how each person votes at the gorge and why . What does each vote tell you about that person? Cover at least Peter, Edmund, Susan, and Trumpkin.","Is Peter making a good leadership decision or a bad one by voting to go downstream? There is no single right answer — defend your position with evidence from the chapter.","“Lucy came last of the party, crying bitterly.” Why does Lewis choose to end the chapter this way? What effect does this sentence have on the reader?"],"rwm":"“Lucy came last of the party, crying bitterly.”"},"words":[]},{"w":28,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 28.4: Prince Caspian — Response & Reflection","file":"lesson-28-4-pc-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-28-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 28.4: Prince Caspian — Response & Reflection. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["What does it cost to be right when no one believes you? Think about Lucy — she follows the group even though she knows they’re wrong. Is she being weak, or is there a kind of strength in that? What do you think?","Pick one choice from this week and judge it. Was it a good choice? Point to the moment in the chapter that proves your answer.","What did Caspian learn at the council about leading people who disagree?","What did Peter learn at the gorge about making decisions for others?","What did Edmund show by standing up for Lucy, even when the vote had already been taken?"],"chapterQs":["Compare Caspian’s leadership at the council (Ch. 7) with Peter’s leadership at the gorge (Ch. 9). How does each handle disagreement? Who do you think handles it better, and why?","Edmund stands up for Lucy even though he’s outvoted. What does this tell you about what it means to support someone? Why does Edmund’s support matter even though it doesn’t change the outcome?","Think about all three chapters this week. Who showed the best leadership — Caspian, Peter, Edmund, Trumpkin, or Lucy? Write a claim and support it with at least TWO pieces of evidence from the text."]},"words":[]},{"w":28,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 28 Fluency: Prince Caspian","file":"lesson-28-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-28-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[]},{"w":29,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 29.1 — Prince Caspian Ch. 10","file":"lesson-29-1-pc-ch10.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-29-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 29.1 — Prince Caspian Ch. 10. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice theme through repeated choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Lucy is asked to trust Aslan when nobody else can see him, and the others are asked to trust Lucy. Where in this book has someone already had to decide whether to believe Lucy? What is the same about that moment and this one?","When Lucy first wakes and goes out alone, she feels called — even before she sees Aslan. What does it feel like to be called toward something you can’t quite explain? Have you ever felt that way?","Aslan says he is not bigger — but that Lucy will find him bigger every year she grows. What does this mean? Why would Aslan seem bigger the older you get?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Lucy go out alone at night? What is she feeling — and what text evidence shows you this? Use a detail from the chapter in your answer.","Aslan says: “Every year you grow, you will find me bigger.” What do you think he means by this? Why would Aslan seem bigger the older Lucy grows?","The others can’t see Aslan at first — but Lucy can. Why do you think Lucy can see him when no one else can? What does this tell you about Lucy as a character?"]},"words":[]},{"w":29,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 29.2 — Prince Caspian Ch. 11","file":"lesson-29-2-pc-ch11.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-29-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 29.2 — Prince Caspian Ch. 11. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Notice theme through repeated choices. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Susan and Edmund change their minds about Aslan, and Peter owns up to a mistake of his own. Line those choices up next to Lucy’s choice from Chapter 10. What one word do all of them share?","The trees in this poem tremble and bow — they respond to something powerful that can’t be seen. In Chapter 11, the trees of Narnia wake up and dance when Aslan roars. How is Aslan’s power like the wind in this poem? What do both passages say about what it means to feel something greater than yourself?","Susan says, “I see him now. I’m sorry.” She was one of the children who doubted Aslan earlier. What do you think changed? What does it mean to admit you were wrong about something you once refused to believe?","Aslan roars, and the trees of Narnia wake up and begin to dance. Why would trees waking up feel important in this story? What does this moment tell you about what Aslan’s return means for all of Narnia, not just the children?"],"chapterQs":["Susan says, “I see him now. I’m sorry.” What was she sorry about? How do you think she feels in this moment — and how has her attitude toward Aslan changed?","Why do you think the trees wake up when Aslan roars? What does this tell us about Aslan’s power — is it only power over people, or something bigger?","Peter says he’s been “leading them wrong” ever since they started. What does it take for a leader to admit a mistake? What do you think changes for Peter — and for the others — after he says this to Aslan?"],"rwm":"“The trees were all around them; they were closing in, towering overhead.”"},"words":[]},{"w":29,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 29.3 — Prince Caspian Ch. 12","file":"lesson-29-3-pc-ch12.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-29-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 29.3 — Prince Caspian Ch. 12. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Nikabrik goes one way and Peter goes another. Use both of them to finish this sentence: this book seems to believe that when people have to choose whom to trust, …","Nikabrik wants to call up the White Witch. He says any power that will help them win is worth using. Why is Nikabrik willing to go this far? What has his life been like? What has made him so desperate?","After the fight, Peter says to Caspian: “I haven’t come to take your place, but to put you into it.” What kind of leader says something like this? What does it cost Peter to give up power so willingly?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Nikabrik want to call up the White Witch? What has driven him to this point? Use details from the story to explain the choices and experiences that brought Nikabrik here.","Peter tells Caspian, “I haven’t come to take your place, but to put you into it.” What does this tell us about Peter’s character? What kind of leader says something like this — and what does it cost him?","Caspian says Nikabrik “had gone sour inside from long suffering and hating.” Do you think suffering can turn a person bad? Why or why not? Use what you know from this story — and from your own thinking — to explain."],"rwm":"“I’m a beast and we don’t change. I’m a badger, what’s more, and we hold on.”"},"words":[]},{"w":29,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 29.4 — Prince Caspian Discussion","file":"lesson-29-4-pc-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-29-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 29.4 — Prince Caspian Discussion. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Which character in Chapters 10–12 shows this kind of steadiness — keeping their head while others lose theirs? Think about Peter admitting his mistake, Lucy following Aslan alone, or Aslan waiting patiently. What do you think Kipling’s poem and C. S. Lewis have in common?","Say your theme sentence for this week. Now test it on a character from Chapters 10, 11, and 12 you have not talked about yet. Does it still hold? What would you have to change to make it fit both of them?","Lucy finally finds Aslan. He says he is not bigger — but she is older. What does that mean? What does their reunion say about trust?","Aslan has appeared at last, and Peter confesses his mistakes to him. How do Lucy, Susan, and Peter each respond once they see they were wrong? What’s the difference between their choices?","Three responses to being wrong, spread across the week: back in Chapter 10, Aslan tells Lucy that nobody is ever told what would have happened. In Chapter 11, Peter admits his mistake. In Chapter 12, Nikabrik turns to dark magic instead. What do those three responses tell you about how C. S. Lewis thinks we should handle regret?"],"chapterQs":["Compare how Lucy, Susan, and Nikabrik each respond when they realize they were wrong. What is different about their choices?","Aslan tells Lucy that people can never know “what would have happened.” Peter admits his mistakes to Caspian. Nikabrik turns to dark magic. What do these three different responses to regret teach us about how C. S. Lewis thinks people should handle being wrong?","What does “true loyalty” look like in Chapters 10–12? Use evidence from at least two characters to support your answer."]},"words":[]},{"w":29,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 29 Fluency — Prince Caspian","file":"lesson-29-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-29-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[]},{"w":30,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 30.1 — Prince Caspian Ch. 13","file":"lesson-30-1-pc-ch13.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-30-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 30.1 — Prince Caspian Ch. 13. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how resolution reveals theme. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Peter could settle this with a whole army behind him. Instead he offers to settle it himself. Write down the question this book still has to answer — then say what Peter’s way of answering it shows about what he values.","Peter chooses to challenge Miraz to single combat rather than risk a full battle. Why might a leader choose to put himself in danger to protect his army? What does this tell you about Peter?","Glozelle and Sopespian trick Miraz into accepting the challenge by suggesting he is a coward. Why does this work? What does it tell us about Miraz — and about Glozelle and Sopespian?"],"chapterQs":["Why does Peter challenge Miraz to single combat instead of a full battle? What does this choice tell us about Peter as a leader?","How do Glozelle and Sopespian trick Miraz into accepting the challenge? What does this reveal about their loyalty to Miraz?","Reepicheep, the Bulgy Bear, and Wimbleweather all want to help in different ways. What does each character’s moment in this chapter tell us about how they show loyalty?"]},"words":[]},{"w":30,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 30.2 — Prince Caspian Ch. 14","file":"lesson-30-2-pc-ch14.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-30-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 30.2 — Prince Caspian Ch. 14. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Explain how resolution reveals theme. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Miraz trips, and Peter steps back instead of striking. The fight is still settled either way — but that one moment changes what the settling means. What idea about winning does the book prove right there?","These soldiers ride forward even though they know what waits for them in “the valley of Death.” In Chapter 14, Peter steps into single combat with Miraz, knowing that if he loses, the battle is lost. How is Peter’s courage like the Light Brigade’s? What does it mean to go forward when you’re afraid?","Peter tells Edmund, “Give my love to everyone at home, Ed, if he gets me.” He says this quietly, before the combat resumes. Why does Peter say this now? What does this moment tell you about what Peter believes might happen — and what kind of person says something like that before a fight?","Miraz trips on a tussock of grass. Peter steps back instead of striking. Edmund thinks “Need he be as gentlemanly as all that?” Why does Peter step back? What does it cost him? And what does that choice say about the difference between winning and fighting with honor?"],"chapterQs":["Peter steps back when Miraz stumbles instead of striking. Edmund thinks, “Need he be as gentlemanly as all that?” Why does Peter make this choice — and what does it cost him?","Two stories happen at the same time in this chapter — Peter’s combat at the battlefield, and Aslan’s joyous march through the countryside. How are these two stories different from each other? What does each one accomplish?","Glozelle stabs Miraz and then blames the Narnians, shouting “Treachery!” What does this tell us about the difference between Telmarine loyalty and Old Narnian loyalty?"],"rwm":"“Grabbed Miraz’s arm as it came down,” said Trumpkin, dancing with delight. “There’s a man for you! Uses his enemy’s arm as a ladder.”"},"words":[]},{"w":30,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 30.3 — Prince Caspian Ch. 15","file":"lesson-30-3-pc-ch15.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-30-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 30.3 — Prince Caspian Ch. 15. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["By the end of this chapter Caspian is crowned, and Peter and Susan learn something hard about coming back. Who has changed the most since the first page of this book — and what did the change cost them?","Aslan asks Caspian if he feels “sufficient” to be king. Caspian says he doesn’t. Aslan says that is proof he IS ready. What does Aslan mean? Why would a king who feels ready be more dangerous than one who feels unready?","All the mice offer to cut off their tails so Reepicheep won’t be alone. Aslan says, “You have conquered me.” What does it mean to “conquer” Aslan? What kind of thing moves him so deeply that he gives in?"],"chapterQs":["Aslan tells Caspian that feeling “not sufficient” is actually proof that he IS ready to be king. What does Aslan mean? Why is doubt a sign of good leadership rather than weakness?","The mice all offer to cut off their tails so Reepicheep will not be alone without his. Aslan says, “You have conquered me.” What conquers Aslan — and what does this tell us about what he values most?","Peter and Susan learn they will never return to Narnia — they are “getting too old.” Peter says, “It’s all rather different from what I thought.” Why can’t they come back? What do you think Peter means? What has Narnia given them that they get to keep?"],"rwm":"“You come of the Lord Adam and the Lady Eve. And that is both honor enough to erect the head of the poorest beggar, and shame enough to bow the shoulders of the greatest emperor on earth.”"},"words":[]},{"w":30,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 30.4 — Prince Caspian Discussion","file":"lesson-30-4-pc-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-30-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 30.4 — Prince Caspian Discussion. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. As you read, pay close attention to the characters' actions and motivations. Think about how events connect to the bigger ideas in the story. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["A “bar” is a sandbar — a crossing point, a threshold. Tennyson writes about going through it without sadness or struggle. How does this connect to Peter and Susan’s farewell at the end of Prince Caspian ? What does it mean to leave something behind without mourning it? Is that possible?","Finish this sentence and get ready to defend it: Prince Caspian believes that … Then find the moment in Chapters 13 to 15 that proves it.","Peter steps forward as High King and takes command. What changes for the Old Narnians once somebody finally takes charge? What does Peter do that makes them willing to follow him?","Peter faces Miraz in single combat and steps back when Miraz falls. Glozelle and Sopespian turn on their own king. Two choices, one battlefield — what separates them?","The war is won and Caspian is crowned. Then Aslan tells Caspian where real worth comes from, and a door opens in the air for the goodbyes. Put the crowning, the lesson and the farewell side by side — what do they tell you about how C. S. Lewis chooses to end a story?"],"chapterQs":["Peter fights with honor — stepping back when Miraz falls — while Glozelle and Sopespian betray their own king. Compare these two kinds of leadership. What does C. S. Lewis want us to understand about honor?","Aslan tells Caspian that his ancestors were pirates — but also says he comes from “the Lord Adam and the Lady Eve,” which is “honor enough.” What is Aslan teaching Caspian about where real worth comes from?","Think about the whole novel . What is C. S. Lewis saying about what it means to be a true king? Use evidence from at least two characters — Caspian, Peter, Miraz, or another character of your choice."]},"words":[]},{"w":30,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 30 Fluency — Prince Caspian","file":"lesson-30-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-30-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling?"]},"words":[]},{"w":31,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 31.1 — Year-End Synthesis: Comparing Themes","file":"lesson-31-1-synthesis-compare.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-31-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 31.1 — Year-End Synthesis: Comparing Themes. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare themes across texts. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Choose one idea — courage, loyalty, leadership, or growth — and choose two of your novels. Then write one line saying why those two books belong together for that idea.","Think about the people you met in all five books. Which one do you think showed the most courage? What did they do that showed it?","Which character changed the most over the course of their story? What caused that change? Was it something that happened to them, or something they chose?"],"chapterQs":["What does the word “theme” mean? In your own words, explain what a theme is and how an author shows it through a story. Give one example from ANY novel you read this year.","Look at this list of possible themes: courage, loyalty, leadership, growth, sacrifice, belonging. Which ONE theme do you want to explore this week? Why does that theme interest you? Which novels do you think show it most clearly?","Think about the theme you chose. Name ONE character from ONE novel who connects to that theme. What did that character do or say that shows this theme? Use a specific detail from the book."]},"words":[]},{"w":31,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 31.2 — Year-End Synthesis: Identifying Themes","file":"lesson-31-2-synthesis-compare.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-31-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 31.2 — Year-End Synthesis: Identifying Themes. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Compare themes across texts. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Write your idea as one question you could ask of any novel on your shelf — something like what does this character risk? Now ask it of each book and write down the one moment that answers it.","Many of the characters you read about this year faced choices — two roads. Pick one character from any novel. What “two roads” did they face? Which road did they take, and what happened because of that choice?","What moment in Mrs. Frisby’s story connects to your theme? What did she do or say?","What moment in Anne’s story connects to your theme? What did Anne do or say?","What moment in Annemarie’s story connects to your theme?","This one is a biography — a true life. What moment in Harriet Tubman’s story connects to your theme?","What moment in Narnia connects to your theme? Think about Caspian, Peter, Lucy, or Reepicheep."],"chapterQs":["You chose a theme yesterday. Today, name TWO characters from TWO DIFFERENT novels who show this theme. For each character, describe ONE specific moment from the book that proves it. Use details — not just “they were brave,” but WHAT they did.","Compare your two characters. How are they SIMILAR in the way they show your theme? How are they DIFFERENT ? Think about what motivates each one.","Which character’s example of your theme feels STRONGEST to you? Why? What makes their moment more powerful or convincing than the other?"],"rwm":"“Anne came dancing home in the purple winter twilight across the snowy places.”"},"words":[]},{"w":31,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 31.3 — Year-End Synthesis: Applying Themes","file":"lesson-31-3-synthesis-compare.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-31-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 31.3 — Year-End Synthesis: Applying Themes. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Put your two moments side by side and finish this sentence: both of these books show that … Then check it. Does your sentence say what the books believe, or only what the characters are like?"],"chapterQs":["Write a comparison paragraph (5–7 sentences) that compares how TWO characters from TWO different novels show your theme. Include: your theme statement, evidence from both novels, and an explanation of how they are similar and different.","Reread your paragraph. Does it include evidence from BOTH novels? Does it explain HOW the characters are similar and different — not just THAT they are? What is one thing you could add or change to make it stronger?","Think about your theme across all five books you read this year. Is there a person in a third book who also shows this theme? How does their example compare to the two you already wrote about?"],"rwm":"“Annemarie relaxed the clenched fingers of her right hand, which still gripped Ellen’s necklace.”"},"words":[]},{"w":31,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 31.4 — Year-End Synthesis: Final Reflection","file":"lesson-31-4-synthesis-discussion.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-31-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 31.4 — Year-End Synthesis: Final Reflection. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Dickinson says books are ships that carry us to other lands. Think about the novels you read this year — which one carried you the farthest from your own life? Why? What made that story feel like another world entirely?","Your books agree about your idea, but they do not show it the same way. Name one clear difference — then say why that difference makes sense, given the world each story happens in.","This was your first book of the year, back in Weeks 1–6. What do you still remember best about it? Finish this out loud: the part I still think about is ___ .","Anne arrived at Green Gables unwanted, and left it beloved. How did she change? Which moment showed you she had truly grown?","Annemarie said bravery means not thinking about the dangers — just thinking about what you must do. When did she show that most clearly?","This one was a biography — a real life, not a made-up story. What is one thing Harriet Tubman did that you keep thinking about? Say why it stayed with you.","Prince Caspian is about who you become when you are called to lead. Looking across all five books: what theme runs through all of them? What does that theme mean in real life — not just in stories?"],"chapterQs":["You’ve spent this week comparing themes across novels. Now step back: what did you learn from doing this comparison? What did you notice about your theme that you didn’t see when you were reading just one book?","Which book from this year meant the most to you? Why? This isn’t about which was easiest or hardest — it’s about which story stayed with you. Use a specific moment or character to explain.","If you could ask one question to any character from any novel you read this year, what would you ask and why? Name the character and the novel."]},"words":[]},{"w":31,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 31 Fluency — Favorite Passages","file":"lesson-31-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-31-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of this passage?"]},"words":[]},{"w":32,"d":"1","title":"Lesson 32.1 — Year-End Project: Polishing Your Comparison","file":"lesson-32-1-project-final.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-32-1","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 32.1 — Year-End Project: Polishing Your Comparison. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Reflect on how reading habits have grown. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Read your comparison paragraph out loud, then find one place where you did something a good reader does on purpose. Name that move in your own words — that is a habit you now have.","Read your opening sentence. Does it name both books and hint at the theme? Or does it just say “Both books are about…”? How could you make it sharper?","Look at your evidence sentences. Are they specific enough? Could you name a chapter, a character action, or a moment? Or are they too general?"],"chapterQs":["Reread your comparison paragraph from last week. Copy it here, then revise it — fix any unclear sentences, add a detail you missed, or strengthen your opening or closing.","Check your paragraph for CUPS: C apitalization, U sage, P unctuation, S pelling. List at least TWO specific corrections you made.","Read your paragraph one more time as if you’re a reader seeing it for the first time. Does it make sense? Is your theme clear? What grade would you give yourself and why?"]},"words":[]},{"w":32,"d":"2","title":"Lesson 32.2 — Year-End Project: Reader's Growth","file":"lesson-32-2-project-final.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-32-2","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 32.2 — Year-End Project: Reader's Growth. In your warm-up, you'll focus on 📚 What Good Readers Do — Reflect on how reading habits have grown. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Think about the first novel you opened this year and the last one you finished. Then finish this sentence honestly: I used to … and now I …","Frost says that beautiful, early things don’t last forever. Think about the characters you read about this year. Which character had something “gold” — something precious — that changed or couldn’t stay? What happened, and what did they learn from that change?","Mrs. Frisby and the Rats of NIMH was your first book of the year. What was hard about reading back then — the length, the words, keeping track of who was who?","Then came Anne of Green Gables , Number the Stars , and Freedom Train . Which one of those three was the turning point for you as a reader, and what changed?","Prince Caspian was your last book of the year. What did you do while reading it that your September self would not have thought of — and what would you pick up next if nobody assigned it?"],"chapterQs":["Write a Reader’s Growth Paragraph (4–6 sentences). Think about who you were as a reader at the beginning of the year and who you are now. What can you do now that you couldn’t do before? What feels easier or more natural?","Name ONE specific reading skill you got better at this year. Then give a concrete example from one of the novels that proves it. Don’t just say “I got better at comprehension” — name the skill and show how you used it.","If a friend asked you, “Should I read one of the novels you read this year?” which one would you recommend and why? Be honest — don’t just say “it was good.” What made it worth reading?"],"rwm":"“Anne came dancing home in the purple winter twilight across the snowy places.”"},"words":[]},{"w":32,"d":"3","title":"Lesson 32.3 — Year-End Project: Portfolio and Presentation","file":"lesson-32-3-project-final.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-32-3","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 32.3 — Year-End Project: Portfolio and Presentation. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Look back through the writing you have done this year and find the one sentence that proves your habit best. Then say in your own words what that sentence shows you can now do."],"chapterQs":["Create your portfolio page: Choose your BEST piece of writing from this year (your comparison paragraph, a writing connection you’re proud of, or your reader’s growth paragraph). Copy it here as your showcase piece.","Why did you choose this piece? What makes it your best work? What specific skill does it show — use of evidence, strong vocabulary, clear organization, powerful voice?","If you could go back to Week 1 and give yourself one piece of reading advice, what would it be? What do you know now that you wish you’d known then?"],"rwm":"“Anne came dancing home in the purple winter twilight across the snowy places.”"},"words":[]},{"w":32,"d":"4","title":"Lesson 32.4 — Year-End Celebration: Looking Back, Looking Forward","file":"lesson-32-4-project-celebration.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-32-4","done":false,"script":"Welcome to today's lesson: Lesson 32.4 — Year-End Celebration: Looking Back, Looking Forward. In your warm-up, you'll focus on ✏️ Daily Oral Language — Study the model, then fix the sentence.. There is no new chapter today. Think about the books you have already read this year and the writing you have already done, and use your journal to plan before you draft. Use your journal to capture your thoughts and any questions that come up.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Hughes says to “hold fast to dreams.” Think about the characters you read about this year. Which character held fast to a dream — even when it was hard? What was their dream, and what happened because they didn’t let go?","You are about to tell somebody how you grew as a reader this year. Decide who is listening, then say your first sentence out loud twice — and change any word that person would not understand.","Who are you telling? Picture one real person — a classmate, a younger kid, a grown-up at home. Now say your first sentence to that person in your head. Which words would you change for them?","Pick one book off your shelf and one piece of your own writing from this year. Which pair shows your growth best? You are not reviewing the book — you are showing what you can do now.","Walk your listener along all five books in order, one short line each — when you read it and what it was like to read it. End on Prince Caspian , then finish with the sentence that starts I used to … and now I … Say the whole thing out loud once before you write it down."],"chapterQs":["Write your Final ELA Reflection (5–7 sentences). Look back on the whole year — the novels, the writing, the vocabulary, the grammar, the discussions. What are you most proud of? What surprised you about yourself as a reader and writer?","Which character from any novel this year stays with you ? Not your favorite — the one you think about even when you’re not reading. Who are they, and why do they stick with you?","Set a reading goal for next year . This isn’t “read more books.” Make it specific: a genre you want to try, a skill you want to sharpen, a kind of book you’ve been curious about, or a reading habit you want to build."]},"words":[]},{"w":32,"d":"fluency","title":"Week 32 Fluency — Favorite Passages","file":"lesson-32-fluency.html","clips":[{"id":"lesson","label":"Lesson video","path":"g4ela-32-fluency","done":false,"script":"Welcome to this week's fluency practice. Today you'll work on reading with accuracy, expression, and appropriate pacing. Listen carefully to the model reading, pay attention to where the reader pauses, speeds up, or changes tone. Then practice reading the passage on your own, focusing on making it sound natural and engaging. Record yourself and listen back to hear your improvement.","notes":true}],"cards":{"pauses":["Did I say each word correctly?","Did my reading sound smooth?","Did my voice show the feeling of this passage?"]},"words":[]}],"poems":[{"id":"poem","label":"Hope Is the Thing with Feathers (Mrs. Frisby)","path":"poem/hope-is-the-thing-with-feathers","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"poem","label":"Who Has Seen the Wind? (Anne of Green Gables)","path":"poem/who-has-seen-the-wind","done":true,"script":""},{"id":"poem","label":"There Will Come Soft Rains (Number the Stars)","path":"poem/there-will-come-soft-rains","done":true,"script":""},{"id":"poem","label":"Sympathy (Freedom Train)","path":"poem/sympathy","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"poem","label":"The Forest Remembers (Prince Caspian)","path":"poem/forest-remembers","done":false,"script":""},{"id":"poem-full","label":"There Will Come Soft Rains: whole poem, first poetry day (Number the Stars)","path":"poem/there-will-come-soft-rains-full","done":true,"script":"Hi, friends. Every book we read this year comes with a poem, and today you get to meet the one that goes with Number the Stars. It's called \"There Will Come Soft Rains,\" and it was written by an American poet named Sara Teasdale near the end of the First World War. Under the title she wrote two small words in parentheses: War Time. Keep those words in mind, because they matter.\n\nToday we're not memorizing anything. Today we just meet the poem. So before I read it, let me show you how I read a poem when I see it for the first time.\n\nFirst, I listen to the whole thing before I worry about any one word. Second, I read it out loud, slowly, and I pause at the commas and periods, not at the end of every line. If a line doesn't have any punctuation at the end, I keep right on going into the next line. Third, I notice the gaps. This poem comes in pairs of lines, and each pair paints one picture. When I reach a gap, I stop and let myself see that picture. And fourth, I watch for the turn, the place where a poem changes direction.\n\nNow listen to the whole poem, and try to see each picture.\n\nThere will come soft rains and the smell of the ground,\nAnd swallows circling with their shimmering sound;\n\nAnd frogs in the pools singing at night,\nAnd wild plum-trees in tremulous white;\n\nRobins will wear their feathery fire\nWhistling their whims on a low fence-wire;\n\nAnd not one will know of the war, not one\nWill care at last when it is done.\n\nNot one would mind, neither bird nor tree\nIf mankind perished utterly;\n\nAnd Spring herself, when she woke at dawn,\nWould scarcely know that we were gone.\n\nDid you feel the turn? The first lines are full of spring: rain, birds, frogs, plum trees covered in white blossoms. Then, right in the middle, the poem says the birds won't know about the war at all. Nature just keeps going. The poet is wondering how small a war might look to the rest of the world, and that's a big, quiet thought to sit with.\n\nNow look at the lines in gold on your page, the ones about the robins and the war. Those four lines are yours. Over the next few weeks, you'll learn them by heart, a little at a time, and by the end of the book you'll be able to say them without looking.\n\nA few words to know before you go. Tremulous means trembling, like blossoms shaking in the breeze. Feathery fire is the robin's bright red-orange chest. And whims are little sudden ideas, so the robins are whistling whatever comes into their heads.\n\nNow it's your turn. Read the whole poem out loud once or twice, pausing at the punctuation, and think about the question under the poem. Why would a poet fill a war poem with rain, birds, frogs, and trees?"}],"teacher":[{"id":"scrapbook","label":"Anne Character Growth Scrapbook — teacher video","path":"teacher/g4ela-anne-scrapbook","done":true,"script":""}],"teacherDocs":{"10-4":"# Lesson 10.4 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 20 · A Good Imagination Gone Wrong. Guide this lesson: Marilla. Each script plays right after Marilla's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-10-4\n\nHi, friends. This is the last lesson of week ten, and Chapter 20 wraps up five chapters about courage. You already know Anne can be brave about real dangers. Today, her own imagination is what scares her. You'll finish the week's word work with some checking activities, read Chapter 20, and look back across the whole week in your answers. Then in Writer's Workshop, you'll name the one quality that makes your main character special.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-10-4-welcome\n\nThink back to when Anne first came to Green Gables and told Marilla about Katie Maurice, the friend she pretended lived in the bookcase glass. Imagining helped Anne get through some lonely years, so it's a real part of who she is. Keep that in mind as you read. When the strongest part of a person starts causing trouble, it's worth asking why, and you'll ask that same question about your own character at the end of the lesson.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-10-4-dol\n\nSomething new shows up in today's sentence. Earlier this week, you fixed sentences by adding a period and a capital to split two thoughts. Today's model from the novel has someone talking. The words \"said Marilla\" sit outside the quotation marks because they aren't part of what she says. You'll need this in your own story whenever a character speaks, and one more rule writers follow is that each new speaker gets a new paragraph.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-10-4-guide1\n\nYou've practiced every one of this week's word skills already, so today is all about checking. That's the same thing you'll do when you reach the Edit step in your Writer's Workshop tracker. Your own mistakes are the hardest to spot, because your brain fills in what you meant to write. So slow down and point to each word with your finger as you go, even the ones that look fine.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-10-4-morphology\n\nYou've known these four \"not\" prefixes since the start of the week, so this match should feel familiar. One extra thing to notice in the examples box: that's why words like illegal and irregular have double letters. One l comes from the prefix and the other comes from legal. Knowing that will save you from a spelling mistake that plenty of grown-ups still make.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-10-4-vocabulary\n\nBy now you've decoded these four words and matched them to their meanings, so today you get to show that you own them. A good test is whether you can use a word for someone outside the book. Who do you know who's inexorable once they've decided something? If you pick the word parts option, notice that incapable and inexorable share a \"not\" prefix from this week's morphology.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-10-4-grammar\n\nYou sorted these verb forms on the first day and found them in longer sentences on day two, so you know this rule. One reminder: the s on a verb works the opposite way from the s on a noun. One dog, two dogs, but one dog barks, two dogs bark. And two names joined by \"and,\" like Anne and Diana, count as more than one. Every sentence on this page comes from Chapter 20.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-10-4-spelling\n\nYou've worked with this rule all week: c and g usually turn soft before e, i, or y, and stay hard before a, o, or u. You also hunted down rule breakers like girl. Think back to day one, too, when you saw that the rule works letter by letter, so a long word like gorgeous can have a hard sound and a soft sound. Use the rule box on this page as your guide.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-10-4-guide2\n\nAll week, the Growing-Up Watch box has followed Anne's courage, from walking up to Mrs. Barry's door in Chapter 16 to running through the snow in Chapter 18. Both times the danger was real. As you read today, notice how the author shows Anne's fear without simply naming it, through what she pictures and how she moves. That's something you can borrow when your own character gets scared.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-10-4-wgrd\n\nAll week you've compared how different people see the same event: Anne's tea party, Minnie May's croup, and now the Haunted Wood. Today's page adds one new idea. The story stays close to Anne on purpose, so you'll feel what she feels and forgive her more easily. That doesn't mean she's always right. A strong reader enjoys being close to Anne and still asks what someone else would say.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-10-4-read\n\nThis chapter closes out our week. Picture Prince Edward Island more than a hundred years ago, with no streetlights and no flashlights. The woods between farms were truly dark at night, so a spruce grove after sunset would feel spooky to almost anyone, even without a ghost story. Notice that this chapter can make you laugh and feel sorry for Anne at the same time, and keep both feelings handy for the Pause and Think stop.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-10-4-guide3\n\nEvery question today asks for a quotation from the book. You practiced quotation marks in today's Daily Oral Language, so use them the same way here. Copy the words exactly as they appear in Chapter 20. Then add a sentence after the quote that explains what it shows. That explaining sentence is the part most people skip, and it's what turns an okay answer into a strong one.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-10-4-chapter\n\nFor question two, a strong answer admits the other side has a point before it makes its own case. You could start with \"Some readers might think...\" and then give your reason. For question three, look at the hint under it. It gives you a beginning and an ending to compare, so use words like \"at first\" and \"by the end\" to make the change easy to see.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-10-4-workshop\n\nYou've built a lot this week: a first sentence, then Scene 1, then Scene 2 with a thought inside your kid's head. Today is lighter. Look at Sam's gift and problem for Mateo; they're one quality seen from two sides. Picture a really honest kid. People trust her, but one day her honesty could hurt a friend's feelings. Find your own kid's version of that.\n","10-fluency":"# Week 10 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 10 Fluency · Anne of Green Gables, Ch. 18, Anne saves Minnie May. About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-10-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's fluency passage comes from the night Anne saved Minnie May. Everyone else in that kitchen was frightened, and Anne was the one who knew what to do, because she'd taken care of three sets of twins before she came to Green Gables. So when you read her words out loud, don't make her sound scared. She sounds calm and quick, like someone giving directions. Notice the word \"First.\" Anne is making a plan, step by step, and your voice can show that. Pause a little at each period, so every instruction lands. And on your recording, read at the speed you'd want someone to talk to you in an emergency: steady, clear, and sure.\n","11-1":"# Lesson 11.1 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 21 · A New Departure in Flavorings. Guide this lesson: Marilla. Each script plays right after Marilla's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-11-1\n\nHi, friends, and welcome to week eleven. Last week ended with Anne running out of the Haunted Wood and deciding she'd had enough of imagining scary things. This week, some new people come to Avonlea. Mr. Phillips leaves the school, and a new minister arrives with his wife, Mrs. Allan. Our big idea for the week is being seen, which means having someone notice who you really are, even on a day when things go wrong for you. Today you'll start by thinking about how a place shapes a story. Then you'll do your word work and read Chapter 21. In Writer's Workshop, you'll begin the middle of your own story, and you'll find out that the order you tell things in matters a lot.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-11-1-welcome\n\nTake a second look at today's chapter title, \"A New Departure in Flavorings.\" That's a pretty fancy title for a chapter about a cake. A departure is a leaving, like a train departing from the station. But it can also mean doing something different from the usual way. So the title is a little joke. Something about this cake is going to be very different from the usual, and not in a good way. Authors pick chapter titles on purpose, and this one is having fun with you before you've read a word. One more thing to watch for: one of today's word parts is hiding inside that title. See if you can find it once you get to Word Study.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-11-1-wgrd\n\nSetting is also when a story happens, not only where. Anne lives more than a hundred years ago, and in a small village back then, the minister was one of the most important people in town. Having the minister and his wife come to tea was a very big deal. Families brought out their best dishes and their best cooking. So in this time and place, a cake is more than a cake. It's a way of showing respect. Think about last week, too, when one afternoon tea with Diana turned into trouble with Mrs. Barry. And when you write your own story, notice your setting. Sam put Mateo on a field full of boys, so every mistake has an audience.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-11-1-dol\n\nThe mistake in today's sentence has a name. It's called a run-on sentence, and it's one of the most common mistakes writers make, even grown-up ones. To find where one sentence should end, try this test. Look for a chunk that tells who did something and what they did, and could stand all by itself. When you find two of those chunks crammed together, they need to be separated. Now look back at the model sentence for one tricky thing. There's a period after \"Mrs.,\" right in the middle of the sentence. That period doesn't end anything. It just shows \"Mrs.\" is a short way of writing a longer word, so the sentence keeps going after it.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-11-1-guide1\n\nYou might wonder why the page asks you to name the groups yourself instead of just telling you the rule. It's because a rule you figure out sticks in your head far better than a rule someone reads to you. Scientists work this way, too. They look at examples first and then figure out the pattern. So when you sort, slow down and ask what the cards in each group have in common. Today's grammar and spelling also connect to each other. When you write about something that already happened, you often add an ending to a word, and sometimes that ending changes the spelling. If you get stuck on the typing activity, use the hint before you guess.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-11-1-morphology\n\nThese two word parts help you with science words, and you'll see them a lot in middle school. An antiseptic is something a nurse puts on a cut to fight against germs. When you defrost the freezer, you're taking the frost away. And if your bike tire deflates, the air goes out and down. So when you meet a long, scary-looking word, check the front of it first. Sometimes one small chunk tells you which direction the meaning goes. Be careful, though. Not every word that starts with the letters d and e has this prefix. In a word like \"desk,\" those letters are just part of the word. Cover the chunk and see if a real word or root is left behind.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-11-1-vocabulary\n\nNotice that each of these sentences comes straight from the book, so the author has left you clues on purpose. One kind of clue is a word nearby that means almost the same thing, like \"eager\" sitting next to \"vivacious\" in the example. Another kind is what's happening in the scene, like someone balancing or wobbling. With \"kindred,\" look for a smaller word hiding inside it. If you know what your kin are, you're already halfway there. Also look at the word \"another\" in that same sentence. It tells you Anne has found people like this before. Once you've matched them all, try using one of these words to describe someone in your own life.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-11-1-grammar\n\nOnce you've named your groups, watch for two things in your own writing. Many past-tense verbs end in -ed, but some of the most common ones don't follow that pattern at all. Think about a minister who came to tea. He didn't \"comed.\" The same goes for ate and ran. Your ear usually knows these, so trust it. The other thing to watch is staying in the same time. If your story starts in the past, with Mateo tripped and Mateo fell, it should stay in the past. Switching back and forth between past and present is one of the most common slips in a draft, so check for it when you reread your scenes.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-11-1-spelling\n\nAfter you've sorted and named your groups, try your rule on some words that aren't on the page. Check what your rule says should happen to \"funny\" when you make it \"funnier,\" to \"monkey\" when there's more than one, and to \"study\" when you add -ing. If your rule works on words you've never sorted, you've really got it. This rule also matters for your grammar today. Lots of past-tense verbs end in a y before you add -ed, like \"cry\" and \"worry.\" There are a few rule breakers, too. \"Say\" has a vowel before the y, so you'd expect it to keep the y, but its past tense is \"said.\" English has a handful of those, and they just have to be memorized.\n\n## Say It Three Ways · g4ela-11-1-copia\n\nWhen you change the verb, think about how the new word changes the feeling. Girls who sniffled all the way home feel very different from girls who wailed all the way home, even though the rest of the sentence is the same. Pick the verb that fits how sad you think they really were. When you move a when or a where to the front, put a comma after it, before the main part of the sentence starts. And notice that starting with a when is exactly what you'll practice in Writer's Workshop today. Words like \"after school\" or \"that afternoon\" at the front of a sentence help the reader know where they are in time.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-11-1-guide2\n\nThink about where Anne came from before Green Gables. In the homes she lived in, she was mostly useful. She helped with the work and looked after babies. People noticed what she could do, but not very many noticed who she was. That's why being seen matters so much to her. Notice something about the box, too. Being seen isn't the same as being praised. The person who truly sees you doesn't pretend your mistake didn't happen. They just don't let the mistake be the only thing they see. That's harder to do than it sounds. When you answer the question about your character, think about who in their life could do that for them.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-11-1-read\n\nThis chapter is funny, and part of what makes it funny is that the author lets you notice things before Anne does. Authors often plant a small detail early on that seems unimportant, and then it turns out to matter a lot later. As you read the part before the tea, keep an eye out for anything that seems a little off, even if Anne doesn't pay attention to it. If you spot it, you'll enjoy the rest of the chapter even more. At the second stop, think about what you know about Anne from the whole book so far. She's made lots of mistakes, but has she made the same one twice? That will help you decide whether her idea is a comfort or a worry.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-11-1-guide3\n\nBefore you start typing, read each question slowly and count how many parts it has. The first question asks two things, and so does the third. The most common reason a good answer loses points is that it answers only the first part and stops. A trick that helps is to look for the word \"and\" in the question. That often tells you there's a second part waiting. When you finish an answer, go back and check that you answered every part. And when you use someone's words from the book, put them in quotation marks and say who said them, just like the sentences we practiced last week with Marilla's words.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-11-1-chapter\n\nQuestion one is really a chain of events, where one thing leads to another. The strongest answers tell the chain in order, using words like \"because\" and \"so\" to connect each link. If you skip a link, your reader won't see how the mistake happened. For the second question, Anne's own explanation is your best evidence. Use her words in quotation marks, then add a sentence of your own that explains why her words answer the question. For the third question, remember that it has two halves. Lots of readers write a great answer about Mrs. Allan and then forget to tell how Anne responds. Look at how Anne feels at the very end of that scene.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-11-1-workshop\n\nSome transition words in the bank do a special job. \"Suddenly\" and \"Just then\" tell the reader something surprising is about to happen, so save one of them for your worst moment. If every scene starts with \"suddenly,\" nothing feels sudden anymore. The same goes for \"then.\" It's a fine word, but when every sentence starts with it, your story starts to sound like a list. Notice how Sam mixed his up. Also, think back to last week, when you named your kid's gift and the problem it causes. The middle scenes are where that problem grows. Each scene should feel a little harder for your character than the one before it.\n","11-2":"# Lesson 11.2 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 22 & 23 · Anne Is Invited Out to Tea; Anne Comes to Grief in an Affair of Honor. Guide this lesson: Diana. Each script plays right after Diana's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-11-2\n\nHi, friends. In the last lesson, Anne's cake went wrong, and Mrs. Allan was kind about it in a way Anne will never forget. Today you'll read two chapters that feel like opposites. In Chapter 22, Anne goes to tea at the manse and has to be on her very best behavior. In Chapter 23, she's at Diana's party, and things get a lot less careful. This week's reading move is about how a place shapes what happens, and these two chapters show it clearly. Your word work today asks you to hunt for words that break a pattern. Then, in your writing, you'll build the worst moment of your own story. Keep an eye on the roof scene, because it's a model for exactly that.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-11-2-welcome\n\nYou can tell Diana loves Anne, because she teased her about pride and still sounded fond of her. In Chapter 23, Diana is the friend standing on the ground, watching. Most stories about a dare focus on the person doing it. But being the friend who watches is hard in its own way. You can see the danger, but you can't make the choice for them. Maybe you've been that friend at a sleepover. As you read, pay attention to what Diana says and does while Anne makes up her mind. Ask yourself what you would have said if you'd been in that garden.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-11-2-wgrd\n\nDiana would pick the garden party in a heartbeat, and honestly, most of us would too. In the last lesson, you saw how a small village makes one mistake feel enormous, because everyone hears about it. Today, add one more piece to what we mean by setting. A setting isn't only a room or a garden. It includes who is there. At tea with Mrs. Allan, Anne is with a grown-up she admires. At the party, she's with a crowd of girls her own age, and some of them, like Josie Pye, aren't exactly her friends. So when you ask what a place allows, also ask whose opinion matters most to Anne in that spot. That question will help you with the roof.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-11-2-dol\n\nDiana is right that Anne's sentence sounds grand. Anne never uses a small word when a big one is handy. Look at one word in the model sentence: \"shall.\" People in Anne's time said \"I shall\" where we'd usually say \"I will.\" It points to the future, just like \"will\" does. Then notice the word \"perish.\" It means to die. Anne could have said she'd try her best, but that wouldn't sound like Anne. She talks as if every moment is the biggest moment in history. Keep \"shall\" in mind for the sentence you're fixing. Words that point ahead in time are good clues that a thought has moved somewhere new.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-11-2-guide1\n\nPoor Ruby. Diana is a little hard on her, but tapping every word you see really is an easy habit to fall into. Your grammar and spelling work are closer friends than they look. Every time you put a verb in the past tense, you add an ending, and that's exactly when the spelling rule about y kicks in. Think of the word \"cry.\" Yesterday the baby cried. The y changed to an i because you added an ending. So the spelling you're practicing isn't only for word lists. It shows up whenever anyone tells about something that already happened. Today's chapters are told that way, so you'll meet plenty of these endings once you start reading.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-11-2-morphology\n\nDiana's clues about the stairs and leaving home are good ones, so let me add a warning to sit beside them. Not every word that starts with the letters d and e has the prefix in it. Think about \"desk\" or \"deep.\" If you take the d e off, nothing meaningful is left. A real prefix comes off and leaves a meaning behind. Sometimes what's left isn't a word on its own, but it's a root that shows up in other words. You'll see one of those today. Try it on your own with \"defrost.\" What's left when you take off the prefix, and what does the full word mean now?\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-11-2-vocabulary\n\nDiana's Josie Pye test made me laugh. Some words simply refuse to fit some people. Look closely at the word \"kindred.\" Hiding inside it is the little word \"kin,\" an old word for family. Kindred people aren't related by blood, but they feel like family. Think about why that idea would mean so much to Anne, a girl who came to Green Gables with no family at all. When a word connects to a character's life like that, you'll remember it a lot longer. One more thing. Once you've matched three, the last one drops into place by itself, and it's easy to skip checking it. Read that word and its meaning together anyway.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-11-2-grammar\n\nDiana is proud of reading slowly, and for once I'd take her side over Anne's. Two things catch people on a sentence like this. First, not every past tense verb ends in e d. Think of \"ran,\" \"went,\" and \"fell.\" Those are past tense, but they don't wear the usual ending. So don't hunt only for e d. Second, not every word that ends in e d is a verb. \"Red\" and \"bed\" end that way, and they aren't actions at all. Time words can help you, too. Words like \"yesterday\" or \"next week\" tell you when something happens, even though they aren't verbs themselves. Use them as clues, then find the action word that goes with them.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-11-2-spelling\n\nDiana wants you looking for reasons, so let me give you one of the reasons behind this rule. Try changing the y to an i in \"cry\" and adding i n g. You'd get c r i i n g, with two i's in a row. English almost never puts two i's together, so the y stays to keep the word readable. Words like \"key\" and \"monkey\" show another reason, because a vowel sits right in front of the y. And watch for words that break even the exceptions. \"Day\" becomes \"daily,\" and \"pay\" becomes \"paid.\" Those don't follow any pattern you've learned, so you just have to know them. Use what you notice to explain your answer in the box.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-11-2-guide2\n\nDiana says she's only telling you to watch, but I think she knows more about this summer than she's letting on. The Growing-Up Watch reminds you that Anne has been here before. She broke a slate over Gilbert's head and she shouted at Mrs. Lynde, both times because her pride got hurt. So pride isn't new for Anne. What could be new is whether she notices it. One test that really helps is to ask, \"Would I still do this if nobody were watching?\" If the answer is yes, it might be brave. If the answer is no, it's probably the dare talking. When Anne reaches the roof, try that test on her.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-11-2-read\n\nDiana can hardly keep a secret, can she? She nearly told you more than she meant to. A little background will help today. In Anne's time there were no phones, so a letter was a big event, and the way an envelope was addressed said something about who you were. Keep that in mind at the pause. Then look at the title of Chapter 23. It says Anne comes to grief in \"an affair of honor.\" Long ago, that was a fancy name for a duel, a fight two men had when one felt insulted. The author is being playful. She uses a grand, serious phrase for a party game in a garden.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-11-2-rwm\n\nDiana knows exactly how Anne moves when she's happy. That's the kind of thing a best friend notices.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's how Montgomery wrote it: \"She had come dancing up the lane, like a wind-blown sprite, through the mellow sunshine and lazy shadows of the August evening.\"\n\nLet's make sure every word in this sentence is working for you. A sprite is a small, quick fairy kind of creature, so being wind-blown makes Anne light and fluttery. Now look at \"lazy shadows.\" Shadows can't really be lazy. The author gives them a feeling a person has, and that one word tells you a lot about the evening. Writers use that trick all the time, and it works because it's surprising. When you write your own sentence, pick a time of day you know well and give one thing in that place a feeling of its own.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-11-2-guide3\n\nPoor Diana. That must have been the most frightening afternoon of her life, and I'm so glad Anne was all right in the end. Anne talks a lot, and two of today's questions ask for a quotation. You don't have to copy a whole speech. Choose the few words that prove your point most clearly, put them in quotation marks, and say who said them. Then, just like last week, follow it with a sentence that explains what it shows. The first question doesn't need a quote, but it does need two details, so make them two different moments from the visit. If you see an extra challenge line after a question, give it a try.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-11-2-chapter\n\nOf course Diana was watching Anne. Anyone in that garden would have been. Marilla's moment is one you get to see even though Diana didn't. For question three, make sure you know what \"revelation\" means. A revelation is when something you didn't see before suddenly becomes clear, all at once. So the question is really asking what Marilla suddenly understood. The word \"pierced\" is doing a lot of work, too. Her heart wasn't really stabbed. The author is showing how sharp and sudden the feeling was. For question two, give Anne's reason in her own words, then go one step further and tell what you think is underneath it.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-11-2-workshop\n\nI believe Diana completely. Holding your breath in that garden sounds exactly right. When the moment matters most, a writer slows the clock down. Sam's draft moves fast. He covers the whole fall in one sentence, and next week he'll work on that. You can do it now. Picture a kid about to hang up a drawing at the art show, one she isn't sure about. Instead of \"she was nervous,\" write what her hands are doing and the one thing she keeps looking at. Short sentences help, too. They make the reader read in little gulps. Spend more words on the few seconds before the choice than on anything else in the scene.\n","11-3":"# Lesson 11.3 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 24 · Miss Stacy and Her Pupils Get Up a Concert. Guide this lesson: Miss Stacy. Each script plays right after Miss Stacy's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-11-3\n\nHi, friends. Welcome to the third lesson of week eleven. So far this week, Mrs. Allan saw past a ruined cake, and Anne got a letter addressed to Miss Anne Shirley. Today, in Chapter 24, a new teacher comes to the Avonlea school. Your word work is mostly building, so you'll use what you practiced in the last two lessons on your own. Then you'll read, borrow one of the author's sentence shapes, and write Scene 4.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-11-3-welcome\n\nI'm a teacher too, so I know how Miss Stacy feels. We'd better be ready for a question like that. This whole week has been about being seen. You already watched Mrs. Allan notice Anne on a bad day. Miss Stacy is brand new, so as you read, pay attention to how fast Anne makes up her mind about her.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-11-3-dol\n\nMiss Stacy sounds pleased to find her own schoolroom in a sentence, and I don't blame her. You already know the run-on test from the start of the week. Find the chunks that could each stand alone. The verbs give you one more clue. When the time in a sentence suddenly jumps, a new sentence often begins right there. Compare your fix with the model before you check.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-11-3-guide1\n\nBetter than Miss Stacy? I doubt that, but she's right to trust your ear. In the first lesson you sorted, and in the second you hunted for the words that broke the pattern. Today the page hands you the whole job. Each activity still has its reminder box at the top and a hint button. Try each one yourself first, then the hint, and only then press Check My Work.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-11-3-morphology\n\nI love that Miss Stacy takes her class outside to study ferns. That kind of slow looking works on words, too. You've met anti- and de- twice this week, so use the test from earlier: a real prefix comes off and leaves some meaning behind. When you finish, try one bonus word in your head. A train can derail. What does that first chunk tell you happened?\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-11-3-vocabulary\n\nA line from the book hidden in the activity? That's a fun surprise to watch for. These are the same four words you've matched all week. Today they sit inside sentences about people from the book. Think about what you already know about each person. Then check that the word's meaning fits the person, and not only the sound of the sentence.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-11-3-grammar\n\nThat yesterday trick of Miss Stacy's is a good one, and I use it myself. You learned this week that past tense tells what already happened and future tense tells what hasn't happened yet. Now you make the verbs yourself. After the blanks, the Show What You Know box asks for both times in one sentence. Think about what a reader learns about Anne from having both.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-11-3-spelling\n\nSketching a leaf before drawing it properly sounds just like Miss Stacy, careful and patient. You've worked with this y rule since the first lesson. Today the Show What You Know box asks for a y word from Chapter 24, so jot one down when you spot it while you read. Then come back and build it. Explaining why you spelled it that way shows you truly understand.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-11-3-guide2\n\nYou've only just met Miss Stacy, and already her care with names tells you a lot about her. Earlier this week, in Chapter 22, Anne opened a letter addressed to Miss Anne Shirley and was thrilled. Today the thing that matters is even smaller, just one letter at the end of her name. You know how much that e has mattered to her since her very first day at Green Gables.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-11-3-wgrd\n\nCounting heads in a room is such a teacher thing to suggest. Miss Stacy notices everyone. In the last lesson, a garden full of girls, Josie Pye among them, helped push Anne onto a roof. Today the crowd is different, first a class and then a whole audience. A crowd makes some people braver and others shyer. As you read, decide which kind Anne is, and whether you'd be the same.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-11-3-read\n\nMiss Stacy promises not to listen, which is polite of her, though I suspect she'd like to. This chapter starts a little while after the roof. Anne has been home healing, so notice what she's been missing at school while she was away. That will help you understand why her first days back feel so big to her.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-11-3-rwm\n\nMiss Stacy is a little embarrassed by this one, and I think that's sweet. Most of us blush when someone describes us.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's the sentence from Chapter 24: \"Miss Stacy was a bright, sympathetic young woman with the happy gift of winning and holding the affections of her pupils and bringing out the best that was in them mentally and morally.\"\n\nEarlier this week you saw how one word, like lazy in front of shadows, can carry a whole feeling. This sentence stacks describing words to introduce a person. For your Mimic, choose a real person, like a grandparent or a neighbor. A true detail beats a fancy one every time.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-11-3-guide3\n\nCompositions is the old word for pieces of writing, and I like how grand it sounds. Check how many parts each question has before you start, the same habit you used earlier this week. The first two questions each ask for two things. The third asks about two groups of people, so make sure both get a fair turn in your answer.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-11-3-chapter\n\nIt's generous of Miss Stacy to admit the careful grown-ups have a point. Not every teacher would. Question three reaches back to Chapter 22, when Mrs. Lynde called a lady teacher a dangerous innovation. An innovation is a brand new way of doing something. So think about what's new in Miss Stacy's schoolroom compared with the school Marilla and Mrs. Lynde would remember.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-11-3-workshop\n\nOnce there's a concert to practice for, nobody can keep Anne still, and Miss Stacy saw that right away. In the last lesson you built the worst moment and slowed the clock down. Scene 4 comes right after it. Look at Sam's draft. Diego offers a hand, but every action after that belongs to Mateo. Check your own actions the same way. If someone else is doing the hard part, give it back to your kid.\n","11-4":"# Lesson 11.4 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 25 · Matthew Insists on Puffed Sleeves. Guide this lesson: Matthew. Each script plays right after Matthew's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what he says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-11-4\n\nHi, friends. This is the last lesson of week eleven. All week you've watched grown-ups who truly see Anne, first Mrs. Allan after the cake and then Miss Stacy. Chapter 25 gives Matthew his turn, and he has to walk into a store to do it. Your word work today is a review, so trust what you learned at the start of the week. After you read, you'll answer the chapter questions and write a rough ending for your story's first draft.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-11-4-welcome\n\nMatthew calls it the hardest thing instead of the bravest, and I think for him those might be the same. Way back in Chapter 11, Anne wished for puffed sleeves, and today's chapter title tells you that wish comes back. Authors often plant a small wish early and answer it much later, after you've nearly forgotten it. Watch for that as you read Chapter 25.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-11-4-dol\n\nMatthew claims he's no writer, but he spotted that run-on faster than plenty of grown-ups would. Now check the verbs. The model says Matthew will give, which points to the future. The sentence you're fixing uses unwrapped and watched, which tell what already happened. That's the same past and future tense from grammar this week. Read your fixed sentence back and make sure both halves stay in the past.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-11-4-guide1\n\nPoor Matthew, he can't even mention the store without blushing. You'll find out why soon enough. You already know every rule on this tab, because you worked them out yourself earlier in the week. Before you start the grammar or the spelling, try saying the rule to yourself in one sentence. If it won't come, peek at your notes. When you can say it plainly, the Show What You Know boxes get easier.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-11-4-morphology\n\nMatthew says he's no scholar, but that cellar-stairs trick of his is a good one. Use the same test from earlier this week: cover the front chunk and look at what's left. Today two of the cards are roots, so that leftover piece gets a meaning of its own. The title of Chapter 21, A New Departure in Flavorings, holds a big word whose halves are both on these cards. Use it to check your thinking after you match.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-11-4-vocabulary\n\nI believe Matthew completely. Explaining something out loud is one of the best ways to find out whether you really know it. You've seen these four words all week, so today you get to use them your way. Whichever choice you pick, try tying your word to a real moment from the book, like Anne on the Barry roof or the liniment cake. If the word fits that moment, you've got it.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-11-4-grammar\n\nMatthew and Marilla disagreeing politely is about the most Green Gables thing I can picture. Earlier this week you learned that past tense doesn't always end in e-d, and that future tense pairs will with the plain verb. Keep both in mind, because these sentences retell the fall from the roof in Chapter 23. When you correct one in the box below, read the fixed sentence aloud. It should sound like a true retelling.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-11-4-spelling\n\nI'd love to see Matthew squinting at a misspelled store sign. He'd never say a word, but he'd notice. Quick reminder from earlier this week: look at the letter just before the y, and then at the ending being added. Those two checks tell you which of the three cases you're in. The box at the bottom asks you to name the case, so explain it the way you'd tell a friend who missed this week.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-11-4-guide2\n\nMatthew may not enjoy being noticed, but he's a careful noticer himself, and that matters today. At the start of the week, you learned that being seen isn't the same as being praised. Mrs. Allan saw Anne past the ruined cake. Today the person doing the seeing hardly talks at all. As you read, keep the box's question in mind, and watch who notices Anne without saying a word.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-11-4-wgrd\n\nMatthew's already squirming just thinking about it, and I don't blame him one bit. You've been testing setting all week, from the tea at the manse to the party at Diana's. Today the box adds one more job: looking back across Chapters 21 to 25 to choose the chapter where Avonlea mattered most. Jot a note about the store while you read. Then compare it with the roof and the cake before you decide.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-11-4-read\n\nI think Matthew knows more about why than he lets on. Think back to the very start of the book, when he drove to the station and found a girl waiting instead of a boy. He was shy then, and he still is. Keep that picture in your head, because it shows how big this errand is for him. Let that early chapter help you with the Pause and Think question.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-11-4-guide3\n\nMatthew can laugh about that sugar now, and you're allowed to smile at it too. Two of today's questions want a quotation, so bring back the habit from earlier this week. Choose only the words that do the proving, put them in quotation marks, and follow them with a sentence of your own. Matthew says very little in this chapter, so his actions count as evidence too.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-11-4-chapter\n\nMatthew's trying to talk you out of choosing him, which is exactly what a shy person would do. Same move from the start of the week: each question has two parts, so find the second half before you write. Question three asks how Anne changed, and that's the half people forget. Go back to the chapter for the adult you choose, find a moment right after they see her, and ask what Anne does differently.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-11-4-workshop\n\nMatthew took his time too, and he still got there, so don't fret over making it perfect. Look at the box that says Anne did it too. A wish from Chapter 11 gets answered in Chapter 25, and your ending is where your kid's want from Scene 1 gets answered. In Now You Try, check that the trouble really climbed from Scene 1 up to Scene 3, your worst moment. Sam's ending is messy on purpose, and yours can be too.\n","11-fluency":"# Week 11 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 11 Fluency · Anne of Green Gables, Ch. 21, after the liniment cake. About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-11-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's passage has two voices in it, so you get to be an actor. First there's Anne, right after the liniment cake, when she's tired and embarrassed but still hopeful. Her line is a question, so let your voice rise a little at the end, and say \"a new day with no mistakes in it yet\" slowly, like you're imagining it. Then comes Marilla. She isn't being mean. She's teasing Anne in her dry, matter-of-fact way, and there's a little fondness underneath. Try making your voice a bit lower and flatter for Marilla, so a listener can tell the two people apart without hearing their names. Practice the switch between them a few times before you record.\n","12-1":"# Lesson 12.1 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 26 · The Story Club Is Formed. Guide this lesson: Diana. Each script plays right after Diana's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-12-1\n\nHi, friends, and welcome to week twelve. Anne is thirteen now, and in Chapter 26 she and her friends start a club where they make up and write their own stories. That makes this a fun chapter for you, because you're in the middle of writing your own story, too. Today's lesson pulls in two directions, and that's on purpose. In the warm-up and the chapter questions, you'll practice summarizing, which means leaving things out and keeping only what matters most. Then in Writer's Workshop, you'll start revising, and today's step is adding. A good writer needs both. You have to know which parts to keep short and which parts deserve more room. Watch for that as you work through the page today.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-12-1-welcome\n\nDiana's teasing, but she isn't wrong. Anne could stretch one sentence into fifty and enjoy every one of them. Miss Stacy gave the class a writing assignment, and Anne turned it into a club. Real writers do that too. Grown-up authors often meet in small writing groups, where they share drafts and talk about what's working. It helps to have friends who are waiting to find out what happens next. As you read Chapter 26, notice what the girls do with their stories once they're written, and how each of them feels about sharing them with the others.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-12-1-wgrd\n\nDiana's way of ending stories turns out to be pretty handy for summarizing. Sometimes knowing when to quit is the whole trick. A lot of people think a summary means telling the first few things that happened. A good summary covers the whole chapter, start to end, in very few words. One pattern helps: someone wanted something, but there was a problem, so they did something, and then it ended a certain way. Try it on a movie you know well before you try it on Chapter 26. Also watch for your own opinion sneaking in. A summary tells what happened, not whether you liked it.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-12-1-dol\n\nDiana sounds a little offended about that missing capital, and honestly, I would be too. Take a closer look at the word \"read\" in the model sentence. It's spelled the same in the present and the past, but it doesn't sound the same. Today I read, said like \"reed,\" and yesterday I read, said like \"red.\" The rest of the sentence tells you which one it is. For owning something, try a quick test. If you can flip it to \"the horse of Matthew,\" then it's Matthew's horse, with an apostrophe. But if you mean the whole Barry family, you write \"the Barrys,\" with no apostrophe at all.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-12-1-guide1\n\nPoor Ruby. Diana's right, though, the middle column is no place to hide. Both sorts today are about words that break a rule, and that's not an accident. English has lots of rules and lots of words that don't follow them, and strong spellers know the exceptions as well as the rules. On the sorts, you figure out the pattern first. The page explains it afterward, in the part called \"Now check your thinking.\" Try not to read that until you've sorted everything and named the groups yourself. A pattern you discover sticks much longer than one somebody tells you. If you miss one, stop and figure out why it belonged somewhere else.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-12-1-morphology\n\nDiana's wagon-and-magnet trick is a clever one. I'd never have pictured a magnet, but it fits perfectly. These two roots come from Latin, the language of ancient Rome, and once you know them you'll see them everywhere. A porter carries bags at a train station or a hotel. A tractor is built to pull a plow across a field. You even use one of these roots in math class. When you subtract, you pull one number away from another. If you're unsure about a word, think of another word you know with the same root and see if the meanings match.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-12-1-vocabulary\n\nThat sounds just like Anne, and Diana's careful way is the better one here. Once you've flipped a card, there's one more check worth doing. Put the meaning back into the book sentence in place of the word, and read it aloud. If it still makes sense, you've got it. If it sounds strange, take another look. Word endings help too. When a word ends in l-y, like \"carefully\" or \"quietly,\" it usually tells how something is done. And think about the word \"diligent\" while you read today. Making up whole stories and writing them out for a club, just for fun, takes a lot of diligence.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-12-1-grammar\n\nOnly Anne would think rule-breaking could be beautiful, but these verbs really do it in style. You might wonder why they don't just add e-d. It's because they're some of the oldest and most-used words in English. People said them so often, for hundreds of years, that the old ways of changing them stuck around. That's why little kids say things like \"I runned\" or \"I bringed.\" They aren't being silly. They've figured out the regular rule and they're using it everywhere. You already met one irregular verb today in the Daily Oral Language. Notice that the verbs we use most are the ones most likely to break the rule.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-12-1-spelling\n\nDiana makes it sound easy, and once you see the pattern, it really is. This rule ties right back to week ten. Back then, you learned that c and g usually make their soft sounds before e, i, or y, and their hard sounds before a, o, or u. Keep that in your head while you sort. Try saying a word as if the silent e had disappeared, and listen to what happens to the c or the g. Then ask yourself what job that little e might be doing. Words like these trip up plenty of grown-ups, so knowing why the e is there means you'll never have to guess.\n\n## Say It Three Ways · g4ela-12-1-copia\n\nI'm with Anne on this one, though I like that Diana keeps a little doubt. When you move a when or where part to the front of a sentence, it usually needs a comma after it, right before the main part begins. When you swap the verb, try it out loud, because not every verb fits. \"Diana said her aunt a letter\" doesn't work at all. By the way, that's the same Aunt Josephine whose spare-room bed Anne jumped onto back in Chapter 19. For the third way, think about what a reader would want to picture as Diana wrote. Was she in a hurry, or taking her time?\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-12-1-guide2\n\nDiana doubts Anne is cured, and I'd say Diana knows her best friend pretty well. Read Anne's words in this box closely, because there's something funny in them. She says she can't talk about Josie Pye without being unkind, so she never mentions her at all. And then she mentions her, right there in the same breath. \"Uncharitable\" means unkind, or not generous toward someone. Anne hasn't fixed her mistake yet, but she catches it, and that's a real step. Most of us learn this way. We notice ourselves doing the thing before we can stop doing it.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-12-1-read\n\nI love that Diana is the one who noticed Avonlea never shows up in the club's stories. She sees more than people give her credit for. In Anne's time, many of the stories people loved were very dramatic, full of daring rescues and tragic deaths, and young writers often copied them. So when the club writes about faraway places and huge events, they're writing what they've read. Keep one more fact in mind at the second stop. The author, Lucy Maud Montgomery, grew up on Prince Edward Island and set this book in a small farming town a lot like her own. That doesn't answer the question for you, but it's worth thinking about.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-12-1-guide3\n\nDiana has a point about Anne, though I suspect Anne would argue for a longer answer anyway. One habit makes every answer stronger. Turn the question into the start of your answer. If a question asks why Anne thinks something, your first sentence can begin with \"Anne thinks a moral is important because.\" That way, anyone reading knows exactly what you mean, even without the question in front of them. Use names, too, instead of only \"she\" or \"they.\" There are four girls in this club, and your reader needs to know which one you mean. When you finish, read each answer as if you'd never seen the book, and add anything that's missing.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-12-1-chapter\n\nI'm on Diana's side. Squeezing a story that dramatic into a couple of sentences would make anyone groan. For question two, be careful which story you summarize. It doesn't want Chapter 26. It wants the story Anne wrote, \"The Jealous Rival,\" a story inside the story. Use the same summarizing thinking from the warm-up. For question three, it helps to know exactly what a moral is. It's the lesson a story teaches. You know some from fables. In \"The Tortoise and the Hare,\" the moral is that slow and steady wins the race. Once you've explained why Anne cares about morals, say what that shows about her as a writer.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-12-1-workshop\n\nDiana likes the quiet moments best, and plenty of good readers agree with her. Sam's fall is a good model for choosing where to add. He didn't add details everywhere. He picked the most important moment in his scene and slowed down right there. Think of an instant replay in a sports game. They don't replay the whole game in slow motion, just the big play. Your story's big play is probably a moment when your character feels something strong, like fear or embarrassment. That's the spot to stretch. When you're done, check your word count. It should go up today. If it didn't change, go back and find your spot.\n","12-2":"# Lesson 12.2 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 27 & 28 · Vanity and Vexation of Spirit; An Unfortunate Lily Maid. Guide this lesson: Marilla. Each script plays right after Marilla's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-12-2\n\nHi, friends. This is lesson two of week twelve, and today you get two chapters instead of one. In the last lesson, Anne learned something about writing stories at her story club. Today she learns two lessons the hard way. In Chapter 27 she tries to change the one thing about herself she has always hated, and in Chapter 28 a game on the pond goes very wrong. Our big idea this week is learning from mistakes, so keep asking what each scrape teaches her. Your reading move is summarizing, which means deciding what to leave out. And in Writer's Workshop, you'll cut a sentence from your own story. Those two jobs are really the same job, so the practice you get in one will help you with the other.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-12-2-welcome\n\nMarilla's right that Anne will explain her lessons in great detail. I don't think Anne has ever used ten words when a hundred would do. Think back to the first chapters of this book. On the buggy ride from Bright River, Anne told Matthew how much she hated her red hair. When Mrs. Lynde made fun of it, Anne stamped her foot and shouted at her. When Gilbert called her \"Carrots,\" she broke her slate over his head. So her hair has been at the center of some of her biggest moments. For Anne, changing it would mean getting rid of something that has hurt her since she was little. As you read, see if you can feel how badly she wants it, even when you can guess it won't go well.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-12-2-wgrd\n\nMarilla knows how big Anne can make the smallest thing, and she isn't wrong about that. When you're not sure where to start a summary, try a frame that a lot of readers use. Somebody wanted something, but something got in the way, so they did something, then it ended a certain way. Somebody, wanted, but, so, then. If you can fill in those five words for a chapter, you have a solid summary, and the fun extras have nowhere to go. Try it once for Chapter 27 and once for Chapter 28. Also watch for your own opinion sneaking in. A summary tells what happened. What you think about Anne's choices belongs in your answers on the Assignment tab.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-12-2-dol\n\nPoor Marilla. I don't think she enjoyed seeing that sentence in print, even if she can't argue with it. A sentence with two complete thoughts and nothing between them is called a run-on, and you'll find plenty of them in first drafts, even in grown-up writing. Today's fix uses a period, but there's a second way, and the model sentence shows it. Look at how the author joins two thoughts with a comma and the word \"but.\" You could do the same with \"and\" or \"so,\" as in, \"The rain stopped, so we went outside.\" Both fixes are correct. Writers pick a period when they want each thought to land on its own, and a comma with a joining word when the two thoughts belong together.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-12-2-guide1\n\nMarilla has followed Anne down a lot of side paths, so I'd take her advice on this one. Every week, the word work moves through four days. Last time, you noticed and sorted. Today you identify, which means hunting for examples. Next you'll make the words yourself, and on the last day you'll check a draft for mistakes. So if something felt confusing last time, today is the day to get it settled. Look at the two tapping activities side by side. In grammar you're looking for the verbs that break the regular rule. In spelling, you're looking for one particular group, not the whole list. Your grammar Show What You Know answer gets saved to your Assignment tab, so write it in complete sentences.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-12-2-morphology\n\nNew company is a nice way to put it, Marilla. Those little beginnings will feel friendly soon enough. Once you know these roots, you'll start spotting them everywhere. A tractor is a machine that pulls a plow or a wagon across a field. When you subtract, you pull a number away. A seaport is a place where ships carry goods in and out, and a report carries news back to someone. Think about the peddler in today's chapter, too. A peddler walks from house to house with everything he sells right there with him, a whole little store on the road. As you match, look at what each beginning adds, because the same root can mean something quite different depending on what comes before it.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-12-2-vocabulary\n\nI'd love to know who Marilla had in mind for those sentences, but I doubt she'll ever tell us. The ending of a word can help you narrow down a match. Words that end in \"ly,\" like \"quietly\" or \"gently,\" usually tell how someone does something. Words that end in \"ity,\" like \"curiosity,\" name a thing or a feeling. Words that end in \"ent,\" like \"patient,\" usually describe a person. That won't give you every answer, but it can rule out a meaning that doesn't fit. When you've matched three, don't let the last one be automatic. Check it as carefully as the first. One of these words shows up in the Growing-Up Watch box on the Reading tab today, so see if you can find it.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-12-2-grammar\n\nMarilla wants every last one found, and she's right to be strict here, because these verbs hide well. Have you ever heard a little kid say, \"I goed to the park\"? Kids say that because they've figured out the regular rule and use it everywhere. The verbs that break the rule tend to be the oldest and most common words in English, the ones people have said so often for so long that they wore into their own shapes. That's why short, everyday verbs are where you should look hardest. They're easy to read right past. One tricky case to watch for is a verb that comes right after the word \"to,\" like \"wanted to swim.\" That one isn't in the past at all, even if the sentence is.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-12-2-spelling\n\nI'm with Marilla on liking a rule you can count on, and this one is very steady. This week's quiet e shows up in more places than the six words on the page. The letter y can keep c and g soft, too. Ice becomes icy, and sponge becomes spongy, and the e can go, because the y does its job. On the other side, when a suffix starts with a consonant, the e almost always stays, as in peaceful or arrangement. A consonant can't make the c or g soft, so the e has to stay and do the work. When you write your Show What You Know, explain what the e does to the sound, not only which letter it is. Telling why shows you really understand the rule.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-12-2-guide2\n\nMarilla really has been right there for every one of those scrapes, and I think she's earned the right to wonder. Notice that the quote in this box comes from Anne herself.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's how it ends, in Anne's own words: \"Dyeing my hair cured me of vanity. … And today's mistake is going to cure me of being too romantic.\"\n\nIn it, she does something most people find hard. She names exactly what each mistake taught her. It's easy to say \"I'll never do that again.\" It's much harder to say what you learned. But naming a lesson and living by it aren't the same thing. The real test is what she does the next time. A lot of chapters in this book work this way, where one scrape leads to one lesson. Today you get two chapters, so you get two fresh chances to check whether she acts any differently.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-12-2-read\n\nMarilla's opinion would be fun to hear, but she's right to let you make up your own mind first. Both chapter titles today have a story behind them. \"Vanity and vexation of spirit\" is an old phrase from the Bible. Vexation means being troubled or upset, so the title warns you trouble is coming. The second title comes from a long poem by Alfred, Lord Tennyson that Anne's class studied at school. In it, a young woman named Elaine, called the lily maid, dies of a broken heart, and her body is floated down a river in a boat. That's the scene Anne and her friends decide to act out. It sounds gloomy, but to Anne it's the most romantic thing in the world.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-12-2-rwm\n\nMarilla wasn't even there, and she still read that boy perfectly. That tells you how well this sentence works.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's how Montgomery wrote it: \"Gilbert sprang into his skiff with an angry color in his cheeks.\"\n\nLook closely at the verb. Gilbert doesn't step into the boat or get into it. He springs. That one word tells you how fast he moves and how he's feeling, without the author ever naming the feeling. Then notice \"an angry color.\" Most writers would say \"red cheeks,\" but Montgomery tells you what the color means. Those are two small word choices, and they do a lot of work. For your Mimic sentence, spend the most time on your verb. \"Stomped\" and \"tiptoed\" would give you two very different people. Watch for strong verbs like that all through today's chapters.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-12-2-guide3\n\nMarilla would make a strict teacher, but a fair one, and that advice is worth keeping. Before you start typing, look for the word \"and\" in each question. The first two questions each ask two things. The first one wants what her hair turned and what had to be done to fix it. The second wants how Anne saves herself and who rescues her. The most common reason a good answer loses points is that it only answers half. So after you write, go back and check off each part. Use today's reading move here, too. You don't need to retell the whole chapter to answer one question. Pick the part that answers it, say it clearly, and stop.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-12-2-chapter\n\nMarilla saying no more is probably wise, since I doubt she wants to remember that afternoon. For the first question, notice that the fix doesn't happen all at once. There's trying, and then there's what finally works. A strong answer shows that order with words like \"first\" and \"finally.\" For the second question, keep the two people separate. Anne does something for herself before anyone helps her, so give her credit for that part before you get to the rescue. For the third question, explain the mistake for someone who hasn't read it. Use the story map on the page to find it. The brooch is in Chapters 13 and 14, the Haunted Wood in Chapter 20, the liniment cake in Chapter 21, and the hair dye is today.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-12-2-workshop\n\nA story as tidy as Marilla's kitchen is a high bar, but it's a good one to aim for. A useful test for deciding what to cut is to ask whether a detail ever comes back. Sam's sentence about Mateo's dad shows up once and then disappears, so it was really just in the way. If you have a sentence you love that doesn't fit, copy it into your notebook before you delete it. Now look carefully at Sam's moved sentences. Before the move, it said \"He lay there.\" After, it says \"Mateo lay there.\" When you move a sentence, a \"he\" or an \"it\" might not make sense anymore. After you move yours, read the lines before and after it to make sure everything still works.\n","12-3":"# Lesson 12.3 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 29 · An Epoch in Anne's Life. Guide this lesson: Miss Barry. Each script plays right after Miss Barry's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-12-3\n\nHi, friends. This is Day 3 of week twelve, and you already know our big idea is learning from mistakes. In Chapter 29, Anne and Diana go to Charlottetown to stay with Miss Barry, the same aunt whose bed they once jumped on in the dark. Your word work uses the skills from earlier in the week, but today you build every answer yourself. Then you'll read, answer three questions, and make three swaps in your story.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-12-3-welcome\n\nTwo extra girls is a lot for Miss Barry, but I suspect she loved every minute of it. Anne has wanted a grander, fancier life ever since she asked Marilla to call her Cordelia. Now she finally gets a real taste of one in the city. As you read, keep checking whether the real thing matches what she pictured for so long.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-12-3-dol\n\nMiss Barry doesn't mind being the star of a sentence one bit. You fixed a run-on last lesson, so this one should feel familiar. The clue is a new who showing up partway through, like a \"she,\" with an action of its own. That's usually where one thought ends and the next begins. And \"prima donna\" is Italian for the star singer of an opera.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-12-3-guide1\n\nA slate with nobody to help sounds hard. I'm glad you have Check My Work instead. You already noticed and sorted, then hunted for examples, and now the words come out of your own head. If Check My Work says something's off, read the hint and think about the rule before you try again. Your spelling Show What You Know gets gathered into your assignment, so take your time.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-12-3-morphology\n\nTwo choices is a friendly number when you're stuck, so Miss Barry has a point. You've met port and tract twice already this week, so these roots should feel like old friends. If you get stuck, use the check from the start of the week. Think of a word you know well, like transport or attract, and see if its meaning fits the definition next to the blank.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-12-3-vocabulary\n\nMiss Barry has never been shy about calling silly things silly, and her ear test is a good one. You've seen these four words all week. Today the job flips. Instead of matching a word to its meaning, you're choosing the word a sentence needs. Every sentence is about something from this week's chapters, so the story itself is a clue.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-12-3-grammar\n\nMiss Barry wants good sentences, and I'd guess she has strong opinions about what counts as good. You've noticed these verbs and hunted for them, and today you make them yourself. Some change just a vowel, and some turn into a whole new word. When you write about Chapter 29, try to pick verbs that change in different ways, so you have two different changes to name.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-12-3-spelling\n\nI'm sure Miss Barry could spot a careless spelling from across the room. You already know why that quiet e sticks around. It's guarding the soft sound of the c or g. For your Show What You Know, words in Chapter 29 that end in -nce or -nge are good ones to try. Explain what the e does to the sound, like you practiced last time.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-12-3-guide2\n\nI love that Miss Barry owns up to changing her mind. That isn't easy for anyone. You've watched Anne catch herself all week. Back in the first lesson, she said something unkind about Josie Pye in the very breath she promised not to. Now read Anne's moment in the Growing-Up Watch box.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nAnne says it herself: \"I was real glad she did. And I was glad that I felt glad, for it shows I'm improving, don't you think, Marilla, when I can rejoice in Josie's success?\"\n\nShe's glad Josie won a prize, and she sees that gladness as proof she's improving. Small changes like that still count.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-12-3-wgrd\n\nMiss Barry won't tell us which moment she'd pick, so you'll have to decide for yourself. Earlier this week you summarized with a frame: somebody wanted, but, so, then. Today adds the turning point, the moment your whole summary hangs on. Keep in mind that a turning point can be quiet, like a thought someone has alone in bed. And take another look at the chapter title. The box tells you what an epoch is.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-12-3-read\n\nI'd have laughed too, Miss Barry. Anne's opinions are always worth hearing. The Exhibition in this chapter was a lot like a county or state fair today, with prize animals, baking, and horse races. Much of the trip reaches you through Anne telling Marilla about it afterward, so you're seeing the city through her excited eyes.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-12-3-rwm\n\nMiss Barry not keeping cows made me smile. I can't picture her in a pasture at all.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's the sentence from Chapter 29: \"The cows swung placidly down the lane, and Anne followed them dreamily, repeating aloud the battle canto from Marmion.\"\n\nLast lesson, one verb showed you how fast Gilbert moved. Look for that same kind of word here. \"Swung\" shows how the cows move, and \"placidly\" means calmly. What makes the sentence fun is the gap between that calm lane and the poem racing through Anne's head. Try to build that same gap into your Mimic sentence.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-12-3-guide3\n\nI believe Miss Barry about the decorations, since she's read plenty of Avonlea stories. Last lesson you practiced spotting questions that ask two things, so keep that habit going. Before you submit, look at the checklist at the bottom. It includes your three Read Like a Writer answers and your spelling response, so make sure both of those are finished.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-12-3-chapter\n\nIce cream as a cure for big feelings is very Miss Barry, and honestly not a bad idea. Same habit as last lesson: count the parts in each question. For question one, \"specific\" means something a reader could find on the page, so \"they had fun\" won't do. For question three, a decision means comparing, so a strong answer names both places before it tells what Anne concluded.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-12-3-workshop\n\nMiss Barry still sounds a little pleased about that kiss, and I don't blame her. This is your third revision tool this week. You added, then you cut and moved, and today you swap. For your verb, look back at the Read Like a Writer sentence. Montgomery wrote that the cows \"swung,\" and you can see them move. Sam's \"slammed\" shows that a strong verb can be short and ordinary. Make one swap where the moment really matters.\n","12-4":"# Lesson 12.4 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 30 · The Queen's Class Is Organized. Guide this lesson: Miss Stacy. Each script plays right after Miss Stacy's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-12-4\n\nHi, friends. This is the last lesson of week twelve, and it's a looking-back day. Over the week, Anne went from green hair to a boat sinking under her on the pond. In Chapter 30 things finally calm down, and she takes stock of where her life is headed. You'll do some looking back, too, summing up five chapters and reading your own story from start to finish. You already know how to do all of it.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-12-4-welcome\n\nI like that Miss Stacy admits she can't see everything. Readers sometimes catch more than teachers do. Earlier this week the chapters were full of scrapes, and Chapter 30 is calmer, with a lot of talk about the future. Quiet chapters still matter. Sometimes a person changes without anything dramatic happening, and only notices later. As you read, ask which parts of Anne seem the same as always, and which seem new.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-12-4-dol\n\nMiss Stacy sounds pleased to show up in a warm-up sentence, and I don't blame her. You've mended a run-on like this before. In lesson two you learned two ways to do it, and the model uses one of them. Either way works on the bottom sentence, so pick the one that sounds best. One small extra about Latin. Languages always get a capital, but most school subjects, like math and science, stay lowercase.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-12-4-guide1\n\nTraps is the right word, and it's kind of Miss Stacy to warn you ahead of time. You've already used two irregular verbs today, in the warm-up sentence about Anne learning Latin. In grammar, think about why a writer might make a verb sound wrong. In spelling, think about what that quiet e does for a c or a g. If you can say the rule to yourself before you tap, you'll know what you're hunting for.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-12-4-morphology\n\nI'd have loved those fern afternoons, though I probably would have named one too soon. You've lived with these two roots all week, in words like tractor and report. As you match, give the front parts some attention. Export carries things out of a country and import carries them in, so the front part changes which way the carrying goes. Watch for look-alikes, too. A portion of pie has those letters, but it isn't carrying anything.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-12-4-vocabulary\n\nAnyone with a little brother or sister knows Miss Stacy is right about all those questions. These four words have been with you since the first lesson, and two of them came right out of chapter titles. Vanity named the green hair chapter, and epoch named Anne's trip to town in Chapter 29. Whichever option you pick, show what the word means in a real moment, the way those chapter titles did.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-12-4-grammar\n\nMiss Stacy has a generous way of looking at mistakes, and I agree with her. You already know why these happen. Earlier this week we talked about little kids who learn the regular rule and then use it on every verb. That's the thinking to describe in your box. One spot to watch is a helper word like had. Some verbs change shape after a helper, the way she ate becomes she had eaten.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-12-4-spelling\n\nGiving a silent letter a job to do is such a nice way to think about it. Think back to the start of the week, when you sorted words by what happens to that e. The clue is the letter right after the c or g, because some vowels keep those letters soft and some don't. If you're stuck, test the rule on a word that isn't in this draft, like peaceable, then come back to the six.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-12-4-guide2\n\nForgiving a mistake and never bringing it up again is one of the kindest things about Miss Stacy. Earlier this week we wondered whether Anne would act differently after a mistake. This box is where you start to find out. Read Anne's moment slowly, especially where she wishes she hadn't been so proud.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nListen to how the book puts it. Anne hopes no one will ever \"suspect how sorry she was and how much she wished she hadn't been so proud and horrid!\"\n\nHer anger at Gilbert goes all the way back to the slate she broke over his head in her first days at school.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-12-4-wgrd\n\nSqueezing twelve months of history into a couple of lines sounds painful, and I can picture her older pupils groaning. You already have a tool for this from the start of the week, the somebody, wanted, but, so, then frame. Today you stretch it across five chapters. Ask what Anne wanted over all of them, not in a single scrape. Keep checking for your opinion sneaking in, and use words like so and because to hold it together.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-12-4-read\n\nReaders getting to know more than the characters do is one of my favorite parts of a novel. Chapter 30 covers months, not one afternoon, so watch for clues about time. When the author skips ahead, that's the same leaving-out move you practiced in your summaries. Also think about what Miss Stacy's plan meant for a girl back then. Going on past the little Avonlea school took hard work and some very hard tests.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-12-4-guide3\n\nI'd hang Miss Stacy's advice about rushing right over my desk. Bring along the habits from earlier this week. Turn each question into the start of your first sentence, and use names instead of only she. When you add a quote, set it up with a few words of your own, like Anne admits, and copy only the part you need. The Growing-Up Watch box on the Reading tab is a good place to look back.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-12-4-chapter\n\nMiss Stacy claims the book knows more than she does, but I suspect she guesses more than she lets on. You know to check every question for two parts. For the second one, the hint underneath gets you started, so spend most of your effort on the why. For the third, words like before and after can show how Anne changed. For a challenge, try the extra question about whether she could have learned it another way.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-12-4-workshop\n\nLeaving everything alone is harder than it sounds, and Miss Stacy knows writers love to tinker. All week you've been revising, adding in one lesson and cutting in another. Today you see what that work did. When you name your strongest part, say why it works. Sam's Scene 3 is the fall he slowed down at the start of the week. If a scene still feels thin, that's okay. Sam leaves his ending alone, because Week 14 is for endings.\n","12-fluency":"# Week 12 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 12 Fluency · Anne of Green Gables, Ch. 28, after the Lily Maid. About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-12-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's passage is Anne looking back at her own mistakes, and it's one of the most grown-up things she's said so far. She isn't crying or making excuses. She's being honest, and she's even a little funny about it. Read it like someone telling a story about themselves that they can finally smile at. There's a long first sentence, so take a breath before you start and use the comma to rest. Then each mistake gets its own short sentence. Say the name of each mistake clearly, like the Haunted Wood and dyeing her hair, because those are moments you've read about. Let your voice slow down a bit at the end, so your listener can tell she's thought about this.\n","13-1":"# Lesson 13.1 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 31 · Where the Brook and River Meet. Guide this lesson: Marilla. Each script plays right after Marilla's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-13-1\n\nHi, friends, and welcome to week thirteen. Last week ended with Anne joining Miss Stacy's class to get ready for the Queen's entrance exam. In Chapter 31, a lot of time passes, starting with a whole summer, and Anne comes out of it looking and sounding different. Today's reading move is about theme, the big idea a story teaches about life. Your word work fits the chapter nicely. Linking verbs are how we say what someone seems like, and comparing words like taller and quieter are how we talk about change. Then in Writer's Workshop, you begin editing your own story, starting with capitals and end marks. As you go, watch for what it looks like when a person changes on purpose.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-13-1-welcome\n\nMarilla missing Anne's chatter is a surprise, isn't it? After all that shushing, she misses the noise. Have you ever had a relative visit after a long time and say, \"Look how tall you've gotten!\" You probably didn't feel any taller. When you see yourself every day, the change is too slow to notice. It takes someone who's been away, or an old photo, to show you. Anne was eleven when she first rode to Green Gables beside Matthew, talking the whole way. This chapter lets you hold that girl next to the one in front of you. As you read, think about which changes Anne sees herself and which ones other people have to point out.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-13-1-wgrd\n\nThat brook turning into a river is a lovely picture, and it fits today's skill well. A lot of readers mix up a topic and a theme. A topic is one or two words, like friendship or growing up. A theme is a whole sentence about that topic, something the story believes is true about life. That's why the box asks you to finish a full sentence. Old fables often end with a moral that spells it out, but most novels don't, so you find the idea in what happens. And when a character states a big idea about life, check it against the story. Characters can be wrong. If the events back it up, you've probably found a real theme.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-13-1-dol\n\nIt's nice to hear Marilla and Miss Stacy agree. Short, plain words suit Marilla perfectly. The model sentence is funny when you know Anne. Near the start of the book, she told Matthew she liked big words, because big ideas needed big words to say them. Now she's quoting Miss Stacy saying the opposite. That one sentence shows you how much she's changed. When you fix the bottom sentence, watch for a word that tempts people to add a capital. Seasons like summer and autumn are not names, so they stay lowercase unless they start a sentence. Months do get capitals, so September gets one, but fall does not.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-13-1-guide1\n\nSorting the mending into three piles sounds exactly like Marilla, tidy right down to the unsure pile. Notice that today's grammar and spelling are partners. Linking verbs join a person to a describing word, and comparing words are describing words that measure one thing against another. Put them together and you get sentences like \"Anne seemed calmer than before.\" That's the kind of sentence you need when you write about how a character has changed. These are notice activities, so the page wants you to find the pattern before it tells you. Try not to peek at the \"Now check your thinking\" part until you've finished sorting and named the groups in your own words.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-13-1-morphology\n\nComparing a root to a good habit tucked away in someone is such a Marilla thing to say. I might borrow it. These two roots show up in a lot of words you already know. A spectator is a person who watches a game, and old-fashioned glasses were called spectacles, because they help you see. The building root is just as busy. A structure is anything that's been built, like a bridge or a barn, and to destroy is the opposite of building. One more word is worth learning. Perspective means the way you look at something, your point of view. Anne's perspective on her own life is changing in this chapter, so you might use that one in your sentence.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-13-1-vocabulary\n\nMarilla can't resist a little poke at Mrs. Lynde, can she? Those two have known each other a long time. Two of today's words are built from smaller words you already know. Broadminded is broad, which means wide, plus minded, so a broadminded person has a wide-open mind. Tactful is tact plus -ful, and tact means knowing what to say so nobody's feelings get hurt. Swap the -ful for -less and you get tactless, which describes someone who blurts out the wrong thing. Ambition comes from a Latin word about going around, because in ancient Rome, people who wanted an important job walked around town asking for votes.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-13-1-grammar\n\nI won't ask which neighbors Marilla has in mind, but I have a guess. Knowing about linking verbs fixes a mistake lots of people make, even grown-ups. The word after a linking verb describes the person, so it shouldn't end in -ly. You'd say, \"I feel bad,\" not \"I feel badly.\" The verbs for your five senses are the sneakiest ones. \"The dog smelled the bread\" is action, but \"the bread smelled fresh\" is linking. Grew can switch too. A farmer grows potatoes, which is action, but the sky grew dark is linking. So if a verb is one of the sense words, slow down and look at what comes after it.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-13-1-spelling\n\nClapping out beats is a good trick, and Marilla promising to keep it a secret is the sweetest part. The page gives you three cases, but a few two-syllable words don't end in y, and they're the tricky ones. Some take an ending, like quiet and quieter. Others sound clumsy with one, so we say more careful, not carefuller. Never use both at once, though. \"More taller\" has two comparing parts doing one job. A few words follow no rule at all. Good becomes better and best, and bad becomes worse and worst. You'll find better in today's model sentence. Use -er when you compare two things, and save -est for three or more.\n\n\n## Say It Three Ways · g4ela-13-1-copia\n\nMarilla would always pick the shortest sentence that still does the job, and I think she's usually right. Think about why those books were up in the attic in the first place. The doctor said no books all summer, so bringing them down is a big moment for Anne. When you swap the verb, choose one that shows how she feels about it. Lugged makes the books sound heavy and dull, and hugged them down the stairs sounds very different. When you stretch the sentence, make sure it's still one sentence. Two complete thoughts with nothing joining them make a run-on, so either add a joining word or split it with a period and a capital.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-13-1-guide2\n\nMarilla is keeping that night to herself, and I'll respect that. You'll find out soon enough. Seeing how you've grown is hard, because growing happens a tiny bit at a time, like your hair getting longer. One trick is to compare yourself with something old. Find a drawing you made in second grade, and you'll see right away how much you've changed. Anne gets a chance like that in this chapter. Think back to the girl who couldn't stop talking about her imagination and her red hair, and compare her with the Anne you're about to meet. Some people announce their changes and some keep them private, and both tell you something about the person.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-13-1-read\n\nAsking you not to think too hard about the second stop is the surest way to make everyone think about it, and Marilla knows that. A little background will help with the start of this chapter. In Anne's time, doctors didn't have most of the medicines we have today. When a child seemed run down, a doctor would often order fresh air and sunshine. So keeping Anne outside all summer was a real prescription, and Marilla treated it that way. As you read, notice who tells you about Anne's change. Some comes from the narrator, and some comes from Anne herself, in her own words. Mark the lines where Anne explains her feelings, because you'll want them later.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-13-1-guide3\n\nMarilla wants answers that stay on track, and I'll admit I'm with her on that one. These three questions don't ask for an exact quotation, but they do want evidence. You can give evidence in your own words. Tell what happened in the chapter clearly enough that a reader could find that part in the book. That's called paraphrasing, and it counts. Each question also has two parts, so before you start, find the word \"and\" in the question and make sure you answer what comes on both sides of it. When you finish, check that every sentence starts with a capital letter and ends with an end mark.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-13-1-chapter\n\nMarilla admitting she enjoyed being miserable is so honest it's almost funny. I think plenty of us know that feeling. For the first question, look in the opening pages for the doctor's words and then Marilla's plan, and make sure your answer covers both. For the second, today's word work helps. A change is a comparison between before and now, so comparing words and linking verbs fit right in. Try a sentence that uses \"used to\" and \"now.\" For the third, the end of the chapter matters most. Read what Marilla is thinking. If you take the challenge, it's fine to decide that Anne has changed and also stayed herself. Just explain how both can be true.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-13-1-workshop\n\nI love Marilla's lamp over the swept floor. It's the best picture of editing I've heard in a while. Run-on sentences are the hardest thing to catch today, so try reading your story aloud, quietly. When your voice drops and you take a breath, a sentence has usually ended, and that's where a period belongs. That's how Sam caught \"today was tryouts\" running into the next idea. Don't go too far the other way, though. A sentence with \"and\" or \"because\" joining two ideas is fine. Check the dialogue in your story, too. The first word inside the quotation marks gets a capital, even in the middle of a line. Then write your three fixes, so you can see the slips you make most.\n","13-2":"# Lesson 13.2 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 32 · The Pass List Is Out. Guide this lesson: Diana. Each script plays right after Diana's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-13-2\n\nHi, friends. Welcome to the second lesson of week thirteen. In Chapter 31, Anne noticed herself growing up, and she said out loud that growing up is a serious thing. Today, in Chapter 32, all of Anne's hard work for the Entrance exam finally pays off, and we find out how she did. You'll start with theme, which is the big idea a chapter is trying to teach. The word work today is all about describing words, the verbs that link a person to a description and the words we use to compare. Then you'll read, answer three questions, and keep editing your story. Today you'll fix the dialogue, which is the talking in your story, and this chapter has some wonderful talking to learn from.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-13-2-welcome\n\nDiana says she won't spoil it, and I believe her, mostly. She's practically bouncing. In Anne's time, the results of a big test like the Entrance were printed right in the newspaper for everyone on the island to read. Imagine your score in the paper for your whole town to see. No wonder everyone in Avonlea is so anxious. As you read, notice how waiting can feel worse than the test itself. Anne got through the exam, and now all she can do is wait. Think about a time you waited for big news. Keep that feeling in mind, because it'll help you understand Anne today.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-13-2-wgrd\n\nDiana's a little hard on herself. Success is a fine place to start, and she found it fast. Today's skill has two words you may not know, stated and implied. A stated theme is one a character or the narrator says right out loud. Yesterday, Anne said growing up is a serious thing, and that was stated. An implied theme is never said. You figure it out from what the characters do. Today's theme is implied, so you'll have to work for it. Watch out for one common mistake. Anne tying with Gilbert is an event, and a theme isn't an event. A true theme still makes sense if you take Anne's name out, because it's about real people, including you.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-13-2-dol\n\nDiana would vote for Orchard Slope, of course, and I can't blame her for loving her own home. Look at the comma between \"dearest\" and \"loveliest\" in the model. When two describing words sit side by side in front of a noun, you can test whether they need a comma. Put the word \"and\" between them. \"The dearest and loveliest spot\" sounds fine, so a comma goes there. But \"a big red barn\" wouldn't work with \"and\" in the middle, so it gets no comma. Now for the sentence you're fixing. To check whether two sentences are jammed into one, look for two people each doing or being something. Each one needs its own sentence, or a joining word to hold them together.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-13-2-guide1\n\nDiana's honest about stopping too soon, and plenty of grown-ups do the same thing, so she's in good company. Each day this week, the word work asks something a little different. On Day 1 you noticed a pattern. Today you identify it, which means finding it out in the wild, in real sentences. Tomorrow you'll build with it, and on the last day you'll catch mistakes. That order is the way people learn anything, like a new card game. First you watch, then you point out the moves, then you play, and finally you can spot when somebody cheats. Pay extra attention to the Show What You Know boxes. Explaining why in your own words proves you really know it.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-13-2-morphology\n\nI'm glad Diana admitted that mix-up, because those two little parts trip up lots of readers. Most prefixes work like little arrows. They point the action in a direction. You already know some that aren't in today's game. Ex- points out, like exit, the door you go out of. Sub- points under, like a submarine that goes under the water. So when you look at the four cards, notice that some of them are actions, like looking and building, and some of them are arrows that tell which way the action goes. Sorting them that way in your head first makes the matching a lot less confusing. Then check each pair against the examples box.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-13-2-vocabulary\n\nChoosing words like a dress is such a Diana thing to say, and it actually works. Some of this week's words have smaller words hiding inside them, and those help you match. Think about \"careful.\" It's care plus -ful, which means full of care. When a word is two words stuck together, ask what each part means on its own. Words also come in families. If you have ambition, you're ambitious. If you keep your composure, people say you stayed composed. Knowing the family helps you spot a word when it changes shape in a book. And notice these words describe what a person is like on the inside. As you read Chapter 32, ask which ones fit the people waiting for that pass list.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-13-2-grammar\n\nDiana's test works for most verbs. A few sneaky ones can switch jobs, though, and that's where this gets tricky. Take the word \"felt.\" If I say \"Anne felt the smooth paper,\" she's touching it with her hands, so that's an action. But in \"Anne felt nervous,\" the word is connecting Anne to nervous, so it's a linking verb. It's the same word doing two different jobs. Words like smelled and sounded can switch the same way. So don't decide by the word alone. Decide by what it's doing in that sentence. One more clue: a linking verb works almost like an equals sign. The word after it describes the person before it, the way nervous describes Anne.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-13-2-spelling\n\nDiana's little-child test made me smile, because small kids really do say things like \"gooder.\" You might wonder why long words use \"more\" and \"most\" instead of an ending. Try saying \"wonderfulest.\" It's a mouthful, and English speakers smoothed those away over hundreds of years. One mistake shows up a lot, even with grown-ups. You compare a word only once. You can add an ending, or you can use \"more,\" but never both, like \"more bigger.\" And a few comparing words break every rule. Good becomes better and best. Bad becomes worse and worst. Those you just have to know. Chapter 32 is all about who came out on top, so listen for comparing words as you read.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-13-2-guide2\n\nMore shrieking sounds exactly like Diana, and I can just picture the two of them racing. The last line in the Growing-Up Watch box says the payment Anne wants is somebody else's face when they hear the news. Think about how different that is from the Anne who broke her slate over Gilbert's head back in Chapter 15. Back then, everything was about her own hurt feelings. Since then, she and Gilbert have raced each other at school for a long time, so the pass list is a big moment in that rivalry. That makes it really interesting to see what she cares about once the moment comes. Hold onto that when you reach the Pause and Think stop at the end.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-13-2-read\n\nEleven times is a lot of rereading, but that's what good friends do with a letter. The Entrance was the test Anne had to pass to get into Queen's, a school in Charlottetown where students trained to become teachers. Students from all over Prince Edward Island took it, so Anne wasn't only competing with her own class. Notice how the author handles time in this chapter. Weeks of waiting go by in just a few pages. Authors do that when nothing much happens, so they can save the slow, detailed writing for the moments that matter. Watch for the spot where the writing slows way down and gives you every little detail. That's the author telling you to pay attention.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-13-2-rwm\n\nDiana's right to love that dash. Grown-up writers use it exactly this way, to stop and point.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's the sentence from Chapter 32: \"She wanted to 'pass high' for the sake of Matthew and Marilla — especially Matthew.\"\n\nDid you notice the little marks around \"pass high\" in this sentence? Those are single quotation marks, tucked inside the regular ones. The author is showing us that \"pass high\" is Anne's own way of saying it. When you quote a sentence that already has a quote inside it, the inside one gets single marks. You'll see that again whenever you copy a line from the book for your answers. Now for your mimic sentence. Make sure the person you single out really is the most important one, and that your reader can tell why. For example: Leo practiced every night for the sake of his mom and his coach, especially his coach.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-13-2-guide3\n\nMiss Stacy and I would get along, because I do plenty of crossing out myself. Take a look at the hints under each question before you start. One of them gives you a piece of a quote, \"gleam with pride.\" That's a clue to where to look, but it isn't your whole answer. Find that spot in Chapter 32 and read the sentences around it. Then you'll understand what the quote means, and you can explain it in your own words. Also, check the list at the very bottom of the page before you submit. It asks for your three Read Like a Writer answers from the Reading tab. Those are easy to forget, because you wrote them before you got here.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-13-2-chapter\n\nOut of breath is the right way to deliver news that big, I think. Question one has two parts, and most people stop after the first. Finding where Anne's name appears is the easy part. The harder part is what it means, so think about everything that has happened between Anne and Gilbert since that first day of school. In question two, look at the word \"nobler.\" It's a comparing word, just like in today's spelling. Nobler than what? The author is putting two of Anne's reasons side by side, so your answer should name the one being compared. For question three, here are Matthew's words.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nMatthew tells Anne: \"I knew you could beat them all easy.\"\n\nChoose one word from what he says and explain what it shows about how he sees her.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-13-2-workshop\n\nDiana and I agree on this one. Dialogue is where a story really comes alive. Let's look at a tricky case. Sometimes the speaker tag lands in the middle of what someone says. \"You came,\" he said, \"even in the rain.\" Notice that \"even\" starts with a small letter, because it's still the same sentence. The tag gets a comma on each side of it. Don't worry about using \"said\" over and over, either. Lots of students think they need fancy words like exclaimed every time. But readers barely notice \"said,\" and that's a good thing, because it keeps their attention on what the characters are saying. Then check your story for one line that would be stronger with a split tag.\n","13-3":"# Lesson 13.3 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 33 · The Hotel Concert. Guide this lesson: Gilbert. Each script plays right after Gilbert's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what he says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-13-3\n\nHi, friends. It's Day 3 of week thirteen, so a lot of today will feel familiar. You already know linking verbs, comparing words, and the roots for looking and building, and today you build with them yourself. The new part is Chapter 33, where Anne recites at a grand hotel concert and freezes up for a moment. After that, you'll keep editing your story, this time hunting for wrong words, misspellings, and run-ons.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-13-3-welcome\n\nGilbert sounds awfully pleased to have been in that room, doesn't he? Earlier this week, Anne saw her name tied at the top of the pass list. Today she faces something scarier than a test: a room full of strangers in fancy clothes. Stage fright happens to brave people too, even grown-ups who perform for a living. As you read, watch for what helps Anne find her voice again. Sometimes it's something very small.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-13-3-dol\n\nAnne really is fussy about her name. She wants it spelled with an e, every time. One thing is new in the model. A linking verb can join a person to a describing word, and it can also join them to a naming word that tells what someone is. Check which kind the model uses. For the bottom sentence, use the trick from Day 2: when a new person starts their own feeling, a break belongs there.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-13-3-guide1\n\nGilbert at the blackboard is easy to picture, and I bet his ear really did save him. Think back to how this week's word work has gone. You noticed the patterns on Day 1 and found them in real sentences on Day 2. Today is the building day. Make a real try before you open a hint, and read the explanation afterward, even when you're right. That's how you know you were right for the right reason.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-13-3-morphology\n\nGilbert picked two great pictures there, and I'll let you decide which goes with which. You met these roots at the start of the week, and the examples box is there if you need a refresher. One new thing to notice today: a root can't stand alone the way a base word like help can. It always needs a part attached. Use the meaning after each dash to check what the whole word should do.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-13-3-vocabulary\n\nGilbert won't say which word fits him, and I have a guess of my own. These four words should feel like old friends by now. You took them apart on Day 1 and matched them on Day 2. Today they're back in sentences about people you know, like Anne, Jane, and Miss Stacy. Before you choose, picture each person in that exact moment and ask what they're like right then.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-13-3-grammar\n\nClose cousins is a good way to put it. These verbs really do look alike. You learned the swap test earlier this week: put was in place of the verb and see if the sentence holds. Every word in today's bank passes that test, so the real question is which shade of meaning fits. Then for Show What You Know, choose a description that tells us what's going on inside Anne while she waits.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-13-3-spelling\n\nI'd love to have watched one of those spelldowns. Those two never let each other win easily. On Days 1 and 2 you practiced each rule by itself. Today both have to work together in the same word. If you get stuck, warm up with a word that isn't on the list, like busy or tiny, and say its comparing form out loud first. In Show What You Know, naming both rules is what proves you didn't just guess.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-13-3-guide2\n\nGilbert's betting against the diamonds, and he sounds pretty sure of Anne. On Day 2, the Growing-Up Watch showed Anne caring more about Matthew's proud face than about beating anybody. Today's box takes that a step further, into choosing what really matters. Think back to the girl who once longed for puffed sleeves. As you read, hold her next to the Anne in this chapter and notice how much has changed.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-13-3-wgrd\n\nOnly smiling, Gilbert says, but in this chapter, a smile carries a lot. You've worked on theme all week. On Day 1 you learned a theme is a full sentence about life, and on Day 2 you sorted stated from implied. Today's is implied. When you write your missing sentence, give it the same test as before: take Anne's name out, and it should still be true about real people.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-13-3-read\n\nGilbert says he thinks she means it, and he sounds honest about that. On Day 2 you watched for the spot where the writing slows down to show a big moment. That move comes back today, so notice where Chapter 33 lingers. In Anne's time, concerts often meant people reciting poems from memory. Keep in mind that we see Gilbert through Anne's eyes, so ask how sure you can be about what his face means.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-13-3-rwm\n\nGilbert likes things in threes, and so do a lot of good speakers. You've seen this before. On Day 2, single quotation marks sat inside the regular ones, and they're back today around everything Anne says.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's Anne's sentence: \"'We are rich,' said Anne staunchly. 'Why, we have sixteen years to our credit, and we're happy as queens, and we've all got imaginations, more or less.'\"\n\nSomething new is the word Why at the start of her second sentence. She isn't asking anything. It's an old way of showing surprise, a bit like saying, Well, of course.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-13-3-guide3\n\nGilbert has a plan for everything, and that one's worth borrowing. Two of today's questions have two parts again, so use the trick from the start of the week and answer both sides of the and. Question three asks you to compare two girls, and joining words like but and while make that easy to follow. Before you submit, check that each answer says clearly which girl you mean, since Anne, Jane, and Diana all show up.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-13-3-chapter\n\nGilbert's a little too curious about question two, if you ask me. Comparing has been our big word all week, and two of these questions are comparisons too. In question one, check whether Anne's feelings about her own things stay the same from the dressing room to the end of the night. In question three, notice that rich sits inside quotation marks. That's a clue Anne means it in a new way.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-13-3-workshop\n\nChecking beats guessing, Gilbert's right about that, and that's true for every writer. This is your third editing pass this week. Capitals and end marks came first, then dialogue, and now word choice and run-ons. For the mix-ups in the chart, try stretching the word out. If they're or you're still makes sense as they are or you are, keep it. There has here hiding inside it, which helps with places. And Sam's run-on is the same kind you fixed in today's warm-up.\n","13-4":"# Lesson 13.4 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 34 · A Queen's Girl. Guide this lesson: Anne. Each script plays right after Anne's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-13-4\n\nHi, friends. This is the last lesson of week thirteen. All week you've watched Anne grow, from her summer outdoors in Chapter 31 to the pass list in Chapter 32. Today, in Chapter 34, she packs up for Queen's. You already know this week's linking verbs and comparing words, so today you hunt for mistakes in them. Then you'll test a theme against a second chapter and read your story aloud for its last edit before you publish next week.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-13-4-welcome\n\nAnne worried about giving a speech, and honestly, I'd have listened to one. It's fine that she's still deciding. Think back to her very first days at Green Gables, when she begged Marilla to call her Cordelia. That girl wished she could be somebody else. Now she's leaving home and working hard to stay herself. As you read, look for the moment where those two Annes feel furthest apart.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-13-4-dol\n\nI'm with Anne on lonesomer. It sounds exactly like the feeling it describes. You've found linking verbs in the model all week, so that part should feel familiar. Look at the small word herself, too. It points back to Anne, so you know who's alone in that hall bedroom. When you fix the second sentence, read it aloud and listen for where your voice wants to rest.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-13-4-guide1\n\nPoor Anne and her geometry. I'm glad she can laugh about it now. Catching mistakes is the last step in the order you've followed since the start of the week. As you work through today's review, notice which slips are hardest for you to spot. Maybe it's the verbs, or maybe it's the endings. Jot your trouble spots in the back of your notebook so you know what to slow down on next time.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-13-4-morphology\n\nAnne adores roots, and so do I. They make long words feel friendly. Spectators and structures came up at the start of the week, both built from these same two roots. Today's matching adds two front parts. Use the examples box on this page as your checker. After you make a match, plug it into one of those example words and see whether the meaning still holds up.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-13-4-vocabulary\n\nI'd happily try every choice on that menu too, Anne, so we're alike there. You've lived with these four words since the start of the week. You already took broadminded and tactful apart into smaller words, so if the word-parts choice appeals to you, half the work is done. Whichever you pick, tie it to Chapter 34. Anne's ambitions get quite a push before this chapter ends.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-13-4-grammar\n\nI believe Anne about her busy verbs. She was never one to sit still. At the start of the week, you saw that sense verbs like smelled can switch jobs, and later you watched felt do the same. A verb that can do both jobs is the easiest one to mislabel, so don't decide from the word alone. Look at what comes right after the verb on each card before you tap.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-13-4-spelling\n\nAnne used to love a grand word, and I'll miss that a little. All week you've sorted comparing words by how many syllables they have, and that count is still your best tool. Before you tap, clap out the base word with its ending taken off. Then ask which rule in the notes box fits a word that size. Save the rule's name for your Show What You Know box.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-13-4-guide2\n\nNobody hands you that answer all at once, and Anne figured that out bit by bit. Chapter 31 had a nature picture in its title, a brook widening into a river. Anne's line in the Growing-Up Watch box uses one too, a tree that's been pruned.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nAnne says it herself: \"I'm not a bit changed — not really. I'm only just pruned down and branched out.\"\n\nGardeners snip off branches on purpose so a tree grows stronger and keeps its shape. Keep that picture in mind while you read.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-13-4-wgrd\n\nTrying a theme on in two mirrors is such an Anne way to say it, and it works. A theme, as you learned at the start of the week, is a whole sentence the story believes about life. Today you're testing it, so watch its size. If it fits every book ever written, it's too big to test. Adding when or because usually shrinks it to something you can check against Chapters 31 through 34.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-13-4-read\n\nAnne promising not to be dramatic made me smile. That might be the biggest change in the whole book. Queen's is in Charlottetown, too far from Avonlea to travel back and forth each day, so Anne has to live in town. Think back to Josie Pye, the girl who dared Anne to walk the ridgepole of a roof. Notice how Anne feels when Josie turns up in that lonely week.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-13-4-guide3\n\nI'd have loved reading Anne's long answers with a poem on the end, but Miss Stacy had a point. Question three looks across four chapters, so you'll need to find your way around the book. The chapter titles help. Today's is \"A Queen's Girl,\" and the first lesson's was \"Where the Brook and River Meet.\" Skim the beginnings and endings, since big moments often land there.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-13-4-chapter\n\nI agree that the third question is the hardest, and I'd also call it the most fun. The challenge on question one asks whether Anne is right that she hasn't really changed. You've been collecting changes all week, starting with the two inches she grew. A strong answer can agree with part of what she says and question another part, as long as you explain why.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-13-4-workshop\n\nIt really isn't bragging, Anne. Writers need to know their own strengths. Earlier this week you caught run-ons by listening for where your voice dropped. Sam's before and after shows that same move working on a whole scene. Slow down on the checklist line about saying a word that isn't on the page. Sometimes the word your mouth chose is better than the one you wrote.\n","13-fluency":"# Week 13 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 13 Fluency · Anne of Green Gables, Ch. 34, the night before Queen's. About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-13-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's passage is Anne talking to Marilla the night before she leaves for Queen's. Marilla is worried that the little girl she loves is gone, and Anne is gently telling her she isn't. So read it softly, the way you'd talk to someone you're trying to comfort. Watch for the dashes. Each one is a small pause, almost like Anne is touching her heart when she says, \"back here.\" The words \"pruned down and branched out\" compare Anne to a tree that's been trimmed so it can grow, so say them like you're proud of it. And slow down on the last part, \"your little Anne,\" because that's the line Marilla needs to hear.\n","14-1":"# Lesson 14.1 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 35 · The Winter at Queen's. Guide this lesson: Gilbert. Each script plays right after Gilbert's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what he says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-14-1\n\nHi, friends, and welcome to week fourteen. This is our last week with Anne of Green Gables, and I'm a little sad about that already. Last week ended with Anne packing up and leaving home for Queen's. Today, in Chapter 35, she's living in town and studying all winter, and you'll get to see how much the girl who arrived at Bright River has grown. Your word work is a review of things you've learned this year, and it's all about catching the kinds of mistakes that sneak into real drafts. That matters, because in Writer's Workshop you start publishing your story today. You'll begin at the very end, by rewriting your last scene so it feels finished.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-14-1-welcome\n\nGilbert is being modest about winning. He's been neck and neck with Anne for years. Think back to Chapter 28. Anne's boat sank, she clung to the bridge, and Gilbert rowed by and helped her. Then he asked if they could be friends, and she said no. Afterward, she felt an odd kind of regret about it. That was a crack in a very old wall. In real life, you see this with athletes all the time. Two runners push each other in every race, then shake hands at the finish line, because each one made the other faster. As you read today, decide whether Anne and Gilbert are getting closer to that kind of rivalry.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-14-1-wgrd\n\nGilbert mentions the slate so calmly now, which tells you something has changed for him too. One mix-up to watch for: growing older and taller isn't the change this box is asking about. Every character gets older in a long book. You're looking for a change on the inside. So start with something Anne did early on. When Mrs. Lynde made fun of her looks and her red hair in Chapter 9, Anne stamped her foot and shouted at her. Hold that next to how she handles things at Queen's. Your sentence frame on the page, \"Anne used to ___, and now she ___,\" works best when both blanks are actions, things you could picture her doing.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-14-1-dol\n\nIt's nice to hear that even Gilbert gets nervous about exams. Even a tiny sentence like \"I will worry\" can point to the future, and the helper word is what puts it there. Take it away and you get \"I worry,\" which is about right now. Now look at the sentence you're fixing. Its two halves happen at different times. The first half reaches back over months of studying, and the second half points ahead to graduation. That's part of why it can't stay as one sentence. Once you've fixed it, read your version aloud. You should hear a little stop in your voice right where the two times meet.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-14-1-guide1\n\nGilbert's professors sound like good teachers. I care more about your reasons than your guesses, too. You might notice the morphology box says \"Final week roots.\" This is the last week of word study for the year, so everything today is review, built from mistakes and words that turn up in real writing. Each Show What You Know box asks you to name the groups and explain the test you used. That's harder than sorting, and it's the part that sticks with you. If you can explain a rule to someone else, you'll catch that mistake on your own later. So if you get one wrong, go back to the \"Now check your thinking\" box and figure out what fooled you.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-14-1-morphology\n\nGilbert studying Latin at Queen's makes sense, because these roots come straight from it. They're hiding in lots of words outside of school. When a doctor gives you a prescription, she writes down what medicine you need. A P.S. at the bottom of a letter stands for postscript, something written after. The throwing root is just as busy. A projector throws a picture onto the wall, and an injection is medicine pushed into you. One spelling tip: when scribe gets the ending -tion, it changes to script, so describe becomes description. That's why the two spellings belong to one family. Anne is writing a lot of exams at Queen's, so this root fits her winter.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-14-1-vocabulary\n\nGilbert is wise not to say which word fits Anne. She'd never let him forget it. Consolation has a cousin you might know, the verb console, which means to comfort someone. If someone is so sad that nothing helps, we say they're inconsolable. That's the \"not\" prefix you learned back in week ten, stuck right on the front. In sports, a consolation prize is what you get for trying when you didn't win. Sanctifying is related to the word sanctuary. An animal sanctuary is a safe place set apart for animals, where nothing can hurt them. So when you see sanct in a word, think of something set apart and treated as special.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-14-1-grammar\n\nI like Gilbert's picture of helpers lined up next to the main verb, like friends at a race. Helping verbs can be hard to spot for two reasons. First, they hide inside contractions. In \"I won't be late,\" won't is short for will not, so there's a helper tucked inside. And \"He'd come anyway\" means he would come. Second, little words can squeeze in between the helper and the main verb. In \"Anne had never studied so hard,\" never isn't a verb at all. It's just sitting in the middle. So find the word that shows the action first, then look in front of it for helpers.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-14-1-spelling\n\nI'd have loved to see those old spelling matches. My guess is they traded wins for years. The doubling rule has a reason behind it, and once you know it, you'll stop guessing. Look at taping and tapping. One p keeps the a long, like in tape. Two p's keep it short, like in tap. The same thing happens with diner and dinner. So the double letter is like a fence that protects the short vowel sound. The y rule has a reason too. If cry plus -ing changed its y, you'd get two i's in a row, which English almost never allows, so it stays crying.\n\n\n## Say It Three Ways · g4ela-14-1-copia\n\nGilbert's teasing Anne about long sentences, but she really did love a long one. Beechwood is Miss Barry's house in town, the same Aunt Josephine that Anne and Diana jumped on in the spare room bed years ago. Back then she was furious, and now Anne visits her in her spare hours. That's a nice little change to notice. Look at the verb in this sentence, too. We say people spend time, the same way they spend money, as if hours were coins in your pocket. We save time and waste it, too. When you swap the verb, try one that changes how the hours feel. Each choice tells the reader something different.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-14-1-guide2\n\nI believe Gilbert that they're still racing. Neither of them knows how to do anything halfway. The Growing-Up Watch box uses the word bitterness. Have you ever tasted a lemon peel or plain baking chocolate? Bitter tastes stay in your mouth long after you've swallowed. Bitter feelings work the same way. They hang around long after the thing that caused them is over. That's what Anne's grudge against Gilbert was like for years after the slate. As you read, watch for the moment the box describes, when Anne hears about Ruby Gillis. Notice what she misses, and whether it's the contest at all.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-14-1-read\n\nGilbert isn't used to being one step behind, so enjoy being ahead of him for once. This chapter moves fast. It covers a whole winter at Queen's in just a few pages, so watch for the small words that tell you time has passed. Phrases about the season or the weather are clues. Queen's is a little like college. Students lived in town, went to classes all week, and studied for big exams at the end of the year. Anne is working toward a medal, and she's not the only one. You'll also meet the model sentence from your warm-up. Jane Andrews says it, and you'll see why.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-14-1-guide3\n\nGilbert sounds like he's been checking his work since he was six, and it shows. That sentence frame from What Good Readers Do, \"Anne used to ___, and now she ___,\" helps in more than one answer today. Question two asks you to put the old Anne beside the new one, and question three can use that move too. A strong answer usually has a then sentence and a now sentence, with a clear word in between, like but or instead. Watch your verbs while you do it. Since you just studied helping verbs, check that your then sentence stays in the past and your now sentence matches it.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-14-1-chapter\n\nGilbert says he didn't know, and I believe him. That's exactly why question one is tricky. The challenge asks whether Gilbert would want the same thing, and we mostly see him through Anne's eyes in this chapter. So look for anything he does that you can actually point to, and use it as your clue. For question two, the challenge asks you to pick one word from your quote. Choose the one that carries the most feeling, then explain why the author picked it. For question three, think back to Chapter 3, the night Anne found out Matthew and Marilla had wanted a boy. What did she want more than anything then? Put that wish next to her dream at the window.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-14-1-workshop\n\nI love Gilbert's idea of a first line and a last line that sound like old friends. That's a good test for any ending. Look at Sam's before version. \"He was happy. The end.\" Lots of writers end that way because they don't know how else to stop. But Sam's after version never uses the word happy, and you still know exactly how Mateo feels. Also avoid wrapping up every problem perfectly. Mateo still expects to be scared next year, and that's what makes it feel true. And don't bring in anything brand new at the end, like a new character or a new problem. If you're stuck, try the frame \"The same ___ that ___ now ___.\"\n","14-2":"# Lesson 14.2 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 36 · The Glory and the Dream. Guide this lesson: Matthew. Each script plays right after Matthew's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what he says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-14-2\n\nHi, friends. This is the second lesson of our very last week with Anne. In the last lesson, in Chapter 35, she spent the winter at Queen's working harder than she ever had. Today, in Chapter 36, the results go up on the board, there's a big ceremony, and Anne comes home to Green Gables. Your reading move is about wishes. You'll compare what Anne wanted when she first arrived with what she wants now. In word study, you'll meet two old roots, match this week's words, and hunt for helping verbs and suffix slips. Then in Writer's Workshop, you'll score your own story with a real rubric and pick one row to make stronger.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-14-2-welcome\n\nMatthew may not be a book person, but I'd trust him on feelings any day. He notices more than he lets on. This week we finish two things at once. Anne finishes her year at Queen's, and you finish the whole book. Coming home to Green Gables means something very different now than it did when Anne was eleven and nobody was sure she could stay. Back then, she was afraid of being sent away. Now Green Gables is simply home, the place she hurries back to when her work is done. As you read, notice the small moments where Anne seems to take in how much has changed, in her and in the people who love her.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-14-2-wgrd\n\nMatthew was the one driving that buggy, so I'm glad he still remembers that first wish so clearly. Wishes are a smart thing to watch, because a character doesn't always say them out loud. Sometimes you figure out what Anne wants from what she works hard for, or from what she'd hate to lose. The third step in the box asks what grew the wish, and that's where your thinking can get really good. Wishes don't change all on their own. Think about who believed in Anne along the way, like Miss Stacy, who started the Queen's class. And a new wish doesn't have to erase the old one. Anne can want something bigger and still love Green Gables with her whole heart.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-14-2-dol\n\nMatthew's glad she wrote home, and I would be too. A letter from someone you miss is the best kind of mail. Look closely at the word must in the model sentence. Some helping verbs can never stand alone. You can say I have a dog, but you can't say I must a dog. Words like must, will, can, and should always need a main verb after them. That main verb stays in its plain form, too. We say she must write, never she must writes or she must wrote. That's a common slip when writers hurry. Anne was excited when she said her line, but the punctuation still has to stay calm and correct.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-14-2-guide1\n\nMatthew's plowing advice made me smile, and he's plowed enough fields to know. All four of these activities are review, and that fits this point in the chapter. This morning, Anne finds out how she did on exams that covered a whole year of studying at Queen's. She couldn't cram all of that the night before. She got there by going over things again and again, the way you're doing here. Before you start, think about which of the four feels hardest to you. Give that one your best attention. The trickiest kind of mistake on this page is usually the one worth an extra look.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-14-2-morphology\n\nMatthew calls himself no scholar, but that seed idea of Anne's is one of the best ways I've heard to describe a root. Today's match mixes roots with the little prefixes that steer them. The root ject always means to throw, but the prefix tells you which way. Re- throws it back, pro- throws it forward, and e- throws it out, which is why some machines have an eject button. The page also hides a bonus root in manuscript. Manu means hand, and you can find it in manual and manicure. In today's chapter, Anne reads her essay aloud at the ceremony. Before typewriters, every essay like hers was a manuscript, written out by hand, line by line.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-14-2-vocabulary\n\nI have a feeling I know which word Matthew is thinking of, and I bet you'll spot it too. The word sanctifying shows up again today on the Reading tab, inside a long sentence near the end of the chapter. When you get there, you'll already know it, and that will help you notice how serious that sentence sounds. The other words you can find in everyday life. Have you ever heard of a consolation prize? Think about who usually gets one and why it might have that name. If two words keep getting mixed up for you, try putting each one into a sentence about somebody you actually know. Real people help words stick better than definitions do.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-14-2-grammar\n\nI'd never thought of helping verbs as neighbors holding up a beam, but Matthew's picture fits. The trickiest helpers are the ones that can also work alone. Have, has, and had can be helpers, but sometimes they're the main verb. In My dog has a bone, has is the main verb, because there's no other verb in the sentence. In My dog has found a bone, has is helping found. So you can't just memorize a list. You have to look at what comes after the word. Watch for a little word that slips in between, like never in I had never seen snow. Never isn't a verb at all. Try finding each main verb first, and then look to its left for its helpers.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-14-2-spelling\n\nMatthew can bring almost anything back to the farm, and he's got a point about fences. When you type, the computer will underline some spelling mistakes, but it can't catch them all. If you write planed when you meant planned, nothing gets underlined, because planed is a real word. A carpenter planes a board to make it smooth. So your own eyes still matter. One quick check works on any word with an ending. Cover the ending with your finger and check the base word. Then ask whether the base word plus this week's rule gives you exactly that spelling. The Show What You Know box also asks about an exception, so be ready to explain what a word does instead.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-14-2-guide2\n\nMatthew thinks he only walks along beside her, but that quiet company matters to Anne more than he knows. The box on this tab says something that might surprise you. Part of growing up is noticing a happy time while it's still happening. Little kids don't usually do that. They just have fun, and that's fine. But you might already know the feeling. Maybe it was the last night of a vacation, or a birthday when everyone you love was in one room, and you thought, I want to remember this. Near the end of this chapter, the writer steps out of the story to speak straight to you, the reader. When you reach that part, slow down and notice how it makes you feel.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-14-2-read\n\nI love picturing Matthew sitting perfectly still through that whole essay. That's how a quiet person shows he's proud. A few words in this chapter might be new. A commencement is a graduation ceremony, the day students finish at a school and celebrate together. At Queen's, some students read essays aloud in front of everyone. You'll also see the results posted on a board. Back then there were no phones or emails, so students crowded around a list on the wall to find out how they did. When you reach the Pause and Think stop, you'll find Matthew's words.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's what he says to Anne: \"My girl — my girl that I'm proud of.\"\n\nRead them a second time in your book, slowly. There's nothing to write there, so give yourself time to just think about what he means.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-14-2-guide3\n\nMatthew likes to tease Anne about her big words, but you can tell he's fond of every one of them. Your three Reading tab answers come along with everything you submit, so take a second look at your Mimic sentence. That pattern uses a semicolon, and a semicolon is worth understanding. It joins two complete sentences that belong together so closely you don't want a full stop between them. Each side has to be able to stand on its own. That connects to today's Daily Oral Language. The sentence you fixed there was two sentences jammed together, and a period was one good fix. A semicolon would have been another. Check that both halves of your Mimic sentence could each be a sentence by themselves.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-14-2-chapter\n\nYou can hear how much Matthew meant what he said, and it makes me want to read that part again. For question one, the hint tells you where Anne planned to go. Think about why she wanted to hide, and then what happened before she got there. Your answer will be strongest if it shows how fast her feelings flip in that one moment. For question two, think back to Chapter 2, when Matthew drove to Bright River expecting someone else entirely. Then look at what he tells Anne as they bring the cows home. Ask yourself why a quiet man like Matthew would say it that way. For question three, stick to what the line itself says and why an author might speak to readers directly.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-14-2-workshop\n\nI'm with Matthew. A two just shows you where the loose rail is, and that's useful to know. Scoring your own work is hard because you already know what you meant. So for each row, find proof in your story before you give yourself a number. For Word Choice, point to one sentence that's specific and vivid. If you can't find one, that row is probably a two. Look at what Sam did in Scene 2. He swapped a plain phrase for one you can actually picture, and the length barely changed. That's usually what moving from a two to a three looks like. You pick one row, but you name two changes inside it. Anne's work was judged at Queen's this week. Today you get to judge your own.\n","14-3":"# Lesson 14.3 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 37 · The Reaper Whose Name Is Death. Guide this lesson: Marilla. Each script plays right after Marilla's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-14-3\n\nHi, friends. Today is Chapter 37, and the title tells you this one is sad. Matthew dies in this chapter. I wanted you to know before you start, so it doesn't catch you by surprise. The page is quieter today. You'll warm up, do your word work, and then read. After that, you'll answer three questions and make a title page for your story. If you feel sad while you read, that's okay. Take a break whenever you need one.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-14-3-welcome\n\nMarilla asked you to go gently, and I'm asking the same. Be kind to yourself today. You've known Matthew since Chapter 2, when he drove to Bright River and found Anne waiting. He was the first one at Green Gables who wanted her to stay. Later, he noticed she longed for puffed sleeves and gave her that brown dress at Christmas. Keep those moments close as you read. Feeling sad for Matthew is one way of remembering him.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-14-3-guide1\n\nI'm grateful Marilla shared that. Steady, ordinary work can help on a heavy day. You already know these roots. Script and scribe mean to write, and ject means to throw. Read the clue beside each blank and ask which action it describes. You also know helping verbs set the time. In your warm-up, would points ahead to something that hadn't happened yet. Your spelling uses the same three rules you've practiced all week.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-14-3-guide2\n\nThat's honest of Marilla, admitting she didn't see it at first. The Growing-Up Watch box has a line worth reading twice. Being grown up is not feeling less. Some readers think a character who doesn't cry must not care. Anne shows that isn't true. All week you've put the old Anne next to the new one. Today, picture the younger Anne in this same hard moment. Read slowly, and stop at both Pause and Think questions.\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-14-3-rwm\n\nMarilla doesn't often talk about her soft side, so it's tender that she points to this sentence herself.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere are the sentences from Chapter 37: \"Anne wept her heart out. Marilla heard her and crept in to comfort her.\"\n\nWept her heart out is an old way of saying someone cried as hard as she could. Look at wept and crept. Neither one ends in e-d, so this week's suffix rules don't fit them. Sleep and slept work the same way. Notice how short both sentences are, too. When something enormous happens, Montgomery doesn't pile up words.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-14-3-guide3\n\nI'm glad Marilla says the laughing surprised her too. Grief can surprise grown-ups as well. Each question has two parts, so check that your answer covers both. The hint under each one shows you where to look. You've answered questions like these all week, so trust what you know. Then you'll write a dedication like Sam's, for his mom, who told him to try anyway. A dedication can go to someone beside you, or to someone you miss.\n","14-4":"# Lesson 14.4 teacher video scripts\n\nAnne of Green Gables, Ch. 38 · The Bend in the Road. Guide this lesson: Anne. Each script plays right after Anne's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-14-4\n\nHi, friends. This is it, the last chapter of Anne of Green Gables. Earlier this week Anne spent a winter at Queen's, won the Avery scholarship, and then lost Matthew. Today, in Chapter 38, she comes to a bend in her road. Your word work reviews the roots, helping verbs, and suffix rules you practiced in the first two lessons, so trust what you know. Then you'll read, look back across the whole book, and share your finished story.\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-14-4-welcome\n\nAnne says she might not judge herself fairly, and I like her even more for admitting it. The model sentence in today's Daily Oral Language comes from Mrs. Rachel Lynde. In the very first chapter, she sat at her window and decided taking in an orphan was a mistake. Now, in the last chapter, she's the one bringing Anne good news. Authors often circle back to where a story began, so watch for familiar faces today.\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-14-4-wgrd\n\nAnne would rather you decide, and that's a real compliment. She trusts your eyes more than her mirror. You already practiced this move at the start of the week with the frame on this page, Anne used to ___, and now she ___. Keep both blanks as things Anne actually does. The new part today is the half about what never changed. Give that one real thought, because it's often the more interesting answer.\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-14-4-dol\n\nI can just picture eleven-year-old Anne talking a mile a minute without stopping for breath. In the trustees' sentence, have decided tells you the deciding is already done. The sentence you're fixing has two halves at different times, one finished and one still ahead. You know from earlier this week what that usually means for where a sentence should stop. Read your version aloud and listen for the pause.\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-14-4-guide1\n\nAnne owning up to all those years of being wrong about Gilbert might be the most grown-up thing she's said yet. Everything on this tab comes from the week you just had. The roots, the four words, the helping verbs, and the suffix rules all showed up in the first two lessons. If one activity feels shaky, think back to the reason behind that rule and use the reason as your check.\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-14-4-morphology\n\nOf course Anne adores word parts. Anyone who loves big words as much as she does would. You've met these roots in both earlier lessons this week, from the projector on the wall to a manuscript written by hand. If a match gives you trouble, look at the examples box, because each example word already shows its parts split apart. The writing root fits Anne's ending, too, since she's about to teach a classroom of her own.\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-14-4-vocabulary\n\nAnne wanting every option on the menu is so like her. I'm proud of her for settling on just one. Obstinate is the word we haven't talked much about. It means stubborn, and Anne was obstinate for years about forgiving Gilbert. If you choose the card about finding a word in Chapter 38, keep ambition in mind as you read, and notice what Anne decides to do with her big plans.\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-14-4-grammar\n\nAnne's ear catching what her daydreaming eyes missed made me smile, because plenty of us read that way. Find the main verb first, then look to its left for a helper. Keep in mind from earlier this week that a verb like has or had can stand alone as the main verb, so a short sentence can still be complete. When you fix one in Show What You Know, say what time the helper puts the action in.\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-14-4-spelling\n\nAnne as the teacher checking everyone's work! Her students are going to know their suffix rules, I promise you that. You already know the reasons behind these three rules, like the double letter that guards a short vowel sound. That reason matters today, because Show What You Know asks how you'll remember the rule. Saying why a rule works is usually the best memory trick there is.\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-14-4-guide2\n\nI like the way Anne talks about that turn. She couldn't stop it, but she decided what to do next, and that takes courage.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nAnne says it herself in today's box: \"I don't know what lies around the bend, but I'm going to believe that the best does.\"\n\nAt the start of the week, this box talked about bitterness and the grudge Anne held against Gilbert. Today's box brings him back one last time and mentions the Carrots day in Chapter 15. As you read, notice how far the two of them have come since then.\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-14-4-read\n\nFive years of waiting without knowing it! Anne sure knows how to make a reader curious. This is the final chapter, and Matthew's death still hangs over Green Gables, so read it gently. Two names to know. Redmond is a college, the next big step Anne had planned after Queen's. Carmody is a nearby town with its own school.\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-14-4-guide3\n\nMiss Stacy would be so pleased to hear Anne passing her advice along. Good teachers' words have a way of sticking. A then sentence and a now sentence, joined by a word like but or instead, makes a change easy to see. Since helping verbs have been our grammar all week, read your answers once just for verbs, and check that Anne's plans for the future sound like the future.\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-14-4-chapter\n\nMatthew finding Anne on that pile of shingles feels like a long time ago now. Each question has a challenge that pushes further. For the first, the challenge asks about four little words.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nAnne tells Marilla: \"There is no sacrifice.\"\n\nThe challenge asks why she says that, so look for her own reason in what she tells Marilla. For the second, put the slate day in Chapter 15 beside the gate. For the third, your thinking from What Good Readers Do is already a strong start.\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-14-4-workshop\n\nSpecific over splendid. I might borrow that from Anne for my own classroom. Sam's after reflection works because he gives proof, a real example from his story about Mateo. When you reflect on your growth as a writer, look back at the rubric row you chose to improve this week. That's an easy place to find your example, because you know exactly what changed. And pick a listener who'll stay with you to the end.\n","14-fluency":"# Week 14 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 14 Fluency · your own published story. About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-14-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's fluency passage is different. You aren't reading Anne's words this time. You're reading your own story, the one you just published. That makes you the author and the reader at the same time, which is pretty special. You know better than anyone how each part should sound, so let your voice show it. If your character is nervous, let us hear that. If there's someone talking, give them a voice that fits. Slow down at the most important moment, the same moment you worked so hard on in Scene 3. And if you stumble over a sentence while you practice, that's useful information. It might be a sentence worth fixing. Be proud when you record this one.\n","15-1":"# Lesson 15.1 teacher video scripts\n\nBridge to Number the Stars, Day 1 · How World War II began. Guide this lesson: Grete, the narrator. Each script plays right after Grete's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-15-1\n\nHi, friends, and welcome to week fifteen. This week is a bridge. We just finished Anne of Green Gables, and next week we open a new novel called Number the Stars. It takes place in Denmark during World War II, so before we start it, we're spending four lessons on the real history underneath it. Today's reading tells how the war began, with Germany, Adolf Hitler and the Nazis, and the Allies who fought back. As you read, collect the names of countries and leaders, so they already feel familiar when the story starts. In Writer's Workshop, you'll begin a brand new opinion project. This is hard history. It's okay if it feels uncomfortable, and it's worth staying with.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-15-1-welcome\n\nSaying goodbye to Anne is a little sad. I'm glad Grete gave us a moment to notice it before we move on. Number the Stars is a kind of book called historical fiction. That means the main characters are made up, but the time and place around them are real. Some kids think that because a book is fiction, none of it happened. In historical fiction, the war, the countries, and the dates are true, and the author builds a story inside them. That's why we read real history first. The novel takes place in Denmark in 1943, right in the middle of the war. So we'll start at the very beginning, and the story will make more sense when we get there.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-15-1-wgrd\n\nI love Grete's suitcase. I'm the kind of packer who forgets a toothbrush, so I'll be making a list. A good suitcase isn't stuffed with everything, though. You don't need to memorize every date in the passage. Pack the facts the story will lean on, like who was fighting and which side each country was on. Then try the third step in the box. After each fact, picture a kid your age. If your country were at war, what might change? Maybe school, maybe food, maybe whether your family could stay together. On today's page there's a real photo of children in London in 1940, walking to a train that took them to the countryside to keep them safe. Facts like that are worth packing.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-15-1-dol\n\nGrete's right that every capital in the model has a job. I like treating them like little clues. Months get capitals because each one is a name, like April in the model. Seasons don't. Spring, summer, fall, and winter stay lowercase unless they start a sentence, and that one catches a lot of people. For finding where one thought ends, try this. Read the sentence out loud and listen for each complete idea. Each one needs its own who and its own action. When you hear a second who doing a second action, a new sentence has started, and it needs a stop in front of it. Then check your fix the same way you checked the model, one capital at a time.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-15-1-guide1\n\nI agree with Grete. The rules I figured out myself as a kid are the ones I still remember. Some of today's word work might feel strange at first, because you're asked to sort or guess before anybody explains. A wrong guess isn't a failure here. It's part of the job. The trap is jumping straight to the check box. If you peek first, your brain just copies, and the rule slides right back out again. In grammar and spelling, you'll also name the groups yourself. Your names don't have to match anyone else's. They just have to fit what you noticed. Then the Show What You Know boxes ask you to explain your thinking, and that's where the learning really sticks.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-15-1-morphology\n\nA bridge that grows one plank at a time is a lovely picture, and I can't wait to see ours reach the far shore. Lots of word parts at the front of words are about direction or position, and you already know some. Sub means under, like a submarine that travels under the water. Pre means before, like a preview you watch before a movie comes out. One thing to watch. Pre and pro look almost the same, but they don't mean the same thing, so read every letter. And when someone calls a great player a pro, that's just a short way to say professional. It's a whole word, not the word part on this page. So always check the meaning, not just the first few letters.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-15-1-vocabulary\n\nGuessing first really does make a word yours. It's like finding the trail instead of being handed a map. One of today's words has a second meaning you might already know. Occupation can mean a person's job, like nurse or teacher. That isn't the meaning on your card, so let the sentence about Denmark steer you. Notice that Resistance in the example sentence has a capital R. That's because it's the name of a real group of people, the same way the Allies is a name. You'll hear more about that group this week, and someone in the novel belongs to it. When you flip each card, a close guess in your own words counts.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-15-1-grammar\n\nI like that Grete wants you to name the groups. Names you make up yourself really do stay put. These little words cause one of the most common slips in English. People say things like, Me and my friend went to the park. Should it be me or I? The trick is to cover up the friend. You'd never say, Me went to the park. You'd say, I went to the park. So it's, My friend and I went. The same trick works the other way. Grandma drove my friend and I sounds fancy, but cover the friend, and you'd never say, Grandma drove I. When you sort today, use that same cover-up thinking. Put each word into a short sentence of your own before you decide where it goes.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-15-1-spelling\n\nYour ear knows more than you think. I love that, and it's true. You've been hearing these sounds since you were a baby. A vowel team is two letters sharing one sound, and sometimes one of those letters is a w or a y. The tricky part is that letters don't promise a sound. Think about the pair o and w. In snow it says one thing, and in cow it says something completely different. So don't sort with your eyes. Say each word at a normal speed, the way you'd say it to a friend, not stretched out like a robot. Then ask yourself what sound you actually heard, and sort by that sound, not by the letters you see.\n\n\n## Say It Three Ways · g4ela-15-1-copia\n\nI like Grete's idea that each new version shows something the first one left out. That's my favorite part of this practice, because there's never just one right answer. When you pick a stronger verb, make sure it still tells the truth. A verb like exploded or vanished might sound exciting, but wars don't vanish. Choose a verb that's strong and still accurate. For the new beginning, try moving a piece that's already in the sentence to the front, or start with a word about time. Read it aloud afterward to make sure it still sounds like one smooth sentence. For the detail, ask yourself where or how, then check the passage so your detail is a real fact, not a guess.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-15-1-guide2\n\nCalling it a small brave thing is just right. Reading hard history really does take a little courage. Courage doesn't have to be big to count. It can be telling the truth when you broke something, or sitting with the new kid when nobody else does. In both cases, you feel nervous and you do it anyway. The Courage Watch box asks you to name one small moment when you were brave and scared at the same time. Think of a real one, even if it seems tiny. All quarter, we'll watch people in real history and in our novel make choices like that. If something in today's reading makes you sad or worried, that's normal. You can always talk about it with a grown-up you trust.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-15-1-read\n\nPicturing a child your age is such a good way to read history. I'll be doing it right along with you. As you make your two lists, watch for one country that shows up in both of them. That isn't a mistake in the passage. A country can be taken over and still have people fighting to win it back. Remember too that a country's name stands for millions of people who didn't choose any of this, most of them just trying to stay safe. One more thing to notice. Norway is on the list, and Denmark, where our novel takes place, was taken on the very same morning in April 1940. You'll read all about that in the next lesson.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-15-1-guide3\n\nGrete's advice to keep the passage open is the best tip I know. Even grown-up writers check their sources. When a question asks for details from the passage, a strong move is to name where your proof comes from. You can start with words like, The passage says, or, According to the passage. Then give the exact fact, a name or a date or a country. One habit to break is copying a whole paragraph and calling it evidence. Pick only the part that answers the question, then add a sentence in your own words about what it shows. Check your spelling of names, too. Countries and leaders are names, so each one gets a capital letter, just like in your Daily Oral Language sentence.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-15-1-chapter\n\nI like how Grete puts the three questions together into one shape, like three puzzle pieces. Each question also has a challenge line, so read it before you answer. One asks how long the war lasted, so you'll need two dates and a little subtraction. Another asks what the invasions tell you about Hitler's goal. That's a thinking question. The passage gives you the facts, and you explain what they add up to. On question two, make sure every country you list is one the passage says was invaded, so read that paragraph carefully. For question three, the challenge asks why it mattered that so many countries joined together. Think about what changes when many stand together instead of one standing alone.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-15-1-workshop\n\nGrete makes it sound simple, and Sam's two questions really do the work. One sneaky thing. Checkable doesn't mean true. If I said the moon is made of cheese, you could check that, so it belongs in the fact pile. It just fails the check. An opinion is different. No amount of checking can prove Sam's pizza sentence right or wrong, because it's about what someone likes. Little words can be clues, too. Best, worst, fun, and should usually mean you're looking at an opinion. When you write your own, pick something you really care about, like your favorite game or your pet's funniest habit. Opinions are much easier to write when you actually mean them.\n","15-2":"# Lesson 15.2 teacher video scripts\n\nBridge to Number the Stars, Day 2 · Denmark in 1940. Guide this lesson: Grete, the narrator. Each script plays right after Grete's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 30 to 60 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-15-2\n\nHi, friends. Welcome to the second lesson of our bridge week. In the last lesson, you learned how World War II began in 1939, and how Germany took over one country after another until the Allies joined together to stop it. Today we slow down and look closely at just one of those countries, Denmark. That's where our new book takes place, so this is the history the story will expect you to carry in with you. You'll read about what changed in Denmark after the German army arrived, and what stayed almost the same. Then you'll meet the Danish king and a quiet kind of courage. In Writer's Workshop, you'll take an opinion and shape it into one clear claim.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-15-2-welcome\n\nI like that idea of zooming in, like pulling a map closer until one small country fills the whole screen. So let's find it. Denmark sits in the north of Europe, right on top of Germany, and the two countries share a border. Most of Denmark is a peninsula, which means land with water on three sides, and the rest is islands. It's a small country, smaller than the state of Florida. Its capital is Copenhagen, on the east side, and that city is where the novel's main character, a girl named Annemarie, lives. As you read, keep a picture in your head of that small country sitting right next to a huge, powerful neighbor. That picture will help a lot of today's facts make sense.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-15-2-wgrd\n\nA number holding a whole picture inside it. I had never thought of a date that way, and it's true. A date can feel like a fact for a test, but real people lived through that day. Try it with something closer to home. If the power goes out for one hour, it's a little adventure with flashlights. If it goes out for a whole month, the picture changes completely. Same kind of fact, very different lives. Just remember that circling the numbers is only the first step. A circled number with no thinking next to it is decoration. And sometimes the smallest number is the most surprising one. Keep today's numbers handy, because the novel opens in 1943, three years into all of this.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-15-2-dol\n\nI love Grete's way of saying that names start tall. It's a nice way to picture a capital letter standing up straight. Look at the name in the model, King Christian X. That X isn't a letter in his name. It's the Roman numeral for ten. When kings and queens share a name, they get numbers so people can tell them apart, and we read it aloud as Christian the Tenth. The word king gets a capital here because it's part of his title, right before his name. When you just say the king, with no name after it, it stays small. For the run-together part, read your sentence aloud and listen for where your voice wants to drop and stop.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-15-2-guide1\n\nGrete's warning about answers that look almost right is a good one. Those near misses are what make finding harder than sorting. In the last lesson, you sorted every word into a group, so each one had a place to go. This time, some words are meant to be left alone, and that takes a little self-control. A trick that helps is to ask the same question about every single word before you tap. Don't tap a word because it looks familiar. Tap it because it passed your test. Also read each set of directions twice. One activity asks for the words that break the pattern, not the ones that follow it, and that's easy to flip by accident.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-15-2-morphology\n\nGrete's tip about letting the meaning lead is smart, because a word can sound strange and still make perfect sense once you know its parts. Look at the two examples on the page. When a radio station transmits a song, it sends the song across the air to your house. When a town crier proclaims the news, he calls it out forward so the whole square can hear. The part pro has a second meaning on this page, too. It can mean in favor of. If you've ever made a list of pros and cons, the pros are the reasons in favor of something. So when a word starts with pro, ask yourself whether it's moving forward or cheering something on.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-15-2-vocabulary\n\nGrete turned the cards into a guessing game, and a guess made first really does help a word stick. Look for smaller words hiding inside bigger ones. Inside one of today's cards you'll find govern, the same word that hides in governor. The ending ment turns an action into a thing, the way move becomes movement and pay becomes payment. Watch out for a sneaky one, though. Arrest has the word rest inside it, but it isn't about taking a nap. A hidden word can steer you wrong, so always check it against the sentence. For the Quick Match, you met those four words in the last lesson, so see how many you remember before you start tapping.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-15-2-grammar\n\nGrete's swap test is a lifesaver, and I use it on my own writing. Another clue is where the word sits. The words that receive the action usually come after the action word, or after a small word like to, for, with, or from. Dad made pancakes for us. Come with me. The dog followed me home. In each one, the pronoun comes after, and it's the receiving kind. Now flip that last one around. I followed the dog home. Same two characters, but now I'm doing the following, so the pronoun moved to the front and changed its shape. As you tap today, ask where each word sits and what it's doing there. The same person can be a doer in one sentence and a receiver in the next.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-15-2-spelling\n\nSoft and mumbly is the perfect description. Those quick parts of a word are where spelling gets slippery. Think of captain and curtain. Say them normally, and the second half sounds like tin, not like rain. That's why people sometimes write captin. You can't hear the right letters, so you have to remember them with your eyes. One trick is a spelling voice. Say the word in a slow, silly way that rhymes the end with rain, so your brain stores the letters you can't hear. Use that voice only for practice, though. When you hunt for today's exceptions, say each word slowly, then say it once more inside a normal sentence, because that's where the mumbling hides.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-15-2-guide2\n\nShowing up again and again. Grete said it in five words, and that is most of what courage is. It rarely comes with a parade. The picture on this tab shows King Christian riding through a crowded Copenhagen street on his seventieth birthday, in September of 1940, a few months after the German army arrived. Look at how many people came out to see him. A king riding past might not sound brave, but every ride said something without any words at all. Raising your hand again the day after you got an answer wrong in front of everyone is the same kind of courage. It isn't loud, but people notice. The children in our novel still talk about the king and his horse, so keep this picture.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-15-2-read\n\nGrete's idea of a surface and an underneath is a good way into this passage. Picture a pond that looks still on top while the ducks paddle hard underneath. That is Copenhagen in the novel, where German soldiers stand on the street corners while ordinary life goes on around them. One word first. Here's the line.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nThe passage says: \"The Danish king, Christian X, stayed on his throne.\"\n\nThat means he was still the king. The chair stands for the whole job. The passage comes from the United States Holocaust Memorial Museum in Washington, D.C., which teaches this history carefully. Be gentle with yourself as you read. One sentence is about people being arrested just for speaking up, and it's fine to feel uneasy about that. Notice the feeling, and keep reading.\n\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-15-2-rwm\n\nTwo small words turning a corner.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's the sentence from today's article, Denmark Under German Occupation: \"The German leaders wanted to keep the Danes calm so that food and supplies would keep flowing to Germany.\"\n\nRead it aloud yourself and you can feel it bend right at so that. Watch out for a close cousin, though. The word so by itself and the words so that do different jobs. In I was tired, so I went to bed, the word so tells you what happened next. In I went to bed early so that I would have energy for my game, so that tells you why someone did something on purpose. It points to a goal. The writer's sentence uses the goal kind. When you write your Mimic sentence, check that the second half explains why the group wanted something, not just what happened after. Your group could be a team, a class, a family, or a town.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-15-2-guide3\n\nKeeping the passage close is good advice, since the proof for every answer is sitting right there. This passage has two groups of people in it, the Danes and the Germans. When you write about both, the word they can get confusing fast. If you write, The Germans watched the Danes, and they were careful, your reader can't tell which group was careful. When that happens, use the group's name again instead of they. It might feel repetitive, but clear is better than short. The same goes for he when you write about the king and a German leader in the same answer. Before you move on, reread each answer and stop at every they. Ask yourself who it points to.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-15-2-chapter\n\nGrete's last point matters. When someone chooses something hard on purpose, it tells you a lot about them. For question one, notice that it asks two things, what happened and how the king responded. Answer both, and use what the passage says for the second part, not a guess about his feelings. For question two, the hint gives you a strong clue with the word not. Sometimes history is about what didn't happen. Write each way as its own complete sentence so your reader can count them. For question three, the passage tells you what the Resistance was, but it never says exactly why people joined. That part is yours to think through, so explain it in your own words and point to the passage for support.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-15-2-workshop\n\nNo maybe and no kind of. I'm going to borrow that, because wobbly words sneak into lots of writing. Two things trip people up. First, a claim can't be a fact. Dogs have four legs is true, but nobody would argue with it, so it can't be a claim. You learned that difference in the last lesson, and it matters even more now. Second, a claim isn't a question. Which is better, dogs or cats? doesn't take a side at all. Also watch for other wobbly words, like pretty, sort of, and probably. Compare Kids should probably get more recess with Kids should get more recess. The second one sounds like you mean it. Pick a topic you'd really argue about with a friend.\n","15-3":"# Lesson 15.3 teacher video scripts\n\nBridge to Number the Stars, Day 3 · The yellow star and Denmark's answer. Guide this lesson: Grete, the narrator. Each script plays right after Grete's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-15-3\n\nHi, friends. This is the third lesson of our bridge week. You've learned how the war began, and how Germany took over Denmark while King Christian kept riding through Copenhagen. Today's reading is about the yellow star the Nazis forced Jewish people to wear in many countries, and how Denmark answered differently. It's a hard subject, so go gently. Keep the title Number the Stars in mind, and make a guess about it. In Writer's Workshop, you'll test whether your claims can really be argued.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-15-3-welcome\n\nGrete calls it hard and important, and both are true. The six-pointed star in today's reading is called the Star of David. It's a symbol of the Jewish people and their faith, carried with pride for a very long time. The Nazis took a symbol people loved and turned it into a way to single them out. If that feels unfair and upsetting, your feelings are right on track. A necklace with this star turns up early in the novel, so remember what it means.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-15-3-wgrd\n\nHolding on to a puzzle is something I do with mystery books all the time, so I'm glad to see it here. In the last lesson, you circled numbers and pictured the lives inside them. Today is different. You aren't explaining a fact yet. You're keeping it safe for later. Star it, write one line of guessing, and move on. A wrong guess is no problem at all. You'll check it once the story begins.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-15-3-dol\n\nI like Grete's reminder that a capital can show up in the middle of a sentence, not just at the start. Words for a people or a language come from names, so they keep their capital wherever they go. That's why we write French toast and English class, but math class stays small. Math isn't anyone's name. Then use what you practiced earlier this week to find where the first complete thought stops.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-15-3-guide1\n\nYour ear catching what your eye misses is so true. I catch my own typos that way. Earlier this week you sorted, and then you found. Today you build, which is the hardest step, because no answer is sitting there waiting to be tapped. Write your best word first, before you check anything. If the check shows a mistake, don't just erase it. Figure out which rule slipped, so the fix sticks.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-15-3-morphology\n\nTwo lamps glowing behind us is a lovely sight, like the bridge keeping a light on for the way back. The part meaning across shows up in words you might not expect. When you translate, you carry words across from one language into another. A propeller pushes a boat forward through the water. Today you type the part yourself, so spell it carefully. Remember that pro and pre look alike but point in different directions.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-15-3-vocabulary\n\nHard words for a hard subject. I'm glad Grete didn't pretend otherwise. This page walks through require one step at a time, so use those same three steps on every card. Watch one word that can trick you. Refuse, said with the push on the first part, is a different word that means trash. Same letters, new meaning. Let the sentence above the card tell you which one you're reading.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-15-3-grammar\n\nGrete boiled it down to one question, and I love a rule that fits in a pocket. Try it with we and us. We cleaned the classroom. We're doing the cleaning. The coach thanked us. The thanking happens to us. Earlier this week you sorted and found these forms. Now you're the one writing them, so read each finished sentence aloud. If it sounds odd, like Us cleaned the classroom, swap it.\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-15-3-spelling\n\nStretching out the middle of a word is a good trick. I always picture pulling taffy. The second decision is about stress, which just means the part of the word you say a little louder. Say explain. The push lands on the end, and the team there sounds clear. A team in a quiet part can mumble, like the words you hunted earlier this week. So find the loud part first, then choose.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-15-3-guide2\n\nGrete's list of ordinary people makes me think of my own neighbors, the ones who'd notice something wrong. In the last lesson, one person, the king, showed courage just by riding out. Today the box widens to teachers, fishermen, and schoolchildren. None of them had a crown, and the novel is told through a schoolgirl's eyes for exactly that reason. When the box asks for your own small brave choice, pick one you could really do, like speaking up when a classmate is left out.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-15-3-read\n\nA story that isn't quite true. I'm curious what you'll decide about that one. Before you start, look over both Pause and Think questions, just like the page says. One sentence tells what happened to Jewish families in other countries. It's short and serious, and it's okay to slow down there. The passage names four countries before it turns to Denmark.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nThen it says: \"Denmark was different. The Danish government refused to require Jewish people to wear the star.\"\n\nKeep them in mind, because that comparison is the heart of this reading.\n\n\n## Read Like a Writer · g4ela-15-3-rwm\n\nWhen Grete says the end lands with weight, read the sentence aloud and you'll hear exactly where.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nHere's the sentence from today's article, The Star of David Law: \"The Nazis used this badge to mark Jewish people as different — and as targets.\"\n\nDon't mix up the dash with a hyphen, the short mark inside a word like well-known. A dash is longer, and it sits between words to make you wait. For your Mimic, pick a real object or rule you know well, like a fence, a lock, or a uniform. Then save the dash for the part that's harder to say.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-15-3-guide3\n\nGrete's right to slow you down on that last question. It's the one where your own thinking shows most. Earlier this week you practiced naming where your proof comes from, and using a name again when they gets confusing. Use both today. When you compare, name both sides in the same answer, Denmark and the other countries, so your reader can see the difference without guessing.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-15-3-chapter\n\nGrete's idea that an untrue story can still hold something true is one I think about a lot. Think about family stories, like Grandpa catching a fish as big as a canoe. Maybe it wasn't that big, but the story shows how proud everyone was of him. For the first two questions, the hints send you to a paragraph, so use the passage's words, not a guess. For the last one, explain your idea, then point to a line that backs it up.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-15-3-workshop\n\nA smart, fair person is a great judge to picture. I imagine a friend who'd politely disagree with me about pineapple on pizza. One trap, though. Adding I think doesn't turn a fact into a claim. I think the sun is hot still has no one on the other side. Change what the sentence says, not just how it starts. And when you write what someone might say back, give them a real reason, not just no, it isn't.\n","15-4":"# Lesson 15.4 teacher video scripts\n\nBridge to Number the Stars, Day 4 · The October 1943 rescue. Guide this lesson: Grete, the narrator. Each script plays right after Grete's audio clip in the same spot, so it adds to what she says instead of repeating it. About 20 to 30 seconds each, since this is a review day.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-15-4\n\nHi, friends. Our bridge is almost finished. Three lessons of history are behind you, and today's reading is the one all of them were leading to. You'll read about October 1943, when ordinary Danish people helped thousands of Jewish families escape across the sea. Your word work is review, so trust what you practiced. In Writer's Workshop, you'll choose your strongest claim. Next week, the story finally begins.\n\n\n## Welcome · g4ela-15-4-welcome\n\nFear and courage in the same story. Grete's right, and they usually do show up together. Think about how long the Danes had been waiting by then. The German army arrived in April 1940, and this rescue came in October 1943. That's more than three years of living under someone else's rules. Annemarie, the girl in the novel, is ten when it opens, so the soldiers arrived when she was about six. Keep that long wait in mind as you read.\n\n\n## What Good Readers Do · g4ela-15-4-wgrd\n\nFour passages, each explaining a little of the next. I like picturing them as four puzzle pieces finally clicking together. Try the first step in the box quickly. Write just one short line for each day, not a summary. Then look for a link, like a fact from the first lesson that today's passage suddenly makes clearer. The last step is my favorite. Your big question is something only the novel can answer, so save it for next week.\n\n\n## Daily Oral Language · g4ela-15-4-dol\n\nFishermen and the sea is a fitting model for today. I can almost smell the salt water. A word made from a country's name keeps its capital, even when it describes something else. Think of a Danish pastry, Swedish meatballs, or a German shepherd. All three come from country names, so they stand tall. Earlier this week you capitalized countries and a king's title. This is the same idea, just one step further.\n\n\n## Before the word work · g4ela-15-4-guide1\n\nCareful readers check before they change. I might put that on a sign in my classroom. Earlier this week, you hunted for words that broke a pattern. Today the hunt is for mistakes, and some items are already right. Try reading each activity all the way through once without tapping anything. Then go back for a second pass. Mistakes are much easier to spot when you aren't rushing to find them.\n\n\n## Morphology · g4ela-15-4-morphology\n\nThe last planks, and every lamp lit. I'll admit I'm a little proud of this bridge. You know both parts well now, across and forward, and you also remember that the forward part can mean in favor of. On today's fill-ins, read the meaning first and ask which direction it points. Then say the whole word aloud. If it sounds like a word you've really heard, you've probably laid the plank in the right spot.\n\n\n## Vocabulary · g4ela-15-4-vocabulary\n\nPicturing the moment is such a good habit. Some of these sentences really do play like a movie in your head. Look at the example word survive on this tab. The part that means to live shows up in other words, too. When a doctor helps someone who fainted wake back up, we say they revive the person, or bring them back to life. For the Quick Match, you've seen those four words all week, so trust your memory.\n\n\n## Grammar · g4ela-15-4-grammar\n\nReading out loud really does help. My ear catches mistakes my eyes skip right over. Earlier this week you used the cover-up trick, taking away the other person to hear the pronoun alone. That still works here. Cover up the extra person, say the sentence with just the pronoun, and trust what you hear. Then ask the question from the first lesson: is this word doing the action, or receiving it?\n\n\n## Spelling · g4ela-15-4-spelling\n\nSoft and quiet vowels in the middle of a word are sneaky, and Grete's warning is a good one. Earlier this week you practiced a spelling voice, saying a word slowly and a little silly so you could hear every letter. Use it again here, then trust your eyes. When you find a word you think is wrong, picture it written on a sign. Does it look the way you've seen it before? If you aren't sure, leave it alone.\n\n\n## Before you open the book · g4ela-15-4-guide2\n\nChoosing not to forget is a quiet kind of courage, and I think it's one of the most important kinds. Remembering can start very close to home. Maybe a grandparent once helped a neighbor, or an aunt stood up for someone at work. Asking them to tell you that story is a way of remembering, too. The Think question in this box asks about someone in your life. Pick a real person you'd be proud to write about someday.\n\n\n## Before you read · g4ela-15-4-read\n\nNot one hero, but many. That's what makes this story so special to me. The photo on this tab shows the water between Denmark and Sweden. At its narrowest, the Swedish coast is less than three miles away, though many boats had to cross much wider stretches. Picture doing that in a small fishing boat in the cold of October.\n\n[QUOTE ON SCREEN: press 6, read the line, press 6 again]\n\nThe passage says it simply: \"Fishermen offered their boats.\"\n\nAnnemarie's uncle is a fisherman, so this water matters in the novel. One sentence names where the Nazis planned to send people. Read it gently.\n\n\n## Before you write · g4ela-15-4-guide3\n\nThe week's last writing already! It went by faster than I expected. The third question asks you to look across all four readings. The one line per day you wrote for What Good Readers Do is a perfect place to start. The challenge line for that question asks for facts from at least two different days, so name which reading each fact comes from. That way your reader can follow you across the whole week.\n\n\n## Chapter Thinking · g4ela-15-4-chapter\n\nA comparison is only as strong as its facts. I'm going to remember that one. Comparing gets easier with the right small words. Try but, while, or unlike to join your two sides. For example, unlike my brother, who loves spicy food, I can't eat even a little. One side, then the other, in one smooth sentence. Make sure each side has its own real fact from the passage, not a general feeling.\n\n\n## Writer's Workshop · g4ela-15-4-workshop\n\nHaving the most to say beats feeling the most strongly. Wise tip from Grete. Count your reasons on your fingers, and check that each finger is really a new reason. A later bedtime is fun, and a later bedtime is nice, is one reason said twice. A later bedtime gives me time to finish my chapter is a new one. Pick topics from your own life, like chores, screen time, or the best board game. You'll find more reasons when you know a topic well.\n","15-fluency":"# Week 15 fluency teacher video script\n\nWeek 15 Fluency · how Denmark answered the Nazis (background reading). About 30 to 60 seconds.\n\n## Lesson video · g4ela-15-fluency\n\nHi, friends. This week's fluency passage isn't from a novel. It's real history, four sentences about Denmark during the war, and it's the background for the book we start next week. Real history asks for a careful voice. Not sad, and not dramatic, just steady, the way you'd tell someone something important you want them to believe. Slow down on the date so your listener can hold onto it. Then listen to how the passage turns. It starts with a country giving up in a few hours, and then the word But changes everything. Let your voice lift a little on extraordinary, because that's the word the whole passage leans on. Read it three times, and record the one that sounds most like you mean it.\n"}};
